INSERT INTO unit_detail (slug, author, author_short, title_arabic, title_language, study_track, blurb_html, edition_note, cover_key, pdf_key, published_at, source_commit) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'Asif Hussain', 'Asif Hussain', 'سورة الفاتحة', 'ar', 'theology', '<p>This book is a series of spoken sessions, delivered aloud to an audience and afterward set down as prose. It takes the opening chapter of the Quran, Surah Al-Fateha, and reads it slowly, almost phrase by phrase, drawing out of each word far more than a first hearing would suggest is there.</p>', NULL, 'surah-al-fateha/cover.png', 'surah-al-fateha/book.pdf', '2026-09-01T23:56:32Z', '9dcd3c856fc68c1ebd2ae397f48bd2f04fc08e61');
DELETE FROM chapter WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha';
INSERT INTO chapter (slug, anchor_key, idx, title, html, word_count) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'introduction to the book', 1, 'Introduction to the Book', '', 228);
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>This book is a series of spoken sessions, delivered aloud to an audience and afterward set down as prose. It takes the opening chapter of the Quran, Surah Al-Fateha, and reads it slowly, almost phrase by phrase, drawing out of each word far more than a first hearing would suggest is there.</p>
<p>The series belongs to a larger conversation about knowing one''s master. It begins where the speaker says understanding must begin: not with theology but with friendship, and from friendship with love, because the name Allah reaches back to a root the sessions approach through the Quranic idea of love. From there the talks move outward — the linguistic meaning of Allah, of Rabb, of REHMA, and the striking image of the womb as the source of mercy. They ask what worship truly is, what it means that Allah is the Lord of all the worlds, and how being differs from attribute. They turn to the Day of Judgment, to the motives that move a person toward piety, and at last to the two petitions the surah teaches us to make: guidance to the straight path, and the steadfastness to remain upon it.</p>
<p>The speaker is the author of these sessions; he gave them, and they carry his voice throughout. What the reader receives is a patient, close reading meant to make a familiar prayer new again.</p>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'introduction to the book';
INSERT INTO chapter (slug, anchor_key, idx, title, html, word_count) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'quranic friendship', 2, 'Quranic Friendship', '', 6066);
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>The self has already received close attention, as has the theme of the Quran. What opens now is a new group of topics, gathered under the heading &quot;Know Thy Master.&quot; Within this group, the focus falls on Allah — His various attributes, His relationship with us, and what He expects of us. The ground to be covered includes His proof of existence, His mercy, and the matter of fate and destiny.</p>
<p>Any serious discussion of Allah begins with the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>, because the name Allah grows from the root <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>. To grasp the depth of that root, however, requires first understanding the Quranic concept of love: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وِلع</span>. The Islamic conception of love differs sharply from what Bollywood and Hollywood offer under that name. Understanding <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> therefore demands a clear account of love — and understanding love, in turn, demands starting with friendship. Love always begins there. No genuine love for a person takes root until friendship with that person has first been established. Real friendship, when it deepens, flowers into pure love.</p>
<p>Lust needs no friendship. This is why one-night stands and casual, meaningless sex spread through society — they are rooted in lust, in the base animal instinct of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُوَّۃُ الشَّھْوَانِیَّۃِ</span> studied earlier. Animals do not need to become friends before they mate. Love, however, always begins in the sentiments of friendship. The Arabs say that whenever the plant of love grows, it grows in the soil of friendship. Before turning to the term <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>, then, the various shades of love must first be laid out — and before that, the different types of friendship the Quran speaks of must be understood.</p>
<p>In the present age, genuine friendship has grown extraordinarily difficult to form. The world has moved toward virtual connections — the kind sustained by social media platforms and electronic devices. Human beings have grown so enslaved to their phones that a simple fifteen-minute conversation between two people, uninterrupted by the pull of a screen, has become nearly impossible. The more tightly connected people have become to their devices, the more disconnected they have become from one another. Real, unhurried conversation between human beings has all but vanished. Instead of owning these devices, people are owned by them. The device has become the master; its user, the obedient slave. The moment it sounds, everything else is dropped and attention snaps to the screen in total submission — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سمعنا و اطعنا</span> — I hear and I obey.</p>
<p>The parallel is almost comic. People with asthma reach for an inhaler every few minutes because they cannot breathe. Phones have made us the same way. The frantic search for a signal — the inability to simply sit without one — has become its own kind of respiratory crisis. Not long ago, I was telling my daughter about the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">کعبۃ</span> and about going for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عمرۃ</span>. I asked her what she would like to know about Mecca. The first thing she asked was whether they have Wi-Fi there. As sad as it is, that is what life has been reduced to.</p>
<p>The Quranic concept of friendship, then, deserves serious attention — and the angle from which it is approached makes all the difference. There are two ways to enter this subject:</p>
<ul>
<li>The first is to ask what kind of friends one should look for.</li>
<li>The second is to ask what kind of friend one should be to others.</li>
</ul>
<p>The first approach is the selfish one, and the Quran does not endorse it. The correct approach is the second: not what kind of friends to have, but what kind of friend to be. The distinction matters enormously. When a person becomes the right kind of friend to others, Allah places the right kind of people in that person''s path.</p>
<p>The Quran uses at least ten distinct words for friendship, each carrying its own shade of meaning and appearing in contexts that illuminate something specific about the relationship. Moving through some of these terms reveals what Allah is teaching about the different kinds of bonds between people.</p>
<h3 id="protective-friend">Protective Friend (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ولی</span>)</h3>
<p>The first term is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ولی</span>. It derives from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوَالات</span>, which conveys the idea of protection. A <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ولی</span> is someone who manages another''s affairs and always has that person''s back. When trouble strikes — of any kind — the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ولی</span> is there to guard and shield. He does not merely help after the fact; he handles the situation entirely. A <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ولی</span> therefore denotes not friendship in a general sense but something stronger: a dependable ally who can be counted on both to pull someone out of a crisis and to keep that person from falling into one in the first place. One of the names of Allah is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الولی</span>.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Baqarah: 257</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱللَّهُ وَلِىُّ ٱلَّذِينَ آمَنُوا۟ يُخْرِجُهُم مِّنَ ٱلظُّلُمَتِ إِلَى ٱلنُّورِ ۖ وَٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوٓا۟ أَوْلِيَآؤُهُمُ ٱلطَّغُوتُ يُخْرِجُونَهُم مِّنَ ٱلنُّورِ إِلَى ٱلظُّلُمَتِ ۗ أُو۟لَٓئِكَ أَصْحَبُ ٱلنَّارِ ۖ هُمْ فِيهَا خَلِدُونَ</span></p><p class="tr">Allah is the <em>Wali </em> of those who believe. He brings them out from darkness into light. But as for those who disbelieve, their <em>Auliya </em> are <em>Taghut </em>, they bring them out from light into darkness. Those are the dwellers of the Fire, and they will abide therein forever.</p></blockquote>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Baqarah: 257</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أللَّهُ وَلِىُّ ٱلَّذِينَ آمَنُوا۟</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Allah is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ولی</span> of those who believe. He is a protective friend to those who believe.</p>
<p>A related form of this same root is the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مولا</span>. It describes that same caring, concerned friend who also holds the ability to protect — specifically when he takes on a position of authority. The idea is this: when someone is entirely convinced that another person truly cares for them, truly has their best interests at heart, and they simply submit to that person''s authority and heed what he says, that person will protect them from trouble, or help them out of it when they are already in it. The one who stands in that position of authority over their affairs is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مولا</span>.</p>
<p>This is precisely why the phrase <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْلَانَا عَلِیٌّ</span> carries such weight. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مولا</span> is used there not only to affirm his authority over our affairs, but, more fundamentally, to establish that he is our caring and concerned friend — the one who is there to protect us, whose teachings exist to protect us and keep us from harm. He is someone who always has our back; his teachings always have our best interests at heart. By placing the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مولا</span> before his name, the testimony affirms his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ولایۃ</span> — his genuine, sincere love, care, and concern for us — precisely in order to protect us.</p>
<p>There is a striking adjective the Quran applies to this word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ولی</span> in one verse. That word is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حمیم</span>. Allah says:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Fussilat: 34</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَا تَسْتَوِى ٱلْحَسَنَةُ وَلَا ٱلسَّيِّئَةُ ۚ ٱدْفَعْ بِٱلَّتِى هِىَ أَحْسَنُ فَإِذَا ٱلَّذِى بَيْنَكَ وَبَيْنَهُۥ عَدَوَةٌۭ كَأَنَّهُۥ وَلِىٌّ حَمِيمٌۭ</span></p></blockquote>
<blockquote><p>The good deed and the evil deed cannot be equal. Repel with one which is better, then verily! he, between whom and you there was enmity, as though he was a close friend.</p></blockquote>
<p>Even where there is open hostility between two people — where one speaks with the most aggressive language imaginable while the other remains kind and respectful — Allah''s instruction is to respond as a protective friend would: to continue behaving as though genuinely looking out for that person, to answer in the gentlest way possible. Then Allah adds the adjective <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حمیم</span>.</p>
<p>The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حمیم</span> comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُمَّۃ</span>, which means fever — a word Arabic uses for intense heat. In this verse, that heat describes the intimacy of friendship. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حمیم</span> is the closeness in which there is a warmth between two people, a felt nearness. The heat here is not romantic; it is the inner warmth that rises when someone''s company moves a person deeply. A <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حمیم</span> is a friend whose presence leaves one feeling all warm and fuzzy inside. And the Quran places this standard precisely where it is hardest to meet: toward someone who is being aggressive, someone acting with the coarsest hostility imaginable. If that is how a believer must conduct himself toward an enemy, the standard toward a fellow believer is higher still. SubhanAllah — the Quran sets a breathtaking measure for what it means to live as a believer. And yet Islam is called a violent religion.</p>
<p>That, then, is the first kind of friend a believer is called to be toward another — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ولی حمیم</span>.</p>
<h3 id="sincere-friend">Sincere Friend (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صدیق</span>)</h3>
<p>The second kind of friend is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صدیق</span>. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صدیق</span> comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صدق</span>, which carries two meanings: sincerity and truthfulness. A <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صدیق</span>, then, is a friend of the most genuine and truthful kind. To be a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صدیق</span> to someone means the friendship rests on nothing but a sincere desire to be with that person. It is not friendship pursued because the association raises one''s standing at school or at work, or because others will think more highly of the company one keeps, or because the friend is wealthy and some advantage might follow, or because the friend is learned and proximity to learning lends an air of scholarship. A <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صدیق</span> carries no hidden agenda. The friendship has no strings attached. It is grounded in who the person is — not in what they own, not in the status they hold, not in anything artificial or superficial. Strip all of that away, and what remains — a bond that endures for its own sake alone — is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صدقۃ</span>. That friend will be there, no matter what.</p>
<p>Being a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صدیق</span> is not easy. Having a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صدیق</span> is even more difficult. Casual acquaintances — people to chat with, to gossip with — are easy to find. But a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صدیق</span> is something rarer and harder, because this is the kind of friend who never gives up, no matter how many times one fails.</p>
<p>That is the first nuance: sincerity. The second is truthfulness. The Arabs have a saying:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الصَّدِیقُکَ مَن صَدَقَت</span></p></blockquote>
<p>A friend is someone who is true — genuinely honest — not merely someone who is <em>friendly</em>. When things are going wrong, a true friend speaks plainly: &quot;Listen! I care about you. I don''t want you to do this, because I believe it will hurt you. Stop.&quot; A <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صدیق</span> is not there simply to look good or to supply the warm, comfortable feeling one craves. He tells the harsh truth — the truth one does not want to hear. That is precisely why the word grows from the root <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صدق</span>, meaning truthfulness.</p>
<p>A <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صدیق</span> to someone, then, is a person who will be genuinely honest with his friend whatever the cost. A <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صدیق</span> does not simply agree, knowing full well that the path his friend has chosen leads to ruin. He is not paralyzed by the fear that speaking plainly will shatter the friendship. <em>I don''t want to say anything because he might get really upset</em> — that is not a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صدیق</span>. It is precisely that silence, that comfortable evasion, that costs people their friendships. Honesty is offered, and it is not wanted. But the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صدیق</span> does not surrender to that. He does not give up on his friend, however painful the truth becomes.</p>
<p>There is a striking dimension to this word when it appears in the Quran. Allah describes certain people who, on the Day of Judgment, find themselves on the wrong side — people heading toward the Fire. He records what they say, in verses 26:99–101:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Ash-Shu''ara: 99</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَآ أَضَلَّنَآ إِلَّا ٱلْمُجْرِمُونَ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Their claim is that the fault is not theirs. They did not end up in this state through their own choices alone — these criminals, these evil people, misled them. The blame, they insist, belongs elsewhere. And Allah then records more of what they say:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Ash-Shu''ara: 100</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَمَا لَنَا مِن شَفِعِينَ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>There is nobody on our side to plead for us. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شافعی</span> is one who cares for a person and, when trouble comes, steps forward and says: he is good, he is fine, he is with me. The way a teacher might walk into the principal''s office and say: this is a good student — do not worry about him, I will answer for him. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شفاعۃ</span>. These people are saying that on this day, no one has come to speak for them. No one is standing in their corner. And then they say:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Ash-Shu''ara: 101</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَا صَدِيقٍ حَمِيمٍۢ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>This is the only place in the Quran where <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صدیق</span> is used in quite this direct way. They are saying that today they have no true, sincere, close friends — no one near to them who loves them. SubhanAllah. And in recording that complaint, Allah reminds us that the opposite is equally true. If those on the wrong side of Judgment Day are lamenting the absence of close friends, then those on the right side — the people of paradise — will indeed have a close friend standing with them. Who is that ultimate <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَدِيقٍ حَمِيمٍۢ</span> available to the believers? The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شفاعۃ</span> of Rasul Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>, inshaAllah. May Allah qualify all of us for that.</p>
<p>What those disbelievers — those destined for the Fire — lament on that day is the absence of a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَدِيقٍ</span>. In the world, whenever someone offered them sincere and honest counsel, they turned it away. They mocked. They ridiculed. They broke off those very friendships. And now, at the hour of judgment, they wish they had kept those <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَدِيقٍ</span> close. A <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَدِيقٍ</span> is worth holding — however harsh and painful their truth may be — because they prove to be the truest of friends in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آخرۃ</span>.</p>
<h3 id="companion">Companion (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَاحِب</span>)</h3>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صاحب</span> is among the most comprehensive words the Quran uses for a friend. It can describe the most casual of acquaintances — someone seated beside a traveler on a train or a bus for a few hours. At that minimal level of contact, the word already applies.</p>
<p>Yet a lifetime companion is equally a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صاحب</span>. What the term holds in common across all its uses is a single quality: a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صاحب</span> always means well. They want something good for the one they accompany. When trouble comes, they help to whatever degree they can. And the better they know a person, the greater their capacity to help — and the deeper their concern. This is why one of the names Allah gives to Rasul Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> in the Quran is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صاحب</span>:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">At-Takwir: 22</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَا صَاحِبُكُم بِمَجْنُونٍۢ</span></p><p class="tr">And your companion is not a madman;</p></blockquote>
<p>The people of Mecca were calling him insane. And what does Allah say in response? Not: my Messenger is not insane. Not: my Prophet is not insane. Not: the one I love most is not insane. Not: the greatest man ever to walk this earth is not insane. He says: YOUR companion — the one who genuinely cares for you — is not insane. That is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بلاغۃ</span> of the Quran: a precision of eloquence that the ancient Arabs felt immediately, and that could entirely transform a person''s way of thinking.</p>
<p>What <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صحبۃ</span> calls for is to be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أصحاب</span> to one another. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صحبۃ</span> is something that is also gradually disappearing at a societal level. It operates at the level of the individual, but it also works at the level of society. The companions during the time of the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> celebrated ordinariness; they did not construct artificial standards of living. Today, that spirit of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صحبۃ</span> has largely vanished. A person who buys an expensive phone and makes sure to display it at every opportunity — &quot;Oh, you still have an iPhone 6? Poor guy! It''s okay, don''t worry — I''ll still hang out with you, but only when we''re alone&quot; — has entirely lost the sense of what a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صاحب</span> is. A <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صاحب</span> is always concerned about a companion''s feelings. They do not go out of their way to show off in order to make a companion feel inferior.</p>
<p>The performance of false modesty — &quot;Oh, this old thing? It''s Gucci. It was on sale for eight hundred&quot; — offered to someone who could never afford it, reveals an absence of that concern. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صاحب</span> is a delicate term in that respect.</p>
<h3 id="trustworthy-friend">Trustworthy Friend (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ولیجۃ</span>)</h3>
<p>The next word the Quran uses for a friend is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ولیجۃ</span>. It appears only once in the Quran. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ولیجۃ</span> comes from the verb <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَجَ، یَلِجُو</span>, which in Arabic means to penetrate deeply. Allah says:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saba: 2</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَعْلَمُ مَا يَلِجُ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ وَمَا يَخْرُجُ مِنْهَا</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Allah knows what goes into the earth and what comes out of it.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ولیجۃ</span>, then, is a friend who is deeply immersed in one''s affairs — someone who knows everything happening in a person''s life and is actively present in it, day to day. The trust extended to a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ولیجۃ</span> is total. This is not merely someone to confide in; it is someone genuinely involved in the workings of one''s life.</p>
<p>This is not an ordinary friendship built on pleasantries and occasional contact. It is close, rare, and exceptionally demanding of trust. A <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ولیجۃ</span> is the friend trusted with a bank account, with children, with all one''s gold and jewelry, without a second thought — trusted in any affair of life. The signed blank check handed over before a long journey, <em>I''m traveling for a couple of months; please write out these checks for my bills</em>, with no anxiety whatsoever: that is the trust of a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ولیجۃ</span>. Such friendship is extremely rare today.</p>
<p>What makes the Quranic use of this word remarkable is that Allah limits the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ولیجۃ</span> relationship to three specific entities. He says:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">At-Tawbah: 16</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَمْ حَسِبْتُمْ أَن تُتْرَكُوا۟ وَلَمَّا يَعْلَمِ ٱللَّهُ ٱلَّذِينَ جَهَدُوا۟ مِنكُمْ وَلَمْ يَتَّخِذُوا۟ مِن دُونِ ٱللَّهِ وَلَا رَسُولِهِۦ وَلَا ٱلْمُؤْمِنِينَ وَلِيجَةًۭ ۚ وَٱللَّهُ خَبِيرٌۢ بِمَا تَعْمَلُونَ</span></p><p class="tr">Do you think that you shall be left alone while Allah has not yet tested those among you who have striven hard and fought and have not taken <em>Walijah</em> besides Allah and His Messenger, and the believers. Allah is Well-Acquainted with what you do.</p></blockquote>
<p>Do you think that you shall be left alone</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Ali ''Imran: 142</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَمَّا يَعْلَمِ ٱللَّهُ ٱلَّذِينَ جَهَدُوا۟ مِنكُمْ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>while Allah has not yet tested those among you who have striven hard and fought</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">At-Tawbah: 16</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَمْ يَتَّخِذُوا۟ مِن دُونِ ٱللَّهِ وَلَا رَسُولِهِۦ وَلَا ٱلْمُؤْمِنِينَ وَلِيجَةًۭ ۚ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>and have not taken <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ولیجۃ</span> besides Allah and His Messenger, and the believers</p>
<p>In this verse, Allah calls Himself a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ولیج</span> to us. Life brings trials, and through those trials He tests how deeply His love and His commands have penetrated the most personal corners of a person''s life. What is His role in family decisions? What is His role in financial decisions, in business decisions, in choices of career and education? Is He a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ولیج</span> — genuinely intimate in those innermost concerns?</p>
<p>The verse places the Messenger <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> alongside Allah. The question, then, is how much a person consults the Prophet''s legacy in the most personal matters of life. When trouble comes, how much does one turn to his life and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سیرۃ</span>, to his example? When difficulties arise — with people, with Islam — how much does one seek the counsel of Rasul Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>? How deeply is he involved in the most intimate matters?</p>
<p>When the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سنۃ</span> of the Messenger comes up today, what tends to be celebrated and emphasized are its most outward forms — the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سنۃ</span> of prayer, of keeping a beard, of being the first to say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سلام</span>, the mannerisms and etiquette passed down from our beloved Messenger <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>. In many respects, these remain public forms. The most intimate decisions are another matter entirely: whether to enter a particular financial transaction, what clothes to buy, whether to go along with friends to a certain gathering, whether to go along at all. The question is whether the Messenger has been brought into those decisions — the ones touching the most private affairs. How far does the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سنۃ</span> reach in such matters? Even in the ways one speaks, in one''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أخلاق</span>, in the way one carries oneself — does the Prophet''s example give guidance?</p>
<p>The way people speak to one another today is deeply disordered. There is no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سنۃ</span> in it. There is no respect for elders. There is no humility in mutual conversation. The Quran tells us that speech should be respectful and kind — that of a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ولی حمیم</span>, a protective and warm friend — even toward enemies. And yet even among believers, the moment any disagreement arises, the instinct is to bludgeon with evidence, to demean, to mock. Even in something as simple as a conversation, there is a profound departure from the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سنۃ</span> of Rasul Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>.</p>
<p>The verse then includes the believers — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْمُؤْمِنِينَ</span> — within this same relationship of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ولیجۃ</span>. When Allah is truly intimate in one''s life, and when the teachings of the Prophet are truly intimate in one''s personal affairs, Allah gives the gift of the finest kind of believers — those who can be trusted to the very depths of one''s personal life. What a gift that is, especially now, when trust has all but disappeared. People cannot be trusted in the workplace; even those called closest friends carry doubt. That kind of trust is gone from life. And Allah (Azza Wa Jal) gives, in this verse, the secret to finding it again: when He is admitted into the intimate life, and when the Messenger <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> is admitted alongside Him, the gift that follows is true believers worthy of exactly that trust. SubhanAllah!</p>
<h3 id="confidant">Confidant (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِطانۃ</span>)</h3>
<p>Another kind of friendship is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِطانۃ</span>, which comes from the root <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بطن</span> — the Arabic word for stomach. A <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِطانۃ</span> is a friend who guards secrets. Where the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ولیجۃ</span> is immersed in all of one''s personal matters, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِطانۃ</span> protects what is entrusted to them. A secret shared with a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِطانۃ</span> goes with them to their grave.</p>
<p>The contrast is painfully familiar. A secret is shared — &quot;please don''t tell anyone&quot; — and the friend puts the call on speaker and begins texting it along. &quot;Hey, listen up! Don''t tell anyone, but —&quot; That is not a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِطانۃ</span>. There are people with what one might call a very light stomach — they simply cannot hold a secret.</p>
<p>The verse in which this word appears in the Quran is:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Ali ''Imran: 118</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَٓأَيُّهَا ٱلَّذِينَ آمَنُوا۟ لَا تَتَّخِذُوا۟ بِطَانَةًۭ مِّن دُونِكُمْ لَا يَأْلُونَكُمْ خَبَالًۭا وَدُّوا۟ مَا عَنِتُّمْ قَدْ بَدَتِ ٱلْبَغْضَآءُ مِنْ أَفْوَهِهِمْ وَمَا تُخْفِى صُدُورُهُمْ أَكْبَرُ ۚ قَدْ بَيَّنَّا لَكُمُ ٱلْآيَتِ ۖ إِن كُنتُمْ تَعْقِلُونَ</span></p><p class="tr">O you who believe!</p></blockquote>
<p>Do not take as <em>Bitanah</em> those outside your religion, for they will not fail to do their best to corrupt you. They desire to harm you severely. Hatred has already appeared from their mouths, but what their breasts conceal is far worse. Indeed, We have made plain to you the signs if you understand.</p>
<p>This verse belongs to the context of the Battle of Uhud, in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَۃُ آلِ عِمْرَانَ</span>. The Battle of Uhud was a difficult military situation for the Muslims. Medina was home to different communities — Christians and Jews among them — and some were cooperating with the enemy. In that context, the Quranic instruction was clear: do not share secrets with those outside the faith who carry this propensity, because in a state of war the chain of disclosure is unpredictable. Many would leave no stone unturned and actively seek to cause harm. The verse is not a blanket judgment on all Christians and Jews — it addresses those who carry that specific intent.</p>
<p>The context is a state of war, where a casual word can cost lives. A passing remark — that I had seen the Prophet that day, that he would be at a certain place at a certain time — may be innocent in intent, yet for someone plotting against the Messenger, that information is gold. The one confided in may mean no harm, but the one they speak to next might. The disclosure travels down the chain. The instruction, therefore, is to keep intimate secrets within the innermost circle. That is the context of this word.</p>
<p>Beyond that context, the verse illuminates another shade of friendship — the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بطانۃ</span>, the one who knows the deepest and darkest secrets.</p>
<h3 id="deep-seated-friendship">Deep Seated Friendship (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلیل</span>)</h3>
<p>The next word the Quran uses for a friend is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلیل</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلیل</span> comes from the root word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلۃ</span>. Another word from the same root is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلال</span>, which means flossing — what passes in between one thing and another. When thread is drawn between teeth, that action is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلال</span> in Arabic.</p>
<p>A <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلیل</span>, then, is someone whose friendship has penetrated deep within the heart. The love for them runs so deep that one thinks of them constantly, feels their absence constantly, and wishes to be with them at any given moment. That is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلیل</span>. It is, again, a very rare kind of friendship — and it is precisely this kind of friendship that Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> had with Ibrahim <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَیْہِ السَّلَامُ</span>:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">An-Nisa: 125</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَٱتَّخَذَ ٱللَّهُ إِبْرَهِيمَ خَلِيلًۭا</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Allah took <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِبْرَاھِیْمُ عَلَیْہِ السَّلَامُ</span> as a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلیل</span>.</p>
<p>Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> uses the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلیل</span> in remarkable ways throughout the Quran.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Baqarah: 254</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَٓأَيُّهَا ٱلَّذِينَ آمَنُوٓا۟ أَنفِقُوا۟ مِمَّا رَزَقْنَكُم مِّن قَبْلِ أَن يَأْتِىَ يَوْمٌۭ لَّا بَيْعٌۭ فِيهِ وَلَا خُلَّةٌۭ وَلَا شَفَعَةٌۭ ۗ وَٱلْكَفِرُونَ هُمُ ٱلظَّلِمُونَ</span></p></blockquote>
<blockquote><p>O Believers! Spend from that which we have provided for you, before a Day comes when there will be no bargaining, nor friendship, nor intercession. And it is the disbelievers who are the <em>Zalimun</em>.</p></blockquote>
<p>He also says:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Az-Zukhruf: 67</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْأَخِلَّآءُ يَوْمَئِذٍۭ بَعْضُهُمْ لِبَعْضٍ عَدُوٌّ إِلَّا ٱلْمُتَّقِينَ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>The closest of friends — those who shared deep love and intimacy in this world — will become each other''s enemies on the Day of Judgment. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أخلاء</span> is the plural of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلیل</span>. Allah declares that no matter how close two people were in this life, that bond turns to enmity when the Day arrives. The sole exception is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المتقین</span>, the pious. And grammatically, this is the strongest form of exception the language permits: the only ones spared are those who were careful, in this world, about the kind of friends they kept. Every other friendship — however fierce and devoted it felt — dissolves on that Day.</p>
<p>The lesson is clear: friendship must be chosen on the basis of a person''s consciousness of Allah, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, and on the seriousness with which they carry their religious responsibility. The wrong person must never become one''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلیل</span>.</p>
<p>Because when the Day of Judgment arrives, the wrongdoer says:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَيَوْمَ يَعَضُّ ٱلظَّالِمُ عَلَىٰ يَدَيْهِ يَقُولُ يَلَيْتَنِى ٱتَّخَذْتُ مَعَ ٱلرَّسُولِ سَبِيلًۭا</span> ‎<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">۲۷</span>‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوَيْلَتَىٰ لَيْتَنِى لَمْ أَتَّخِذْ فُلَانًا خَلِيلًۭا</span></p></blockquote>
<blockquote class="quran"><p class="tr">And the Day when the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظالم</span> will chew up his own hands, He will say: &quot;If only I took on a path alongside the messenger <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>.&quot; &quot;Ah! Woe to me! Would that I had never taken so-and-so as a friend!&quot;</p></blockquote>
<p>The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سبیل</span> means a path — a beautifully precise linguistic image for an entire way of life. The wrongdoer is not lamenting a single decision but a whole direction of living: had he chosen the lifestyle of the messenger <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>, he would not stand in ruin on that Day. The verse carries extraordinary force, and the one that follows it sharpens it further.</p>
<p>No other verse in the Quran concentrates despair quite like this one. It binds two words of regret together:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوَيْلَتَىٰ لَيْتَنِى</span></p></blockquote>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حسرۃ</span> is already an exaggerated form of lament. Then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لیتنی</span> is layered on top of it — an expression even more intense, carrying the weight of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حسرۃ</span>. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حسرۃ</span> means deep regret. This verse stands among the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آیات</span> most saturated with sorrow in the entire Book. And what does all that sorrow come down to? A man wishing he had never taken a certain person as a friend.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Furqan: 28</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَمْ أَتَّخِذْ فُلَانًا خَلِيلًۭا</span></p></blockquote>
<p>There is something striking in the phrasing. The verse does not say &quot;I wish I had chosen better friends&quot; — it says I wish I had not taken <em>this person</em> as my friend. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلیل</span> is someone held with profound love. A person loved that deeply would at the very least be known by name. Yet on the Day of Judgment, regret floods the heart and hatred fills it so completely that the name is gone. The verse therefore reads <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فُلَانًا خَلِيلًۭا</span> — &quot;so-and-so,&quot; an unnamed, anonymous figure. That is the depth of this person''s regret on the Day of Judgment.</p>
<h3 id="comforting-friend">Comforting Friend (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رفیق</span>)</h3>
<p>The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رفیق</span> in Arabic comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رفقۃ</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رفق</span>, and it means comfort. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِرفِقَۃ</span> is the pillow placed behind an aching back. Those small cushions on a couch are also called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِرفَاق</span>, because a person leans on them and they take the pressure away, allowing the body to relax.</p>
<p>A <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رفیق</span>, then, is the friend one turns to in stress, in serious trouble, in difficult times — for comfort. The image is of a pillow one can lean on at any moment. And there is something telling in that image: a person reclining on a pillow is always at ease, never sitting formally. A <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رفیق</span> is a friend with whom no performance is required. There is no need for stiff formality, no need to measure every word. This is someone in whose company a person can simply be themselves — completely, without pretense. That is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رفیق</span>.</p>
<p>Now Allah says:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">An-Nisa: 69</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَن يُطِعِ ٱللَّهَ وَٱلرَّسُولَ فَأُو۟لَٓئِكَ مَعَ ٱلَّذِينَ أَنْعَمَ ٱللَّهُ عَلَيْهِم مِّنَ ٱلنَّبِيِّۦنَ وَٱلصِّدِّيقِينَ وَٱلشُّهَدَآءِ وَٱلصَّلِحِينَ ۚ وَحَسُنَ أُو۟لَٓئِكَ رَفِيقًۭا</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Those who obey God and obey the Prophet will receive special favors from Allah. They will be counted among the elect: they will find themselves in the company of prophets, of those who did righteous deeds, and of those who gave their lives for the sake of Allah. The verse closes:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">An-Nisa: 69</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَحَسُنَ أُو۟لَٓئِكَ رَفِيقًۭا</span></p></blockquote>
<p>What an awesome company of comforting friends. To be in the presence of Rasul Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> and feel no anxiety — to be at ease in the company of Maulana Ali (AS), in the company of the Imams. SubhanAllah.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رفیق</span>, then, is another kind of friend — one whose presence brings comfort, in whose company there is no unease. It is a quality worth striving to offer others.</p>
<h3 id="compatible-friend">Compatible Friend (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قرین</span>)</h3>
<p>The next kind of friend is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قرین</span>, and it is a remarkable category. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قرن</span> is used in Arabic for the rope that holds two camels together. When two camels stand side by side and a single rope binds them, that rope is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قرن</span>. A <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قرین</span>, then, is someone who is always at one''s side — joined at the hip, as the expression goes. When one moves, the other is pulled along. They move as one.</p>
<p>The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قرین</span> is the friend who is a constant presence: always nearby, always at the other end of the line, always the first to receive a message, always there. When one is happy, the other becomes happy; when one grieves, the other feels that grief. That inseparability is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قرین</span>.</p>
<p>A <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قرین</span> is also someone with whom one shares a great deal — because a rope cannot bind two camels of wildly different height or speed. There must be some equivalence between them; the pairing only holds when they are well matched. So a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قرین</span> is someone who shares one''s mind and one''s heart. They think as one thinks; they feel as one feels. That deep correspondence, that mirroring of thought and feeling, is the relationship of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قرین</span>.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قرین</span> can also mean people of the same community — all bound together by the rope of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَرِینُ عَہْدٍ شَرِیفٍ</span>, all belonging to the same creed. That shared belonging makes them a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قرین</span> as well. The Quran presents this word in a striking and sobering way. In <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَۃُ صِفَاتِ</span>, some of those who make it to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جنۃ</span> begin to inquire about others — family members, people from their community — who are not there with them.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَأَقْبَلَ بَعْضُهُمْ عَلَىٰ بَعْضٍۢ يَتَسَآءَلُونَ</span> ‎<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">۵۰</span>‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَالَ قَآئِلٌۭ مِّنْهُمْ إِنِّى كَانَ لِى قَرِينٌۭ</span> ‎<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">۵۱</span>‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَقُولُ أَءِنَّكَ لَمِنَ ٱلْمُصَدِّقِينَ</span> ‎<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">۵۲</span>‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَءِذَا مِتْنَا وَكُنَّا تُرَابًۭا وَعِظَمًا أَءِنَّا لَمَدِينُونَ</span> ‎<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">۵۳</span>‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَالَ هَلْ أَنتُم مُّطَّلِعُونَ</span> ‎<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">۵۴</span>‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَٱطَّلَعَ فَرَآهُ فِى سَوَآءِ ٱلْجَحِيمِ</span></p><p class="tr">Then they will turn to one another, and question each otherA speaker of them will say: &quot;Indeed, I had a companion, Who used to mock me!</p></blockquote>
<p>who used to mock me and say: &quot;Are you among those who believe? Are you among those fools who call themselves Muslims? Are you among those idiots who say that when we die and become dust and bones, we shall indeed receive reward or punishment?!&quot; In response he will answer: &quot;Look down.&quot; So he will look down and see that person in the midst of the Hellfire.</p>
<p>May Allah protect us from being among them. The choice of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قرین</span> is therefore one of the most consequential a person can make. A <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قرین</span> can elevate a person to the doorsteps of Paradise, or he can be the very means of his ruin in the Hellfire.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Az-Zukhruf: 36</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَن يَعْشُ عَن ذِكْرِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ نُقَيِّضْ لَهُۥ شَيْطَنًۭا فَهُوَ لَهُۥ قَرِينٌۭ</span></p><p class="tr">And whosoever turns away from the remembrance of the intensely merciful, We shall appoint for him a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شیطان</span> to be a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قرین</span> to him.</p></blockquote>
<p>This is Allah speaking in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَۃُ الزُّخْرُفِ</span>. These are not idle words. The prospect of having <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شیطان</span> as a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قرین</span> is a grave one. May Allah protect us all from that.</p>
<h3 id="deceptive-friend">Deceptive Friend (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَذُول</span>)</h3>
<p>The Quran describes good friends, but it also describes bad ones. The word for this evil friend is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خزول</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خزل</span> in Arabic is close in meaning to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خضع</span>, which means deception. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خزول</span> is a friend who only acts like a friend — until the moment of truth arrives and the true colors show. This person was present through the easy times, right there at every turn, always around, a genuine <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قرین</span>. But when things grew difficult, he vanished. The one who depended on him found no one there. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خزول</span>, and it is what is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِیغَۃُ مُبَالَغَہِ</span> — not ordinary deception, but deception of the most extreme kind. This is the profoundly disappointing friend who abandons at the worst possible moment. He earns trust so completely, so patiently, that a blank check is handed over without hesitation — and by morning he is gone with everything. That level of betrayal is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خزول</span>. It takes years of sustained, consistent effort to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خزول</span>-ofy someone to that degree.</p>
<p>This is precisely how the Quran characterizes <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شیطان</span>. His ultimate deception is to keep us from the remembrance of Allah — that is his entire game. He works to distract and divert attention toward <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دنیا</span>.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَّقَدْ أَضَلَّنِى عَنِ ٱلذِّكْرِ بَعْدَ إِذْ جَآءَنِى ۗ وَكَانَ ٱلشَّيْطَنُ لِلْإِنسَنِ خَذُولًۭا</span></p></blockquote>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شیطان</span> is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خزول</span> to human beings. Allah says this because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شیطان</span> acts like a friend throughout this life. He draws a person into things while convincing them that those things serve their own good, their own enjoyment. He makes a person watch films and delight in filth. He makes them buy, travel, speak, and build the wrong kinds of friendships — all of it — and all the while that person believes life is good, that they are living it fully. Every last bit of it is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خزل</span>. Because when Judgment Day arrives, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شیطان</span> is nowhere to be found. He steps forward and says:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Ibrahim: 22</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'quranic friendship';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَا كَانَ لِىَ عَلَيْكُم مِّن سُلْطَنٍ</span></p></blockquote>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Ibrahim: 22</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَّآ أَن دَعَوْتُكُمْ فَٱسْتَجَبْتُمْ لِى</span></p></blockquote>
<p>I simply suggested that you do these things! I didn''t hold a gun to your head! You were the one who actually chose to do all these things!</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Ibrahim: 22</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَلَا تَلُومُونِى وَلُومُوٓا۟ أَنفُسَكُم</span></p></blockquote>
<p>So now don''t blame me! Blame yourself for the situation you''re in; and let''s just enjoy the barbecue together.</p>
<p>That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خذول</span> — and that is precisely what will happen on Judgment Day.</p>
<p>This is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خزول</span>. The question it forces is whether any of us are that kind of person — the kind of friend who deserts and abandons in the moment of need. The phone was dead. The call was missed. The message went unseen.</p>
<p>The recognition was there — but the moment passed without action. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شیطان</span>.</p>
<p>Reliability is among the most important qualities a friend can possess, and its absence is one of the surest ways to lose a friendship. A person may have genuine and rare friendships — and lose them not through malice but through sheer irresponsibility, through failing to hold up their end. That neglect is precisely the character of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شیطان</span>.</p>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'quranic friendship';
INSERT INTO chapter (slug, anchor_key, idx, title, html, word_count) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'the stages of love', 3, 'The Stages Of Love', '', 5591);
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>The Arabs have what they call <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَرَاتِبُ الْحُبِّ</span>, the stages of love. Unlike friendship, where we learned that the perfect friend must possess all the relevant qualities, love is described as having ten stages, or ten levels. People generally understand these stages in a worldly sense, which is perfectly acceptable, but the true purpose of understanding them is to learn how to love Allah.</p>
<h3 id="1---ilaqah">1 - ILAQAH</h3>
<p>The first level is what they call <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">علاقۃ</span>, and it is:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَعَلُّقِ القَلب بِی المحبُوب</span></p></blockquote>
<p>The connection of the heart to the beloved. This is the point at which the heart becomes connected or attached to the beloved. In our terms, we might call it attachment or a “crush.” We say, “I have a crush on that person,” or, “I am attached to this person or that person.” That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">علاقۃ</span>.</p>
<p>This kind of attachment may develop into something deeper, or it may be very temporary. At this stage, it is simply a connection or attraction, without anything more.</p>
<h3 id="2---iradah">2 - IRADAH</h3>
<p>The second level is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ارادۃ</span>, which is willful love. This is where you actually begin to seek the beloved—<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تُرِیدُہُ</span>. You make an effort to find the beloved. At this stage, the lover is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرید</span> because he sets out with a goal and a purpose: to find the beloved.</p>
<p>The first level was simply a connection of the heart. At this stage, there is an active pursuit of what the heart desires. It is considered the beginning of the path. Thus, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ارادۃ</span> is the stage at which you begin to desire the company of the beloved.</p>
<h3 id="3---sababah">3 - SABABAH</h3>
<p>The third level is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سبابۃ</span>. This <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سبابۃ</span> comes from the Arabic expression <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَبَّ الماءُ</span>. The Arabs use this term to describe a waterfall. In English, we can think of this stage as “falling in love.” It is the point at which you begin to lose control. That is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِنسِباب</span>.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سبابۃ</span> is the stage at which you begin to lose control, and you realize that you are falling in love with this person.</p>
<h3 id="4---gharaam">4 - GHARAAM</h3>
<p>Then comes <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَرام</span>, which carries the sense of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُلازمۃ</span>. The punishment of the Hellfire is described as:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Furqan: 65</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَٱلَّذِينَ يَقُولُونَ رَبَّنَا ٱصْرِفْ عَنَّا عَذَابَ جَهَنَّمَ ۖ إِنَّ عَذَابَهَا كَانَ غَرَامًا</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Indeed, the punishment of the Hellfire is unceasing. It does not stop. The idea here is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لا یَنْفَکّ</span>—something that does not let go, something never-ending.</p>
<p>This <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَرام</span> is the state the Arabs refer to as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُغْرَم بہ</span>. It means that the lover simply wants to be with the beloved. There is a very strong desire to remain with the beloved at all times, and distance becomes painful at this stage.</p>
<p>One sign of people at this stage is that they constantly look at pictures of their beloved, or repeatedly return to read their letters, emails, or text messages. Doing so gives them a sense of closeness to the beloved and reduces the pain of separation.</p>
<h3 id="5---wud">5 - WUD</h3>
<p>The fifth stage is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وُد</span>, and the lover at this stage is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الوِداد</span>. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وُد</span> means pure love. It is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَفوَۃُ المَحَبَّۃ</span>—the purity of love. It is a love purified of the inclinations of the self.</p>
<p>In the previous stages, there was still some <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نفس</span> involved. There were still selfish reasons for loving the beloved: wanting to see them, desiring them, thinking about them. At those earlier stages, it is really more about <strong>you</strong> than about the beloved. But when you enter the stage of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وُد</span>, love begins to become pure. It begins to become selfless.</p>
<p>This is why the Messenger of Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَزَوِّج الوَلُود الوَدُود</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Marry a fertile woman who is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ودود</span>. Marry a woman who possesses this kind of pure love, a woman whose love is not selfish. And, obviously, in Arabic there is always <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مفحوم المُخالَفۃ</span>, which means that the converse is also true: a woman should likewise look for a husband who possesses this quality.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الوُد</span> is pure love.</p>
<p>Interestingly, this is also one of the names of Allah. He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الودود</span>:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Hud: 90</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَٱسْتَغْفِرُوا۟ رَبَّكُمْ ثُمَّ تُوبُوٓا۟ إِلَيْهِ ۚ إِنَّ رَبِّى رَحِيمٌۭ وَدُودٌۭ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Ask forgiveness of your master and turn to Him in repentance. Indeed, my master is Most Merciful, Most Loving.</p>
<p>He is selfless in His love. Thus, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الودود</span> has two meanings:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الوَادُّ فی عِبادِہِ والمَودُود</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Allah is the One who loves His servants, and He is the One whom His servants love. It is a relationship of pure love in both directions. Allah has a pure, selfless love for His creation, and creation has a pure, selfless love for its master. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الوُد</span>.</p>
<p>This is also how Allah describes the true purpose of a man and a woman coming together:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Ar-Rum: 21</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمِنْ آيَتِهِۦٓ أَنْ خَلَقَ لَكُم مِّنْ أَنفُسِكُمْ أَزْوَجًۭا لِّتَسْكُنُوٓا۟ إِلَيْهَا وَجَعَلَ بَيْنَكُم مَّوَدَّةًۭ وَرَحْمَةً ۚ</span></p><p class="tr">And among His Signs is that He created for you wives from among yourselves, that you may find <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سکون</span>, repose, in them, and He has placed between you pure love and mercy. Verily, in that are indeed signs for a people who reflect.</p></blockquote>
<p>Allah says that He placed <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مودۃ</span> between a man and a woman, which is pure love. This is the kind of love in which a husband will not divorce his wife when she becomes sick, when she is no longer beautiful, or when she becomes fat. If you have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مودۃ</span> for your wife, then even if she becomes fat, you will still love her. Seriously. If she becomes sick, you will still love her, because she will still love you even if you become fat or ugly. She will still love you.</p>
<p>A woman''s love is quite remarkable; it does not change in the same way a man''s love can change.</p>
<p>Shakespeare said:</p>
<p>Love is not love which alters when it alteration finds; or bends with the remover to remove</p>
<p>O no! it is an ever-fixed mark that looks on tempests and is never shaken</p>
<p>That is real love. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الوُد</span>. What he means is that love is not truly love if it changes merely because circumstances change. Your wife may be beautiful today, and you love her. But if one day she is no longer beautiful, that should not change your love. If it does, then that is not love. That is lust.</p>
<p>I know of a woman who was in an accident and suffered burns on one side of her face. Her husband simply divorced her because she was no longer beautiful. He divorced her and sent her back to her parents. That is not love. That is sick. Yet that is the state of some people.</p>
<p>That is not real love. This is why, if you observe carefully, women often have a much deeper capacity for this kind of love than most men do. If the same accident had happened to the husband and his face had been burned, she might have spent the rest of her life with him, caring for him and loving him in the same way. That is real love, and women have a much greater capacity to love in this way.</p>
<p>This is why Rasul Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبِّبَ الییَّ النِّساء</span></p></blockquote>
<p>I was made to love women.</p>
<p>That statement has nothing to do with lust.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَالتِّیْب وَالمِسوَاک</span></p></blockquote>
<p>And I was made to love perfume and to love flossing.</p>
<p>What is the connection between women, perfume, and floss? Perfume simply gives. Perfume dissipates its own existence for others—for us. When you put on perfume, it diffuses and gives up its own existence in order to make you smell pleasant.</p>
<p>Floss enters a filthy mouth and cleans it at the expense of its own existence. It sacrifices itself to clean our mouths. What does the floss gain from that experience? Nothing. What does perfume gain from being applied? Nothing.</p>
<p>That is the quality in women that the Prophet loved: the capacity for sacrifice that comes so naturally to a woman and that men often find so difficult. That is why love is what we learn from our mothers. That is why Rasul Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الَجنَّۃُ تَحتَ الاَقدَامِ الاُمَّھَات</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Paradise is UNDER the feet of mothers. UNDER the feet of mothers.</p>
<p>That is where we learn love best. That is where we see it most fully embodied.</p>
<p>That is why, in a HADEES, Rasul Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ُشَقَقْتُ الرَّحَم مِن أِسمِیَ الرَّحمٰن ۔ فمََنْ قَطَعَہُ فَقَد قَطَعْتُہ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>I have derived the womb—<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شققت</span> literally means “to split.” He says, “I have split the womb from My name <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span>, the Merciful.”</p>
<p>That is unique to women. Men do not have wombs. Allah gave that only to women. It is that space at the center of a woman that becomes the source of all mercy in the world. It was our first abode. All of us were once in that abode of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمہ</span>.</p>
<p>And the mercy of the womb is extraordinary: it grows in order to accommodate what is inside it. That is what mercy is. It is an emotion that grows according to the need of the servant. It expands to meet the needs of others. That is mercy. That is love.</p>
<p>That is why our Prophet is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَۃً لِی الْعَالَمِینَ</span>:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Anbiya: 107</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَآ أَرْسَلْنَكَ إِلَّا رَحْمَةًۭ لِّلْعَلَمِينَ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>We have not sent you but as a mercy to all of humanity—because he expanded himself to meet the needs of others.</p>
<p>That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وُد</span>: pure love. It is not a utilitarian kind of love, a love tainted by one''s own selfish needs. It is not the kind of love that asks, “What am I going to get out of this? What is in it for me?”</p>
<p>It is a love that transcends all of that. It is the kind of love that remains when beauty is present and remains when beauty is gone. If service is no longer possible because of a stroke or some other incapacity, the love remains. That is real love.</p>
<p>It is not love when the beloved matters only because they are useful to me. That is not love. That is something else.</p>
<p>And we have become a society largely devoid of this kind of love. Everything in our lives is about us: What is in it for me? Why should I help you?</p>
<p>After the battle of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سفین</span> was over, a water carrier narrated that he went across the battlefield searching for dying soldiers. In those days, there would be a man whose duty was to carry a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مشکیزۃ</span>, a water container, in order to quench the thirst of dying soldiers. They say that at the moment of death a person becomes extremely thirsty, and water helps ease the transition.</p>
<p>The man narrated that he was carrying water across the battlefield of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سفین</span>, searching for thirsty soldiers, when he came upon a man whose lower body had been severed. Only his torso remained on the scorching ground, and he was crying out for water.</p>
<p>The water carrier approached him and brought the cup close to his lips. At that moment, they heard another man calling for water. The dying man closed his lips and refused to drink so that his brother could be given water first.</p>
<p>The water carrier rushed to the second man. As he brought the cup close to his lips, they heard a third man calling for water. The second man likewise closed his lips and refused to drink.</p>
<p>The water carrier hurried to the third man, but by the time he reached him, the man had died. He then returned to the second man, but he too had died. Finally, he returned to the first man, and by then he also had died of thirst.</p>
<p>That was love. That was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وُد</span>—the love these people had for one another. Where is our love? How do we compare?</p>
<h3 id="6---shaghaf">6 - SHAGHAF</h3>
<p>The next stage is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شغف</span>. What they call <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَغَافُ الْقَلْبِ</span> is the pericardium. This is the stage at which the heart is surrounded by love. Love encompasses the heart.</p>
<p>This is the state of obsession. Wherever you are, whatever you see, it reminds you of the beloved.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِدَھرْ دِیکھْتَا ہُوں تُو ہِی تُو ہَےْ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>You see the beloved everywhere: in the sunrise, the sunset, the rose, the birds, the music. Everything reminds you of the beloved.</p>
<h3 id="7---ishq">7 - ISHQ</h3>
<p>Then comes the stage of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">العشق</span>, which is a very dangerous state of love. People who enter this state of love for another person, or for God, can end up losing their sanity. It is an extremely dangerous phase. This is the stage in which the lover takes pleasure in pain and suffering for the sake of the beloved. It is a state of insane love.</p>
<p>The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عشق</span> actually comes from the name of a vine, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَشَقَ</span>. When this vine surrounds a tree, it draws the life out of the tree and eventually kills it. The image is of the tree destroying itself in order to nourish and sustain the vine. It kills itself so that its beloved can live.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عشق</span> is the kind of love in which there is total constriction. The lover begins to suffer for love of the beloved and actually takes pleasure in that suffering.</p>
<p>The Arabs say:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَعَا الْعِشْقُ الْاِتِّصَالَ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>The only cure for this intense level of love is finally meeting the beloved.</p>
<p>During the time of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فرعون</span>, there was a woman who had become Muslim but had not disclosed her Islam to anyone. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فرعون</span> eventually discovered this and summoned her to his court. She was arrested and brought before him with her two daughters. The older daughter was ten years old, and the younger was only a few months old.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فرعون</span> said to the woman, “I have heard that you now believe in the God of Moses. I give you two choices. The first is that you reject the God of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">موسی</span> and accept me as your god. If you do so, I will grant you a palace in which you and your daughters will live as royalty. You will enjoy every comfort you can imagine for the rest of your lives.</p>
<p>“The second choice is that you reject my offer. If you do, I will burn both of your daughters alive before your eyes, and then I will burn you alive as well.”</p>
<p>She rejected his offer.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فرعون</span> ordered his men to fill a large cauldron with oil and heat it until it began to boil. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فرعون</span> asked her again, “Do you accept my offer?”</p>
<p>She said, “I am already in love with Moosa''s God. How can I betray Him for you?”</p>
<p>This is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عشق</span>.</p>
<p>At <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فرعون</span>''s command, his soldiers dragged her ten-year-old daughter forward and threw her into the boiling oil. There are mothers in the group. Only a mother can understand what befalls a mother''s heart when she sees her child burned to death before her eyes.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فرعون</span> asked her again, “Do you accept my offer?”</p>
<p>Again, she refused.</p>
<p>So they took her infant daughter, only a few months old, and threw her into the boiling oil as well. Still, she did not say a word.</p>
<p>At that moment, it is said that the veils of the sky were removed before her eyes, and she saw her two daughters in Paradise saying to her, “Mother, be patient just a little while longer. We are soon going to be reunited in Heaven.”</p>
<p>Then came the woman''s turn. She said to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فرعون</span>, “I have one final request: after you kill me, bury my bones together with the bones of my daughters.”</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فرعون</span> said, “Your wish will be granted.”</p>
<p>Then they threw the woman into the burning oil as well, and she was reunited with her children. Her final wish was fulfilled, and their bones were buried together.</p>
<p>Two thousand years later, Rasul Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> is being taken on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">معراج</span>. When <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">براق</span> flew over <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَیْتُ الْمُقَدَّسِ</span>, the Prophet smelled the fragrance of Paradise. He asked <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جبرئیل</span>, “Where is this fragrance coming from?”</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جبرئیل</span> narrated this entire incident to the Prophet and said that, from the day the bones of that woman and her daughters were buried, this fragrance had been rising from their graves. Only the angels enjoy this fragrance; we do not possess the sense by which such a fragrance can be perceived. But this was our Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>, who sensed that fragrance and asked about it.</p>
<p>This is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عشق</span>. This is the story of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عشق</span>: how this woman loved Allah and how Allah loved her in return. Two thousand years later, the Messenger <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> was sensing the fragrance of Paradise rising from their graves. SubhanAllah!</p>
<p>The story of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">کربلا</span> is the story of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عشق</span>. Every <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">محرم</span>, we hear that tale of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عشق</span>. Karbala is the tale of this extraordinary, consuming love.</p>
<p>You may have heard of the woman in Imam Hussain''s camp whose son went into battle and returned to her covered in blood and wounds. He came back to the camp, called out to his mother, and asked, “Are you pleased with me, Mother?”</p>
<p>What mother could bear to see her son covered in wounds and bleeding from head to toe? Yet his mother told him, “Not yet, my son. I will not be pleased with you until you sacrifice yourself for Imam Hussain.”</p>
<p>This is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عشق</span>.</p>
<p>He returned to the battle and fought until he was martyred. They cut off his head and threw it toward the camp. His mother came out, took his head, placed it in her lap, and began kissing his forehead. Then she screamed and died.</p>
<p>This is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عشق</span>.</p>
<h3 id="8---taim">8 - TAIM</h3>
<p>The next phase is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَتَیَُم</span>. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تیم</span> is used for a slave, though not at the level of an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبد</span>. It describes a high degree of love accompanied by a kind of servitude. This is the stage at which you enter the service of the beloved.</p>
<p>Love has now reached the point of asking, “What can I do for you? How can I serve you?” At this stage, the lover finds pleasure in serving the beloved.</p>
<h3 id="9---taabbud">9 - TAABBUD</h3>
<p>The next stage is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تعبد</span>, where the lover becomes a complete slave to the beloved. At this stage, servitude is joined by complete obedience. It is the point at which, when the beloved says, “Jump,” the lover asks, “How high?” There is unquestioning obedience to the beloved.</p>
<p>This is why the poet says:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تُعصِی اِلٰہَ وَ تُظْھِرُ حُبُّہُ</span></p></blockquote>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ھَذا الامْرُکَ فِی قِیَاسِ بَدِیعُ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>You claim to love your God, and yet you disobey Him? Such a love makes no sense to me.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبد</span> is the stage of complete obedience in love. And this is an honorific title of the Messenger of Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Isra: 1</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَنَ ٱلَّذِىٓ أَسْرَىٰ بِعَبْدِهِ لَيْلًۭا</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Glorious is the One who took His slave on a journey by night.</p>
<p>Not “messenger,” not “prophet,” but <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبد</span>—His slave. This title of the Prophet is even greater than the title of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رسول</span>. This is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبودیۃ</span>.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تعبد</span> is adoration. That is why the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عابد</span> is the one who adores. “Adore” is a Latin word connected with love. Thus, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبودیۃ</span> is the realm of adoration, where there is complete stillness before the beloved and unconditional obedience to the beloved.</p>
<h3 id="10---khullah">10 - KHULLAH</h3>
<p>The next stage of love is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُلۃ</span>, and it is the level of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلیل</span>. The Prophet said:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ان اللہ أِتَّخَذَنِی خَلِیلاً کَمَا التَّخَذَ ابراھیمَ خلیلا</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Allah took me as a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلیل</span> just as He took Ibrahim as a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلیل</span>.</p>
<p>This is the highest level of love, where love:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَتَخَلَّلُو خَلَایَ الجَسَد</span></p></blockquote>
<p>The love completely permeates every cell of the body.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تخلیل</span> means to penetrate deeply. For example, in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">احکام</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غُسُل</span>, when you do <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تخلیل الشَّعَر</span>, you must reach every hair follicle. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُلَّۃ</span>. It is a very high degree of love, where love has permeated the entire soul.</p>
<h3 id="muhabbah---combination-of-all">MUHABBAH - Combination of All</h3>
<p>The final stage of love is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">محبۃ</span>, and there is really no single word in the English language that can encompass the depth of this love. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">محبۃ</span> comes from the root word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُب</span>, which means seed. It is a never-ending, eternal kind of love.</p>
<p>What happens when you sow a seed? You reap a harvest. You receive an exponential return. And what happens after the harvest? It grows again. You harvest again, and it grows again. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُب</span>. That is an enduring love.</p>
<p>And this is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مقام</span> of the Prophet. He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَبِیبُ اللَّہِ</span>—the one whom Allah loves.</p>
<p>Adam is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَفِیرُ اللَّہِ</span>, the one Allah sent; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نوح</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نجی اللہ</span>, the one Allah saved; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ابراھیم</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِیلُ اللَّہِ</span>, the one Allah befriended; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">موسی</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">کَلِیمُ اللّٰہِ</span>, the one with whom Allah conversed; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عیسی</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">روح اللہ</span>, the one into whom Allah breathed His spirit; but Rasul Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَبِیبُ اللَّہِ</span>. He is the one Allah loved.</p>
<p>And this is an astonishing thing if you reflect on it: the Creator is in love with His created being. What does that say about the character of our Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>, that the Creator loves him in this way? The entire creation is concerned with pleasing Allah, while Allah is concerned with pleasing Rasul Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Ad-Duha: 5</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَسَوْفَ يُعْطِيكَ رَبُّكَ فَتَرْضَىٰٓ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>And indeed your master will give you so much that “you” will be well pleased.</p>
<p>Allah promises His Messenger that He will give him and continue giving him until HE is well pleased. That is the kind of love Allah has for the Messenger, and that is the kind of love the Messenger has for Allah.</p>
<p>This is why to love the Messenger of Allah is to love Allah, and to love Allah is to love the Messenger of Allah:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Ali ''Imran: 31</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُلْ إِن كُنتُمْ تُحِبُّونَ ٱللَّهَ فَٱتَّبِعُونِى يُحْبِبْكُمُ ٱللَّهُ</span></p><p class="tr">Say: “If you love Allah, then follow me; Allah will love you.”</p></blockquote>
<p>May Allah grant us the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مقام</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">محبۃ</span>, increase us in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">محبۃ</span>, and make us people of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">محبۃ</span>.</p>
<p>The Prophet said:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وللہ لا تَدْخُلَ الجَنَّۃ حتی تؤمنُ۔ ولا تُؤمن حتی تَحابُّ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>You will not enter Paradise until you believe, and you will not believe until you love one another—until you spend on one another and care for one another.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Ali ''Imran: 92</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَن تَنَالُوا۟ ٱلْبِرَّ حَتَّىٰ تُنفِقُوا۟ مِمَّا تُحِبُّونَ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>You will not attain any good until you spend from that which you love.</p>
<p>How much do we spend in the way of Allah? Those who truly believe love Allah more than they love their <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دنیا</span>:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">والذین امنو أشدو حب لی اللہ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Those who believe love Allah more than they love their <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دنیا</span>.</p>
<p>And we should think about what people are doing for their <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دنیا</span>. It is remarkable. We have to ask ourselves whether we are doing even one-tenth for our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آخرۃ</span> of what they are doing for their <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دنیا</span>. Just one-tenth.</p>
<p>They work twelve hours a day, seven days a week, amassing all these goods. People learn the most unusual things simply because they are interested in them. Yet we do not learn Arabic in order to understand our Creator''s Book.</p>
<p>There is an advertisement for learning Italian. It shows a farm boy who is in love with an Italian celebrity. She will be in his town for one evening, so he has one opportunity, one chance to meet her. It is a television advertisement.</p>
<p>The point is that the advertisers know they are speaking to people who understand that, when you are in love, you will do anything for the beloved. So this boy gets Italian-learning software and tries to learn Italian in a single night just so that he can speak to that celebrity.</p>
<p>This is what love does. This is what lovers do for the Beloved.</p>
<p>We are loveless people. We want to talk about love, but we do not want to <strong>do</strong> anything. We do not want to make any effort in the name of love. We do not learn Arabic. We do not take the time to understand the language of our Beloved. We do not want to make the effort to understand the language in which He speaks to His creation.</p>
<p>We are lazy people. We claim love, but none of it is true. We are false lovers.</p>
<p>But there are lovers. There are true lovers.</p>
<p>So if we are not in love, if we cannot be true lovers, then at the very least we should stand in awe of these lovers, such as Rasul Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> and Maulana Ali. At least we should wish to love them.</p>
<p>The Arabs say that sometimes the ear falls in love before the eyes do. Sometimes you hear about someone and fall in love with them.</p>
<p>I hope that, at some point, we all experience true love. Allah made this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عشق</span>, this love in the world, a sign showing us what divine love is. It is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حجۃ</span> against us. Romeo and Juliet are a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حجۃ</span> against us. LAILA and MAJNOON are a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حجۃ</span> against us.</p>
<p>If this is the love of one created being for another created being, then what kind of love should a creature have for his Creator? If these are the depths to which you will go out of love for another human being, then what should be the depth of your efforts for your Creator?</p>
<p>This pure love is a beautiful thing. People long to be in love. Some want it so badly that they keep falling in love again and again simply for the experience of love. These are foolish people—<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُشَّاق</span>—because, as I said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عشق</span> is a disease.</p>
<p>But the cure for love is meeting the beloved. You may long to meet someone so intensely that you will go to the ends of the earth to find that person.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَلْمَانُ الْفَارِسِیُّ</span>—look at his love. Look at what he did.</p>
<p>He was a man who was unjustly sold into slavery. He was a slave. He traveled far and wide simply to find the Messenger. Whenever he heard of a righteous man, he would go to him and ask about the Prophet. His journey to the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> is extraordinary.</p>
<p>Because of his sincerity, Allah gave him the signs by which he would recognize the Prophet. The first time he met the Prophet, he brought him dates and said, “This is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صدقۃ</span>.” Then he watched as the Prophet did not eat from them, because he had been told that the Prophet would not eat from charity.</p>
<p>The next time he came, he brought dates and said, “This is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ھدیۃ</span>; it is a gift.” Then the Prophet ate from them. Salman knew. “Ah! Just as I was told.”</p>
<p>Then Salman became Muslim.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَلْمَانُ مِنَّا اَھْلَ الْبَیْتِ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Look where love takes you. Salman is from my family. He is part of our family.</p>
<p>He was an extraordinary man of love.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَبُو اَیُّوبَ الْاَنْصَارِیُّ</span>—when the Prophet came to Medina, he let go of the reins of his camel because everyone wanted the Prophet to stay with them. The Prophet was an extremely diplomatic person. He did not want to upset or offend anyone. So he said, “I will leave the reins of my camel, and wherever she stops, I will stay with that person.”</p>
<p>The camel stopped in front of the house of Hz Abu Ayyub Al-Ansari. He rushed forward and took the Prophet''s luggage. The Prophet said, “A man is with his luggage.”</p>
<p>So Abu Ayyub brought the Prophet into his house and placed him on the lower floor because he wanted to honor him. It was easier to stay on the ground floor than upstairs: there were no stairs to climb, and one could go outside more easily. He wanted to give the Prophet the greatest possible ease, so he housed him downstairs while he and his wife stayed on the floor above.</p>
<p>But then Abu Ayyub became deeply uneasy. He could hardly walk on the floor above, thinking, “I do not want to place my foot where the Prophet may have rested his head below.”</p>
<p>This is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ادب</span>. We no longer have this kind of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ادب</span>.</p>
<p>So he went and begged the Prophet: “Please, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">یَا رَسُولَ اللّٰہِ</span>! We cannot stay above you.”</p>
<p>And so the Prophet changed places with him.</p>
<p>His wife would take food upstairs to the Prophet, and after he had eaten, she would bring the food back. Abu Ayyub would ask her, “Show me where the Messenger''s fingers touched the food,” and then he would eat from that spot.</p>
<p>This is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَـتَـبُّـعُ الْآثَارِ</span>—following the traces. It came from love. These people had so much love for him.</p>
<p>Look at <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">میمونۃ</span>, the Prophet''s wife. Thirty years after she married the Prophet, as she was nearing death, do you know where she wanted to go? To the place where she had spent her first night with the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>. That is where she is buried.</p>
<p>Look at the love. Look at that love. This is what these people had.</p>
<p>Today, we want to remove love from Islam and turn the religion into something else. But this religion is nothing if it is not about love:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا یُؤْمِنُ اَحَدُکُمْ حَتَّی یُحِبَّ اَخِی</span></p></blockquote>
<p>No one is a believer until he loves his brother.</p>
<p>He did not use another word there. He said: until you love.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُلْ اِنْ کُنْتُمْ تُحِبُّونَ اللَّہَ فَتَّبِعُونِی</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Say: If you love Allah, then follow me, and God will love you.</p>
<p>Because:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَامَۃُ الْحُبِّ اتِّبَاعُ الْحَبِیبِ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>The sign of love is that you follow the Beloved. You do what the Beloved says.</p>
<p>Look at the love of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُوَیْسُ الْقَرَنِیُّ</span>. Look at that man''s love.</p>
<p>When he heard that the Prophet''s tooth had been broken in the battle of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">احد</span>, he became overwhelmed. What had his Messenger felt? How much pain had his Messenger experienced when they struck him?</p>
<p>To experience that pain himself, he pulled out one of his own teeth. But that was not enough. What if it had not been that tooth, but another? So he pulled out another. What if it had been this one? He pulled out another. What if it had been that one? He continued until he had pulled out all of his teeth.</p>
<p>That is love.</p>
<p>These were people of extraordinary intensity. They had <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عشق</span>. How are we ever going to compare with their love?</p>
<p>This is why I say that, if we cannot love Allah, we should try to love those who loved Allah. And if we cannot love them, then we should love those who loved those who loved Allah.</p>
<p>Love is struggle. When you love, you struggle and you sacrifice. That is the nature of love: you sacrifice for the one you love. If you are not sacrificing, there is no love. It is not real love when everything is only me, me, me.</p>
<p>People have gone to extraordinary lengths in love:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَمُرُّ بِدِّیارِ دِیَارِ لَیلَۃ أُقَبْلُ لم جِدَارُ وَلا الجِدَارَۃ</span></p></blockquote>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَا حُبُّ الدِّیَارِ شَغَفْنَا قَلبِی؛ وَلاکِنْ حُبُّ مَنْ سَکَنَ الدِّیَارَۃ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>The poet says, did you not ask about that place? About that neighborhood that no longer exists? Did you not ask about the place where <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لیلۃ</span> used to live?</p>
<p>He says: Indeed, I asked. But how can dead stones answer me? Where did everyone go?</p>
<p>Then he says: I pass by the stones, the dwelling places of my beloved, and I kiss this wall and I kiss that wall.</p>
<p>And then he says:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَا حُبُّ الدِّیَارِ شَغَفْنَا قَلبِی</span></p></blockquote>
<p>It is not love of the walls that has enraptured my heart, but love of the one who used to live within those walls.</p>
<p>This is why we go and visit these places. It is not the places themselves. It is the Beloved. He was there. That is why we go there: because he walked there.</p>
<p>Our religion is a religion of love. Everything we do in our religion must be done out of love, not merely out of fear.</p>
<p>Allah is worthy of fear, but even the fear of Allah should be rooted in love. It is like the fear a child feels at the thought of upsetting or disappointing a parent. That fear is really rooted in pure love. The child loves the parents so deeply that he or she is afraid to upset them.</p>
<p>That should be our fear of Allah. It should be rooted in love.</p>
<p>Allah is—<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّہُ اَکْبَرُ</span>—greater than anything that can be said in His praise:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لا نُحْصِی الثَّنَاء عَلیکَ۔ اَنتَ کَمَا اَثْنَیتُ عَلی نَفسِکَ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>We cannot praise You as You have praised Yourself. You are as You have praised Yourself.</p>
<p>Anything I say about Allah will be deficient.</p>
<p>So we all need to think about our lives. This is our time, and what we do with this time will determine what happens when we leave it. We are all going to depart from this world.</p>
<p>It is therefore essential that we ask ourselves: How are we spending our time? What are we doing with it? Are we doing useful and worthwhile things? Beneficial things? Are we drawing closer to Allah? Are we coming to know our Prophet better?</p>
<p>Because he is worth knowing. He is worth studying. The stories about him are worth knowing. His HADDES, his family, his etiquette, and his character are worth knowing. It is worth knowing about his family members—who they were and what they went through.</p>
<p>May Allah guide us all. May He open our hearts and place us all on the path of love.</p>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'the stages of love';
INSERT INTO chapter (slug, anchor_key, idx, title, html, word_count) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'linguistic meaning of allah', 4, 'Linguistic Meaning Of Allah', '', 4040);
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>I now turn to the Surah. The first <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آیۃ</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَۃُ الْفَاتِحَۃِ</span> is</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللَّہِ الرَّحْمَنِ الرَّحِیمِ</span></p>
<h3 id="ilah">ILAH</h3>
<p>I begin with the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللہ</span>. The word Allah comes from the root word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>, a term I generally use to mean God. In reality, however, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> is a very general term, comparable to words such as &quot;book&quot; or &quot;car.&quot; There are many kinds of books and cars; neither term is specific. In the same way, there can be many kinds of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>. English has two kinds of articles: the indefinite article &quot;a,&quot; as in &quot;a book&quot; or &quot;a car,&quot; and the definite article &quot;the,&quot; as in &quot;the book&quot; or &quot;the car.&quot;</p>
<h3 id="a-vs-the">A vs THE</h3>
<p>The distinction between &quot;a book&quot; and &quot;the book&quot; is important. &quot;A book&quot; is generic and could refer to any book. But when I say &quot;the book,&quot; I am no longer referring to just any book; I have a particular book in mind. The same principle applies to the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>.</p>
<h3 id="ilah-vs-al-ilah">ILAH vs AL-ILAH</h3>
<p>The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> is a generic, indefinite term that may apply to many things. There can be many kinds of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>, just as there can be many kinds of books. But when the Arabic definite article <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ال</span>, meaning &quot;the,&quot; is attached to it, the combination <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ال</span> + <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> produces the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللہ</span>. In other words, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللہ</span> is the specific, definite form of the generic word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>. At its root, Allah is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ال</span> + <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>. By the rules of Arabic grammar, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الف</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللہ</span> is dropped, and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ال</span> merges with the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ل</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ہ</span> to become Allah. To understand the linguistic meaning of the word Allah, then, the first step is to understand the meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>.</p>
<h3 id="composite-words">Composite Words</h3>
<p>The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> is a compound word that carries a range of meanings. A composite word may be compared to a jigsaw puzzle: each piece carries a distinct meaning, and all the pieces together form the complete picture. If <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> carries four meanings — A, B, C, and D — then a full understanding of the word comes from all four taken together. Its meaning is not A alone, nor B alone, nor C or D in isolation. It is A + B + C + D. This is what I mean by a composite word, and the concept is worth keeping firmly in mind because many of the words examined in these pages are composite words of this kind.</p>
<h3 id="meanings-of-word-ilah">Meanings Of Word ILAH</h3>
<p>The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> carries several distinct meanings, each of which contributes to the whole.</p>
<h4 id="yalaa">YALAA</h4>
<p>The primary root is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>, drawn from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلَہَ یَعلُہُ</span>. At its core, the verb <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلَہَ</span> means to worship. The first meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلَہَ</span>, therefore, is: the one who is the object of all worship and obedience.</p>
<p>Worship itself is an extreme form of gratitude — the highest degree of thankfulness, in which the one who has given becomes so precious and indispensable that the heart is brought to its knees. It is a state of complete surrender when a person''s will yields entirely to the will of the beloved. When that happens, the beloved becomes one''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>. This is the first meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلَہَ</span>.</p>
<p>The principle extends into ordinary life. A person who is so consumed by a career that it is placed above every other person and every competing claim has made that career an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>. Whatever commands and rules the heart, whatever a person effectively bows before — whether video games, sport, television, children, or wealth — fits this definition. Such things become, in a meaningful sense, an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>. Likewise, when love for another person becomes so total that it governs every thought, action, and decision, that beloved has become an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>. For <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> is precisely this: the one worshipped, the one obeyed and submitted to without question.</p>
<h4 id="alaha">ALAHA</h4>
<p>The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَہَ</span> is also used for a child desperate for its mother''s milk, crying out for it with everything it has — that, too, is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلِہَ</span>. There is a particular hunger in a baby''s face at such a moment, and a particular intensity in the way it reaches for its mother. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَہَ</span>: the one to whom a person turns desperately and inevitably, with passion and need, in moments of happiness and grief alike, without restraint or control. That, too, is an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَہَ</span>.</p>
<p>Some people are enslaved to sugar; others to cigarettes, drugs, parties, or films. Whatever a person turns to desperately and uncontrollably the moment happiness or depression strikes, whatever becomes the source of relief and comfort, is by this definition their <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>. Peace is sought in its arms. Obedience to it no longer feels like a choice but a compulsion. When the craving commands a person to smoke, the person obeys. When the desire for ice cream becomes irresistible, the person obeys. Watching one more episode or spending hours every day playing video games may cease to be merely casual habits and become acts of total loyalty and devotion, driven by the conviction that happiness and satisfaction are impossible without them. The person turns to these things with the same desperation with which a baby, burning with thirst, turns to its mother.</p>
<p>This applies to every one of us. We all have such <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> in our lives. That does not make us horrible people; it makes us human. By learning these meanings, we begin to recognize these masters for what they are. That recognition is the first step toward change.</p>
<h4 id="walaha">WALAHA</h4>
<p>The next word is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَہَ</span>. It describes someone with whom one is passionately in love — someone one cannot stop thinking about, someone who occupies the mind completely. The derivative <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَہَ</span> is used when a person is driven to madness by the loss of a beloved. A mother who loses her child is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوَلَّا</span>: a mother enduring the excruciating pain and grief of her child''s loss. She is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوَلَّا</span>. It is this kind of passionate, consuming love. In Urdu, the expression is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَالِھَانَہَ مُحَبَّتْ</span> — mad love, crazy love — and that, too, comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَہَ</span>. When someone in one''s life has that kind of hold on the heart — when one thinks of that person constantly, when separation drives one to the edge of reason, when one cannot stop calling or texting, when one repeatedly returns to old photographs and messages, missing that person with every part of oneself — that is the state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَہَ</span>, and by definition that person is one''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>.</p>
<h4 id="aliha">ALIHA</h4>
<p>The next meaning, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلِہَ</span>, applies literally — not linguistically — to something on which a person leans, something that bears their full weight. When exhaustion sets in and one leans against something, that supporting thing is literally called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلِہَ</span>. Thus, if the weight of life rests on finances, property, houses, cars, a job, social standing, friends, neighbors, or children — anything on which one psychologically leans — then each of those things is an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>. Whatever holds all one''s eggs in a single basket, so to speak, becomes one''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>.</p>
<h4 id="waliha">WALIHA</h4>
<p>Another derivative of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِہَ</span>, which means to become astounded, astonished, or mesmerized. People make role models of actors, musicians, and hip-hop artists. This is especially true of young people who want to be like them, live like them, think like them, dress like them, talk like them, and walk like them. When someone shaves his head to look like Amir Khan in the film Gajni, he has made Amir Khan his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>. By definition, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> is the one by whom a person is completely and utterly captivated — the one to imitate, the one to become. It may be a person, an object, a profession, or anything else. Whatever mesmerizes and grips a person so completely that it takes control of their decisions becomes their <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>.</p>
<p>These, then, are the various meanings gathered into the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>. Returning to the question: who or what is an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>?</p>
<ul>
<li>The one worshipped and obeyed is one''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>.</li>
<li>The one to whom complete devotion and loyalty are given is one''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>.</li>
<li>The one to whom a person turns in utter desperation, seeking comfort, is one''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>.</li>
<li>The one a person loves passionately, the one who becomes an obsession, is one''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>.</li>
<li>The one on whom a person leans for support — physically, mentally, emotionally, or psychologically — is one''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>.</li>
</ul>
<p>This, then, is what the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> means. And because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> is a composite word, it carries the sum of all these meanings at once. The one a person passionately loves is also the one to whom that person is completely devoted; the one on whom they lean; the one to whom they turn desperately for comfort and support. Separation or distance from that person drives them to the edge of sanity. They submit to and obey everything that person says — they worship that person, in the fullest sense described here. All these meanings converge to form a single, complete picture. That is how a composite word works.</p>
<p>Given these meanings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>, it becomes clear that every person carries numerous <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> in their life, embodying these definitions in countless forms:</p>
<ul>
<li>Some <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> take the form of people — family, friends, children, parents, actors, musicians.</li>
<li>Some <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> take the form of objects — houses, cars, one''s own body, jewelry, food, video games, movies.</li>
<li>Some <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> take the form of pursuits and compulsions — jobs, careers, recreation, drugs, sex.</li>
</ul>
<p>Every person carries hundreds, if not thousands, of entities in their life that meet the definition of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>.</p>
<h3 id="idols">idols</h3>
<p>What follows is where the discussion becomes uncomfortable. Every person and thing in our lives that meets the definition of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> gives physical form to one of the meanings of that word. By definition, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> is the one to whom we are loyal and devoted; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> is the one to whom we turn for comfort. So if smoking, movies, or video games are what someone turns to for comfort, those activities become a physical manifestation of that definition of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>. The definition is conceptual; its manifestation is physical. A person physically does something to please that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>.</p>
<p>Consider another definition: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> is the one who astounds and astonishes. If Shahrukh Khan, Sylvester Stallone, Adele, Beyonce, or Lady Gaga are the ones who astound, astonish, and impress a person to the point that they want to be like them, then the definition of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِھَا</span> has been given a form. That definition has been personified in Shahrukh Khan, Stallone, or whoever else it may be.</p>
<h3 id="as-idols"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> as Idols</h3>
<p>An idol, by dictionary definition, is an image or representation of a god used as an object of worship — the personification or objectification of someone or something. Idols are human creations. And who personifies and objectifies the definitions of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>? We do. We decide to turn to smoking, movies, drugs, and alcohol for comfort. We decide to give our complete loyalty and devotion to our jobs, careers, Facebook, and Instagram. We choose to fall passionately in love with wealth, power, and fame. No one makes those choices for us. We make them ourselves. In this sense, we become the creators of our own <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>, just as idols are human-made creations. The similarity is exact.</p>
<p>A common assumption is that being Muslim places a person entirely outside the world of idol worship. The frightening reality, according to this understanding, is otherwise. We are surrounded by thousands of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> — self-created idols — and we worship them daily. Every person, object, concept, and idea that we personify or objectify becomes, through that act, an idol fashioned by our own hands. In that sense, we are all idol-worshippers. It is a painful reality, but it is true. The concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شرک</span> is not so alien after all. This is precisely why Rasul Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الشِّرکُ فِی أُمَّتِی أَخفٰی مِن سَیرِ النَّمَلِ فِی لَیلَۃِ الظُّلمَۃِ عَلی سَخْرَۃِ السَّوْدَا</span></p><p class="tr">Shirk is more hidden in my nation than the movement of a black ant in a dark night on a black stone.</p></blockquote>
<p>That is how unaware we may be of our own idol worship.</p>
<p>At the center of all my idols stands one figure: myself. And what drove me to fashion these <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>? My desires. Every idol I have ever built is a product of my passions and caprice; each exists because I wanted it to exist. Allah says:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Furqan: 43</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرَءَيْتَ مَنِ ٱتَّخَذَ إِلَهَهُۥ هَوَىٰهُ</span></p><p class="tr">Have you seen the one who takes his own desire as his god?</p></blockquote>
<p>Allah is asking the messenger <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> whether he sees this person — the one who has taken his car, house, job, wealth, fame, and the pleasures and sensations of his body as his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>; the one who has taken smoking, drugs, alcohol, sex, movies, and video games as his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>; the one who has taken Shahrukh Khan, Salman Khan, Amir Khan, Adele, and Stallone as his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>. Allah asks Rasul Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>,</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Furqan: 43</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَفَأَنتَ تَكُونُ عَلَيْهِ وَكِيلًا</span></p></blockquote>
<blockquote><p>Do you really want to be responsible for this person on the day of judgment?</p></blockquote>
<p>Worship of the self is the worst form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شرک</span> there is. When gratitude curdles into self-gratification, it becomes the worst kind of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شرک</span>. Idolatry has nothing over self-idolatry. We are therefore the ones who continually commit <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شرک</span> by taking our desires and making them our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>. Human beings exist in a constant state of self-worship, and it is precisely this condition that prevents us from truly worshipping Allah.</p>
<p>This is the default condition of mankind. Islam addresses it at the root: through the negation and destruction of these self-created idols, the understanding of Allah emerges. Allah is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا اِلَہَ اِلَّا</span> — He is everything other than what is taken as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>. When every idol born of the desires of the self is broken and destroyed, when the Kaaba of the heart stands empty of them, what is found there is Allah.</p>
<p>The first act of Rasul Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> after the conquest of Mecca was to cleanse the Kaaba of all its idols. Three hundred and sixty-five idols stood in it, around it, and upon it. He destroyed every one without exception. That same exercise must be undertaken in every life. Just as Rasul Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> conquered Mecca, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نفس</span> must be conquered. Just as he cleared the Kaaba of its idols one by one, the Kaaba of the heart must gradually be cleared of these idols — one by one. And the greatest and most powerful idol standing in the Kaaba of the heart is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نفس</span> itself: the passions.</p>
<p>This is why the creedal formula strikes at the very root of the problem: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا اِلَٰہَ اِلَّا اللَّہُ</span>. There is no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> except Allah. No person, thing, concept, principle, or ideology is worthy of being given form — except the one who is free of all form, and that is Allah. Through the negation of self-made idols and the elimination of self-created <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> emerges. When the definite article joins <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>, what results is the ultimate <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>, Allah.</p>
<p>The creedal formula <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا اِلَٰہَ اِلَّا اللَّہُ</span> is the act of negating the multiplicity of self-created <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> in order to affirm the one true <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>, Allah. It is the practice of making <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> into <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ال الہ</span>.</p>
<p>It is the practice of directing every definition of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> toward the one ultimate being — of making Him <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ال</span> + <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللہ</span>, of making Him Allah. It is the work of isolating each definition of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> and making it specific to Allah alone.</p>
<p>Allah is now the only one I worship and obey. If any other <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> conflicts with His commands, I disobey that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> in order to obey the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ال</span> + <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>, which is Allah.</p>
<p>This does not mean that life cannot be enjoyed. The difference is that Allah now stands at the front and center of my worship and obedience. So long as nothing conflicts with that worship and obedience, I can enjoy life to the fullest.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا اِلَٰہَ اِلَّا اللَّہُ</span> now means <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا یُعْلَی اِلَّا اللَّہُ</span>: Allah is the only one to whom I am completely devoted and loyal, above everyone else in my life — even my parents, even my children. I serve only Allah and no one else.</p>
<p>Yet I am glad to serve my parents because Allah has commanded it. I am glad to spend time with my friends, to enjoy life, and to do whatever I wish, so long as my ultimate loyalty and devotion remain with Allah. Whenever anything or anyone challenges that loyalty, I choose Allah over them. That is how I make Him <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ال</span> + <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا اِلَٰہَ اِلَّا اللَّہُ</span> now means <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لا أَلَہَ الا اللہ</span>: Allah is the only one to whom I turn in utter desperation for every need and every comfort.</p>
<p>This does not mean that I cannot seek help from family and friends. Nor does it mean that, when I am hungry and without work, I sit idle because Allah is the appointed provider. Not at all. I turn to whomever I need, for whatever I need. The difference is that I know these people are simply means — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَسْبَاب</span> — through which Allah provides. My ultimate petition, therefore, is always addressed to Him: <em>Ya Allah! I need this. I turn to you for the fulfillment of my needs. Provide me with the means and resources that will fulfill them in a way that pleases you. For it is you, and only you, who are the source of my provision — no one else.</em></p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا اِلَٰہَ اِلَّا اللَّہُ</span> now means <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لا وَلَہَ الا اللہ</span>: Allah is the one I love passionately, the one in whom I am completely absorbed. From the moment I wake until the moment I return to sleep, He is the one I think about.</p>
<p>This does not mean that I spend the entire day on the prayer rug or every hour in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تسبیح</span>. I may do whatever I wish in life. But everything becomes a sign pointing toward Allah, a reminder of Him. That is what love does: the flower, the rainbow, the sunset, the frog in the pond — all recall the beloved. Love is an obsession. It is a constant connection with the beloved.</p>
<p>The story of Hazrat <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أیوب</span> illuminates this. In a hadith of Rasul Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>, Allah spoke to His angels about the patience and gratitude of Hazrat <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أیوب</span> AS. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ابلیس</span> then addressed Allah directly, arguing that the only reason <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أیوب</span> was grateful was because of the abundance he had been given — his wealth and his children. Strip those away, he said, and the gratitude would disappear. So Allah began to take them away. First the wealth went. Then, before his eyes, the house collapsed and killed all his sons. The word that rose instinctively to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أیوب</span>''s lips at that sight was AlHamdulillah. And if that were not enough, a terrible disease then descended upon him: his body became infected with worms that consumed his flesh. Allah put Hazrat <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أیوب</span> through these extreme trials to test his faith and love, and he bore them with extraordinary grace. When the trial was complete, Allah directed him to bathe in a spring prepared for him. He bathed, and the disease was completely cured. Years later, someone asked him: <em>How is your health now, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أیوب</span>?</em> He sighed and replied that the days of his illness had been more beloved to him than these days of health. The man was astonished and asked how that could be. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أیوب</span> replied that during his illness, Allah had asked him each day: <em>How are you today, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أیوب</span>?</em> Now that he was cured, Allah no longer asked, and he missed the love and care that had been showered upon him during those days of trial. This is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِھَا</span>. This is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوَلَّا</span> — extreme, passionate love, in which the absence of the beloved drives a person to the very edge of self.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا اِلَٰہَ اِلَّا اللَّہُ</span> now means <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لا أَلِہَ الا اللہ</span>: Allah is the only one on whom I ultimately lean for support — emotionally, psychologically, financially, physically, in every form.</p>
<p>This does not mean that I will not see a doctor when I am ill, ask my employer for a raise when I deserve one, or turn to family and friends for help when I need it. I will continue to do all of that. But my ultimate support comes from Allah. He is the one who cures my illness, not the doctor. He is the one who ordains the raise, not my employer. He is the one who fulfills my need, not my friends and family.</p>
<p>The liberation this brings is immense. So many of my complaints about people and life would simply disappear. There is no longer any need to blame someone for a disappointment, because that person was never meant to be the one on whom I ultimately depended in the first place. Disappointment requires expectation. When all my ultimate expectations are from Allah, no one in this world can truly disappoint me. If I receive what I wanted, Allah was the one who gave it. If I do not receive it, it was not meant for me — and who can say that receiving it would not, in the long run, have proved harmful?</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا اِلَٰہَ اِلَّا اللَّہُ</span> now means <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لا وَلِہَ الا اللہ</span>: Allah is the only one who ultimately astounds and impresses me.</p>
<p>This does not mean that I should appreciate no one, praise no one, or find no one impressive. It means that I see Allah behind every cause for praise. If my child does well in school — MashaAllah! Praise to Allah, who made my child capable. If I am mesmerized by the sunrise or sunset — SubhanAllah! Praise to Allah for creating this beauty, and for granting me the eyes, the time, and the willingness to appreciate it. I take the praise inspired by the beauty of the sunset and attribute it to its Creator.</p>
<p>Through this practice, the true concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">توحید</span> comes into view. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">توحید</span> is not the idea that Allah is the supreme deity within a hierarchy of deities. It is the recognition that there is no other deity at all. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لا الہ</span> — the complete elimination of every other deity. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الا اللہ</span> — except the one and only, Allah. It is the transference of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الھیۃ</span> from its generic form to the one specific being who has no form. This is the challenge and the goal of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا اِلَٰہَ اِلَّا اللَّہُ</span>.</p>
<p>The vehicle that carries a person along this path is Salaat. Within its very nature, Salaat contains the act of breaking idols. The moment the time for prayer arrives, whatever one is doing must stop, whoever one is attending to must be left, and the face must turn toward Allah. Salaat is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا اِلَٰہَ اِلَّا اللَّہُ</span> enacted.</p>
<p>That is the journey of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا اِلَٰہَ اِلَّا اللَّہُ</span>. It is a difficult journey, and every journey requires provisions to sustain the traveler. The provision for this journey is knowledge of Allah. The greater that knowledge, the greater one''s amazement at His glory. The greater the amazement, the more passionate the love. The more passionate the love, the deeper the absorption. The deeper the absorption, the more complete the worship and submission to His commands. The more complete the worship, the more fully one turns to Him for refuge. The more one finds shelter under His protection, the more one leans on Him for support — for love engenders love. The more one comes to understand how deeply He loves, the more one loves in return. And the more that love grows, the more intensely His blessings and mercy are experienced.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Ibrahim: 7</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَإِذْ تَأَذَّنَ رَبُّكُمْ لَىِٕن شَكَرْتُمْ لَأَزِيدَنَّكُمْ ۖ وَلَىِٕن كَفَرْتُمْ إِنَّ عَذَابِى لَشَدِيدٌۭ</span></p><p class="tr">And when your master proclaimed: &quot;If you are grateful, I will give you more, but if you are ungrateful, indeed! My Punishment is indeed severe.&quot;</p></blockquote>
<p>Salaat is the act of breaking idols. It begins with Wudu — the purification of the very parts of the body used in the service of those idols.</p>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'linguistic meaning of allah';
INSERT INTO chapter (slug, anchor_key, idx, title, html, word_count) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'what is worship?', 5, 'What is Worship?', '', 2478);
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ بِسْمِ اللَّهِ الرَّحْمَنِ الرَّحِيمِ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ صَانِعِ الْخَلْقِ عَلَى أَكْمَلِ نِظَامٍ وَأَحْسَنِ قِوَامٍ الْوَادِي عَلَى أَشْرَفِ أَجْدَرِ الْمَقَامِ وَأَرْفَقِ مَسَامٍ وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى الْمُؤَيَّدِ أَبْلَغِ الْكَلَامِ مُحَمَّدٍ الْمَبْعُوثِ صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَعَلَى آلِهِ وَسَلَّمَ لِهِدَايَتِكَ فَاتِحِ الْأَنَامِ إِلَى سُبُلِ السَّلَامِ وَإِلَى مَا دَلَّ يَالِي وَلَا يَمِّ وَالْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي لَمْ يَتَّخِذْ وَلَدًا وَلَمْ يَكُنْ لَهُ شَرِيكٌ فِي الْمُلْكِ وَلَمْ يَكُنْ لَهُ وَلِيٌّ مِنَ الذُّلِّ وَكَبِّرْهُ تَكْبِيرًا وَالْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي أَنْزَلَ عَلَى عَبْدِهِ الْكِتَابَ وَلَمْ يَجْعَلْ لَهُ عِوَجًا الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي نَحْمَدُهُ وَنَسْتَعِينُهُ وَنَسْتَغْفِرُهُ وَنُؤْمِنُ بِهِ وَنَتَوَكَّلُ عَلَيْهِ وَنَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنْ شُرُورِ أَنْفُسِنَا وَمِنْ سَيِّئَاتِ أَعْمَالِنَا مَنْ يَهْدِهِ اللَّهُ فَلَا مُضِلَّ لَهُ وَمَنْ يُضْلِلْهُ فَلَا هَادِيَ لَهُ وَنَشْهَدُ أَنْ لَا إِلَهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ وَحْدَهُ لَا شَرِيكَ لَهُ وَنَشْهَدُ أَنَّ مُحَمَّدًا عَبْدُ اللَّهِ وَرَسُولُهُ أَرْسَلَهُ اللَّهُ تَعَالَى بِالْهُدَى وَدِينِ الْحَقِّ لِيُظْهِرَهُ عَلَى الدِّينِ كُلِّهِ وَكَفَى بِاللَّهِ شَهِيدًا وَنَشْهَدُ أَنَّ مَوْلَانَا عَلِيَّ بْنَ أَبِي طَالِبٍ وَصِيُّهُ وَوَزِيرُهُ فَصَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِمْ وَعَلَى آلِهِمْ وَسَلَّمَ عَلَيْهِمْ تَسْلِيمًا كَثِيرًا كَثِيرًا اللَّهُمَّ لَا عِلْمَ لَنَا إِلَّا مَا عَلَّمْتَنَا إِنَّكَ أَنْتَ الْعَلِيمُ الْحَكِيمُ اللَّهُمَّ عَلِّمْنَا بِمَا يَنْفَعُنَا وَانْفَعْنَا بِمَا عَلَّمْتَنَا اللَّهُمَّ إِنَّا نَعُوذُ بِكَ مِنْ عِلْمٍ لَا يَنْفَعُ وَمِنْ قَلْبٍ لَا يَخْشَعُ وَمِنْ عَيْنٍ لَا تَدْمَعُ وَمِنْ نَفْسٍ لَا تَشْبَعُ</span> Faamma baadi akkullu <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى في كِتابِهِ الكَريمِ</span> baada akkulla <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ بِسْمِ اللَّهِ الرَّحْمَٰنِ الرَّحِيمِ رَبِّ اشْرَحْ لِي صَدْرِي وَيَسِّرْ لِي أَمْرِي وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِنْ لِسَانِي يَفْقَهُوا قَوْلِي اللَّهُمَّ ثَبِّتْنَا عِنْدَ الْمَوْتِ بِلَا إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ وَاللَّهُمَّ اجْعَلْنَا مِنَ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَعَمِلُوا الصَّالِحَاتِ وَتَوَاصَوْا بِالْحَقِّ وَتَوَاصَوْا بِالصَّبْرِ آمِينَ يَا رَبَّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span></p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span>. In the last session, I t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>ked about the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهْ</span> and its various flavors and meanings. We learned that when the definite article the or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span> in Arabic, when it gets applied to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهْ</span>, it becomes <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِلٰه</span>. And both of those words then fuse together to form <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. So, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l the various meanings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهْ</span> then become exclusive for the one that we c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. That''s what the Al does. So now, if I take the meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهْ</span> as a powerful entity that demands submission, then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> becomes the one, the only one for me.</p>
<p>Right? He becomes <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِلَٰه</span> for me. The only one I deem worthy of my submission. He becomes the only one I must be completely loy<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span> to. Because there is no one more powerful than Him. And it is He who created the heavens and the earth in re<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>ity. And the day He says be, it becomes. His word is the ultimate truth and He has <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l dominion on the day the trumpet will be blown. He is the knower of the unseen as well as the seen. And He is wise and He knows everything. And then the same formula applies to the rest of the meanings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَٰه</span>. If we take the meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَٰه</span> as seeking protection and refuge, then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is the only one I exclusively turn to for protection and refuge in desperate times.</p>
<p>If I take <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهْ</span> in the meaning of love, then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> becomes the only one I choose to be passionately in love with. He is the only entity, the only deity that I choose to be completely obsessed about. For <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l the infinite favors that He bestows upon me, knowing well that I am an extremely ungrateful and unworthy creature, He does not hold back in bestowing His favors upon me. ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَآتَىٰكُم مِّن كُلِّ مَا سَأَلْتُمُوهُ ۚ وَإِن تَعُدُّوا۟ نِعْمَتَ ٱللَّهِ لَا تُحْصُوهَآ ۗ إِنَّ ٱلْإِنسَنَ لَظَلُومٌۭ كَفَّارٌۭ</span> ‎. And He gave you of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l that you asked for. And if you try to count the blessings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, you will never be able to count them. Indeed, no doubt about it, man is an extreme wrongdoer, a disbeliever, an ungrateful being.</p>
<p>Now, if I take the meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهْ</span> in the sense of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَاللَّهَ</span>, of astonishment, then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> becomes the only one whose glory and whose magnificence astonishes me. I re<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>ize that even when I set out to understand Him, when I venture to recognize Him, I do so with the re<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>ization that His loftiness will exhaust my limited capabilities of comprehension and understanding. I will be dumbfounded every single time I attempt to approach Him.</p>
<p>Because there is just nothing that is comparable to Him. And there are no words that can express His glory. If the sea were to become ink, in order to write the words of my master, indeed the sea would be exhausted before the words of my master would be finished. Even if he brought something similar to the oceans to assist, the words of my master cannot be captured. The praise of my master cannot be captured. So that''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَالْحَمْدُ</span>.</p>
<p>Fin<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>ly, if I take <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهْ</span> in the meaning of the one that I lean on for support, then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> becomes the only one that I choose to lean on. He becomes the only one I choose to put my entire weight of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l my spiritu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span> burden on for support. Because His support is the only support that matters. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن يَنصُرْكُمُ ٱللَّهُ فَلَا غَالِبَ لَكُمْ</span>. If <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> helps you, no one can overcome you. And if He forsakes you, then who is there after Him that can help you? And in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, let <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>one the believers put their trust.</p>
<p>So, in other words, there are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l these different meanings, these various rivers of knowledge that flow out of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهْ</span>, and they <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l then become exclusive. They <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l converge into one entity when it reaches the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِلَٰه</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. But the re<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span> question is, what do we get out of the union of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l these meanings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهْ</span>? So if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is the only powerful entity deserving submission, if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is the only one capable of providing refuge and protection, if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is the only one worthy of passionate love and obsession for my thoughts, and if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is the only one who astonishes me, astounds me, if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is the only one I choose to lean and depend on, then the union of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l these meanings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهْ</span> with its exclusivity of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, making Him <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, what does the union of these meanings produce for me? What does <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l of this mean to me? What does this make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> to me? They make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> worthy of my worship. They make Him worthy of my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>.</p>
<p>This is why He created me. And I did not create jinn or the humans except that they should worship me. And this is why He says that. Because the sum of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l these meanings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهْ</span>, they make Him worthy of my worship. This is why every single day, in every single <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَمَاز</span>, I say, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ</span> ‎. It is only you that we worship, and it is only you that we seek help from. This concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِلٰه</span>, what it leads us to is the idea of worship, the idea of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>. And the one we worship is c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>led <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَعْبُود</span>. We are the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدُ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَعْبُود</span>.</p>
<p>Now, what is worship? The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> is commonly translated as worship, right? But linguistic<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>ly speaking,</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/154/60534835-e6af-4fc4-8820-31a58bca68fb.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> actu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>ly means a combination of two things. It means to worship and it <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>so means to obey, both at the same time. They''re not interchangeable like many other words in the English language. No. Both meanings of worship and obedience are embedded inside this one word of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> in the Arabic language, in classic<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span> Arabic. Together, they both mean <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>. You cannot take one meaning out of this word. It is worship and it is obedience.</p>
<p>But gener<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>ly, when we think of the word worship, what comes to our mind? The five prayers, fasting, paying <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زَكَاة</span>, performing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الحَجّ</span>, right? These are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l different forms and the ritu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>s of worship.</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/152/5e2c8227-d756-43cf-9baa-783172ee5603.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>So it is worship. But these are the ritu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>s of worship. But what happens is that we think that''s it, and we stop at <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l of those activities. And that''s the point that I want to highlight today. The problem with this translation of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> exclusively in the meaning of worship is that it does not necessarily include the concept of obedience. Especi<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>ly an obedience that stems out of love. That is critic<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span> to understand. That is extremely important to understand. Worship is a love-based obedience. Because the word obedience, it can have a very negative connotation to it, right? Because people can be forced into obedience. Children are often bullied into obedience by the use of either threats or violence.</p>
<p>So it is possible for obedience to have a very negative emotion attached to it. But this is not the kind of obedience that we''re t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>king about here when we say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> is an obedience that comes natur<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>ly from the appreciation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l these various meanings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهْ</span> that we learned. When we choose to love <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> passionately, when we choose to become obsessed about Him, when we are completely devoted and loy<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span> to Him, when we completely trust Him and lean upon Him, When we''re tot<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>ly impressed and amazed by His loftiness, His greatness, His magnificence, when we know that we can <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>ways uncondition<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>ly turn to Him for protection and refuge, then the natur<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span> outcome of the sum of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l of these love-based emotions is obedience. So remember that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> is an obedience that stems out of love. It is a lover''s obedience to make the beloved happy. And this is why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> created us. And this is why He gave us this ability to love. He did not create us and desire disobedience from us. He <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>lows disobedience. His magnificence enables this capability of disobedience within His creation because disobedience is a product of free will.</p>
<p>which is c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>led <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خِذْلَان</span>. But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is not content with it, because disobedience is completely contrary to the concept of love. You know, there''s a poet by the name of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">محمُودٌ الوَرَّاقُ</span>, and in one of his poems he says, You disobey <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and then you feign the love of God? This is an impossible and unknown kind of logic. Had your love been sincere, you would have been obedient. Because a lover is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>ways obedient to the one he loves. So, it is the nature of love that we want to obey the one that we love. In another one of his poems, he says, . You know, a woman once asked someone about the state of this person who loves her with this burning passion. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَاشِق</span> is the one who is burning up with passion. Right?</p>
<p>They say, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَوَاءُ الْعِشْقِ الِاتِّصَالُ</span>. The only cure for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشْق</span> is to be connected with the beloved, to be in the embrace of the beloved. So, she''s t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>king about this man, she''s asking about this man who loves her, and she''s inquiring about his love for her. And this is something that lovers do, right? They want to know, they inquire from others about how much their beloved loves them. So, she asks this man, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لِلَّهِ صِفْهُ، وَلَا تَنْقُصْ وَلَا تَزِدِ</span>. For <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهِ</span> sake, describe him to me and don''t stint in your description and don''t exaggerate. In other words, you know, I want to know the truth. I want to hear the truth. So, this man, he says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَوْ كَانَ رَحْلِيلُ الْمَوْتِ بِالدُّمَاءِ</span>. If your lover thought that he would die from thirst, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَقُتِيَ قِيفَانَ وُرُودَ الْمَاءِ لَمْ يَرِدِ</span>. And if you said don''t touch that water, he wouldn''t touch it.</p>
<p>So her lover knows that he is going to die of thirst, but if the woman said don''t touch that water, he wouldn''t touch it. He''d rather die than disobey. So the point of that, what he''s emphasizing there is that the nature of love is obedience. So the more you love <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, the more you will want to obey Him. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> has these two concepts embedded within it: worship and obedience. Both of which stem out of this passionate love for Him and not from some kind of forced compulsion or coercion. And to put this in practic<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span> terms, to give you an example, let''s say, you know, we are about to pray in 15 minutes, right? Let''s say it''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَجْر</span> time. And so we have to pray. We have to do our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>. We have to go through the ritu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span> of prayer.</p>
<p>Now, what kind of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> is prayer, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span>? Is it worship or is it obedience? It''s worship, right? But then from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَجْر الْفَاتِحَة</span>l the way up till <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظُهْر</span>, we will not be worshipping him through this ritu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span> of prayer, but if we are sincere in our love for him, we will be doing what? We will be obeying him. We will be in a state of obedience with him. Because it is the dream and the desire of every lover to make their beloved happy. And so the beauty of this word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> is that it covers the entire spectrum of love, putting us in a constant state of worship. You see, when we are performing the ritu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span> acts of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span>, we are in a state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>. But then when we are in between two prayers, by being in a state of obedience, we are still in the state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>. You see, this is why</p>
<p>The concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> is so comprehensive in Islam. We are constantly in a state of worship, if not through the form<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span> ritu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>s, then through obedience. So what this means is that if you go in the morning and earn a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَلَالٌ</span> income through your job, that is an act of obedience, that is an act of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>. When you go to school and you sincerely accomplish the purpose of education, you''re in a state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>, you''re worshiping. When you love and respect your elders, when you are kind to your siblings, when you''re generous to the needy, when you''re courageous in the face of temptations, no matter what you do, as long as you''re in a state of obedience and servitude to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, anything and everything that you do becomes an act of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>. It becomes worship. It turns into worship.</p>
<p>Going to the toilet becomes <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>. Imagine that. When you go to the toilet to relieve yourself, if you obey the commands of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> to cover your head and feet and seek His protection and recite <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l those <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاءٌ</span>, you''re in a state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>. So you can see that this concept of worship, of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>, is re<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>ly incredible in Islam. And may <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> give us <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>l the strength and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْفِيق</span> to become His obedient slaves and to fulfill the purpose of our creation.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِلَاءً</span>...</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَآخِرُ دَعْوَانَا أَنِ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ، وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى رَسُولِهِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ خَاتَمِ النَّبِيِّينَ وَسَيِّدِ الْمُرْسَلِينَ، وَعَلَى أَخِيهِ وَوَصِيِّهِ عَلِيِّ بْنِ أَبِي طَالِبٍ أَشْرَفِ الْوَصِيِّينَ، وَعَلَى الْأَئِمَّةِ مِنْ ذُرِّيَّتِهِمَا الطَّيِّبِينَ الطَّاهِرِينَ، وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَيْهِمْ أَجْمَعِينَ، الْفَاتِحَة</span></p>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'what is worship?';
INSERT INTO chapter (slug, anchor_key, idx, title, html, word_count) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'linguistic meaning of rehma', 6, 'Linguistic Meaning Of REHMA', '', 3963);
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>I have studied composite words such as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> combines several meanings derived from:</p>
<ul>
<li><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلَہَ یَعلُہُ</span>, signifying the one worthy of worship.</li>
<li><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَہَ</span>, which describes a child yearning for its mother''s milk, representing the one we desperately turn to for our needs.</li>
<li><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَہَ</span>, indicating the one we passionately love.</li>
<li><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلِہَ</span>, meaning the one we rely on or place our trust in.</li>
<li><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِہَ</span>, which expresses being astounded by someone''s glory and magnificence.</li>
</ul>
<p>I combine all these meanings to understand <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>.</p>
<p>I then discussed our creedal formula of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا اِلٰہَ اِلَّا اللَّہُ</span>. This formula aims to eliminate the multiplicity of generic objects of worship, represented by <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>, and to identify the one specific object of worship, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>. This involves applying the definite article &quot;the&quot; to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>, which then becomes <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ال</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>, or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللہ</span>.</p>
<p>Next, I examined the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>, which is also a composite word, combining</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلْمَالِكُ وَالسَّيِّدُ وَالْمُرَبِّي وَالْمُرْشِدُ وَالْمُنْعِمُ وَالْقَيِّمُ</span></p></blockquote>
<ul>
<li><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المَالِکُ</span>: The absolute owner.</li>
<li><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّیِّد</span>: The one with complete authority.</li>
<li><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المُرَبِّی</span>: The one who nourishes.</li>
<li><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المُرْشَدُ</span>: The one who guides and puts to purpose.</li>
<li><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المُنعِمُ</span>: The benevolent benefactor; the one who showers with undeserved gifts.</li>
<li><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">القَیِّمُ</span>: The maintainer.</li>
</ul>
<p>Combining the meanings of all these words leads to an understanding of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>, which I translate as master.</p>
<p>I then looked at the concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبد</span>, which, from a Quranic perspective, means a &quot;free slave.&quot; To understand this relationship, I examined the master-slave relationship of this world. I then compared it to the master-slave relationship that Allah has with us, concluding that even though Allah is our master, he is unlike any other master we have encountered in this world.</p>
<h3 id="mercy">Mercy</h3>
<p>I now examine the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span>. This is the primary attribute of Allah, and it is an attribute most dear to him. This quality is so fundamental to him that he has made it obligatory upon himself. Allah has imposed certain obligations on Muslims, such as prayers, fasting, zakah, and Hajj. He has made many actions compulsory for Muslims. However, he has made one action compulsory upon himself: the action of his mercy, his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span>.</p>
<p>In a hadith, Rasul Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> says:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-hadith"><span class="q-band q-band--hadith"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Prophetic tradition</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إنَّ الله لَمَّا قَضٰى الخَلق،كَتَبَ فَوقَ عَرْشُه اِنَّ رَحمَتِی تَغلِبُ غَضَبِی</span></p><p class="tr">Indeed, after Allah established all his creation, he wrote on his throne, &quot;Indeed my mercy shall overcome my wrath&quot;</p></blockquote>
<p>Indeed, when Allah established all of creation, he wrote a statement on his throne. This statement was:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-hadith"><span class="q-band q-band--hadith"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Prophetic tradition</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِنَّ رَحمَتِی تَغلِبُ غَضَبِی</span></p><p class="tr">Indeed, my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span>—my mercy for my creation—will always overcome my wrath.</p></blockquote>
<p>In other words, in my dealings with creation, my mercy will always supersede my anger. This is a rule that our Almighty Creator imposed upon himself in his dealings with us. SubhanAllah! This statement is not literal; there is no actual physical throne. Rather, it is a graphic representation of an emotion, depicting Allah''s love for his creation.</p>
<p>I now turn to the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span>. It is a very powerful word. I will first examine the general definition of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span>. A more graphic representation of the word will follow, to help visualize this concept properly, InshaAllah.</p>
<p>The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span> is generally translated in English as &quot;mercy.&quot; This translation, however, distorts the word''s true meaning. I do not claim it is incorrect, but rather that it is a weak rendering. The reason is that the word &quot;mercy&quot; is generally used when someone is spared from punishment. When one hears the words, &quot;the soldiers showed mercy,&quot; it implies they possessed the power to inflict something terrible but chose to spare everyone. The criminal begged the judge for mercy, meaning he asked to be spared from punishment. Thus, the general understanding of &quot;mercy&quot; involves an impending negative or painful event, from which someone then decides to withhold the action. This is what it means to show mercy. Therefore, a subconscious association with punishment exists when the word mercy is used, coupled with the idea of escaping that punishment. When I consider mercy, the underlying assumption is often punishment.</p>
<p>When I consistently use the word &quot;mercy&quot; for Allah, carrying that subconscious taint of escaping punishment, and repeat it frequently, it subtly creates the impression that Allah intended to inflict something harmful upon us, but then He decided against it. This implies He showed us mercy by not punishing us. This fosters a subtle, subconscious image of Allah as a tyrant in some sense— <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَعُوذُ بِاللّٰہِ</span>.</p>
<p>In reality, this is not the true intent of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span>. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span> has absolutely nothing to do with punishment. It contains no concept of punishment. I will now examine the linguistic meaning of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span>.</p>
<p>Definition of rehma</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/79/9da9df6f-f8e4-4a81-9768-38023d7a120f.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" style="--img-h:335px" /></figure>
<p>In classical Arabic, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span> has four meanings:</p>
<ol>
<li value="1">Unconditional love</li>
<li value="2">Care</li>
<li value="3">Genuine concern</li>
<li value="4">Mercy</li>
</ol>
<p>Mercy is the final piece of this puzzle, not the initial one. Unconditional love comes first, followed by care, then concern, and finally mercy. This is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span>. These four words are self-explanatory. Unconditional love is affection without conditions. Allah loves His creation unconditionally, like a mother who loves all her children equally, regardless of their obedience or disobedience. It does not matter to her regarding her love for them. Allah loves us in the same way, and this is fitting. We are all His creation, after all. He created us with His own hands.</p>
<p>Allah is caring. He looks after us, provides for us, and fulfills all our needs.</p>
<p>He is genuinely and sincerely concerned for our salvation, happiness, and well-being.</p>
<p>Finally, Allah is merciful, in that He shows compassion toward our weakness. He graciously forgives our sins and transgressions, and He accepts our weak and feeble acts of worship, even though they may lack the sincerity and honor He deserves.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span> combines these four words: unconditional love, extreme care, genuine concern, and mercy. Linguistically speaking, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span> means to show love, care, concern, and mercy for the purpose of benefiting someone. The ancient Arab would say:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمۃُ، رِقَّۃٌ تَقتَدِ الأحسانُ أِلی المَرحوم</span></p></blockquote>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span> signifies a form of love or tenderness specifically intended to benefit the beloved. In other words, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span> is precisely targeted toward someone''s welfare and must manifest as a beneficial action; otherwise, it is not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span>.</p>
<h3 id="two-manifestations-of-mercy">Two Manifestations of Mercy</h3>
<p>2 Manifestations</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/79/40b93a03-5882-4ae6-9177-d9f5d781701c.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>I find two common manifestations of this word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span> in the Quran: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحیم</span>. Both originate from the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span>. I will first examine how these words are commonly translated. English translations of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللَّہِ الرَّحْمَٰنِ الرَّحِیمِ</span> often render the phrase as:</p>
<ul>
<li>&quot;In the name of Allah – the most gracious, the most merciful.&quot;</li>
<li>&quot;In the name of Allah – the most beneficent, the most merciful.&quot;</li>
<li>&quot;The most merciful, the most kind.&quot;</li>
<li>&quot;The merciful, the beneficent.&quot;</li>
<li>&quot;The beneficent, the beneficent.&quot;</li>
</ul>
<p>The difficulty with these translations lies in how challenging these words are to process and visualize in English. The word &quot;beneficent,&quot; for instance, rarely appears in everyday English conversation. One would not typically use it to express gratitude for assistance, saying, &quot;You are so beneficent.&quot; The fundamental purpose of translation is to enhance understanding of the text. However, employing words in translation that are unfamiliar or unrelatable to the reader undermines this very purpose.</p>
<p>I will first clarify the meaning of these two words. As I said, both <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحیم</span> are manifestations of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span>. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span> signifies a combination of four elements, all intended to benefit someone:</p>
<ul>
<li>Unconditional love</li>
<li>Care</li>
<li>Genuine concern</li>
<li>Mercy</li>
</ul>
<p>When Allah is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحیم</span>, I acknowledge these four qualities of Allah. This means that Allah has intense love for us; He cares for us; He is genuinely concerned for us; and He shows us mercy. This is the implicit acknowledgment in any word that derives from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span>. Allah states this point repeatedly in the Quran:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Qasas: 73</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمِن رَّحْمَتِهِۦ جَعَلَ لَكُمُ ٱلَّيْلَ وَٱلنَّهَارَ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Out of His love, He created the night and the day:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Kahf: 16</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِّن رَّحْمَتِهِۦ وَيُهَيِّئْ لَكُم مِّنْ أَمْرِكُم مِّرْفَقًۭا</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Out of His love, He will make our tasks easy for us.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمِنْ آيَتِهِۦٓ أَن يُرْسِلَ ٱلرِّيَاحَ مُبَشِّرَتٍۢ وَلِيُذِيقَكُم مِّن رَّحْمَتِهِ</span> Out of His love, He sends the winds that deliver good tidings, so that one recognizes His love. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَّا يَفْتَحِ ٱللَّهُ لِلنَّاسِ مِن رَّحْمَةٍۢ فَلَا مُمْسِكَ لَهَاۖ</span> When Allah opens the doors of His love for someone, nothing can close them.</p>
<p>The purpose of all such verses is to explain one thing. I discussed Allah as our Master. When one considers the word &quot;master&quot; in the worldly sense, or the word &quot;authority,&quot; the last thing that comes to mind in a master-slave relationship is unconditional love. But Allah immediately reminds us, at the beginning of every recitation: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللَّہِ الرَّحْمَٰنِ الرَّحِیمِ</span>. He reminds us that He is a different kind of Master. He reminds us that one will never, ever find a master in this world as powerful as Allah, yet one who possesses the kind of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span> that He does. SubhanAllah!</p>
<p>No other master in this world will show unconditional love to their slaves. They might take care of their slaves once in a while, but they will never truly love them. Masters will always show love to their own kin, never to their slave. But this Master—our Master, Allah—the very first emotion He exhibits toward us is unconditional love. No other master will care for us the way Allah cares for us. No other master will be genuinely concerned about us, the way Allah is concerned for us. No other master will show us mercy when one disobeys them and fails to complete the task to their standards. But Allah always has mercy on us.</p>
<h3 id="rehman">REHMAN</h3>
<p>I will now explain the linguistic meanings of the words <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحیم</span>. This understanding, God willing, will be profound and will deepen one''s appreciation of Allah.</p>
<p>The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمن</span> follows a specific phonetic pattern that conveys intensification, ferocity, severity, and extreme force. Any Arabic word following this phonetic pattern, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آن</span>, shares these characteristics. Examples include:</p>
<ul>
<li><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غضبان</span> (extremely angry; intensely angry)</li>
<li><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جوعان</span> (extremely hungry; ferociously hungry)</li>
<li><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عطشان</span> (extremely thirsty)</li>
</ul>
<p>Following this same pattern, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمن</span> means &quot;extremely merciful&quot; or &quot;intensely merciful.&quot;</p>
<p>Rehman</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/79/bd725d5f-a767-46d9-b83c-e8e7d526de0a.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>Any word that follows this phonetic pattern possesses three characteristics:</p>
<ol>
<li value="1">It is extreme.</li>
<li value="2">It is immediate.</li>
<li value="3">It is temporary.</li>
</ol>
<p>All words matching this pattern consistently possess these three characteristics: they are extreme, immediate, and temporary.</p>
<p>The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمن</span> signifies Allah''s love, care, concern, and mercy. This is not merely love as humans understand it; rather, it is an extreme and intense form of love, care, concern, and mercy that transcends human imagination or any worldly example. Thus, the first characteristic of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span> is that any love, care, concern, and mercy one anticipates from Allah will surpass all human imagination and expectation. No example of such love can be found in this world.</p>
<p>The second meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span> is that it is something happening immediately. One does not have to wait for it; it is occurring right now. I consider this point by observing a distinction in English between someone who <strong>is</strong> patient and someone who is <strong>being</strong> patient. The latter demonstrates patience in the present moment. For example, if I state that a woman <em><strong>is</strong></em> patient, I cannot determine if she is currently losing patience with her children. She might be yelling at them, or she might be throwing them against the wall; I do not know that. When I say a woman is patient, I mean she is generally a patient person. This does not necessarily mean she is acting patiently at this very second. However, when I say, &quot;she is <strong>being</strong> patient,&quot; I refer to her present state: right now.</p>
<p>The love and care of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span> does not manifest generally or at some distant point in time. Rather, that extreme and intense form of love is happening right now. In other words, through <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span>, the Quran teaches that one does not have to wait for Allah to show one love at some future point in time. One does not have to wait for Him to care for one, or be concerned for one at a later point in time when some event occurs, or when one is in the middle of a calamity. No, this is not so. All of that is happening right now. He loves one right now. He cares for one right now. He is genuinely concerned for one right now, and He is being merciful to one right now.</p>
<p>All of that is happening, in its extreme form, right now. The first characteristic of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span> is its extreme nature, and the second is its immediacy. The third is its temporary quality. The love of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span> is extreme, intense, and immediate, but it is temporary; it is not permanent. This third aspect, its temporary nature, may be difficult to accept initially, and it can feel counterintuitive. I will explain the reasoning shortly.</p>
<p>Thus, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span> carries three essential qualities:</p>
<ul>
<li>Extreme</li>
<li>Immediate</li>
<li>Temporary</li>
</ul>
<p>All words following this phonetic pattern share these three characteristics.</p>
<p>As an example, I offer <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غضبان</span>. This term signifies extreme anger, present at that very moment. However, this extreme anger must be temporary. An individual cannot physically sustain such intense rage indefinitely; it would be physically impossible, potentially leading to collapse or even death. Even a figure of immense rage eventually calms.</p>
<p>Similarly, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جوعان</span> denotes extreme hunger, present at that moment, yet temporary. This is because one either eats, and the hunger subsides, or one starves, and the hunger ceases with life itself. In neither scenario does the hunger persist permanently.</p>
<p>Likewise, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عتشان</span> signifies extreme thirst, present at that moment, yet temporary. One either drinks, alleviating the thirst, or succumbs to it. The thirst does not endure indefinitely.</p>
<p>Allah''s intense mercy is temporary because we would not be able to handle the intensity and severity of His love for a long period. I offer an example that illustrates this point clearly. I recall moments with a mother in a very cuddly and huggy mood. She wraps her arms around me and squeezes until I cannot breathe, saying, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أو میرا بیٹا</span>! <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أو میری بیٹی</span> - Mua Mua Mua.&quot; My face is left dripping wet after this display. This is a manifestation of intense love, a burst of affection a mother showers. This intense love is a beautiful thing when one feels:</p>
<ul>
<li>down,</li>
<li>depressed,</li>
<li>or in need of serious TLC.</li>
</ul>
<p>One would appreciate such intense love. However, if this were to happen every time one walked by the mother, with her saying, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أو میرا بیٹا</span>! <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أو میری بیٹی</span> - Mua Mua Mua,&quot; every single time, one would not be able to tolerate it. One would feel annoyed and irritated. The natural reaction would be to exclaim, &quot;Mom!!! Leave me alone! Stop marinating my face with your saliva every time I walk by!&quot;</p>
<p>Allah''s intense love is extreme and immediate, but it is temporary. If His love were constant, its intensity and severity would prevent us from functioning. We would literally go insane; we would lose our minds.</p>
<p>Story of Eisa and the Rabi.</p>
<h3 id="raheem">RAHEEM</h3>
<p>Raheem</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/79/0946d4dd-bfcb-45eb-be4c-2bebdaa9f080.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>The next word I consider is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحیم</span>. Allah''s love, care, concern, and mercy also manifest through the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحیم</span>. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحیم</span>, and all others following its phonetic pattern—<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">کَرِیمٌ، سَلِیمٌ، حَکِیمٌ، جَمِیلٌ، جَلِیلٌ</span>—possess two distinct qualities:</p>
<ul>
<li>First, it is permanent, not temporary.</li>
<li>Second, it is not necessarily immediate; its manifestation is long-term. For instance, if I describe my aunt as loving, I refer to a lasting quality. This does not imply she is actively demonstrating that love at this precise moment. She might not be showering me with affection right now, yet I remain certain her love is always present.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="rehman-before-raheem">Rehman before Raheem</h3>
<p>Throughout the Quran, I observe that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span> is consistently mentioned before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحیم</span>. This consistent pairing prompts an inquiry into why these two words are always used together.</p>
<p>If Allah had used only <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span>, His love, care, concern, and mercy would have been extreme and immediate, yet not permanent. Conversely, if Allah had used only <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحیم</span>, His love and mercy would have been permanent, but not extreme. How, then, can I describe Allah''s love, care, concern, and mercy as simultaneously extreme, beyond expectations, immediate, permanent, and long-term? The only way to express these combined qualities is through <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحیم</span>. SubhanAllah! No better terms exist to describe the qualities of Allah''s love and care than <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحیم</span>.</p>
<p>I offer an example to illustrate this point. You are at work. Today is pay day. Your boss is not there at the office; and you have a flight booked to Timbuktu for 6:00 p.m. This means you need to leave the office exactly at 5:00 o''clock or you will not make it to the airport. It is already 4:45 p.m., and you still have not received your paycheck. For some reason, you desperately need your paycheck, because that money is for your trip. You were counting on this paycheck for the expenses of your trip. So now you are getting desperate. It is 4:50 p.m. now, and your boss is still not there. You are getting extremely anxious. Your co-worker says to you: &quot;Don''t worry. Our boss is reliable. He''ll be here. You''ll get your paycheck. Don''t worry! Relax!!&quot; Now it is 4:55 p.m., and you are totally freaking out. Your co-worker is still telling you: &quot;Don''t worry! He''s reliable! He''ll be here.&quot; What might be said to the co-worker at that moment? A rude response is to be avoided; such an utterance would be highly impolite.</p>
<p>You''ll say, &quot;I know he is reliable, but I need him to be reliable right now, this very minute! I need his quality of reliability immediately.&quot; When I am in serious trouble, my need for Allah''s help is immediate. Therefore, I need the name of Allah that provides immediate assistance. That name is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span>.</p>
<p>However, I also need the other name, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحیم</span>, because it addresses my future. When Allah says <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحیم</span>, it signifies permanence, which guarantees the future. Thus, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span> guarantees His love, care, concern, and mercy for the present, while <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحیم</span> guarantees these same qualities for the future. In other words, the attributes of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحیم</span> encompass both short-term and long-term aspects of His love.</p>
<p>I offer another example. Teenagers may find this example particularly relatable. I return home, intensely hungry. I had missed breakfast, and too much work at the office prevented me from having lunch. Thus, I arrive home starving. I sit at the dining table, and my wife asks, &quot;So what do you want me to cook for next week?&quot; I respond, &quot;Woman! Who cares about next week? Give me something to eat right now!&quot; When one is extremely hungry, the sole thought is of the immediate present. Once I have finished eating my dinner tonight and I go, &quot;BURP. Aaaah. AlHamdulillah!&quot; only then do I become interested in what we are eating next week.</p>
<p>Only after paying this month''s bills does one begin to worry about next month''s. Similarly, only after paying this semester''s fees does one start to consider next semester''s. Thus, we consider the future only when our immediate concerns have been addressed. Allah says I am <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span>. I am taking care of your immediate needs right now. But when your immediate concerns are taken care of, I will also be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحیم</span> to take care of all your future concerns.</p>
<p>Someone once asked <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَبْنُ عَبَّاسٍ رَضِیَ اللہُ عَنْہُ</span> about the difference between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحیم</span>. He said, &quot;Allah is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span> in this life, and He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحیم</span> in the afterlife.&quot; This is because Allah''s love and care will cease for disbelievers on the Day of Judgment. Until that day, however, He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span> for both believers and disbelievers. He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span> for all of us—every human being. In other words, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span> does not only love and care for me or for you. He loves and cares for every single being He ever created: plants, animals, and humans. This includes people of every religion, color, or race, whether Muslim or non-Muslim. He even loves and cares for those who hate Him and deny His existence. His doors of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمنیۃ</span> are always open to everyone. He constantly hopes they will choose to return to Him. His invitation never expires, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span> is a universal quality.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَتِی وَسِعَتْ عَلَیٰ کُلِّ شَیْءٍ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>My mercy extends to all. Rain descends from the sky for all human beings to drink, and from it, they all draw their sustenance.</p>
<ul>
<li>The good drink, and the wretched drink.</li>
<li>The sinner drinks, and the pious drinks.</li>
<li>The friend of Allah drinks, and the enemy of Allah drinks.</li>
<li>The scholar drinks, and the ignorant drinks.</li>
</ul>
<p>Everybody drinks! This is the universal mercy of this world.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَا مِنْ يَوْمٍ إِلَّا وَالْبَحْرُ يَسْتَأْذِنُ رَبَّهُ فِي أَنْ يُغْرِقَ ابْنَ آدَمَ، وَالْأَرْضُ تَسْتَأْذِنُ فِي أَنْ تَبْتَلِعَهُ، وَالْمَلَائِكَةُ تَسْتَأْذِنُ فِي أَنْ تُعَاجِلَهُ وَتُهْلِكَهُ</span>.</p></blockquote>
<p>Not a single day passes without the ocean asking its Master, &quot;Ya Allah! Give me permission to drown these disbelievers! They enjoy your blessings and rebel against you?! Let me teach them a lesson!&quot;</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">والارضُ یَستَئذِنُ فی أَن تَبتَلِعَہُ</span> The earth begs its Master, &quot;Oh Ya Allah! Allow me to swallow them alive where they stand. They walk on your earth, eat its produce that you grow for them, and yet do not prostrate before you in abject humiliation?! Let me remove them from the face of this earth!&quot;</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">والملائکۃ یَستَئذِنُ فی ان تُعاَجِلَہُ وَ تُھلِکُہ</span> The angels say, &quot;..... Ya Allah, give us permission! We will destroy them! We will annihilate them!&quot;</p>
<p>The ocean, the earth, and the angels—these are all creations of Allah, just as I am. They observe my sins and rebellion, growing furious. But what does the Creator say, the One against whom I rebel?</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَعُوا عَبْدِي فَأَنَا أَعْلَمُ بِعَبْدِي مِنْكُمْ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنْ كَانَ عَبْدَكُمْ فَشَأْنُكُمْ بِهِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَإِنْ كَانَ عَبْدِي فَمِنِّي وَإِلَيَّ عَبْدِي</span>.</p></blockquote>
<p>Let them be! Leave them alone! I love my creation way more than that. If they are your creation, do whatever you please with them! But if they are my creation, then hand them over to me, and you step out of the way. I will deal with my creation!</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">یَسْأَلُ الْمَلَائِکَۃَ</span>: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فلماذا عقدُہُ؟</span></p></blockquote>
<p>The angels ask: What are you waiting for?</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">یَسْتَجِیبُ اللَّہُ</span>: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إنني أتطَّلِع إلى التوبتَہُ</span> I am waiting for their repentance. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أِنْ اَتَانِی نَھَارًا قَبِلْتُہُ أِنْ اَتَانِی لَیْلًا قَبِلْتُہُ</span> If he approaches me during the day time, I will accept him; if he approaches me during the night time I will accept him. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَن تَقَرَّبَ أِلَیَّ تَلَقَّیتُہُ مِن بَعِید</span> If anyone takes one single step toward me, I approach him by running toward him.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَنْ أَعْرَضَ عَنِّي نَادَيْتُهُ مِنْ قَرِيبٍ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَوَلَكَ رَبٌّ غَيْرِي؟</span></p><p class="tr">And if anyone walks away from me, I go after him to bring him closer and ask him: Do you have another master who is more loving than me?</p><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَسَنَةُ عِنْدِي بِعَشْرِ أَمْثَالِهَا وَأَزِيدُ</span></p><p class="tr">Do you not know that I take one good deed from you and reward you ten times for it, even more?</p><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَوَصِيَّةٌ عِنْدِي بِمِثْلِهَا وَأَعْفُو وَأَغْفِرُ</span></p><p class="tr">But I count your sin as a single sin, and when you repent, I even forgive that and reward you for your repentance.</p><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَأَيْنَ تَذْهَبُونَ</span></p><p class="tr">Then where are you going?</p></blockquote>
<p>I find that this HADEES truly portrays the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمنیۃ</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحیمیۃ</span> of our Creator. Love is an emotion that cannot be quantified in words; it can only be judged through action.</p>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'linguistic meaning of rehma';
INSERT INTO chapter (slug, anchor_key, idx, title, html, word_count) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'womb - the source of mercy', 7, 'Womb - The Source Of Mercy', '', 3330);
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<h3 id="womb---reham">Womb - REHAM</h3>
<p>REHM</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/101/69b7af1e-c0ca-4a3f-9149-ff599bc840cc.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span> derives from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحَم</span>. In Arabic, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحَم</span> refers to a mother''s womb when she carries a child. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحَم</span> is the place where a baby resides inside the mother for nine months before birth. I considered why Allah chose a word for mercy that derives from a mother''s womb. The mother''s womb describes the relationship between Allah and His creation, which makes this connection beautiful.</p>
<p>Both parents love their children. However, I observe that in the Quran, when Allah compares His love for His creation, He never uses the example of a father. He always uses the example of a mother. Therefore, to truly understand the meaning of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span> and the nature of Allah''s relationship with us, I must first understand the bond between a mother and her child during pregnancy.</p>
<p>A famous Hadith describes Allah speaking directly to the womb. Here, the womb represents the archetypal womb, as the mother''s womb is the means by which all of humanity comes into existence. All of us originate from our mothers'' wombs. Allah spoke to the womb, saying:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِنِّی سَمَّیتُکَ بِاسْمی</span></p></blockquote>
<p>I named you from My names.</p>
<p>I am <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span> and I am <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحیم</span>, and from these I derive the name for My unconditional love, My constant care, My genuine concern, and My infinite mercy. I name My <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span> based on your womb, your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحَم</span>. I make you a symbol of My <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span> and My unconditional love, because every human being who will exist through time will come through you. You will remind them of My love for them. Your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحم</span> is the very first thing they will experience before they come into this world, and My <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span> is what they will experience once they are born into this world until the day they return to Me. That is the conversation the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمن</span> had with the womb, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحَم</span>. SubhanAllah!</p>
<p>What is it about a mother''s womb that is important for understanding? Several points are important. The first question is: when a child is inside its mother''s womb, is it being punished? Is there any association with punishment for the baby while it is inside its mother''s womb? Absolutely not. The mother''s relationship with the baby is one of pure love. This serves as a reminder of the very nature of Allah''s relationship with His creation. It is based on pure love and has nothing to do with punishment. The moment a woman discovers she is pregnant, she falls in love with the baby. She has not even seen the baby yet. She has not even met the baby yet, but she still falls completely and unconditionally in love with her baby.</p>
<p>I consider whether the womb exists before conception, or if the body creates and discards it with each pregnancy. The womb exists within every woman, whether she carries a baby or not; it is always present. Similarly, Allah''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span>—his unconditional love, care, concern, and mercy—is always present. His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span> does not depend on our existence, nor on the existence of humanity or creation. Instead, his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span> is perpetually present and will endure even after we, all of humanity, and this universe cease to exist, just as the womb remains after a child is born.</p>
<p>A child inside its mother''s womb does not know it is being cared for, nor does it know of the mother''s existence. It remains unaware. Care is provided in ways it cannot imagine. The fetus in the womb cannot even imagine the womb''s role in sustaining its life, let alone comprehend or appreciate that care.</p>
<p>Similarly, Allah cares for us in ways we cannot imagine or realize. His care is so subtle that His presence, let alone His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span>, remains invisible to us. This subtlety is why one of His names is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لطیف</span>. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لطیف</span> means something extremely subtle. It is present and constantly working for us, yet we do not even feel its presence. This is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لطیف</span>.</p>
<p>It is remarkable that in the Quran, every mention of the attribute <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لطیف</span> is accompanied by another attribute. That attribute is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خبیر</span>, the All-Knowing.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-An''am: 103</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَّا تُدْرِكُهُ ٱلْأَبْصَرُ وَهُوَ يُدْرِكُ ٱلْأَبْصَرَ ۖ وَهُوَ ٱللَّطِيفُ ٱلْخَبِيرُ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Our eyes cannot see Him, but He is aware of what our eyes perceive. He is extremely subtle, yet ALL-KNOWING simultaneously. Thus, the child does not even know that its mother is caring for it.</p>
<p>I next consider the womb, and the idea of protection immediately comes to mind. The mother constantly protects the baby. This protection manifests in many ways:</p>
<ul>
<li>She watches for sharp table corners when she walks.</li>
<li>She tries to stay safe.</li>
<li>She sleeps on her side.</li>
<li>She monitors what she eats.</li>
<li>She constantly caresses the baby.</li>
</ul>
<p>Her love and care for the baby drive this extreme protectiveness.</p>
<p>Allah provides this same constant protection for His creation. People do not simply arrive at school without incident. I consider the multitude of things that can go wrong between leaving home and arriving at school. Indeed, countless potential dangers exist. Yet Allah protects and keeps His creation safe. Every day, people leave their homes, and not all of them return that night. Those who die in car crashes or accidents do not know they will not return home that night. When their appointed time arrives, their life ends. Until that time, Allah''s constant protection, His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span>, shields them from accidents and other harms. Thus, just as the womb constantly protects its creation, Allah constantly protects His own creation. The mother''s womb serves as a reminder of this truth.</p>
<p>A mother endures many difficulties for her baby. She deals with constant morning sickness. Some have to take these horribly painful tests. Some have to take extremely nauseating medicines for the health and growth of the baby. They undergo constant blood work, experiencing one painful procedure after another. A mother endures so much pain, all for the sake of her child. The child, however, remains completely unaware of this. The child takes the mother completely for granted, just as we take the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span> of Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> for granted. Moreover, the child technically does nothing for the mother, yet the mother does everything for the child. The baby consumes her food. The mother eats, but within minutes the baby takes everything, leaving her hungry again shortly after a large meal. Consequently, the husband may find himself running for ice cream in the middle of the night. The baby takes everything from her and gives nothing back to its mother. In fact, when the baby does thank her, it does so by kicking her from the inside. It might:</p>
<ul>
<li>Stretch from time to time.</li>
<li>Perform an insanity workout whenever it feels like it.</li>
<li>Bench press with her ribs.</li>
</ul>
<p>The mother silently and happily endures these actions. This is incredible. The mother remains in a constant state of discomfort because of this baby, yet she happily endures it. We do absolutely nothing for Allah, but Allah still does everything for us. We do nothing for Allah.</p>
<p>I can do nothing for Allah, even if I wished. I cannot add anything to his kingdom, nor can I take anything away. My good deeds do not increase his kingdom, and my sins do not diminish it. I cannot make him wealthier or poorer than he already is. I cannot praise him more than he is already praised. I cannot do anything for him. The fact remains clear: I can offer Allah absolutely nothing. Yet, he constantly acts for me, often without my even realizing his presence, and in many cases, without my acknowledging it.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Ar-Rahman: 29</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كُلَّ يَوْمٍ هُوَ فِى شَأْنٍۢ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Every moment he is engaged in work to keep this universe and everything within it functional. SubhanAllah!</p>
<p>The womb reminds me that all the needs of the child are met by its mother. The child is completely surrounded and enveloped by its mother''s love and care. Its food, its drinks, and even the disposal of its waste are all managed by the mother''s body. Everything the child needs is fully provided.</p>
<ul>
<li>Rent is paid.</li>
<li>Housing and lodging are free.</li>
<li>It does not pay for utilities.</li>
<li>All meals are included.</li>
</ul>
<p>The baby is completely wrapped in its mother''s love and care.</p>
<p>Similarly, I am completely wrapped in the unconditional love, care, concern, and mercy of Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. He provides my sustenance and gives me the luxuries of a good life. He makes the outside world functional for me, and He keeps the inside world functional. My heart beats, my organs work, and my breath comes in and out. I can walk. I can speak. He makes all this happen for me. He gave me parents, teachers, knowledge, and a family. He surrounded me with all the comforts of my life.</p>
<p>I often complain about paying bills, mortgage, and utility expenses. Yet, I consider: how much do I pay for my sight? How much do I pay Allah for my ability to hear, to speak, or to breathe? Setting aside all else, how much do I pay for each breath I take? How much would I pay for my breath if I could not breathe and Allah asked me to pay for the next one?</p>
<p>I consider the price I would pay for my next breath. I reflect on the worth of my hands. My life is immensely blessed with His uncountable bounties. I am blessed with the riches and provisions He created for us, all of which I take completely for granted. Yet, He accepts this, and He continues to serve me.</p>
<p>I have discussed the benefits of the child. I now turn to the mother''s motivation. The baby does not pay the mother; it gives nothing back to her. What, then, motivates this mother to carry on? Despite all the discomfort this child causes her, what could possibly be her driving force?</p>
<p>The mother''s sole motivation for her baby is an extreme form of unconditional love. This point bears consideration. As the child grows within the mother''s womb, it increasingly presents physical challenges for her. With each passing day, as the embryo increases in size, it becomes more demanding on the mother''s body. Yet, her love for the baby intensifies. Her affection grows constantly, which is remarkable. Then, at birth, the baby''s arrival nearly takes her life. As if nine months of torture were not enough, the baby inflicts the incredibly intense pain of childbirth. I have been in the delivery room twice and have witnessed that pain. Nazneen was in labor for eight hours when Erum was born. I know that many mothers experience far worse, but I have personally never seen anyone endure such profound pain. Yet, when Erum finally emerged, I recall the doctor attempting to hand her to me. She was covered in the substances of birth, and I reacted with an exclamation of disgust: &quot;What is this?! Can you please clean this thing first?&quot;</p>
<p>I remember when she brought Erum to Nazneen; with incredible love, she touched her cheeks and said, &quot;Hi baby!&quot; Then she passed out. That was an insane moment in my life. I still do not comprehend it, and I probably never will. I had thought she would hate this baby who caused her so much pain! I had expected her to yell at her, to scream at her, to curse her out, saying, &quot;You almost killed me! I hate you!&quot; But that is not what happened. Instead, the first time they brought Erum to her, she gave her a big hug and kissed her. I had never seen anyone in such profound pain and simultaneously so happy! SubhanAllah!</p>
<p>Anyone else who causes such immense pain, who makes one bleed in that manner, would certainly not receive a hug. We made our mothers bleed, almost to death. Yet, as soon as we emerged from her womb, she showered us with her love. Her love for us actually increased; she fell more deeply in love with us than ever before, mere seconds after we had nearly killed her.</p>
<p>What kind of relationship is this? It is a completely illogical relationship, one that defies all understanding. It transcends all logic because this dynamic does not occur in any other relationship. No other relationship functions in this way, where one never gives—never, ever gives—and only takes, yet still receives more love in return. One continues to demand more, and she continues to give more.</p>
<p>And a mother bleeds profusely to give life. After childbirth or surgery to bring forth life, she must immediately feed the newborn. Feeding can also be a very painful experience. Anyone else in this world who undergoes surgery or such intense pain says, &quot;I need six weeks off. I need two months off. I need a break. I need a vacation.&quot; Does a mother receive a two-month break after giving birth? No. A broken leg, for instance, grants six months of recovery. Having nearly died in childbirth, she is immediately on duty. From the moment of birth, the mother works full-time.</p>
<p>What does the infant offer in return?</p>
<ul>
<li>She provides milk, love, and care.</li>
<li>She is constantly concerned about it and protects it.</li>
<li>She bathes it, oils it, and changes diapers every few minutes.</li>
</ul>
<p>This continues through countless sleepless nights, month after month, year after year. Does the infant express thanks, show gratitude, or sing praises for the mother? No. Yet the mother continues to love and care for her infant repeatedly. She does. The only reward the infant offers is through:</p>
<ul>
<li>Urinating on her</li>
<li>Defecating on her</li>
<li>Vomiting on her</li>
</ul>
<p>Even then, does the mother cast it aside? No. She finds it endearing: &quot;Aww, so cute,&quot; as she cleans it, &quot;Oh, it''s okay, my little baby.&quot;</p>
<p>&quot;Don''t worry, I will change you. All better now!&quot; I consider this an incredibly beautiful relationship. Yet, at the same time, I also find it an extremely insane relationship.</p>
<p>This is what Allah does for us. His actions on a much larger scale also defy understanding. Allah''s love for us simply defies logic; it is beyond all rational thought. I cannot confine His love to what common sense dictates, for common sense suggests we do not deserve such love from Him. When I honestly judge our actions and consider our ingratitude toward Allah, I find no reason for Him to continue caring for us.</p>
<p>We do not even dedicate two hours a day to remember Him five times. That is all it takes. Yet, we readily spend time on trivial activities:</p>
<ul>
<li>watching a three-hour Indian movie,</li>
<li>watching a five-hour soccer match,</li>
<li>watching an all-day cricket match, rewinding and replaying it.</li>
</ul>
<p>We find time for all these trivialities, but not two hours for our Creator. Not even thirty minutes to perform the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فرائض</span>. We do not even manage that much, simply to express gratitude. We constantly disobey Him despite everything He does for us. We do all that He forbids. Some do not even acknowledge His existence, questioning if Allah exists or caring if He does.</p>
<p>Yet, He continues to give, to feed, to provide, and to forgive us. This is truly incredible. Despite my constant ingratitude, my constant disloyalty, and my constant defiance of His every command, I still wake each morning. I still breathe oxygen.</p>
<p>I am still going about my day, eating and drinking the delicious foods He provides for me. I am still enjoying life, even attending parties. He takes none of that away from me. This does not make sense. It simply does not make sense.</p>
<p>I observe our actions concerning food. How much of it do I see wasted? This food represents His greatest blessings for humankind, the very means of our sustenance. I see how we disrespect this sustenance.</p>
<p>I reflect on the etiquette of eating. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said: &quot;Sit before your food like a slave sits before his master. Cover your head before you eat out of respect. Do not place your plate on the floor; elevate it from the ground to honor it. When you are done eating, find and eat the crumbs and grains that are left behind.&quot; Such is the profound respect and honor I believe we should show for food in our lives.</p>
<p>I ask myself why this is so.</p>
<ul>
<li>Without it, I cannot even imagine the state to which our lives would be reduced.</li>
<li>I consider the immense effort required behind the scenes to bring that food to our plates.</li>
</ul>
<p>I reflect on the time, the effort, the protection, the management, and the planning involved in taking a seed and cultivating it into a plant. Similarly, I consider the process of raising an animal: nurturing it, feeding it, and protecting it until it reaches the proper age. Then it passes into the hands of the one who performs <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذبیحۃ</span> to make it halal for us. After that, it reaches the supermarket and the meat shop for purchase. Finally, the provision of tools allows for its cooking until it reaches our plates. This entire process represents years of Allah''s labor, now presented before us on our plates.</p>
<p>Yet, I wonder if we truly care. Often, we take only a few bites before discarding it. The reasons given are often trivial: it was not warm enough, not fresh enough, or lacked sufficient salt. Sometimes, it is simply attributed to children''s preferences.</p>
<p>I consider the profound sense of futility that arises from months of labor on a project, only for it to be discarded. Such an experience naturally leads to a reluctance to undertake similar efforts again.</p>
<p>Rasul Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> established these etiquettes in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شریعۃ</span> so that they might serve as a constant reminder. Humanity is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">انسان</span>, a forgetful creature in perpetual need of such reminders. The subject of this remembrance is Allah''s mercy, his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span>. Indeed, all of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شریعۃ</span> functions as a reminder of this mercy. When a decent human being is presented with and reminded of another person''s love, care, concern, and mercy, a natural response of praise and gratitude emerges. This holds true for any individual of sound character.</p>
<p>Unfortunately, we are often ungrateful. We continuously disrespect and disregard Allah''s continuous provision, even as he constantly provides for us, giving us food, clothes, and shelter, each better than the last. This is like a mother who keeps feeding her baby regardless of its actions. A baby does not consider the months of intense effort that went into its growth, from a drop of liquid to a fully developed child, and then to an adult. Yet, a mother never stops loving her child. This is a boundless love. There is no logical reason for Allah to love us in this way, but he does. He continues to do so. This is because he has <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span> for us. This defines the relationship he has with us:</p>
<ul>
<li>unconditional love</li>
<li>constant care</li>
<li>genuine concern</li>
<li>constant mercy</li>
</ul>
<p>As a baby grows, the womb expands to accommodate it. Similarly, Allah''s love, his mercy, his forgiveness, and his compassion constantly grow to accommodate us. This is the love our Creator has for us.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِنِّی سَمَّیتُکَ بِاسْمی</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;Oh Womb! I have named you from my names. I have named you <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحَم</span> to remind you that I am <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحیم</span>. I have made you a symbol of my love, so that every human being recognizes my unconditional love for them — through you and by your example.&quot;</p></blockquote>
<p>Just as we can never fully understand a mother''s love for her child, we can never fully grasp or appreciate Allah''s love and care for his creation. Yet, to offer some understanding of this relationship, he gave us mothers. When one seeks to taste a single drop of that endless ocean of love Allah holds for his creation, the image of a mother comes to mind.</p>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'womb - the source of mercy';
INSERT INTO chapter (slug, anchor_key, idx, title, html, word_count) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'manifestations of rehma', 8, 'Manifestations of REHMA', '', 3666);
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>I have explained the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span> of Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, and I have tried to make clear what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span> means and what it represents.</p>
<p>In sum, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span> joins together unconditional love, constant care, genuine concern, unlimited mercy, and compassion. The two common derivatives of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span> are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحیم</span>.</p>
<p>The attribute <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span> points to the intensity of his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span>: its urgency, its promptness, and also its interim or temporary quality. The attribute <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحیم</span> points to the long-term and permanent qualities of his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span>.</p>
<p>I then turned to the root of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span> itself. It comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحم</span>, which literally means the womb of a mother. The womb gives magnificent examples that depict and demonstrate the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span> of Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>.</p>
<p>I know how the womb takes a shapeless drop of liquid and, by its <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span>, transforms it into a living child. I drew the analogy that the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span> of Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> shapes and transforms our spiritual existence.</p>
<p>His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span> takes human beings in their raw and ignorant state, with no real goal and no true purpose beyond the limits of this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دنیا</span>. It then transforms them into extraordinary personalities, people whose names I do not even mention without adding <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَۃُ اللہِ عَلَیْہِ</span> after them.</p>
<p>This is why Rasul Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَۃً لِی الْعَالَمِینَ</span>. He is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span> for this world because he took on the role of a mother''s womb, shaping and nurturing the spiritual existence of humanity. That was not only for the people of his own time, but for all humanity until the end of time.</p>
<p>I also looked at how a mother suffers in order to give comfort to her child. She protects it, nurtures it, feeds it, and bears hardship, pain, and suffering so that a shapeless mass becomes a living, breathing child. From this I drew the parallel that Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, through his various intermediaries, his Imams, his prophets, and his messengers, shapes our spiritual existence. Just as a pregnant mother suffers, history shows that these people endured many hardships in order to deliver the message of Allah to their nations.</p>
<h3 id="forgiveness">Forgiveness</h3>
<p>I also find one of the most wondrous qualities of the womb, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحم</span>, in the way it expands to meet the needs of the fetus. It gives a beautiful reminder that Allah''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span> expands as well: not only to meet the needs of his creation, but also to forgive their shortcomings, sins, and transgressions.</p>
<p>His vast forgiveness is one of the manifestations of his infinite compassion toward his creation. Allah knows that we are people of very weak character. We are often enslaved by worldly desires. Yet he also knows that we are trying, in whatever ways we can, to move toward him: centimeter by centimeter, inch by inch. So he makes up for that weakness and strengthens what is lacking in us through his forgiveness.</p>
<p>There is a verse in the Quran in which Allah says:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Az-Zumar: 53</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُلْ يَعِبَادِىَ ٱلَّذِينَ أَسْرَفُوا۟ عَلَىٰٓ أَنفُسِهِمْ لَا تَقْنَطُوا۟ مِن رَّحْمَةِ ٱللَّهِ ۚ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ يَغْفِرُ ٱلذُّنُوبَ جَمِيعًا ۚ إِنَّهُۥ هُوَ ٱلْغَفُورُ ٱلرَّحِيمُ</span></p><p class="tr">Say, &quot;O My servants who have transgressed against themselves [by sinning], do not despair of the mercy of Allah . Indeed, Allah forgives all sins. Indeed, it is He who is the Forgiving, the Merciful.&quot;</p></blockquote>
<p>He says:</p>
<p>O My slaves who have transgressed against themselves through their sins. Which slaves does He mean? The disbelievers? The polytheists? The idol worshipers? No. He means us: Muslims, believers. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَعِبَادِىَ</span>. In Arabic, this form of address carries deep nearness, deep love, and deep intimacy. A casual call would have been <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">یَا أَیُّھَا الْعِبَادُ</span>, meaning “O slaves,” without that intimacy or love. But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَعِبَادِىَ</span> expresses intense nearness: O <strong>My beloved</strong> slaves, O <strong>My revered</strong> slaves, O <strong>My cherished</strong> slave. The difference is clear. And what kind of slaves are they? Faithful slaves? Trustworthy slaves? Obedient slaves? No.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Az-Zumar: 53</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلَّذِينَ أَسْرَفُوا۟ عَلَىٰٓ أَنفُسِهِمْ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>I mean the ones who have crossed every limit and boundary in wronging themselves. They have severely harmed their own souls by living in sin, hopelessness, and disobedience to Allah; by choosing a way of life that damages and obstructs their spiritual growth.</p>
<p>These are the slaves He is addressing. Yet the scholars point out something beautiful in this verse: before this description comes the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَعِبَادِىَ</span>. Allah still calls them <strong>My</strong> slaves. No matter who they are, and no matter what they have done, they still belong to Him as His slaves.</p>
<p>There is no abandonment from Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. Just as the womb does not abandon its fetus if it is deformed, Allah constantly preserves the relationship of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span> with us, even if we become the worst of the worst. We may be the most sinful people on the face of the earth, constantly walking on very thin ice, yet Allah still keeps that relationship and connection alive through the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَعِبَادِىَ</span>: My slaves. SubhanAllah. AllahuAkbar.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Az-Zumar: 53</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلَّذِينَ أَسْرَفُوا۟ عَلَىٰٓ أَنفُسِهِمْ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>The ones who have done severe harm to their souls and to themselves.</p>
<p>I now come to Allah''s advice to them:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Az-Zumar: 53</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا تَقْنَطُوا۟ مِن رَّحْمَةِ ٱللَّهِ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Never lose hope in the mercy of Allah. Never, not even by the smallest measure. I will not enter the technical details here, but the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لا</span> indicates absolute and unconditional force, without the slightest doubt. The same force appears in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا اِلَہَ اِلَّا اللَّہُ</span>, which also begins with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لا</span>, because that statement carries absolute finality: there is no god at all except Allah.</p>
<p>So Allah is saying, with complete certainty and under no circumstance, “I am making it <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حرام</span>; I am making it impermissible. I am forbidding you to lose hope in my mercy. Ever.”</p>
<p>Why? Allah never tells us something without also giving us a reason to believe it. So why should I not lose hope in the mercy of Allah?</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Az-Zumar: 53</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ يَغْفِرُ ٱلذُّنُوبَ جَمِيعًا</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Because Allah is able to forgive every sin and every transgression. How can that be? I may have spent an entire lifetime in His disobedience. How can He forgive so many sins?</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Yusuf: 98</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّهُۥ هُوَ ٱلْغَفُورُ ٱلرَّحِيمُ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Because, indeed, without the slightest doubt, He and only He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الغفور</span>: the One who forgives constantly.</p>
<p>The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غفور</span> follows the same linguistic pattern as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحیم</span>. He is not merely forgiving; He is constantly forgiving and constantly merciful. Nothing lies beyond His power. Nothing is outside His reach. Allah can forgive all sins, and He will forgive them. This word, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غفور</span>, is remarkable because it reaches the very essence of His forgiveness.</p>
<h3 id="3-words-for-forgiveness">3 words for forgiveness</h3>
<p>In the Quran, when Allah speaks of His ability to forgive, He uses three different attributes for Himself. All three come from the same root: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفَرَ</span>.</p>
<p>The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفَرَ</span> literally means to cover something. The Arabic word for helmet is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِغفَر</span>. When a helmet is worn, the head is no longer visible. In that sense, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مغفرۃ</span> means to forgive: to cover sins so completely that they are hidden from sight, as though they never existed.</p>
<p>GHAFARA</p>
<p>So Allah calls Himself <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَافِرِ الذَّنْبِ</span> in the Quran.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَنزِيلُ ٱلْكِتَبِ مِنَ ٱللَّهِ ٱلْعَزِيزِ ٱلْعَلِيمِ</span> ‎<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">۲</span>‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَافِرِ ٱلذَّنۢبِ وَقَابِلِ ٱلتَّوْبِ شَدِيدِ ٱلْعِقَابِ ذِى ٱلطَّوْلِۖ لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا هُوَۖ إِلَيْهِ ٱلْمَصِيرُ</span></p><p class="tr">( 2 ) The revelation of the Book is from Allah the All-Mighty, the All-Knower. ( 3 ) The forgiver of sin, acceptor of repentance, severe in punishment, owner of abundance. There is no deity except Him; to Him is the destination.</p></blockquote>
<p>In language, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غافر</span> means the One who forgives at once. He does not hesitate. He does not pause to think before forgiving. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غافر</span>.</p>
<p>Then He calls Himself <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غفَّار</span>.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Nuh: 10</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَقُلْتُ ٱسْتَغْفِرُوا۟ رَبَّكُمْ إِنَّهُۥ كَانَ غَفَّارًۭا</span></p><p class="tr">And said, Ask forgiveness of your Lord. Indeed, He is ever a Perpetual Forgiver.</p></blockquote>
<p>I understand <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غفَّار</span> as the One who forgives abundantly. His forgiveness has no limit. He continues to forgive no matter how much sin exists.</p>
<p>Human beings have boundaries. A person may say, “I can forgive anything except betrayal,” or, “I cannot forgive lies,” or, “I cannot forgive rudeness.” Everyone carries some idea of what feels unforgivable. But Allah is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غفَّار</span>: He has no limit in what He can forgive. He can forgive everything when repentance is true.</p>
<p>The third variation is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غفور</span>. As I have said, it follows the same phonetic pattern as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحیم</span>, and it means constantly or permanently forgiving. This is the form used in the verse under discussion:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Yusuf: 98</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّهُۥ هُوَ ٱلْغَفُورُ ٱلرَّحِيمُ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>And indeed, He is constantly forgiving and constantly merciful.</p>
<p>Unflinching loyalty, infinite hope, a permanent relationship that never ends, and forgiveness that is intense, immediate, and abundant are among the essential manifestations of His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span>.</p>
<h3 id="rewards">Rewards</h3>
<p>Another manifestation of the expansion of His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span>, like the expansion of the womb, appears in His infinite rewards for small acts. On certain holy nights, Allah offers an astonishing amount of reward. (Thum Vasheq - 20 Hajj). I want to present one perspective on why Allah offers such rewards, so that the intention behind them becomes clearer.</p>
<p>I see the holy month of Ramadan drawing near. In this month, it is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سنۃ</span> of Rasul Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> to perform <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اعتکاف</span>. This means withdrawing into the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مسجد</span> for a set time, devoted only to the remembrance of Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> in silence. In India and Pakistan, people spend at least three days and nights in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اعتکاف</span>. They pray, recite Quran, do <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تسبیح</span>, reflect on their lives, and more. Allah loves this. Rasul Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> described the reward of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اعتکاف</span> in a HADEES. He said:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنِ اعْتَكَفَ يَوْماً ابْتِغَاءَ وَجْهِ اللهِ بَاعَدَ اللهُ بَيْنَهُ وَبَيْنَ النَّارِ ثَلَاثَةَ خَنَادِقَ كُلُّ خَنْدَقٍ كَمَا بَيْنَ الْخَافِقَيْنِ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Whoever remains in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اعتکاف</span> for one day, only one day, Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> distances him from the Fire by three trenches. Each trench stretches as far as the distance between East and West. SubhanAllah. This is the reward for spending a single day in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مسجد</span>, in the state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذکر</span>. It shows how beloved this act of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اعتکاف</span> is to Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>.</p>
<p>I recall that one day in the month of Ramadan, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِبْنُ عَبَّاسٍ رَضِیَ اللَّہُ تَعَالَیٰ عَنْہُ</span>, the cousin of the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>, entered a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مسجد</span> intending to spend the day in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اعتکاف</span>, seeking the immense reward Allah has promised.</p>
<p>Soon after he began his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اعتکاف</span>, he saw a Muslim brother in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مسجد</span> who looked deeply sad. He went up to him and asked what had made him so sorrowful.</p>
<p>The man says, “O cousin of the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>, I owe a debt to someone, and I need to pay it. But I cannot pay it because I have no money. The weight of that debt, that obligation, is crushing my soul.”</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ابن عباس</span> says to his man: “Would you like me to go to your lender and speak to him on your behalf? Perhaps I can persuade him to forgive your debt, reduce it, or give you more time. Would you like me to do that?”</p>
<p>The man replies, “Of course, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">یَا ابْنَ عَبَّاسٍ</span>, I would be delighted if you did that.”</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ابن عباس</span> immediately stood up, walked out of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مسجد</span>, and began putting on his slippers.</p>
<p>Suddenly, the man remembered: “Oh my God, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ابن عباس</span> was here for his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اعتکاف</span>. Now he is breaking his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اعتکاف</span> for me. What have I done? He came to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مسجد</span> for an act of worship, and I became the reason he had to break his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اعتکاف</span>. That is the last thing I wanted.”</p>
<p>He rushes toward him and says, “<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">یَا ابْنَ عَبَّاسٍ</span>, do you not realize what you have done? Do you not remember why you were here? You have broken your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اعتکاف</span>!”</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ابن عباس</span> looks at the man, and the HADEES says that tears begin to roll down his face. He says: “I remember my Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> telling me that if one of you strives to fulfill the need of his Muslim brother, Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> rewards him with the blessing of ten years of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اعتکاف</span>.”</p>
<p>SubhanAllah. Not one day. Not one month. Not one year. Ten years of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اعتکاف</span>.</p>
<p>The reward for a single day of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اعتکاف</span> is distance from the Hellfire by three trenches, and each trench stretches the distance between east and west. That is the reward for one day of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اعتکاف</span>. Even one day is difficult enough; a full week of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اعتکاف</span> is beyond most people. When did I last complete a week of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اعتکاف</span>? Yet striving to meet the need of a Muslim brother brings the reward of ten years of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اعتکاف</span>. SubhanAllah.</p>
<p>After hearing this HADEES, my first thought is natural: I must not miss any chance to help anyone. This is a great opening to gain nearness to Allah. That is true. At times, the quickest path to Allah''s blessings runs through His creation: through other people, and through doing good to them.</p>
<p>That is why, when people hear this HADEES, they often rush to help others. They keep their ears open for any chance to serve someone. That is a beautiful response.</p>
<p>But I want to point to something higher and deeper in this HADEES. I want to turn the focus toward the intention behind the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> of Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span>. This HADEES is astonishing, and an example will show why.</p>
<p>Suppose I enter a room and see someone there who is very sad. Suppose it is my niece Mariam. I discover that, for some reason, Mariam looks deeply unhappy that day. So I say, “I will give ten million dollars to anyone who makes her smile.”</p>
<p>What will happen? Ten million dollars. Everyone in the room will try to make her smile. They will gather around her and offer their best jokes, all hoping to make her smile.</p>
<p>I know most people will look at that gesture and say, “Oh, you are so rich. You must have so much money that you are willing to give ten million dollars just to make her smile.” But a few will reach the heart of the gesture and say, “You must love her so much. You must love her so much that one smile from her is worth ten million dollars to you. She must mean the world to you.”</p>
<p>That is the difference. The reality is that I love her so much that I am willing to pay ten million dollars to the person who makes her smile.</p>
<p>So when Allah says that He will give the reward of ten years of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اعتکاف</span> to the one who fulfills Asif''s need, the common reading of that HADEES becomes a motivation to act for that reward. Yet most people miss what it reveals: how much Allah loves me. Ya Allah, You love me so much that You would give that much reward to anyone who fulfills my need and removes my constriction for me. SubhanAllah.</p>
<p>So this HADEES can also be heard another way: “O one who feels low. O one who feels depressed. O one who feels burdened and constricted by troubles. It is you I am looking at. It is you for whom I am opening doors, because I want to see you happy. For your happiness, I will reward anyone with this amount of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ثواب</span> if they strive to bring you ease.”</p>
<p>This is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span> He has for us. AllahuAkbar.</p>
<p>My perspective changes completely when I see another person in need. I begin to see that this is a person Allah loves. He loves him so much that He is willing to reward me with immense generosity and immense blessings for helping him. Then I ask myself what place I may gain in His sight if I bring ease to someone He loves.</p>
<p>When someone does something deeply kind for a person I love, that person becomes dearer to me. He comes closer to my heart. In the same way, once I understand this view, it is nearness to Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> that moves me to help another person, more than the reward itself.</p>
<p>I have to appreciate why such immense rewards are attached to such simple acts. They come from the love He has for us. Because of that love, He showers His blessings upon even the smallest acts of kindness. He may grant the reward of ten or twenty Hajj for a few hours of worship. I may act for the reward, or I may act out of gratitude for that divine love.</p>
<h3 id="responding-in-afflictions">Responding in afflictions</h3>
<p>I will give another example. There is a HADEES in which Rasul Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> tells us that when I visit a sick person, I should ask him to pray for me and for my forgiveness.</p>
<p>At first, that sounds reversed. I would expect myself to be the one praying for the sick person, asking Allah to grant him health. He appears to be the one in need of my prayers. Yet the HADEES says that I should ask him to pray for me and for my forgiveness.</p>
<p>What does that mean? I can make it clearer with an example.</p>
<p>My daughter may fall very ill, God forbid. May Allah protect our children from trials. I give this only as an example to make the point clearer. She becomes very sick and is admitted to the emergency room. The doctors come to me and say that she is in severe pain and cannot breathe properly.</p>
<p>As a father, I cannot even imagine the state I would be in if that ever happened. Nothing is more painful for a parent than seeing his own child suffer, whether the suffering is physical, mental, or spiritual. Then the doctor tells me that she cannot breathe and must be put on a ventilator.</p>
<p>At that moment, there would be no other place in the world where I would want to be. I would not leave my daughter''s side even for a second. I would sit beside her because I love her so much. I would remain at her bedside, constantly praying for her.</p>
<p>Then Allah answers my prayers. She wakes up. She says, “Babu! I want ice cream.”</p>
<p>I would be ready at once. “You want ice cream? Of course. What flavor do you want?” If I could not find it there, I would drive wherever I had to drive to get that ice cream for her. That is what I would do.</p>
<p>At that point, I would feel intensely close to her. “What else do you want, sweetheart? Is there anything else I can do for you? Is there anything else I can get for you?”</p>
<p>It so happened that, just before she fell ill, we had a terrible fight. She had disobeyed me and spoken to me very rudely. I was still angry with her when all of this happened.</p>
<p>Then she wakes up and remembers what she did. She says, “Babu, I''m sorry for what I did and said. Please forgive me!”</p>
<p>I know what I would say: “Forgive you? Sweetheart, there''s nothing to forgive. I''m not upset with you at all anymore. Don''t even think about it.”</p>
<p>That is how parents are with their children. There is nothing strange in it.</p>
<p>If this is how deeply I love my child, then Allah''s love for his creation is beyond what I can imagine. According to a HADEES, Allah holds the love of seventy mothers for every single one of his creation. In such an affliction, I felt intensely close to my daughter. When a creation of Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> is going through trials and afflictions, Allah is nearer to him than I can grasp.</p>
<p>I was ready to respond to anything my daughter asked for in that moment, though my ability is extremely limited. But when a human being, a creation of Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, is in pain and asks for something, whether for himself or for someone else, Allah does not refuse him in that moment.</p>
<p>When I am afflicted, Allah is closest to me in that very moment, closer than any human being could ever be. When I turn to Allah for forgiveness with true repentance, there is nothing He would not forgive, whatever sin I may have committed and however terribly I may have behaved. Allah would forgive instantly.</p>
<p>I do not hesitate for a second to forgive my child. The mercy of Allah is far greater. He is the One who makes Himself known through love. He holds the love of seventy mothers for His creation, so He does not refuse forgiveness.</p>
<p>If I were angry with my son, and my daughter woke up and said to me, “Babu! Please forgive my brother,” would I refuse? “Oh my God, you are in such a difficult state. You are in so much pain. You are suffering so much right now, and even in this state you are concerned about your brother? You want me to forgive him? Your request will surely be granted. Of course I forgive him. I will give you anything you want right now. All you have to do is ask.” SubhanAllah</p>
<p>I can now appreciate the beauty of that HADEES. In the very middle of my affliction, I raise my hands and ask Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> to fulfill another person''s need. I may ask for my own forgiveness, or for the forgiveness of someone else. At that moment, there is no way Allah turns down my request, because my trial has brought me extremely close to Him. He is right by my bedside, holding my hand. That is how His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span> manifests.</p>
<p>This is why trials become such a perfect opening for me to ask Allah for what I need. A trial can become one of the best experiences a person ever has: an experience of awareness, nearness, and closeness to the love of Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>.</p>
<p>If Allah is so loving, why does He afflict people in the first place?</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Ash-Shura: 30</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَآ أَصَبَكُم مِّن مُّصِيبَةٍۢ فَبِمَا كَسَبَتْ أَيْدِيكُمْ وَيَعْفُوا۟ عَن كَثِيرٍۢ</span></p></blockquote>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'manifestations of rehma';
INSERT INTO chapter (slug, anchor_key, idx, title, html, word_count) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'linguistic meaning of rabb', 9, 'Linguistic Meaning Of Rabb', '', 5065);
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>I begin, InshaAllah, with the linguistic meaning of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>. Before that meaning, there is a striking sign from the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جمال</span>: the beauty of the Quran. It belongs to the symmetrical beauty of the Quran, especially the symmetry in its use of the words Allah and RAB.</p>
<h3 id="symmetry-of-allah-and-rab">Symmetry of Allah and RAB</h3>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">۱</span>‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِرَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> ‎<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">۲</span>‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">۳</span>‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span> ‎<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">۴</span>‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ</span> ‎<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">۵</span>‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ</span> ‎<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">۶</span>‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَطَ ٱلَّذِينَ أَنْعَمْتَ عَلَيْهِمْ غَيْرِ ٱلْمَغْضُوبِ عَلَيْهِمْ وَلَا ٱلضَّآلِّينَ</span></p><p class="tr">( 1 ) In the Name of Allah, the Most Beneficent, the Most Merciful.</p></blockquote>
<p>( 2 ) All praises and thanks belong to Allah, the Lord of the <em>Alamin</em>. ( 3 ) The Most Beneficent, the Most Merciful. ( 4 ) The Only Owner of the Day of Recompense. ( 5 ) You we worship, and You we ask for help. ( 6 ) Guide us to the Straight Way. ( 7 ) The Way of those on whom You have bestowed Your Grace, not of those who earned Your Anger, nor of those who went astray.</p>
<p>In the first Surah of the Quran, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَۃُ الْفَاتِحَۃِ</span>, I find that Allah is mentioned twice, and that RAB is mentioned once after those two mentions. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فاتحۃ</span> begins with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللَّہِ الرَّحْمَنِ الرَّحِیمِ</span>. Allah is therefore mentioned in the first verse. Then comes <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْحَمْدُ لِلَّہِ</span>, another mention of Allah, followed by <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب العلمین</span>.</p>
<p>So the name Allah appears twice before the word RAB appears once. After that, in the remaining verses, neither Allah nor RAB is mentioned again. This is what stands at the beginning of the Quran.</p>
<p>At the end of the Quran, in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَۃُ النَّاسِ</span>, the pattern appears from the other direction when I work backward.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">An-Nas: 1</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُلْ أَعُوذُ بِرَبِّ ٱلنَّاسِ</span></p><p class="tr">Say: &quot;I seek refuge with the Lord of mankind,</p></blockquote>
<p>In <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَۃُ النَّاسِ</span>, the word mentioned is not Allah but RAB: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُلْ أَعُوذُ بِرَبِّ ٱلنَّاسِ</span>.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Falaq: 1</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُلْ أَعُوذُ بِرَبِّ ٱلْفَلَقِ</span></p><p class="tr">Say: &quot;I seek refuge with the Lord of the daybreak,</p></blockquote>
<p>When I go to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سورۃ</span> before it, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَۃُ الْفَلَقِ</span>, the word mentioned is not Allah but RAB: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُلْ أَعُوذُ بِرَبِّ ٱلْفَلَقِ</span>. RAB appears again.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Ikhlas: 1</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُلْ هُوَ ٱللَّهُ أَحَدٌ</span></p><p class="tr">Say :&quot;He is Allah, the unique!</p></blockquote>
<p>When I go to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سورۃ</span> before that, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَۃُ الْاِخْلَاصِ</span>, the word mentioned is Allah, not RAB: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُلْ ھُوَ اللَّہُ اَحَدٌ</span>. SubhanAllah.</p>
<p>In the beginning, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَۃُ الْفَاتِحَۃِ</span>, Allah is mentioned twice, and then RAB is mentioned. From the end, the pattern reverses: RAB is mentioned twice, and then Allah is mentioned. SubhanAllah.</p>
<p>The last two Surahs use the word RAB, with no mention of the name Allah. In <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَۃُ اِخْلَاصِ</span>, only the name Allah appears, with no mention of RAB. The result is a perfect symmetry: one Surah at the beginning, and several Surahs at the end.</p>
<h3 id="meaning-of-rab">Meaning of RAB</h3>
<p>The root of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> is actually <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبَبٌ</span>. In Arabic, the two <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">با</span> are combined. A <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شدۃ</span> is then placed over the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">با</span>, producing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ربّ</span>.</p>
<p>In most English translations, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> is rendered as “lord.” But the word “lord” does not carry the meaning clearly. For many English readers, it may only call to mind a title, or even a phrase such as “the Lord of the Rings.” That is why Arab linguists do not regard “lord” as a strong translation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>. It fails to reveal the true meaning and power of the word.</p>
<p>RABB</p>
<p>I approach the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> first through its language. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> is a composite word, like <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>, which has already been examined. I have shown that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> is not used only for Allah. It can apply to many other realities in human life.</p>
<p>In the same way, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> is not used only for Allah. The word carries several meanings in the language. It is used for Allah, but it may also be used for human beings and for other created relations, such as parents, teachers, and guardians. The first point, then, is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> is not specific to Allah. In Classical Arabic, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> can be used for Allah, for human beings, and for other things.</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/78/b8244e3f-11ba-4e9b-a182-4022d0ce982f.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>The classical definition of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> is:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَبُّ ھُوَ المَالِکُ وَ السَّیِّد وَ المُرَبِّی وَ المُرْشَدُ وَ المُنعِمُ وَ القَیِّمُ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>I understand a true <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> as one who gathers six meanings. I may also say that Allah, as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>, is all six at once: He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مالک</span>, He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سید</span>, He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مربی</span>, He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مرشد</span>, He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">منعم</span>, and He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قیم</span>. The word RAB therefore does not carry one meaning only. It joins all six meanings together.</p>
<h3 id="al-malik---owner">al-MALIK - Owner</h3>
<p>MALIK</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/78/b16edd18-ca65-470d-bddc-f05a0725a60e.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>I begin with the first meaning: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مالک</span>, owner. Whoever owns something, or has authority of ownership over someone, is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>. The thing owned is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِلک</span>. This is not the English word “milk,” the milk produced by cows. In Arabic, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِلک</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ملکیۃ</span> means property, and the owner of that property is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مالک</span>.</p>
<p>I also find this meaning in ordinary life. Human beings own phones, iPads, computers, cars, and houses. In that limited sense, such ownership makes them <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مالک</span>. Since “owner” is one of the meanings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>, that ownership also makes a human being a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> in that sense.</p>
<p>I see this limited kind of ownership in teachers over their students, and in parents over their young children. Children may protest, “It is my life, Mom. You cannot tell me what to do. You do not own me.” Those feelings are common today, and, to be fair, many of us felt the same way while growing up. Still, parents do have a form of ownership over their children until those children become adults.</p>
<p>But this is not ultimate or absolute ownership. Parents give birth to their children, but they do not create them. Allah created the child, and Allah created the parent. Therefore, ultimate and absolute ownership belongs only to Allah. So, although parents and teachers may be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> in one sense of the word, Allah is the absolute owner and the absolute <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> of His creation. He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مالک</span>. And if He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مالک</span>, then I am His property. I am His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِلکِیَّۃ</span>.</p>
<h3 id="sayyed---authority">SAYYED - AUTHORITY</h3>
<p>SAYYED</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/78/0b293ce1-5d98-44aa-a320-7be45f893cae.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>The second meaning of the word Rabb can be seen in the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سید</span>, which complements the earlier meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مالک</span>. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مالک</span> means owner, while <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سید</span> means someone who has authority. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سید</span> is the one in charge. The difference is simple: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مالک</span> is someone who has ownership, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سید</span> is someone who has authority.</p>
<p>I may own something without having complete authority over it. That is possible. I may own a car, for example, but I still have to register it. I still have to get it inspected every year. I still have to drive within the speed limit. I still have to drive it on the road. I cannot simply drive on the sidewalk because I feel like it, unless I am in Pakistan. So, even though I own my car, I do not necessarily have complete authority over it.</p>
<p>The reverse is also true. I may have complete authority over something without having complete ownership of it. Take the computer in my office. I can do whatever I want with the software installed on it. I can install new programs. I can uninstall programs I do not like. Nobody will stop me or question me about that. But I do not own the computer. My company owns it.</p>
<p>So I may own something without having complete authority over it. And I may have complete authority over something without necessarily owning it.</p>
<p>Allah, as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>, has absolute ownership, and He has absolute authority. He has both at once. He may do whatever He wills with His property, whenever He wills. If He wills to burn it, He may burn it. If He wills to tear it apart, He may tear it apart. If He wills to drown it, toss it away, or discard it, He may do so by His will.</p>
<p>No one has the right to question Him about it, because He has complete ownership and complete authority over us and over everything else in the universe. Even complaint and grief do not bind Him. He is not obliged to heed them. They make no difference to Him.</p>
<p>When parents ground their children, the matter becomes easier to see. If I take my daughter''s phone away, she may cry and complain as much as she wants, but she is not getting it back. In that setting, I am her <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مالک</span>, and she and her phone are my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ملکیۃ</span>.</p>
<p>If our crying and grumbling do not move our parents, though their capacity as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> is limited, then they certainly do not compel the One who is the absolute and ultimate <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>. We are creation. He is our Creator.</p>
<p>I constantly seem to forget that he is not the one on trial. We are. We have reversed the matter completely.</p>
<p>Today, mankind has placed Allah in the witness stand and taken the judge''s seat. We question his every act without shame. People ask why, if he is so powerful, he does not remove evil from the world. They ask how, if he is so loving and merciful, he could allow pain to touch them. They say that they prayed, yet the job or promotion did not come. They insist that they are good people, that they paid their taxes honestly, that they paid their <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زکواۃ</span>, and that they brush their teeth twice every day. Then they ask why he did this to them.</p>
<p>This is the reversal. The reality is that he is the judge. He is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مالک</span>. He is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سید</span>. We are his property and his subjects. He has every right to question us. We have no right at all to question him.</p>
<p>For this reason, I find it astonishing that Allah still allowed us to understand something of the wisdom in his design through the Quran and through his messengers. I see this as an immense act of mercy and generosity from him, because he is not obligated to do anything for us. He is not there to serve us. Yet he does so anyway, because that is what a true <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> does.</p>
<h3 id="murabbee---the-one-who-nourishes">MURABBEE - The One Who Nourishes</h3>
<p>I come to the third meaning of the word: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرَبَّی</span>. This is a powerful word. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرَبَّی</span> means one who ensures growth: someone who cares for a thing, nurtures it, and enables it to develop.</p>
<p>In other words, a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرَبَّی</span> is responsible for the growth and development of whatever has been placed under his care. Ownership and authority alone do not guarantee this care. I may own something and have control over it, yet neglect it. A neglected backyard makes the point plainly. People own cars but never service them. We possess our bodies, yet we often mistreat them. Ownership is present, and a measure of authority is present, but care is missing.</p>
<p>So ownership and authority over something do not necessarily mean that it will be properly looked after. A <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> is the one who owns, has complete authority over what he owns, and also takes complete care of it. He ensures its proper growth. He nurtures it. This act of caring for what belongs to him and nurturing it is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تربیۃ</span>. I find this phenomenon throughout the world.</p>
<p>TARBIYAH</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/78/c49b7488-8dd5-4b26-bd91-725c35cffb79.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>The poet says:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِن دُرَّۃٍ بَیضَآءَ صَافِیَۃً</span> || <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِمَّا تَرَبَّبَ حَائِرَ البَحرٍ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>I see the depth of the ocean nurturing the clear white pearl. The poet calls the ocean a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> because it helps develop the pearl inside its shell. A tree is also called a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> because it nurtures the fruit it bears.</p>
<p>So I find many forms of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تربیۃ</span> throughout the world. The concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تربیۃ</span> is tied to setting limits and drawing boundaries. That is what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تربیۃ</span> means.</p>
<p>The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تربیۃ</span> of a plant means caring for it through ordered limits:</p>
<ul>
<li>watering it at the right time</li>
<li>making sure it receives enough sunlight</li>
<li>protecting it from too much sunlight</li>
<li>protecting it from insects and bugs</li>
</ul>
<p>In other words, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تربیۃ</span> is a set of dos and do nots that continues until the plant matures into a tree and begins to bear fruit. If the plant is simply left to grow on its own, that is not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تربیۃ</span>, because no boundaries have been set.</p>
<p>In the same way, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تربیۃ</span> of children means caring for their physical needs, their diet, and their clothing. It also means caring for their minds by educating them and protecting them. In essence, it is the close watching of their growth from birth until they mature into healthy, sound, decent human beings: physically, psychologically, and socially. This is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَرْبِیَۃُ الْاَوْلَادِ</span>.</p>
<p>I do not understand <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تربیۃ</span> as a quick or easy process. It is long, demanding, and full of labor. In fact, it is among the hardest forms of work: never-ending, thankless, and full-time.</p>
<p>This is why, when I recite <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">التحیات</span> in every prayer, I say: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَللّٰھُمَّ مَغْفَرٌلِی</span> - Oh Allah forgive me <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">و لوالدی</span> and my parents. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَالرَّحْمُھُمَا کَمَا رَبَّیَانِی صَغِیرَۃً</span> and have mercy on them just as they had mercy on me during the period of my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تربیۃ</span> when I was young.</p>
<p>In this statement, I find the primary ingredient of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ربوبیۃ</span>: mercy (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span>). Mercy is the main component of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ربوبیۃ</span>. Without mercy, there can be no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ربوبیۃ</span>. Mercy is made of love, care, and concern. That is what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ربوبیۃ</span> contains.</p>
<p>The image of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ربوبیۃ</span> appears in the care some people give to plants. They spray each leaf with water, then clean it gently with a soft cloth. That kind of love, care, and gentleness shows what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ربوبیۃ</span> is.</p>
<p>It is not harshness. It is not beating. It is not psychological, emotional, or physical abuse. It is not forcing submission through guilt. It is love, patience, and kindness. That is the Islamic way of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ربوبیۃ</span>. Contrary to what appears in movies and in society today, the Islamic way of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ربوبیۃ</span> has always been <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span>. The proof is in the life of Rasul Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>.</p>
<p>Our prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> took the most vicious people, the most barbaric people, and transformed them without a single slap, taunt, sarcastic remark, or insult. He made them <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَضِیَ اللَّہُ عَنْہُ</span>, the greatest title a human being can receive: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَضِیَ اللَّہُ عَنْہُ</span>.</p>
<p>I do not mention the names of these <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صحابۃ</span> without saying <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَضِیَ اللَّہُ عَنْہُ</span>. Our prophet did that. How? Through love, gentleness, kindness, forgiveness, and patience; through his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أخلاق</span>. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ربوبیۃ</span>.</p>
<p>What did he ask of us in return? After giving twenty-three years of his life to our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تربیۃ</span>, in his last sermon, after <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِجَّۃُ الْوَدَاعِ</span>, he said:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">یا أُمَّتَی لا تُسَوِّدَ وَجْھِی یوم القیامۃ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>My people! Do not embarrass me before Allah on the Day of Judgment! When the result falls short, exceptions do not decide the matter. If most students in a class fail, the principal questions the teacher: What is happening? Why have so many students failed in this class?</p>
<p>Our Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>, after twenty-three years of service and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تربیۃ</span>, asked only one thing in return: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">یا أُمَّتَی لا تُسَوِّدَ وَجْھِی یوم القیامۃ</span>. Do not embarrass me before Allah on the Day of Judgment. May Allah make us among those who make our Prophet proud.</p>
<h3 id="murshad">MURSHAD</h3>
<p>MURSHAD</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/78/f84f0843-7482-421c-8a2b-9747e6ab9960.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>The fourth meaning of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرشَد</span>. He is the one who sets things in the right direction, sets them on the right path, and puts them to proper use. This is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرشَد</span>.</p>
<p>The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرشَد</span> comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُشد</span>, which in classical Arabic means <strong>to ripen</strong>, the final stage of fruit. For this reason, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُشد</span> also means rationality, reason, or maturity, because these are the final stages of the intellect.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرشَد</span> is the one who takes something in its raw form and works on it until it ripens and matures. Then He puts it to its proper use and sets it in the right direction.</p>
<p>A teacher may train a student in computer science and then also find that student a job, so the training can be applied. In that case, the teacher has acted as a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرشَد</span>. A <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرشَد</span>is not someone who brings a person to maturity and then leaves that person unused. He sets the person on the path. He guides that person forward from the point of maturity. He provides the opportunity to practice what has been learned, so that the trained person and others can benefit from that learning. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرشَد</span>.</p>
<p>I learn from this that Allah, as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>, does not only own us, hold complete authority over us, and nurture us with love and care. He also creates opportunities for us to put our training to use. He trains us by placing people in our path who help us grow and develop. He finds the perfect friend for us, someone who helps us grow and mature. Then He guides us to put that training to use by becoming good friends ourselves to other people. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرشَد</span>.</p>
<p>Even parents and teachers cannot always do this. They may complete the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تربیۃ</span> of their children, but they cannot always put them to proper use. Children have to find their own path. But Allah, as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>, goes further. He sets us on the right path after our training has been completed. SubhanAllah.</p>
<h3 id="munim">MUNIM</h3>
<p>MUNIM</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/78/80747d2d-bd3b-4e95-a510-940e9d5f1391.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>I take the next meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> from the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُنعِم</span>. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُنعِم</span> is used for the one who bestows gifts, the one who gives blessings. It comes from the root <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعَم</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نعمۃ</span>, which means gifts or blessings.</p>
<p>Slavery is no longer part of ordinary life, but the old example still makes the meaning clear. It is one thing to own a slave. It is another thing to care for him, nurture him, train him, put him to proper use, and then, beyond all of that, bestow gifts on him as well.</p>
<p>The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نعمۃ</span> points to gifts and benefits given beyond what a person deserves. A gift is not something earned. By definition, it is more than what is due. It expresses the love and mercy of the giver. If something is deserved, then it is salary, payment, or earnings. It is not a gift.</p>
<p>Interestingly, the Arabic word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَعَم</span>, which means yes, also comes from the same root as blessing. The connection is beautiful. The natural human response to receiving blessings is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَعَم</span>: yes, surely. This is what I love about this language. Every word is so perfectly connected to its root.</p>
<p>I may own a slave, or an animal, such as a goat. If I own a goat, I take care of it. But I do not buy it gifts. I do not buy it jewelry. There is no need. It is a goat. It is part of my property.</p>
<p>In earlier times, slaves were treated no differently from goats or other animals. They were barely cared for, much less given gifts.</p>
<p>Who is Allah? He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مالک</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سید</span>. He is our absolute owner, with complete authority over us. That makes us His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ملکیۃ</span>: His property.</p>
<p>But Allah as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> is an owner unlike any other. He takes good care of His property. He has complete authority and control over it. He brings it to full growth. He looks after it, protects it, sustains it, and sets it on the right path. Then, beyond all of that, He gives it many generous gifts it did not deserve.</p>
<p>I have to take this seriously: Allah, as our master, owes us nothing. So whatever He gives us, in any form, is a gift. He is not obligated to give it at all.</p>
<p>I did nothing to deserve my eyes. I did not pay for my hand. No price can be placed on the eyes, even one eye. No price can be placed on the hands, or on the ability to walk. These gifts are beyond price.</p>
<p>Allah gave us all these precious gifts, yet He says He will question us about them on the Day of Judgment.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">At-Takathur: 8</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ثُمَّ لَتُسْـَٔلُنَّ يَوْمَئِذٍ عَنِ ٱلنَّعِيمِ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>On that Day, we will be asked about the greatest of all gifts: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ایمان</span>. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نعیم</span> is the superlative form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نعم</span>, and it means the greatest gift of all.</p>
<p>What is that gift? It is the gift for which all the other gifts were given to us: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ایمان</span>. That is the ultimate gift: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ایمان</span>.</p>
<p>On the Day of Judgment, He will ask us about our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ایمان</span>, the greatest blessing of all. Without it, what would be the purpose of giving us these gifts and making us different from animals? We were given them so that we could see the creations, reflect on them, recognize Him, and try to draw closer to Him.</p>
<p>I now understand that Allah as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> is the One who:</p>
<ul>
<li>owns us;</li>
<li>holds ultimate authority over us;</li>
<li>trains us and nurtures us with love, kindness, and patience until we reach maturity;</li>
<li>places us on His chosen path;</li>
<li>then showers us with many undeserved gifts.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="qayyim">QAYYIM</h3>
<p>QAYYIM</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/78/f63ba209-ecbb-4b20-844c-b4731e0c1d62.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>The final meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَیِّم</span>: the one who ensures that things hold together and do not fall apart. It means the one whose attention and care remain fixed on what He sustains. Important things collapse when care is withdrawn. The matter is easy to see in ordinary life. People who garden know that some plants are extremely delicate; if they are neglected for even one day, they die. Some people keep fish as pets; if the fish are not fed for a single day, they end up belly-up. That night, there is NEMO KABAAB for dinner. A house left unattended for one day quickly shows what neglect does.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَیِّم</span> means the Sustainer, the Maintainer, the One whose focus and attention are constantly upon things. That meaning is also embedded in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>. That is why Allah says in the Quran:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّہُ لَا اِلَٰہَ ھُوَ الْحَیُّ الْقَیُّومُ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Allah is the only <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الحی</span>, the Giver of life, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">القیوم</span>, the One who then maintains that life through His constant attention to all things. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قیوم</span> is the superlative form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَیِّم</span>: the One who constantly sustains, constantly maintains, and never takes a break.</p>
<p>In other words, I am not alive because I keep myself alive. My next breath does not come because I eat well, exercise, follow a good diet, or live in a healthy place. None of that is the reason I am alive. I am alive because Allah allows me to live. He sustains me; my own efforts do not.</p>
<p>There are many ways my existence could end in a second: crossing a road, a small earthquake, a snowstorm.</p>
<p>The Indian actress Sri Devi died. She was a perfectly healthy woman, yet she drowned in a bathtub. She likely had plans for the next day, just as I do. She surely had short-term goals and long-term goals. All of them disappeared in a second. I exist because the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَیِّم</span> allows me to exist. I am completely and constantly dependent on Allah.</p>
<p>I see another matter in this ayah: two attributes of Allah are mentioned, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الحی</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">القیوم</span>. He gives life, and He constantly maintains what He gives life to.</p>
<p>These two attributes belong together because the One who gives life also knows how to sustain it. He knows how long it should be sustained. He knows the stages and steps through which it must pass. He knows how and when it will end, and what will happen after it ends. The entire plan for every individual creation, not only for human beings but for all creation, has already been fully set by the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قیوم</span>.</p>
<p>That is part of the meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَیِّم</span>: to have a plan. Nothing can be begun or maintained without a plan. A person who starts a business does not choose the business by playing rock paper scissors. The whole plan must be considered first:</p>
<ul>
<li>the initial investment</li>
<li>how the business will stay afloat</li>
<li>how it will grow</li>
<li>costs and expenses</li>
<li>budget allocations</li>
</ul>
<p>The whole matter is planned before the business even begins. It is impossible that Allah created us without a plan.</p>
<h3 id="rab">RAB</h3>
<p>I now come to the complete definition of the word Rabb.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرب ھو المالک والسَّیِّد و المُرَبِّی والمُرشِد وَالمُنعِم والقَیِّم</span></p></blockquote>
<p>RAB</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/78/7a08c24b-14c5-4e03-baea-0851a90c77f9.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> is the One who is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مالک</span>, the One with absolute ownership. At the same time, He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سید</span>, with complete and unconditional authority.</p>
<p>At the same time, He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مربی</span>. He causes growth, takes care of us, nurtures us, and protects us with love, care, concern, and mercy.</p>
<p>At the same time, He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مرشد</span>. He brings us to physical and spiritual maturity, then sets us on His straight path and puts us to proper use.</p>
<p>At the same time, He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">منعم</span>. He showers us with gifts and blessings that are undeserved and beyond number.</p>
<p>At the same time, He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قیم</span>. He sustains us for the full span of our duration.</p>
<p>Allah, as our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>, gathers all these meanings together, all at once. He is not one of these things at one stage of life and another at a different stage. He is all of them, at the same time. His ownership includes His authority. His authority includes His nurturing. His nurturing includes His guidance and direction. His guidance includes His blessings. His blessings include His sustenance, provision, and maintenance. All of this is present together. SubhanAllah.</p>
<p>I now tie this understanding of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> to a point already discussed: the man of pure essence, someone from the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اُولُو الْاَلْبَابِ</span>, who reflects deeply on the creation of the heavens and the earth, and on the alternation of night and day.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ فِى خَلْقِ ٱلسَّمَوَتِ وَٱلْأَرْضِ وَٱخْتِلَفِ ٱلَّيْلِ وَٱلنَّهَارِ لَآيَتٍۢ لِّأُو۟لِى ٱلْأَلْبَبِ</span> ‎<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">۱۹۰</span>‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلَّذِينَ يَذْكُرُونَ ٱللَّهَ قِيَمًۭا وَقُعُودًۭا وَعَلَىٰ جُنُوبِهِمْ وَيَتَفَكَّرُونَ فِى خَلْقِ ٱلسَّمَوَتِ وَٱلْأَرْضِ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>I find him saying:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Ali ''Imran: 191</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبَّنَا مَا خَلَقْتَ هَذَا بَطِلًۭا سُبْحَنَكَ فَقِنَا عَذَابَ ٱلنَّارِ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Once he recognized that the heavens, the earth, and he himself must have a creator, the word he used for that creator was Rabb. His reflection on the creation of the heavens and the earth led him to that name. In what he saw, the creator was the absolute owner, with complete authority. He was nurturing and developing His creation with love, care, and mercy. He was setting everything toward its purpose. He was pouring out countless gifts and blessings upon every created thing. He was sustaining, maintaining, and providing for them continuously through time.</p>
<p>That is why he said:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Ali ''Imran: 191</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبَّنَا مَا خَلَقْتَ هَذَا بَطِلًۭا</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>, you did not create all of this without a purpose.</p>
<p>This was a beautiful and fitting conclusion, because Allah confirmed it in His revelation. The very first ayah revealed to Rasul Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> was:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-''Alaq: 1</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱقْرَأْ بِٱسْمِ رَبِّكَ ٱلَّذِى خَلَقَ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>I notice that the first revelation says: Recite with the name of your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> who created you. It does not say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱقْرَأْ بِٱسْمِ اللہِ ٱلَّذِى خَلَقَ</span>‎. In this verse, Allah introduces himself to his Messenger for the very first time.</p>
<p>When a person introduces himself, he normally gives his name, not one of his qualities. If I met someone for the first time, and I said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّلَامُ عَلَیْکُمْ</span> I am a teacher; or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّلَامُ عَلَیْکُمْ</span> I can do 200 push-ups, the reaction would be: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَعَلَیْکُمُ السَّلَامُ</span>, You''re also a weirdo! That is not how introductions usually work. A person begins with his name.</p>
<p>For that reason, I would have expected Allah to say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱقْرَأْ بِٱسْمِ اللہِ ٱلَّذِى خَلَقَ</span>: Read with the name of Allah who created you. Instead, he says: Read in the name of your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> who created you. Allah chose to introduce himself as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>. That choice tells me something important.</p>
<p>When I introduce myself as a teacher, I am saying how I want to be known. I am presenting myself through that role. In effect, I am saying that being a teacher matters to me more than my other qualities, even more than my name.</p>
<p>Allah is glorious beyond comprehension. He is magnificent beyond comprehension. He is powerful beyond comprehension. Yet what He says to Rasul Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>, and to us by extension, is that the most important relationship between Him and us is Rabb. That is how He wants me to think of Him: not as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جلیل</span>, not as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عظیم</span>, not as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قوی</span>, not as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">کبیر</span>, not as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">متکبر</span>, but as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>.</p>
<p>The weight of that choice needs to settle in the heart. When a king introduces himself, he does not usually choose a humble description. He does not say, “I''m your neighbor!” or “I''m your dad''s friend!” or “I''m your caretaker.” He says, “I AM YOUR KING. KNEEL. BOW DOWN BEFORE ME!”</p>
<p>If Allah had chosen such a form of introduction in the Quran, it would have suited His majesty. It would have been entirely fitting. Yet the word He chose instead was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>. This shows how important the word is to Him. After the meanings of this word become clear, the loving relationship He wishes to have with His creation also becomes clearer.</p>
<p>This matters deeply for the theme of revelation. In the sequence of revelation, the very first verse says:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-''Alaq: 1</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱقْرَأْ بِٱسْمِ رَبِّكَ ٱلَّذِى خَلَقَ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>But the very last revealed verse says to the messenger <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Ma''idah: 3</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْيَوْمَ أَكْمَلْتُ لَكُمْ دِينَكُمْ وَأَتْمَمْتُ عَلَيْكُمْ نِعْمَتِى وَرَضِيتُ لَكُمُ ٱلْإِسْلَمَ دِينًۭا</span></p></blockquote>
<p>On this day I have perfected your religion for you, and I have completed my blessings on you, and I have selected Islam as the way of life for you.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Ma''idah: 3</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَمَنِ ٱضْطُرَّ فِى مَخْمَصَةٍ غَيْرَ مُتَجَانِفٍۢ لِّإِثْمٍ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>I then come to the final part of the ayah:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Baqarah: 192</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَإِنَّ ٱللَّهَ غَفُورٌۭ رَّحِيمٌۭ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>I have no doubt that <strong>Allah</strong> is All-Forgiving and All-Merciful. The revelation begins with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> and ends with the name Allah. This exactly matches the pattern of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللَّہِ الرَّحْمَنِ الرَّحِیمِ</span>. In that statement comes the name Allah, followed by His two attributes of mercy: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَنِ الرَّحِیمِ</span>. The revelation then ends with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفُورٌۭ رَّحِيمٌۭ</span>: the constantly forgiving and the constantly loving. It begins with His intense and constant love and mercy, and it ends with His constant forgiveness and constant mercy. SubhanAllah!</p>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'linguistic meaning of rabb';
INSERT INTO chapter (slug, anchor_key, idx, title, html, word_count) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'master slave relationship', 10, 'Master Slave Relationship', '', 5988);
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<h3 id="what-is-rab">What is RAB?</h3>
<p>I have examined the linguistic meaning of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>. I learned that a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> brings together six attributes:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المالک والسَّیِّد و المُرَبِّی والمُرشَد وَالمُنعِم والقَیِّم</span></p></blockquote>
<p>A <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> is, first, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مالک</span>: the one who possesses absolute ownership. At the same time, He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سید</span>, meaning that He has complete authority over us.</p>
<p>Within that complete ownership and unrestricted authority, He is also <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مربی</span>: the one who causes growth. He cares for His creation, nurtures them, and protects them. He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مرشد</span>, which means that He not only nurtures His creation until maturity, but also gives each created thing its purpose. He sets every creation on the path of its destiny and supports it along that path.</p>
<p>In doing so, He is also <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">منعم</span>: He showers His creation with countless gifts and blessings that they have not earned. Finally, He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قیم</span>: the one who sustains and maintains them for the whole span of their existence.</p>
<p>All these meanings come together in the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>. I also learned that the primary ingredient in Allah''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ربوبیۃ</span> is love. All six meanings rest on that one fundamental emotion of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span>: intense, constant love and care.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰنُ عَلَّمَ الْقُرْآنَ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>The Loving, the Intensely Merciful, taught the Quran. It is not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْجَبَّارُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَّمَ</span>, or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْقُرْآنَ</span>. Thus <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span> is the most essential element of Allah''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ربوبیۃ</span>. This is why, when a person of sound essence reflected on the creation of the heavens and the earth, the word he used to call upon his Creator was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Ali ''Imran: 191</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبَّنَا مَا خَلَقْتَ هَذَا بَطِلًۭا</span></p></blockquote>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Ali ''Imran: 192</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبَّنَآ إِنَّكَ مَن تُدْخِلِ ٱلنَّارَ فَقَدْ أَخْزَيْتَهُۥ</span></p></blockquote>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Ali ''Imran: 193</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَّبَّنَآ إِنَّنَا سَمِعْنَا مُنَادِيًۭا يُنَادِى لِلْإِيمَنِ أَنْ آمِنُوا۟ بِرَبِّكُمْ فَـَٔامَنَّا</span></p></blockquote>
<p>He used the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> because he saw the whole creation reflecting the many attributes of its Creator, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ربوبیۃ</span>.</p>
<p>I may meet a highly skilled electrician and watch him at work. From his knowledge and skill alone, I can conclude that he studied at a good electrical engineering school, or that a highly skilled teacher trained him in this work. I have not seen that teacher. I may not even know the name of the school he attended. Yet I can still reach that conclusion by observing his work. The reason is simple: in this respect, the electrician is the product of his teacher. Through his skill and knowledge, he displays the training, discipline, and qualities his teacher gave him.</p>
<p>All creation does the same. It continually displays the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ربوبیۃ</span> of its Creator. This principle is true at the smallest level, and it is certainly true at the largest level. The universe, because it is created, continually reflects the attributes of its Creator.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Isra: 44</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تُسَبِّحُ لَهُ ٱلسَّمَوَتُ ٱلسَّبْعُ وَٱلْأَرْضُ وَمَن فِيهِنَّ ۚ وَإِن مِّن شَىْءٍ إِلَّا يُسَبِّحُ بِحَمْدِهِۦ وَلَكِن لَّا تَفْقَهُونَ تَسْبِيحَهُمْ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>The seven heavens, the earth, and everything within them constantly glorify Him. There is not a single thing that does not sing His praise. But you do not understand their glorification.</p>
<p>In other words, the signs and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آیات</span> of who Allah is have been placed into the very fabric of His design. The more I reflect on creation, the more clearly I can appreciate the beauty of that design.</p>
<p>I said that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> is commonly translated into English as “Lord.” Linguists, however, agree that the closest single-word replacement for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> is “master.” A master does not only have ownership, authority, and control over something or someone. A master also possesses complete knowledge and skill. Those qualities are essential for nurturing, guidance, and maintenance. For that reason, <strong>master</strong> comes much closer to the meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> than “lord.”</p>
<h3 id="relationship">Relationship</h3>
<p>I now understand the meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>, and the next point is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> implies a relationship. Some words carry a relationship within them. They need a counterpart.</p>
<p>When I say “teacher,” the word already points to students. I cannot be a teacher if I have no students to teach. When I say “parent,” the word points to children. When I say “employer,” it points to employees. Certain titles require a relationship by their very meaning.</p>
<p>Other words do not work that way. When I say “intelligent,” the word does not necessarily place a person in a direct relationship with anyone else. It is a general attribute. The same is true of “tall.” It describes a quality, not a personal bond.</p>
<p>In the same way, Allah has certain names that do not imply a personal relationship. Allah is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الأعلی</span>. He is lofty. This is a powerful attribute, but it describes His state in relation to creation in a general way. It does not, by itself, establish a personal relationship.</p>
<p>Other names of Allah do establish such a relationship. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خالق</span>, the Creator, is one example. If He is Creator, then there must be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مخلوق</span>, creation. There cannot be a creator without creation. He can be powerful by Himself. He can be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الأعلی</span> on His own, but not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خالق</span> without creation.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غفور</span> is another example. Allah is extremely forgiving, which means there must be someone to forgive. Some attributes of Allah are general in nature, while others imply a very personal relationship.</p>
<p>I therefore understand that when Allah begins His revelation with the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>, and when He says <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِقْرَأْ بِسْمِ رَبِّکَ الَّذِی خَلَقَ</span>, He is introducing Himself as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> to Rasul Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>, and through him, to us.</p>
<p>In doing so, what He is essentially saying is this: “When you think of Me, set all My other attributes aside for a moment, and think of Me first and foremost as your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>. Even if you forget every other relationship I have with you, never let go of this one. At every waking moment of your life, remain mindful that I am your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>. My <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ربوبیۃ</span> will take precedence in all My dealings and interactions with you, and the primary ingredient of My <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ربوبیۃ</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span>: love, care, and concern.”</p>
<p>I find that the statement does not simply say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>. It says <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ربک</span>, “your” <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>. Allah is the master of all creation. He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مالک</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سید</span>, the One who has absolute ownership and complete authority over everything. He could have said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِقْرَأْ بِسْمِ رَبِّ الَّذِی خَلَقَ</span>, “Read in the name of the master who created.”</p>
<p>But He does not leave it there. He makes it personal: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِقْرَأْ بِسْمِ رَبِّکَ الَّذِی خَلَقَ</span>, “Read in the name of <strong>your</strong> master who created you.” It is as though He is saying, “I am <strong>only</strong> your master and nobody else''s.” He makes His relationship that intimate with each one of us. SubhanAllah!</p>
<p>The relationship of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> requires a counterpart. If Allah is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>, if Allah is master, then I must ask what that makes me. What completes the relationship with a master is a slave. If Allah is master, that makes us all His slaves; and the Arabic word for slave is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبد</span>. So when I accept Allah as my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>, by accepting that relationship itself, I am also accepting the fact that I am His slave.</p>
<p>I can accept this, once I think it through. I have already established that he is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مالک</span>: he owns us. I accept that Allah has complete authority over us because he is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سید</span>. I know that we live under his training and nurturing because he is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مربی</span>. I know that we need his guidance if we are to fulfill our destiny because he is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مرشد</span>. I know that we constantly receive countless gifts we never earned because he is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">منعم</span>. I know, too, that he is our final sustainer and provider. We exist only because he allows us to exist, because he is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قیم</span>.</p>
<p>So it is not impossible, or even strange, to accept that we are slaves of this gracious master. The simple truth is that our acceptance does not change the fact. Whether we accept it or not, we are still his property and his slaves. My children could say to me, “We don''t accept you as our father!” The answer would still be, “Well, tough luck. I am still your father.”</p>
<p>So I want to make the ground clear: we are slaves of this master whether we like it or not, and whether we want it or not. That is the relationship.</p>
<h3 id="slave---abd">Slave - ABD</h3>
<p>Having said that, I want to show how beautiful this relationship with our master is. Until I appreciate the beauty of this relationship with him, I cannot gain the benefits of his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ربوبیۃ</span>. This matters because that is how relationships work.</p>
<p>I cannot receive benefit from a teacher until I acknowledge him and accept him as my teacher. I benefit only when I accept that relationship and submit to it.</p>
<p>I can see the same truth in the relationship between a doctor and a patient. A doctor cannot help a patient until the patient accepts the doctor''s prescribed treatment and submits to it. If a patient has diabetes and the doctor tells him to stop eating candy, yet every time he sees candy, he says, “Oh, I know the doctor asked me not to eat it, but what does he know anyway? It is only a little bit. It is not as if I am eating the whole bag. He is never going to find out anyway. Besides, everyone else is eating candy, so why cannot I have it?” then the patient has not truly accepted the doctor-patient relationship.</p>
<p>It does not matter if he calls him Dr. So-and-so. It does not matter how many visits he makes or how many appointments he keeps. He will not benefit, because despite what he says, he has not truly accepted himself as a patient. Only when he fully accepts that relationship and submits to the doctor''s treatment can he receive the benefit the doctor wants him to have.</p>
<p>The relationship works in both directions. What is striking is that the doctor, despite his vast knowledge and skill, is completely helpless here. The doctor has the knowledge to benefit the patient. The doctor truly cares about the patient. He wants to benefit him. Yet with all his knowledge, will, and desire to help, he cannot do anything, because the patient has not completed the relationship.</p>
<p>I am not really speaking about doctors and patients. I am speaking about myself. I claim that Allah is my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>. I know His infinite <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span>. I know that He wants what is best for me. I know the treatment He has prescribed for my soul. I know the duties and the prohibitions.</p>
<p>I may even pray and fulfill my religious obligations. Yet in daily life, I may still do the very things that go against His prescribed treatment. How, then, can my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> help me and benefit me? This is striking: despite the infinite powers of my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>, I leave the relationship incomplete, and so I fail to receive the benefit He wants for me.</p>
<p>If I truly want to gain the benefits my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> offers, I must ask what is required of me.</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/82/cfa7ce2f-61a4-4cb1-9479-ff4021731839.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>I have to complete the relationship. I complete it by submitting myself as His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبد</span>. His relationship with me is that of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ربوبیۃ</span>. I complete that relationship through my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبودیۃ</span> and my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبادۃ</span>: my submission, my servitude, and my worship.</p>
<h3 id="worship---ibadah">Worship - IBADAH</h3>
<p>The words <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبادۃ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبودیۃ</span> must be understood clearly. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبادۃ</span> is generally translated as worship, and worship is an action. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبودیۃ</span> is the inward state that makes me want to worship. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبادۃ</span> are the different forms of worship. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبودیۃ</span> is the feeling of servitude that drives those acts of worship.</p>
<p>IBADAH</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/82/11062766-b962-4709-ae7b-87ee1d9d0120.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>What is striking is that, in Arabic, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبادۃ</span> carries two meanings at once. It means <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُبُودِیَّۃُ وَ الطَّاعَۃُ</span>: worship and obedience together.</p>
<p>Worship is usually understood as prayer, fasting, ZAKAT, and similar acts. These are all forms of worship, but I often stop there and treat them as the whole meaning. That is the problem with translating <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبادۃ</span> simply as worship. Worship by itself does not necessarily include the idea of obedience.</p>
<p>I understand <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبادۃ</span> to include obedience, but not obedience produced by force. It is obedience that rises from love. The word obedience can carry a harsh meaning, because a person may be forced to obey. Children may be bullied into obedience through force or worse. In history, slaves were beaten and tortured into obedience like animals. That is why the word can carry a painful emotional weight.</p>
<p>But that is not the obedience meant here. Forced obedience is not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبادۃ</span>. The Arabic word for that kind of compulsion is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِقَّۃ</span>, which corresponds to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غلامی</span> in Urdu. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رقۃ</span> means forced obedience; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبودیۃ</span> means willing obedience.</p>
<p>Worship is the outward form of that obedience. The seven forms of worship — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وِلَایَۃٌ، طَھَارَۃٌ ، صَلَوَاۃٌ، زَکَوَاۃٌ، حَجٌّ، جِھَادٌ</span> — are all forms in which obedience appears. I perform them because Allah has commanded them. Every act of worship, then, is carried out in obedience to his command.</p>
<p>When I obey willingly and happily, I am in a state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبودیۃ</span>, and that is the desired state. If I do the same act with resentment, without being truly pleased to do it, I am in a state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رقۃ</span>, which is not the desired state.</p>
<p>The outward worship may be the same. I may perform <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صلواۃ</span> in both cases. But if I pray unwillingly, it is no longer <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبادۃ</span>; it is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رقۃ</span>.</p>
<p>I arrive, then, at this: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبادۃ</span> is nothing other than willing obedience to the Beloved, born from passionate and unconditional love. This is why, when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِمَامُ جَعْفَرٍ الصَّادِقِ</span> was asked, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">یا أمام</span>! <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ھَلِ الدِّینُ؟</span> - O Imam, what is the essence of religion? he said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِلَّا الطَّاعَۃُ</span> - nothing but obedience. Then the man asked: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ھَلِ الطَّاعَۃُ؟</span> What is obedience? He replied <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الا الحُب</span> - nothing but love. So the essence of every form of worship is willing obedience, and the essence of willing obedience is love.</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/82/281db322-3bb2-41ea-8b21-09f2524569b1.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>I learned that Allah is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ال الہ</span> - He is “The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>.” He is the One who takes precedence over every form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> in my life. But His relationship with me is more direct than that: He is my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>. The word Allah tells me who He is, and what He is meant to signify in my life in the broadest sense. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> describes His personal relationship with me. In other words, He is the One to be worshiped because He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللہ</span>, and He is also the One to be obeyed willingly because He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>.</p>
<p>I can put this in practical terms. These two names matter because they shape ordinary life between the prayers. Suppose prayer is fifteen minutes away. Suppose <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فجر</span> begins in fifteen minutes, and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبادۃ</span> still has to be made. What kind of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبادۃ</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صلوۃ</span>? It is worship. But from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فجر</span> until <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظھر</span>, a person may not be engaged in worship in the narrow sense. Even then, he is still meant to be obeying Him.</p>
<p>If I truly recognize Him as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>, and if I truly accept His relationship with me as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>, then between two prayers I may not always be performing an act of worship, but I must still remain in obedience to Him. This is why the concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبادۃ</span> is so comprehensive in Islam. Every form of obedience is counted as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبادۃ</span> in Islam.</p>
<p>For that reason, when I explain the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبادۃ</span>, I prefer “obedience” over “worship.” Unconditional, willing obedience is worship.</p>
<p>A person earns a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حلال</span> income at work; that is an act of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبادۃ</span>. A student goes to school and sincerely fulfills the purpose of learning; that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبادۃ</span>. A person obeys, loves, and respects his parents and teachers; that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبادۃ</span>. He is kind to his siblings, generous to the needy, and respectful to his elders; that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبادۃ</span>. He washes his hands before eating, covers his head before going to the toilet, smiles at someone, or makes <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وضو</span> before sleeping; all of these are acts of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبادۃ</span> when they are done in willing obedience and sincere love for the Beloved.</p>
<p>So the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبادۃ</span> in Islam is very comprehensive. It is not restricted to the formal acts of worship.</p>
<h3 id="slave---abd">Slave - ABD</h3>
<p>I now turn from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبودیۃ</span> to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبد</span>, which means a slave. Many Muslims feel uneasy with the word slave because it brings a harsh image to mind. That image comes from the way masters have often treated slaves throughout history. I want to use those very examples as a contrast between the kinds of masters the world has known and the kind of master our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> is.</p>
<h3 id="freedom-of-choice">Freedom of choice</h3>
<p>I begin with the word slave. What is the defining mark of a slave? A slave is someone whose free will has been taken away. He has no choice in the decisions his master makes. He must obey the master''s will without question. He has no independent existence except to serve the master''s desires.</p>
<p>In worldly slavery, the master denies the slave''s freedom and forbids him to choose for himself. That is the deepest humiliation. To take away the freedom of choice from an intelligent human being is to say, “I do not think you are capable of making the right choices.” It is to say, “You are no different from an animal to me. Just as I choose for my cattle and my dog, I will choose for you.” Denying a human being the freedom to choose is the height of humiliation and disgrace.</p>
<p>When I speak of our Master, I ask what the most precious gift is that He gave us. What is the final gift that sets us apart from every other creation in this world? It is free will.</p>
<p>Did He need to give it to us? No. He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مالک</span>. He is my owner. He owns every created thing in this universe. That alone makes every creation His property and His slave. Being His property, or being His slave, therefore has nothing to do with being Muslim or not. Everything belongs to Him. Everything is His slave.</p>
<p>The sun is His property. The moon is His property. The galaxies are His property. This whole universe, and everything within it, including me, belongs to Him.</p>
<p>Does the sun have free will? Does the moon have free will? Do night and day have free will? No. They are all in complete submission to the will of their Creator.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَکُلٌّ فِی فَلَکٍ یَسْبَحُونَ</span>.</p></blockquote>
<p>They are all floating in their orbits. They have been bound by gravity. They have no choice except to float where they have been placed. Allah bound them and took away their freedom of choice.</p>
<p>Could He have done that to us? Could Allah have bound us? Could He have forced us into submission? The One who compelled and enslaved the sun, the moon, the solar systems, and the galaxies could certainly have forced us into submission.</p>
<p>But did He? No. He gave us intellect and freedom of choice, something He did not give to any other creation. SubhanAllah.</p>
<p>I see from the outset that Allah is truly my master. He is my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>, but His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ربوبیۃ</span> is unlike the mastery of any other master I know. Every worldly master first tries to take away freedom of choice. Teachers, parents, employers, courts, doctors: any worldly <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> comes with some form of control, some restriction on the will.</p>
<p>Allah alone is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> who has granted complete freedom of choice. If denying freedom of choice is the deepest humiliation, then granting full freedom of choice is the highest honor. SubhanAllah. Allah honored us above every other creation by giving us the freedom to choose.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Kahf: 29</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَقُلِ ٱلْحَقُّ مِن رَّبِّكُمْ ۖ فَمَن شَآءَ فَلْيُؤْمِن وَمَن شَآءَ فَلْيَكْفُرْ</span></p><p class="tr">And say: &quot;The truth has been revealed by your master.&quot;</p></blockquote>
<p>Then whosoever wills, let him believe, and whosoever wills, let him disbelieve</p>
<h3 id="expectations">Expectations</h3>
<p>What gifts do masters give their slaves? Nothing of worth. They barely feed them, barely clothe them, and barely pay them. When they do provide food and clothing, they give only the minimum, just enough to keep them alive. Yet for the little they give, they expect a great deal in return. Every master I know gives the minimum and demands the maximum from his slaves.</p>
<p>I have spoken about what Allah has given us: the many undeserved gifts that the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">منعم</span> has showered upon his creation. These are precious gifts, far beyond the ordinary logic of exchange. Any company that gave its employees so much would bleed red.</p>
<p>Measured against the quantity and quality of the gifts our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> has bestowed upon us, what does he ask in return? The bare minimum. The very least. SubhanAllah.</p>
<p>Among all created beings, our master has given us the most. The rest of creation remains constantly in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبودیۃ</span> to him. They work around the clock, in constant obedience to Allah, without a moment''s break, in order to serve us.</p>
<p>Yet although he has given us the most, he asks the least from us in return:</p>
<ul>
<li>one hour of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صلواۃ</span> each day;</li>
<li>one month of fasting out of twelve, and even that only from sunrise to sunset;</li>
<li>2.5% of our wealth in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زکواۃ</span>, from wealth he gave us in the first place.</li>
</ul>
<p>It is incredible.</p>
<p>And even then, I find mercy at every turn. If <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صلواۃ</span> is missed, there is no problem; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قضاء</span> is there. If water cannot be found, there is no problem; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تیمم</span> is there. If illness or travel makes fasting difficult, the fasts are completed later. If a saved amount has not remained untouched for a full year, there is no problem; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زکواۃ</span> is not due. If there is no money for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حج</span>, or if the journey is unsafe, there is no problem; the obligation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حج</span> is lifted from one''s shoulders.</p>
<p>This is astonishing. How easy has he made it for us? What kind of master does this? I know of no other master in this world who makes matters so easy for a slave. I know of no other master who gives so much and asks for so little in return. Such a master is unheard of.</p>
<h3 id="explanations">Explanations</h3>
<p>And if that generosity were not enough, he also sent revelation and messengers to explain his reasons to us. Does a master ever explain his reasons to his slaves? Can any slave demand, “Explain the purpose of this command to me”? Can a slave negotiate with his master? Can a slave say, “I will obey only if, and only when, the command is explained to me”? No. Yet this master explained matters to us in the Quran. Our master also showed us, through the living example of Rasul Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>, how to carry out our obligations in practice. He did this to make the relationship easier to accept, and to make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبودیۃ</span> easy for us.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Baqarah: 185</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يُرِيدُ ٱللَّهُ بِكُمُ ٱلْيُسْرَ وَلَا يُرِيدُ بِكُمُ ٱلْعُسْرَ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Allah desires ease for you. He does not desire hardship for you.</p>
<h3 id="who-does-it-benefit">Who does it benefit?</h3>
<p>I ask another question. Who are the acts of worship meant to benefit: Him or us? In every ordinary master-slave relationship, slavery is arranged for the master''s benefit, not the slave''s.</p>
<p>But this Master designed the universe for us. He gave us the most and asked the least in return. He even explained the reasons behind His commands. So the question becomes: everything He has asked us to do, whom does it benefit? It benefits us, and only us. SubhanAllah.</p>
<p>Every slave in the world is bought and put to work for the master''s gain. Every command given to a slave serves the master''s interest. What kind of Master is this, whose every command is for the benefit of His slaves? Not a single command is for His own benefit. If that is not love, if that is not care, if that is not mercy, if that is not ease, if that is not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span>, then what is it? AllahuAkbar.</p>
<p>He is constantly serving us. And what He asks from us in return is also only for our benefit, not His.</p>
<h3 id="judgment">Judgment</h3>
<p>I ask another question. Is a slave''s work judged according to the slave''s abilities, or according to the master''s expectations? In ordinary slavery, a slave''s work is judged by the master''s standards and demands.</p>
<p>How does Allah judge our actions? He judges them according to each person''s knowledge and ability. Nowhere in the Quran does Allah demand perfection. The only thing He asks for in the Quran is effort.</p>
<h3 id="service">Service</h3>
<p>I know of no master in this world who rushes to meet the needs of his slaves. I have never seen even a story in which a slave cries out to his master in need, and the master drops everything and hurries to help him. Yet what does our RAB do?</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Baqarah: 186</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَإِذَا سَأَلَكَ عِبَادِى عَنِّى فَإِنِّى قَرِيبٌ ۖ أُجِيبُ دَعْوَةَ ٱلدَّاعِ إِذَا دَعَانِ</span></p><p class="tr">And when My slaves ask you concerning Me, Indeed I near him in that very moment. I immediately respond to the invocations of the supplicant when he calls upon Me.</p></blockquote>
<p>No master ever does that. History shows the ordinary pattern: slaves are hurried forward to meet the needs of their masters, never the other way around. What kind of merciful master, then, is this, who hastens to fulfill the needs of His slaves?</p>
<p>I reflect more deeply on the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ربوبیۃ</span> of this master, and I see more clearly how astonishing his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span> and mercy are. He is intensely loving, deeply caring, and overwhelmingly merciful. He is unlike any master I know, and unlike any master I will ever come to know in this world. No master does for his slave what our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> does for us.</p>
<p>Yet his subtle love and mercy can make me forget my place before him. I forget that I am his property. I forget that he is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مالک</span>. He is the owner. If he is the owner, then by definition I am owned property. He can do whatever he wills with me. He can break me, tear me apart, burn me, drown me. Nothing prevents him from doing any of it. And if he chooses to do so, nothing remains in my power, or in the power of all of us together, or in the power of everything in the universe combined, to protect me from his wrath.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن يُرِدْنِ ٱلرَّحْمَنُ بِضُرٍّۢ</span> . <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَّا تُغْنِ عَنِّى شَفَعَتُهُمْ شَيْـًۭٔا وَلَا يُنقِذُونِ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>If the Rehman decides to harm me, absolutely nothing in existence will be able to help me. That truth stands plainly.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Ya-Sin: 23</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن يُرِدْنِ ٱلرَّحْمَنُ بِضُرٍّۢ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>If the REHMAN decides to harm me. SubhanAllah. Not</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن يُرِدْنِ الجبار</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن يُرِدْنِ القھار</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن يُرِدْنِ ذوالنتقام</span></p></blockquote>
<p>not the conqueror, not the powerful, not the avenger. If the Rehman decides to harm me: no, no, no.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Ya-Sin: 23</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن يُرِدْنِ ٱلرَّحْمَنُ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>If the REHMAN decides to harm me, then even in His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ضُر</span>, even in His harm, He is merciful. Hidden within it is a benefit. SubhanAllah.</p>
<p>And yet I constantly disobey Allah. I constantly fall short in my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبودیۃ</span>, in my obedience and servitude to Him. Still, He allows me to exist.</p>
<p>My phone was causing problems. It was acting up and freezing on me. Every now and then I had to restart it. I went to the store and bought a new phone. I got rid of the old one. Why? Because it acted up on me occasionally, not all the time, only a few times. So I replaced it.</p>
<p>That is what is done with property when it no longer does its job. It is discarded. I belong to Allah. We all belong to Allah. And we are not doing our jobs, not merely occasionally, not merely sometimes, but in some cases constantly, all the time. What, then, should my Owner do with me? What should my Owner do with property as useless as myself? Discard it. But does He do that? AllahuAkbar.</p>
<p>So even when words such as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبد</span> (master and slave) are used, He is unlike any master imaginable in this world. This Master loves us intensely and constantly. When I realize that, my natural response is to love Him back. That is another unique relationship between a master and a slave.</p>
<h3 id="love">Love</h3>
<p>I know no master in this world whom a slave naturally loves. History shows the opposite: slaves have hated their masters, and at the first chance they have wanted to run away from them.</p>
<p>But I wholeheartedly love my Master. The only direction I want to run is toward Him, not away from Him. I do that by submitting myself to His love and mercy, through willing obedience. This is not the forced obedience known in the world. It is obedience freely given.</p>
<p>This obedience rises from the highest stages of love already described. It appears as a desire for service and servitude, like the service of a passionate lover, an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عاشق</span>, for his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">معشوق</span> - for his beloved. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبودیۃ</span>. That is worship. In that lies my greatest challenge.</p>
<p>I face the greatest challenge, the greatest difficulty, and the hardest trial of every waking moment when I accept Allah as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>. This is the constant challenge I face, and every person in the world faces. It is not the acceptance of the Creator as Allah. That part does not trouble people. Even the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَاھِلِی عَرَبٍ</span> accepted Him as Allah. The real problem, and the real test, is accepting Him as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>: as Master.</p>
<p>That acceptance carries its own demand. If I accept Him as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>, then I must become a slave. I must accept the relationship He has placed upon me. I must give up the false freedom of my passions and desires and wear the collar of His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبودیۃ</span>, becoming His slave. That is where the struggle lies.</p>
<p>I live in an age of absolute and unrestricted self-indulgence. The spirit of the age says: I am in charge. I am in control. I am the master of my own destiny. I am powerful. I am wealthy. I do not have to listen to anyone. No one is the boss of me. I can do whatever I want, whenever I want. I am the king of my domain. This is my life. If I want to do it, I will do it. If I do not want to do it, I will not.</p>
<p>In other words, I make myself my own <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>. I make myself my own master.</p>
<p>And I hear Allah saying: “No! I am your master. I gave you this life. I gave you this authority. I gave you the gift of parents. I gave you the gift of children. I honored you with intellect and free will. I gave you rank and power. I gave you health and strength. I gave you all these things so you would recognize Me and draw nearer to Me, because I am your master, and I am a master unlike any other master you will ever know. Submit to Me, and I will show you what true happiness is.”</p>
<p>My greatest challenge in this life is to accept my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبودیۃ</span> willingly and gladly, and to submit to my master with sincere obedience and true worship. I can overcome that challenge only by recognizing and appreciating the love and mercy of my master. The relationship of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ربوبیۃ</span> becomes complete only when I submit willingly and obey this magnificent master, because love gives birth to love.</p>
<p>If this incredible <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> does so much for disobedient and ungrateful slaves, what would He do for me if I truly became willingly obedient to Him?</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Ibrahim: 7</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَإِذْ تَأَذَّنَ رَبُّكُمْ لَىِٕن شَكَرْتُمْ لَأَزِيدَنَّكُمْ ۖ وَلَىِٕن كَفَرْتُمْ إِنَّ عَذَابِى لَشَدِيدٌۭ</span></p><p class="tr">And when your master said: “If you become grateful, I will give you more, but if you are ungrateful, then indeed my punishment is severe.”</p></blockquote>
<h3 id="if-our-relationship-with-allah-is-of-master-and-slave-then-what-is-the-concept-o">If Our Relationship With Allah Is Of Master And Slave, Then What Is The Concept Of Freedom In Islam?</h3>
<p>What is freedom? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حریۃ</span>. In Arabic, freedom is very different from the way modern English usually defines it. In pre-Islamic Arab culture, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حر</span> stood in contrast to the slave. A free person was someone who was not a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبد</span>. Such a person possessed both <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حر</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبد</span>.</p>
<p>One of the remarkable things about Islam is that it makes a person <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبد اللہ</span>. If I am <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبد اللہ</span>, the slave of Allah, then what is my relationship to everything else? I become free. My <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبدیۃ</span>, my obedience and servitude, belongs only to Allah. It does not belong to anything or anyone else. That is what sets me completely free. So the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حر</span>, the Arabs say:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الحُرُّ عَبْدٌ مَا طَمَع</span></p></blockquote>
<p>The free man remains a slave as long as he is covetous, as long as he is greedy, and as long as he still wants.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">والعَبْدُ حُرُّ مَا طَمَع</span></p></blockquote>
<p>And the slave is a free man if he is content.</p>
<p>This reaches the essence of the Arab understanding of freedom before Islam. Real freedom was moral freedom. It was freedom from want, not freedom to do whatever I wished. In my culture, freedom has been defined almost entirely as freedom “to”: freedom to do whatever I want, freedom to say whatever I want, freedom to have whatever I want. For the Arabs, it was freedom “from”: freedom from desires, freedom from wants, and freedom from needs. That is how they understood freedom.</p>
<p>I understand Islamic freedom through this powerful pattern: I enslave myself to the One and become free from everything else, or I turn away from the One and become a slave to everything. Even enslavement to Allah is, in essence, the acceptance of His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ربوبیۃ</span>, because I am already a slave. I am His property whether I accept that truth or deny it. Freedom, then, begins when I recognize that relationship, draw nearer to Him, and through that nearness reach complete freedom and happiness.</p>
<h3 id="if-allah-is-so-merciful-then-why-did-imam-hussain-and-his-friends-suffer-in-karb">If Allah is so merciful then why did Imam Hussain and his friends suffer in Karbala?</h3>
<p>The question of suffering depends on perspective. Did they truly suffer? The signs of suffering are fear, regret, depression, and excuses to escape.</p>
<p>The black slave John gives the example. He could have played the racist card, but he did not.</p>
<p>Karbala was the clearest example of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبودیۃ</span>. It was a battle to defend the honor of the beloved. Yazeed had challenged the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ربوبیۃ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہیۃ</span> of Imam Hussain''s beloved, and Imam Hussain and his companions stood firm to defend that honor. They also set an example of true love for me. They showed that this is how love acts: it pays any price for the beloved. It sacrifices food, drink, children, family, and life itself, but it never deserts the beloved. Love that flees at the first sign of trouble is no love at all. For the true lover, what good is life without the beloved''s grace?</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَلَيـتَكَ تَحلُو وَالحَيـاةُ مَـرِيرَةٌ</span> . <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَيـتَكَ تَرضـى وَالأنـامُ غِضَـابُ</span></p></blockquote>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَيـتَ الَّذِي بَيـنِي وَ بَينَـكَ عَامِرٌ</span> . <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَبَـينِي وَبَيـنَ العــالَمِينَ خَـرَابُ</span></p></blockquote>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَيالَيـتَ شُـربِي مِن وِدَادِكَ صَافِياً</span> . <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَشُـربِي مِن مَـاء الفُراتِ سَـرَابُ</span></p></blockquote>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إذا نِلتُ مِنكَ الوُدَّ فَالكُلُّ هَيِّـنٌ</span> . <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَكُـلَّ الَّذي فَوقَ الـتُّرابِ تُـرابُ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>If I can only taste the sweetness of Your pleasure, it does not matter if life itself turns bitter for me.</p>
<p>If You are pleased with me, what do I care for the anger and wrath of the whole world?</p>
<p>May what lies between us remain beautiful, even if everything between me and the world turns ugly.</p>
<p>One sip from the wine of Your pure love makes the sweet water of the Euphrates seem like a mirage to me.</p>
<p>All I need is Your love. If I have that, I have everything. Without it, even the greatest treasure is dust to me.</p>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'master slave relationship';
INSERT INTO chapter (slug, anchor_key, idx, title, html, word_count) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'understanding rab al-alameen', 11, 'Understanding RAB Al-ALAMEEN', '', 4132);
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>I begin with REHMA.</p>
<p>The classical definition of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> is:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَبُّ ھُوَ المَالِکُ وَ السَّیِّد وَ المُرَبِّی وَ المُرْشَدُ وَ المُنعِمُ وَ القَیِّمُ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>A <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> joins six meanings in one. He has complete ownership and absolute authority. He nurtures, guides, and assigns each thing its purpose. He pours out undeserved gifts and blessings. He also sustains, maintains, and provides.</p>
<p>After learning what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> means, I must ask what he is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> of. In <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَۃُ الْفَاتِحَۃِ</span>, he tells us:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Fatihah: 2</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>I give all praise and gratitude to Allah, who is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span>. I begin with the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span>, and then I turn to what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> means in practice.</p>
<h3 id="linguistic-analysis-of-alameen">Linguistic analysis of ALAMEEN</h3>
<p>Most English translations render <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَالَمِینَ</span> as “worlds.” They give the meaning as: all praise and gratitude belong to Allah, the master of the worlds, or the master of the universe. That English word gives only a weak and incomplete sense of the Arabic.</p>
<p>The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَالَمِینَ</span> is the plural of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">العالَم</span>. To understand it, I first look at its counterpart, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالِم</span>.</p>
<p>ALAM</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/103/eca60cad-bbc6-4834-8ec1-29abaedfcbd2.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>The two words <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالَم</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالِم</span> have exactly the same spelling. The only difference between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالَم</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالِم</span> is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فتحۃ</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">کسرۃ</span>. Apart from that, the spelling is identical.</p>
<p>In Arabic, when two words share a spelling, they also share a deep connection in meaning. Here, they also share the same root: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">علم</span>. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">علم</span> means knowledge. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالِم</span> is the one who knows, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالَم</span> is what becomes known to him.</p>
<p>The distinction is clear:</p>
<ul>
<li><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالِم</span> is the one who knows; he is the cause.</li>
<li><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالَم</span> is what can be known; it is the effect.</li>
</ul>
<p>The device through which <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالِم</span> comes to know <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالَم</span> is the intellect, which in Arabic is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">العقل</span>.</p>
<p>I, as a human being, can know many things. Yet all the knowledge I gain over a lifetime, and all the knowledge human beings gather over centuries, still does not reach everything. I know some things, and I remain ignorant of others. Allah is unlike this. He knows everything that can be known. The word for the One who knows everything about all that exists to be known is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">علیم</span>.</p>
<p>ALEEM</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/103/1b30e423-708e-4a37-82b8-2822340822cd.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>It is the superlative form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالِم</span>. An <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالِم</span> is one who has knowledge, even if only some knowledge. But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">علیم</span> is the One whose knowledge is absolute and complete, embracing everything.</p>
<p>So three words now stand from the same root, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">علم</span>:</p>
<ul>
<li><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالَم</span>: that which can be known; that which waits to be known, discovered, researched, and understood.</li>
<li><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالِم</span>: the one who studies, researches, discovers, and through that process learns about the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالَم</span>.</li>
<li><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">علیم</span>: the One who knows everything there is to know.</li>
</ul>
<p>Because I am limited in my ability to learn and understand, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">علیم</span> created the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالَم</span>. It is there for study, reflection, and knowledge. Through it, awareness grows, and recognition becomes possible. Recognition of whom? The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">علیم</span>.</p>
<p>COMBO</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/103/5a272c60-5c69-4412-acb0-543f36068b34.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>Allah is infinite. Infinite means without limit. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِمَامُ جَعْفَرٍ الصَّادِقِ عَلَیْہِ السَّلَامُ</span> explained Allah as:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَللّٰہُ اَکْبَرُ مِنْ اَنْ یُحَدَّ وَ یُحْسَرَ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>I understand Allah''s greatness in this sense: Allah has no boundary, and Allah cannot be confined within human comprehension. In other words, Allah cannot be “defined.”</p>
<p>Yet my human understanding works through definition. A definition gives finiteness to something. It limits a reality for me so that I can place it within a frame and understand it. This is what the intellect, or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عقل</span>, does. It sets a boundary. It confines. It defines.</p>
<p>This is the problem Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> solved for me: how can I understand and recognize an infinite Creator through finiteness? The answer is the creation of this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالَم</span>. My ways of learning are limited. I must define something before I can understand it. So how can I, a creature of finiteness and definition, understand a Creator who is infinite and cannot be defined? I understand through the intermediary of a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالَم</span>, which acts like an interpreter.</p>
<p>If a group speaks only English, and I know only Urdu, then I need a middleman to understand what is being said. I need someone who understands both languages, someone who can receive the message in English and translate it into Urdu for me.</p>
<p>The same idea applies to this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالم</span>. In its creation, it is physical. In its design, it reflects the attributes of its Creator. It “defines” the attributes of the Creator''s glory and magnificence, so that I can gain some understanding of my Creator.</p>
<h3 id="universe-manifests-existence-of-allah">Universe Manifests existence of Allah</h3>
<p>In other words, this universe becomes the medium through which I recognize Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, for every sculpture proves the presence of a sculptor. The poet says:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-poem"><span class="q-band q-band--poem"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Verse</span></span><div class="q-verse"><p class="ar bayt-solo" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وُجُودُہُ لَہُ دَلِیلُ قَاطِعٌ</span></p></div></blockquote>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَاجَتُ کُلِّ مُحدَثٍ لِصَانعٌ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>The strongest proof that Allah exists is that every sculpture requires a sculptor.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِکُلِّ عالَمُ لِحَاجَّتاً الی العَالِمٌ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>In the same way, everything that can be known requires the existence of one who knows it, because nothing exists as a mere passive object. Therefore, I come to know that Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> exists through the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آثار</span>, through His signs and the effects He has left in creation. This is like the Greek philosopher who asked the Bedouin:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِمَ عَرَفتَ رَبُّك؟</span></p><p class="tr">“How did you come to know of your master?”</p></blockquote>
<p>he said.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote is-group"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إن البِعرَةُ تَدُلُّ علی البَعِیر</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">والرَّوثَةُ تَدُلُّ علی الحمیر</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">و آثارُ القدمُ تَدُلُّ علی المَسِیر</span></p><p class="tr">He said: The camel''s dung shows me that a camel passed here. The donkey''s dung tells me that a donkey was here. Footsteps in the sand tell me that people walked through this place.</p><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فسمآءُ ذاتُ اَبرَاج</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَالْاَرْضَ ذَاتَ الْفِجَاجِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">واَبحَرُ ذَاتَ الامواج</span></p><p class="tr">So when I look at the sky, with its perfect constellations; when I see the earth, with its valleys and paths laid out with order; and when I look at the oceans, with all their waves.</p><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلَا تَدُلُّ عَلَی لَطِیفِ الْخَبِیرِ؟</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Do all these not point to the presence of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لطیف</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خبیر</span>? SubhanAllah. To him, it was that simple. I make such matters far more complicated than they are. The truth is that Allah''s existence is the clearest and most evident truth for a sound and healthy <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فطرۃ</span>. When that primordial essence of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فطرۃ</span> remains intact, the question of a creator''s existence does not even arise. That is why, when the atheist says, “I wish there was a proof of the existence of Allah,” the believer replies, “I see nothing but the proofs of His existence.”</p>
<p>That is the linguistic meaning of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالَم</span>. It is anything and everything that the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالِم</span> can know, so that he may recognize the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">علیم</span>. For that reason, “world” is not the best translation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالمین</span>. It narrows the word''s range. If the word meant only “world,” then it would refer only to that, and to nothing beyond the world or the universe. Its plural would then have been <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">العوالم</span>, not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَالَمِینَ</span>.</p>
<p>The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَالَمِینَ</span> covers a far wider range than the physical world alone. The statement <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّ الْعَالَمِینَ</span> does not mean only that Allah is the master of worlds, or the master of the universe. It means that He is the master of everything that can be known. Everything in creation reflects the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">علیم</span>. It exists in a passive state, waiting for the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالم</span> to learn about it through reflection, so that he may recognize his Creator.</p>
<p>I therefore understand <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّ الْعَالَمِینَ</span> in a much wider sense than “master of this world” or “master of this universe.” He is the master of every species, every distinct group within creation, every category, every concept, every idea, every postulate, every form of physical matter, every form of chemical matter, and every spiritual entity. Everything that exists, whether physical or metaphysical, falls within the meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالَم</span>. All of it exists for one reason alone: so that its Creator may be recognized and understood.</p>
<h3 id="humility">Humility</h3>
<p>I find many explanations of the term <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّ الْعَالَمِینَ</span>. Here I draw out one interpretation, centered on a quality that is greatly lacking in society: humility. Allah points out that He created different cultures, different creations, different civilizations, and people of different languages. He says in the Quran:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Baqarah: 47</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَبَنِىٓ إِسْرَٓءِيلَ ٱذْكُرُوا۟ نِعْمَتِىَ ٱلَّتِىٓ أَنْعَمْتُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَأَنِّى فَضَّلْتُكُمْ عَلَى ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>I gave you preference over every other nation. The word used here for nations is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالمین</span>.</p>
<p>I understand from this that Allah is the Creator and Master of all nations, and that His creation must therefore be treated with care. Race and religion do not remove that duty. No one may forget that Allah is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّ الْعَالَمِینَ</span>.</p>
<p>This matters because harm done to His creation is never small. A heart may be broken by words. A person may be tortured through a temporary authority that Allah Himself granted. In such moments, the warning remains: I am <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّ الْعَالَمِینَ</span>.</p>
<p>Nor does this apply only to human beings. It includes animals, plants, and the earth itself. Before any cruelty is shown to any part of His creation, I must remember: He is their <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> too.</p>
<p>When a slave is wronged, the offense ultimately reaches the master. In earlier times, wars could begin because someone from one tribe unjustly harmed or killed the slave of another. The master treated that act as a personal insult, because the slave was under his ownership and responsibility. Even if the slave had done wrong and deserved correction, the complaint still belonged before his master.</p>
<p>So Allah says, “I am <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّ الْعَالَمِینَ</span>, so be careful in how you deal with My creation. It does not matter what group, religion, caste, belief system, or country they belong to. It does not matter what relationship they have with you on this earth.</p>
<p>“Before he is your son, he is My slave. Before she is your daughter, she is My slave. Before he is your father, and before she is your mother, they are My slaves. Before she is your daughter-in-law, she is My slave. Before he is your son-in-law, he is My slave. Before he is your employee, your servant, or your student, do not forget: he is My slave.</p>
<p>“I am present, and I am watching. If you treat them unjustly, if you hurt them, if you insult or degrade them, if you misuse your authority over them, you will answer to Me, because I am <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّ الْعَالَمِینَ</span>.”</p>
<p>SubhanAllah.</p>
<p>In one of the sermons of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَہْجُ الْبَلَاغَۃِ</span>, Maulana Ali explains what the title <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب العلمین</span> does: it brings all of us to the same level before the Creator. It marks all creation as His slaves.</p>
<p>No nation and no group is better than another because of race or color. If one person is better than another, then by whose standard is that superiority measured? Who alone can judge it? The Master.</p>
<p>This has become a serious disease in our community. Because we are Muslims, or because we are Sulemanis, we begin to think that we are better than the rest of the world. Yes, we are certainly superior in our knowledge of realities, AlHamdulillah. There is no doubt about that. But that knowledge gives us no right to treat other communities or other religions with spite. It gives us no license to look down on others.</p>
<p>In the world today, we suffer from the disease of superiority, from the feeling that we carry some special claim over others, whether we admit it or not. It may come through religion, race, wealth, social status, or any other reason. I must not forget that we all have the same <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>.</p>
<p>Rank can exist only in the eyes of the Master, and only according to the slave''s servitude. It does not exist in the eyes of the slave.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Hujurat: 13</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ أَكْرَمَكُمْ عِندَ اللَّهِ أَتْقَاكُمْ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>I stand before the same rule: the most honorable in the eyes of Allah is the one with the most piety. Nothing else gives that honor. Even the highest-ranking person is Allah''s slave, and even the lowest-ranking person is Allah''s slave.</p>
<p>This is what we recite in every <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أذان</span>, five times a day:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اشھد ان محمد عبدہ و رسولہ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>I testify that Muhammad <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> is his <strong>slave</strong> and his messenger. AllahuAkbar. The highest-ranking person who ever walked this earth is the one we testify to in every prayer as the slave of Allah. Then no one has the right to claim superiority over another. If Allah has blessed a person with gifts, and the greatest gift is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ایمان</span>, faith itself, then that gift should make him more humble, not arrogant.</p>
<p>The Quran gives the story of one who became arrogant because of his title and rank: the story of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ابلیس</span>.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَنَا۠ خَيْرٌۭ مِّنْهُ</span> . <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلَقْتَنِى مِن نَّارٍۢ وَخَلَقْتَهُۥ مِن طِينٍۢ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>I am better than him. I was created from fire; he was created from clay. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ابلیس</span> was an angel, from the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالَم</span> of angels. Adam was a human being, from the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالَم</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">انسان</span>. Both were slaves of Allah. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ابلیس</span> stood before Allah; He stood before the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّ الْعَالَمِینَ</span> and said:</p>
<p>I am better than him.</p>
<p>This story teaches a clear lesson. What did his Master do with that arrogance? Did it bring him nearer to Allah, or did it cast him out from the Divine Presence? The act for which <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ابلیس</span> was punished cannot be treated lightly when it appears in me. That would not befit Allah''s justice: to punish <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ابلیس</span> for arrogance and remove him from His Presence, then reward the same arrogance in me by bringing me nearer to Him. Impossible.</p>
<p>The matter must be understood plainly. When I judge another slave and decide that one slave is superior to another, what position have I taken for myself? I have placed myself in the position of my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>.</p>
<p>The Quran also tells of someone who did that. Someone said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَنَا رَبُّکُمُ الْاَعْلَی</span>. I am your greatest <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فرعون</span> said that. His end is known.</p>
<p>The reality is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فرعون</span> knew he was human. He knew he was not a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>. He knew his own weakness. Yet he still claimed a station he did not deserve. He even hated the fact that he needed to go to the toilet, because that was not fitting, in his mind, for a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span>.</p>
<p>So he gathered all his physicians and asked them how he could escape this problem. They told him to eat bananas. He ate bananas for forty days, and then he ended up with a much bigger problem.</p>
<p>I keep repeating and stressing this because it is no longer only a Fironic problem. It has become a global problem. Everyone wants to become <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>; everyone wants to become <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">معبود</span>. At some level, we have all become people who want to be worshiped. Yet we forget how Allah defines us in the Quran:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Mu''minun: 12</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَقَدْ خَلَقْنَا ٱلْإِنسَنَ مِن سُلَلَةٍۢ مِّن طِينٍۢ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Human beings come from a chain of dirt, from mud and water mixed together.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">As-Sajdah: 8</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ثُمَّ جَعَلَ نَسْلَهُۥ مِن سُلَلَةٍۢ مِّن مَّآءٍۢ مَّهِينٍۢ</span></p><p class="tr">I understand Allah telling these people: “We created your progeny from a lowly fluid. Who do you think you are? Get down off your high horse. Be a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رجل</span>.”</p></blockquote>
<p>A <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رجل</span> is the one who has <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ِرَجَلَ</span>. As it is said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ِرَجَلَ عن فَرِسِہ</span>. He is the one who comes down from his high horse and plants his feet on the ground. He does not see himself as raised above the earth. He returns to the level from which he came: dust. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرجل</span>.</p>
<p>Yet I see that all of us, at some level, are playing games. We are trying to be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>. Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> says no: “Be a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رجل</span>. Be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بنی آدم</span>. Be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آدمی</span>.” That is what it truly means to accept Allah as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّ الْعَالَمِینَ</span>. Humility, not arrogance, is the mark of a believer.</p>
<p>The acceptance of our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبودیۃ</span>, our slavery and servitude to Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, is a heavy burden that I, as a believer, carry on my back. When a burden rests on the back of a person climbing a mountain, he bends forward; he does not walk upright. The burden makes him bend. It makes him humble. This is why humility is the essence of spiritual progress. Maulana Ali says</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْ تَوَاضَعَ لِی اللَّہُ، رَفَعَ اللَّہُ لَہُ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Whoever humbles himself for Allah, Allah raises him and lifts him in rank. For this reason, the highest-ranking person ever to walk the face of the earth was my beloved Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُحَمَّدٌ مُصْطَفَی ﷺ</span>. The greatest man this world will ever know was also the humblest in character. Indeed, humility was his distinguishing mark. The earlier prophecies that foretold his coming identified him by the greatness of his humility. SubhanAllah. The greatest mark of his prophethood was his humility.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">یُریدُ أَنَّ یَبعَثُ أُمِیِّ مِنَ الأُمِّیِّن۔وأجعل السكينةَ لباسُهُ ، والبِرَّ شَعارُهُ ، والتقوى ضَميرَهُ ، والحِكمَةَ مَنطِقَهُ ، والصِّدقَ والوفاءَ طَبِيعَتُهُ ، والعَفوَ وَالمَعرُوفَ خُلقُهُ ، والَحقَّ شَريعَتَهُ ، والعَدلَ سيرَتَهُ ، والهَدِىُ إمامُهُ ، والإسلامَ مِلَّتُهُ ، وأحمد أِسمُهُ</span> .</p></blockquote>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَهدِي بِهِ بَعدَ الضَّلالَة ، وأعلَمُ بِهِ مِنَ الجَهالة ، وأَرفَعُ به بعد الخَمالَة ، وأعرِفُ به بعد النَّكِرَة ، وأكثَرُ بِهِ بعدَ القِلَّة ، وأغنِي به بعد العَيلَة ، وأَجمِعُ به بعد الفُرقَة</span></p></blockquote>
<p>I recognize here the prediction, in the Bible, of the arrival of Rasul Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>. Allah mentions these beautiful signs of his coming:</p>
<p>I am going to send you a man from your own nation. His garment will be a peaceful temperament; his behavior will be kind; his conscience will be pious; his reasoning will be wise. Honesty and truth will be his nature; forgiveness will fill his dealings; justice will mark his actions; Ahmed will be his name.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَهدِي بِهِ بَعدَ الضَّلالَة ، وأعلَمُ بِهِ مِنَ الجَهالة ، وأَرفَعُ به بعد الخَمالَة ، وأعرِفُ به بعد النَّكِرَة ، وأكثَرُ بِهِ بعدَ القِلَّة ، وأغنِي به بعد العَيلَة</span></p></blockquote>
<p>I will guide a nation through him and bring them out of error. I will give them knowledge through him and bring them out of ignorance. I will raise him after he had been belittled, make him known after he had been unknown, increase him after scarcity, and enrich him after poverty. Allah continues to mention these qualities of His beloved. Then, after all these beautiful traits and astonishing signs, He mentions the greatest sign of all.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَو یَمشِی عَلی القَصَبِ الیَابِس۔۔۔ لَم یُسمَع مِن تَحتِ قَدَمَیہِ مِن سَکِینَتِہِ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>He will be deeply humble. He will walk among people with such humility that, when he steps on dry leaves, the crackle beneath his feet will not be heard. SubhanAllah. This is the Prophet I follow. This is the example I follow.</p>
<p>The honor this slave of Allah received from his Master came through one quality: humility. That is what separates the true believer from the hypocrite.</p>
<p>For this reason, I see atheism as a blatant form of arrogance. The atheist rejects the most obvious and most basic fact of his own existence: he was <strong>created</strong>. Even he cannot truly say that he was not created. If I say that my father created me, and his father created him before him, that is what they call <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دور</span>; that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تسلسل</span>. The chain keeps going backward. Where does it stop? It stops at Adam. Then who created Adam? That is where the matter ends.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وکُلُّکُم مِن آدَم</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَ آدَم مِن تُرَاب</span></p></blockquote>
<p>All of you are from Adam, and Adam is from dust. Arrogance does not befit dirt, because dirt is constantly trampled underfoot.</p>
<p>Maulana Ali saw a man acting arrogantly, and he said to him, “Who do you think you are? Your beginning is a vile, dirty fluid. Your end is a disgusting, decaying, foul-smelling corpse. Between those two stages, you walk through life carrying two loathsome things inside your body: your urine and your feces. Your creation comes from the union of two ugly body parts. What right do you have to act arrogantly before Allah and his creation?”</p>
<p>This is the first matter: I must know my station in this world, and I must know who I am. I am Marboob. I am created. I will die. I will be raised up. This understanding, that I am Marboob, is the station of the slave. It is the knowledge that I am not Mabood. I am not the creator.</p>
<p>Wallahi, I give myself sincere advice: humility belongs in every walk of life. Arrogance must not take root. It is the greatest challenge of our time. Arrogance is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أذان</span>; it is the call of our age. It has become the trademark of worldly success: in schools, in colleges, at work, in families, and in communities. The whole claim is, “I am better than you.” Arrogance does not befit an obedient slave.</p>
<p>May Allah grant us all humility, guide us, and enable us to walk in the footsteps of our beloved prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>.</p>
<p>If I grasp even this much, if I truly understand the meaning of Rabb Alalameen, the world becomes a beautiful place.</p>
<p>If I understand that Allah is the Rabb Alalameen, then I understand that He is the One who guides everyone. He is the King. He is the Owner. He is the One who cares, the One who guides, and the One who gives gifts. Then everything in my life is between me and Him.</p>
<p>He will take care of me. I do not need to worry about anything. I do not need to judge another slave. I do not need to hurt another slave. I do not need to show my authority or power over another slave, even if I am hurt, even if I am wronged, especially if I have been wronged.</p>
<p>My Master will take care of me. My Master will come to my rescue. He will come to my defense on the Day of Judgment, and He will make that wrong right. He will stand behind me and support me. I do not need to worry about anything.</p>
<p>Whether I believe it or not, whether I want it or not, that Day is coming: the Day when the Rabb will gather everyone and exact justice.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Ali ''Imran: 9</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبَّنَآ إِنَّكَ جَامِعُ ٱلنَّاسِ لِيَوْمٍۢ لَّا رَيْبَ فِيهِ ۚ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Oh our Master. Indeed, You will gather all of us on a Day about which there is absolutely no doubt, because You have promised us that.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Ali ''Imran: 9</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ لَا يُخْلِفُ ٱلْمِيعَادَ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>There is no doubt. Allah never goes against His promise. I remind myself of this every time I recite the Fateha. There is a reason I pray fifty-one rakaats in one day, and all fifty-one times I recite Fateha: so that every time I say Rabb Alalameen, I remind myself of this hierarchy.</p>
<p>I understand who He is, and who I am in this relationship.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وآخر الدعوانا عن الحمد للہ رَبُّ الْعَالَمِینَ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>A man gave Rasul Allah a bottle of wine as a gift.</p>
<p>I think of the man who traveled a long distance to meet Rasul Allah (Salawaat). When he reached him, he brought the Messenger a bottle of wine as a gift. He said, “Ya RasulAllah, I got this special wine as a gift for you.”</p>
<p>I know how such a scene often unfolds. A very secularized person arrives at a Muslim home with a bottle of wine as a housewarming gift, and the reaction becomes outrage: “La hawla wala quwwata. How dare you? Do you not know I am a Muslim? Do you not know this is haraam? Get the hell out of my house. Never show me your face again.”</p>
<p>But this man brought the bottle to Rasul Allah, the most pious man I can imagine. He smiled. He smiled and said, “Brother, did you not hear Allah''s command that alcohol has been prohibited?”</p>
<p>He said, “No, Rasul Allah. I never heard that!”</p>
<p>He immediately gave the bottle to his slave and whispered something in his ear. Rasul Allah says, “What did you just say to him?”</p>
<p>The man said, “I told him to sell the bottle in the market.”</p>
<p>Rasul Allah smiled again and said, “The One who has prohibited the consumption of intoxicants has also forbidden its trade.”</p>
<p>So the man told the slave to pour it out.</p>
<p>This is the character of Rasul Allah. This is his humility. This is the Tarbiyyah, his rububiyyah. SubhanAllah. He did not say, “Do you not know who I am? How dare you bring this filth into my presence?” He never did that.</p>
<p>Hearts are never conquered with harshness. Hearts have always been conquered only with love and kindness. I need to study and learn what kind of Rabb, what kind of parent, and what kind of teacher our Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> was. SubhanAllah.</p>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'understanding rab al-alameen';
INSERT INTO chapter (slug, anchor_key, idx, title, html, word_count) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'the divine formula for recognizing allah', 12, 'The Divine Formula For Recognizing Allah', '', 5427);
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>I examined the term <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالَم</span>. It means anything and everything that the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالِم</span> can know, so that he may recognize the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">علیم</span>.</p>
<p>Knowledge needs an object before it can be gained. To learn human physiology, I need the human body. To learn mathematics, I need numbers. To study physics, I need physical things to exist. As human beings, I cannot know a subject unless there is an object for that knowledge. This <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالَم</span>, then, is the object through which I gain knowledge of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">علیم</span>.</p>
<p>I also examined the statement <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّ الْعَالَمِینَ</span>. It shows that Allah is not only the master of this universe, but the master of everything the human intellect can know. Everything in creation therefore reflects the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">علیم</span>. Whether it is physical, metaphysical, conceptual, or imaginable, whatever it may be, it reflects the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">علیم</span>. The fact that he created this infinite universe for us reflects not only his glory and magnificence, but also his generosity and the vastness of his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span>.</p>
<h3 id="divine-formula">Divine Formula</h3>
<p>To place this in perspective, two fundamental questions stand around the understanding of God. The first is: Who is God? The second is: What does he want from us? These are the two essential questions mankind has grappled with from the beginning of time: Who is God, and what does he want from us?</p>
<p>I take the first question, “Who is God?” to include further questions: What is He capable of? What are His attributes? What is His personality? What are His traits?</p>
<p>I take the second question, “What does He want from us?” to include questions such as: Why did He create this universe? What is our purpose on this earth? What does He expect from us?</p>
<p>Allah answers both questions through a very simple and straightforward formula in a verse of the Quran. He says:</p>
<p>Question</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/81/cd8a1733-7cb4-4e8a-a67a-cbd7e47ed8f0.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>In this verse, He mentions two broad categories: the horizons and the soul. The horizons are everything outside us: the physical universe. The soul is everything within us: our spiritual universe.</p>
<p>So how does Allah answer the question, “Who am I?”</p>
<p>Answers</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/81/b813ade8-e959-40c9-8d6a-4fc1a4737099.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>He answers it through the horizons: the physical universe and everything within it.</p>
<p>How does He answer the question, “What do I want from you?” He answers it through the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نفس</span>: through our own creation and existence, and through the creation of our spirit and soul.</p>
<p>This is what I call the genius of Allah''s design. I find it astonishing that He answers such a complex and seemingly impossible question with such a simple formula. In my personal view, this is my absolute proof of the existence of God and of the Quran as the message of Allah, because this kind of perfection in design cannot exist at the human level.</p>
<p>This is also Allah''s solution to the problem that theology faces in modern times: the question of God''s existence. The problem today is that I try to invent and apply human formulas to prove that Allah exists. Yet Allah has already given His own formula in the Quran. It is simple, and it leads to the conclusion because it is the only formula that can work.</p>
<p>If I try to use my own formulas, they fail, because this universe is His kingdom and His design. His rules govern it. The world gives many examples of this. An iPhone works according to Apple''s specifications; used outside those specifications, it does not work as intended. In the same way, Allah''s formula for recognizing Him is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آفاق</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أنفس</span>.</p>
<p>Each part of the formula has to be understood in its place.</p>
<h3 id="horizons">Horizons</h3>
<p>The first part of the formula is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آفاق</span>, the horizons. This part answers several questions:</p>
<ul>
<li>Who is Allah?</li>
<li>What is He capable of?</li>
<li>Why is He worthy of my worship, my willing slavery, and my servitude?</li>
<li>What makes Him so glorious, magnificent, and overwhelming in my sight that I would even consider Him my Master?</li>
</ul>
<p>Allah answers these questions through the signs in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آفاق</span>, His signs in the horizons, meaning His signs in this universe. This subject belongs to the conclusions of reflection. There, I examined the prerequisite for understanding this formula. That prerequisite is this:</p>
<ol>
<li value="1">I must have a sound intellect and a pure heart. The Quran calls this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لُب</span>, the core. It also describes such people as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اُولُو الْاَلْبَابِ</span>: people whose hearts are pure and whose intellects are sound. Only the two together allow me to see reality as it truly is.</li>
<li value="1">The second requirement in the Quran, and in this formula, is an uncontaminated and pure <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فطرۃ</span>. It grows from a pure heart and a sound intellect.</li>
</ol>
<p>The instrument by which I study the universe and learn from it is the intellect, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">العقل</span>. Its work is to define things and set their limits. The intellect is the container, and knowledge is the content placed inside it. What, then, is knowledge (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِلْم</span>)? In simple terms, knowledge is understanding and recognizing the true reality of things.</p>
<p>Concept and judgment</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/81/5f48abc4-58c6-4794-9d67-7a23731cf232.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>That understanding begins with a concept, called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تصور</span> in Arabic. First, the reality is formed in the mind. Then comes <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حکم</span>: a ruling or judgment about the truth of that reality. The Arabs say:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الحُکمُ علی شَیء فرحٌ عَن تَصَوُّرِہِ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Before I can judge a thing, I must define it and understand it in every dimension that can be grasped. Once I have formed a clear concept of that reality, I can judge whether it is true or false, good or bad, and so on.</p>
<p>Propositions</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/81/04fc84c0-c8c1-4ef9-a632-89eb104a4873.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>I ask first how I, as a human being, form a concept of things. Scholars say that propositions are of three kinds.</p>
<p>A proposition is a statement or assertion that expresses a judgment or an opinion. For example, I may state, &quot;fire burns,&quot; or &quot;fire is bright.&quot; That statement is then tested against observation, and from that observation I judge whether it is true or false. This is how a proposition works.</p>
<p>Scholars divide propositions into three kinds:</p>
<ul>
<li>Empirical propositions</li>
<li>Rational propositions</li>
<li>Philosophical or revelatory propositions</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="empirical-propositions">Empirical Propositions</h3>
<p>The first kind is empirical propositions. These rest on observation or experience through the five senses: sight, hearing, smell, taste, and touch. They are also called sensory propositions. They belong to the realm that can be witnessed, which the Quran calls <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالَمُ الشَّھَادَۃِ</span>.</p>
<p>So when I say that fire is hot, that it is bright, or that combustion takes place when it combines with oxygen, I am making empirical or sensory propositions. Their truth can be observed through the five senses.</p>
<h3 id="rational-propositions">Rational Propositions</h3>
<p>The second kind is the rational proposition. It is a logical proposition, such as: 3 + 2 equals 5; therefore, 5 - 3 must equal 2. This kind of proposition works through inference.</p>
<p>If I see smoke rising in the distance, I can infer that there is a fire there, even though I cannot see the fire itself. That inference rests on earlier empirical knowledge: when something burns, it gives off smoke. From that known fact, I can reason that a fire must be present.</p>
<p>Yet if smoke is the only evidence I consider, my inference may be wrong. The smoke might be coming from a large group of people having a cigar party. To preserve the rational proposition that there is a fire, I need more empirical evidence. If I walk toward the smoke and see the area becoming brighter and feel the air becoming warmer, then the likelihood of my rational proposition increases. I now have more sensory data, in the form of light and heat, supporting the claim that there is a fire.</p>
<p>In other words, the more empirical evidence I have, the stronger the rational proposition becomes.</p>
<p>That is how Sherlock Holmes works. He uses empirical data to deduce rational propositions.</p>
<h3 id="philosophical-revelatory-propositions">Philosophical / Revelatory Propositions</h3>
<p>I now turn to the third type of proposition: the philosophical or revelatory proposition. These are the Quranic propositions that speak about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالَمُ الْغَیْبِ</span>, the Unseen Realm. They speak of the existence of a Creator, angels, divine books, messengers, the Day of Judgment, heaven, and hell.</p>
<p>This is, in broad terms, what the Quran does. It gives philosophical or revelatory propositions. It sets before me the reality of the afterlife, the Day of Judgment, heaven, and hell. These are the realities in which I place my faith. My <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ایمان</span> rests on these revelatory propositions.</p>
<p>The question is how I arrive there. How can I believe in propositions that are wholly unseen and metaphysical? The clearest way to disclose the hidden realm to intelligent human beings is to fashion the physical universe on the same principles as the spiritual universe. Then everything in the physical universe points back to the workings of the spiritual universe.</p>
<p>An object that points toward a destination is called a sign. So Allah says:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Fussilat: 53</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَنُرِيهِمْ آيَتِنَا فِى ٱلأٓفَاقِ وَفِىٓ أَنفُسِهِمْ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>He will show them His signs, His evidence. Where? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِى ٱلأٓفَاقِ</span>, in the horizons. What appears in the horizons is empirical evidence. Everything outside me exists as observable data. The physical things around me are things I can see, hear, touch, taste, and smell. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَفِىٓ أَنفُسِهِمْ</span> And within their own selves. As rational human beings, I can deduce and infer rational propositions from empirical data. Therefore:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَنُرِيهِمْ آيَتِنَا فِى ٱلأٓفَاقِ وَفِىٓ أَنفُسِهِمْ</span> -</p></blockquote>
<p>He will show them the evidence of the unseen realm through the empirical and rational data of this universe. How much evidence?</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Fussilat: 53</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَتَّىٰ يَتَبَيَّنَ لَهُمْ أَنَّهُ ٱلْحَقُّ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Unlimited evidence, until it becomes absolutely clear to them that these revelatory propositions are true.</p>
<p>For example, the Quran tells me that Allah is the creator of this universe. The fact that the universe is a created thing is supported by rational and empirical observation. I know that there was a time in the past when this universe did not exist. I also know that a time will come in the future when it will return to non-existence. Existence and non-existence are both attributes of creation. Therefore, by reason, this universe is a creation.</p>
<p>Empirical data also confirm that every creation requires a creator. If this universe is a creation, it must have a creator. So when the Quran proposes:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللہ الذی خلق السماوت و الارض</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Allah is the one who created the heavens and the earth. That revelatory proposition is then backed and supported by rational and empirical evidence in the horizons, evidence that the human mind can understand and process.</p>
<p>Once I can believe this particular proposition rationally, it becomes easier to believe Allah when He says that He also created a spiritual universe. Logically, the One who has the power to create this physical universe must also have the power to create the spiritual universe.</p>
<p>When I understand this phenomenon, I can appreciate the motive of the Quran. This is why, throughout the Quran, Allah asks me to reflect on the creations of the heavens and the earth. He never asks me to reflect on Allah Himself, because that would be impossible without the empirical and rational data of this physical world. That is why He says:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Ali ''Imran: 190</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ فِى خَلْقِ ٱلسَّمَوَتِ وَٱلْأَرْضِ وَٱخْتِلَفِ ٱلَّيْلِ وَٱلنَّهَارِ لَآيَتٍۢ لِّأُو۟لِى ٱلْأَلْبَبِ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Without doubt, in the <strong>creation</strong> of the heavens and the earth, and in the alternation of night and day, there are signs for people of intellect.</p>
<p>The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لُب</span> carries a subtle meaning here. It points to the pith or seed, something hidden beneath the earth. In the same way, my intellect becomes the means by which I understand what is unseen through what is seen. From the plant before me, I can infer the hidden seed and the hidden roots. I do not need to uproot the plant and dig into the soil before I can reach the rational conclusion that roots must exist.</p>
<p>This is why Allah calls me to reflect on His creations: so that I may gather the empirical and rational evidence He has placed before my intellect. That evidence makes it easier to accept the truth disclosed by revelation.</p>
<p>This is why Maulana Ali taught:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا تَفَکَّرْ فِی اللہِ</span> ..... <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَاکِنْ تَفَکَّرْ فِی خَلْقِ اللَّہِ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Do not reflect upon Allah; instead, reflect upon the “creations” of Allah. In witnessing this universe lies the evidence for what exists beyond it. The more I reflect on this physical creation, the more it answers my questions about the spiritual creation, and especially about its creator.</p>
<p>So the Quran proposes:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">An-Naml: 9</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَنَا اللَّـهُ الْعَزِيزُ الْحَكِيمُ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Indeed I am Allah: the One whose power is complete and whose wisdom is perfect. The universe offers empirical and rational evidence for this claim. Its study shows that it could only have been created by One of immense power and immense wisdom, because every created thing exists with purpose. Human creation, therefore, must also have a purpose.</p>
<p>The Quran proposes:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلرَّحْمَنُ</span> ‎<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">۱</span>‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَّمَ ٱلْقُرْآنَ</span> ‎<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">۲</span>‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلَقَ ٱلْإِنسَنَ</span> ‎<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">۳</span>‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَّمَهُ ٱلْبَيَانَ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>The Intensely Merciful taught the Quran, created man, and taught him speech. A careful study of these three acts shows what they disclose:</p>
<ul>
<li>the teaching of the Quran,</li>
<li>the creation of mankind,</li>
<li>the granting of speech.</li>
</ul>
<p>Together, they clearly prove that this could only be the act of a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلرَّحْمَنُ</span>. The Quran proposes:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Baqarah: 255</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ لا إِلَهَ إِلا هُوَ الْحَيُّ الْقَيُّومُ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Allah is the One besides whom there is no deity worthy of worship. He is the Eternally Living and the ultimate maintainer of all things. Empirical and rational evidence shows that life is necessary for maintenance. All life on this earth is temporary; every created being will inevitably die. Whoever dies cannot remain the maintainer, sustainer, or provider for those who depend on him. Therefore, only the Ever-Living can be the ultimate maintainer and sustainer. This supports the next revelatory proposition:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Baqarah: 255</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لا تَأْخُذُهُ سِنَةٌ وَلا نَوْمٌ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>He is never overcome by drowsiness, and He never sleeps. I know that drowsiness weakens mental ability and focus, while sleep removes them altogether for a time. A person who is drowsy, unconscious, or asleep cannot be all-mighty, nor can he be the ultimate maintainer and sustainer. Constant maintenance requires complete attention and vigilance.</p>
<p>The remaining propositions unfold from this:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Baqarah: 255</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَهُ مَا فِي السَّمَاوَاتِ وَمَا فِي الأَرْضِ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Everything in the heavens and the earth belongs to Him.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Baqarah: 255</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْ ذَا الَّذِي يَشْفَعُ عِنْدَهُ إِلا بِإِذْنِهِ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Who can intercede on anyone''s behalf without His permission?</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Baqarah: 255</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَعْلَمُ مَا بَيْنَ أَيْدِيهِمْ وَمَا خَلْفَهُمْ وَلا يُحِيطُونَ بِشَيْءٍ مِنْ عِلْمِهِ إِلا بِمَا شَاءَ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>He knows fully what lies ahead and what has already come to pass. No one shares in that knowledge except those whom He chooses to grant a share of it.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَسِعَ كُرْسِيُّهُ السَّمَاوَاتِ وَالأَرْضَ وَلا يَئُودُهُ حِفْظُهُمَا</span></p></blockquote>
<p>His throne extends over the heavens and the earth. He maintains control over the universe, and that maintenance neither tires Him nor wearies Him.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Baqarah: 255</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَهُوَ الْعَلِيُّ الْعَظِيمُ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>I recognize Him as the All-Mighty and the All-Exalted.</p>
<p>When I study these revealed statements alongside the evidence that the universe itself gives through observation and reason, the first statement becomes easy to affirm: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ لا إِلَهَ إِلا هُوَ</span> Allah alone is the deity worthy of my worship. No created thing in this universe, living or lifeless, bears that exalted description. If I am to choose any being for worship, reason itself points me to Allah alone.</p>
<p>This is the first part of the formula: the design of the horizons, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آفاق</span>. The horizons give observable and rational evidence for the revealed propositions of the Quran. Through them, I am given a path toward knowing my Creator and recognizing the magnificent powers He possesses, because the effects of His divine attributes appear throughout His creation. This is the story of the Martian and the iPad from the discussion on Conclusions of Reflection.</p>
<h3 id="selfsoul">Self/Soul</h3>
<p>The second part of the formula is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أنفس</span>, the soul or the self. The soul answers a different question: what Allah wants from us. That becomes clear when I return to the moment when Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> decided to create the first soul, Adam AS. How did He describe His intention to the angels? He said:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Baqarah: 30</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَإِذْ قَالَ رَبُّكَ لِلْمَلَٓئِكَةِ إِنِّى جَاعِلٌۭ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ خَلِيفَةًۭ ۖ قَالُوٓا۟ أَتَجْعَلُ فِيهَا مَن يُفْسِدُ فِيهَا وَيَسْفِكُ ٱلدِّمَآءَ وَنَحْنُ نُسَبِّحُ بِحَمْدِكَ وَنُقَدِّسُ لَكَ ۖ قَالَ إِنِّىٓ أَعْلَمُ مَا لَا تَعْلَمُونَ</span></p><p class="tr">And when your Lord said to the angels: &quot;Verily, I am going to place generations after generations on earth.&quot; They said: &quot;Will You place therein those who will make mischief therein and shed blood, - while we glorify You with praises and thanks and sanctify You.&quot; He said: &quot;I know that which you do not know.&quot;</p></blockquote>
<p>I begin with His words to the angels: “I am going to create my caliph, my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلیفۃ</span>, on this earth.” I understand this word, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلیفۃ</span>, through its root. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلیفۃ</span> comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلَفَ</span>, which means “back” or “behind.” Its opposite is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَلَفَ</span>, which means “the front” or “what lies ahead.” So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلف</span> means the one who comes behind, the one who follows, or the one who succeeds another. In this sense, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلیفۃ</span> means “representative.” Allah said to the angels, “I am planning to create my representative on earth.”</p>
<p>Caliph</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/81/9ce920c6-9fdc-45d1-a2a2-85e8cc4c7774.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>A representative is someone chosen or appointed to act or speak on behalf of another. If I were to choose someone to represent me accurately, I would look for someone who shared my traits, or at least carried traits close enough to mine to make true representation possible. Without that likeness, the person could not represent me or convey my thoughts faithfully.</p>
<p>For this reason, in the story of Adam''s creation, Allah did not only create Adam AS. He also taught him all His traits.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Baqarah: 31</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَعَلَّمَ آدَمَ ٱلْأَسْمَآءَ كُلَّهَا ثُمَّ عَرَضَهُمْ عَلَى ٱلْمَلَٓئِكَةِ فَقَالَ أَنۢبِـُٔونِى بِأَسْمَآءِ هَٓؤُلَآءِ إِن كُنتُمْ صَدِقِينَ</span></p><p class="tr">And He taught Adam all the names , then He showed them to the angels and said, &quot;Tell Me the names of these if you are truthful.&quot;</p></blockquote>
<p>He taught Adam all the names. He taught Adam all the divine traits. In other words, He trained Adam AS in the divine traits that Allah Himself possessed. Then He presented Adam AS to the angels and challenged them through those traits. What did the angels say?</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Baqarah: 32</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَالُوا۟ سُبْحَنَكَ لَا عِلْمَ لَنَآ إِلَّا مَا عَلَّمْتَنَآ ۖ إِنَّكَ أَنتَ ٱلْعَلِيمُ ٱلْحَكِيمُ</span></p><p class="tr">The angels said: &quot;May You be glorified. We know nothing except what You have taught us. We have not received the training Adam AS received. Therefore, we admit that we cannot be Your true representatives on earth.&quot;</p></blockquote>
<p>That had been their original argument.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Baqarah: 30</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَإِذْ قَالَ رَبُّكَ لِلْمَلَٓئِكَةِ إِنِّى جَاعِلٌۭ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ خَلِيفَةًۭ ۖ قَالُوٓا۟ أَتَجْعَلُ فِيهَا مَن يُفْسِدُ فِيهَا وَيَسْفِكُ ٱلدِّمَآءَ وَنَحْنُ نُسَبِّحُ بِحَمْدِكَ وَنُقَدِّسُ لَكَ ۖ قَالَ إِنِّىٓ أَعْلَمُ مَا لَا تَعْلَمُونَ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>I understand the angels'' question as an objection to Adam''s fitness for this trust: will this representative spill blood and wreak havoc, while they themselves are constantly praising and glorifying Allah? They saw themselves as able to represent Him on earth. They could be His true <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلیفۃ</span>, and so the need for Adam was not yet clear to them.</p>
<p>But when Allah created Adam and trained him to reflect the divine attributes in his character, they all accepted what had been shown to them. In that sense, they validated and confirmed that Adam, and through him mankind, alone possesses the true capacity to represent Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> on the earth.</p>
<p>This is the meaning of Allah creating man in his own image. We possess the attributes that our creator and master possesses. I know that Allah sees. He sees our actions and our deeds:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Baqarah: 265</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَٱللَّهُ بِمَا تَعْمَلُونَ بَصِيرٌۭ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>I know that Allah hears:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Baqarah: 137</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَهُوَ ٱلسَّمِيعُ ٱلْعَلِيمُ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>For this reason, he gave us the ability to hear, so that we could represent his qualities accurately on this earth. More than that, he gave us hearing so that we could understand what it means when he says that he watches our actions and hears our cries and supplications.</p>
<p>I know that Allah acts, because we have the ability to act:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Ar-Rahman: 29</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كُلَّ يَوْمٍ هُوَ فِى شَأْنٍۢ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Every single day, he is busy in work. I know that Allah has the ability to love, because the Quran says that he loves everything good, clean, and pure:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Baqarah: 222</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ يُحِبُّ ٱلتَّوَّبِينَ وَيُحِبُّ ٱلْمُتَطَهِّرِينَ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>For this reason, he gave us the ability to love. That is why it belongs to our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فطرۃ</span>, our natural predisposition, to love what is clean and pure: because that is what our creator loves.</p>
<p>Similarly, I also know that Allah has the ability to hate and dislike.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Baqarah: 205</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَٱللَّهُ لَا يُحِبُّ ٱلْفَسَادَ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>I know that Allah hates and dislikes corruption. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا وَٱللَّهُ يُحِبُّ</span>. For that reason, he placed in our innate nature a dislike for filth and foulness. We recoil from what is dirty because corruption is hateful to him.</p>
<p>This is because we have been created in God''s image. We have been created with the same divine attributes that Allah possesses, in a created and limited way, so that we may serve as his true representatives, his true <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلیفۃ</span>, on this earth. Through these qualities, I come to know and understand my creator.</p>
<p>The way is simple: I look into my own self.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Adh-Dhariyat: 21</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَفِىٓ أَنفُسِكُمْ ۚ أَفَلَا تُبْصِرُونَ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>The verse calls the human being back to reflection on the self. What I seek for myself points toward what Allah wants from me. SubhanAllah. I want justice; Allah wants justice. I want to be loved; Allah wants me to love him. I like to be praised; Allah likes to be praised. I appreciate gratitude; Allah likes his creation to be grateful to him. I feel anger when people violate what belongs to me or ignore my rules; Allah is angered when his possessions and his rules are violated. I want others to give me due priority in their lives; Allah wants me to make him the priority in mine.</p>
<p>I can read the whole Quran from cover to cover and find no demand Allah makes of me that I would not, in some form, expect from someone else. If I want to know what Allah wants from me, I need only reflect on my own self. What do I want for myself from others? That reveals the matter.</p>
<p>I want kind treatment, and Allah wants kind treatment. I want to be treated as though I matter, and Allah wants me to treat Him as though He matters. I want credit for what I make, and Allah wants credit for what He made: for creating me, and for creating this universe for me. SubhanAllah!</p>
<p>The question of who created the universe returns first to the self. I was created in His image. I was created with abilities that reflect, in a creaturely way, abilities Allah possesses. He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خالق</span>. He is the Creator, and He gave me the ability to create and construct. He made me <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خالق</span>. Even a needle cannot exist in my world unless someone makes it. How, then, can a human being created in His image think that this universe came into existence without a creator?</p>
<p>I hear complaints about the state of this world. I hear the question of why I do not do something about it. I already did: I created the human being. I created the human being in My own image. I gave the human being My powers.</p>
<p>The answer, then, turns back upon the one who complains. Poverty and misery are not only matters to lament. I gave wealth so that it could be spent to make life better and easier for others. Injustice is not only a subject to speak about. I gave courage so that injustices could be corrected. Blind people are not only to be seen; a person can become their sight. Weak people are not only to be pitied; a person can become their strength.</p>
<p>I gave everything needed to repair the conditions of this world. Yet human beings often do not want to leave their comfort zones. They want only to complain and to have others solve their problems for them. That is not how it will work, because I created them as gods. I created them as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>. Each and every one of them has been created with the capabilities of an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span>, so that they can recognize and identify the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ال الہ</span>, and so that they can understand what their <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللہ</span> wants from them. SubhanAllah.</p>
<p>If the question is what I want from the human being, the answer lies within the self. By studying one''s own capabilities and desires, a person comes to know what I want.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْ عَرَفَ نَفْسَہُ فَقَدْ عَرَفَ رَبَّہُ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>The one who comes to know his own self comes to know his master, because the slave has been created in the image of his master.</p>
<h3 id="rights-of-allah">Rights Of Allah</h3>
<p>This opens a wide field of reflection. The matter is too large for me to cover within these sessions, and each soul must reflect on it and seek the answers that belong to its own condition. One example, however, belongs here: the question of rights, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حقوق</span>.</p>
<p>Human beings have rights. They are entitled to basic rights. From what has already been established, I must also recognize that Allah has rights. Just as there are human rights, there are divine rights. Human rights rest on divine rights; divine rights do not rest on human rights. If human rights carry weight, then divine rights carry far greater weight.</p>
<p>Among human rights is the right to be treated with honor. After all that a parent does for her child, and after all that a mother endures to bring her child into this world, she gains a right of gratitude over that child. After the hard work a sincere and dedicated teacher gives to her students, she gains a right of gratitude over them: a right to respect, dignity, and honor.</p>
<p>If created beings can understand and value the rights that human beings hold over one another, then what is the right of the One who created them all from nothing?</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَاللَّـهُ أَخْرَجَكُم مِّن بُطُونِ أُمَّهَاتِكُمْ لَا تَعْلَمُونَ شَيْئًا</span></p></blockquote>
<p>What is the right of Allah, who brought the human being out from the wombs of mothers in a state of complete ignorance?</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَجَعَلَ لَكُمُ السَّمْعَ وَالْأَبْصَارَ وَالْأَفْئِدَةَۙ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>What, then, is the right of Allah, who gave the human being hearing, sight, and a beating heart?</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Baqarah: 52</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>So that gratitude might arise.</p>
<p>When one human being provides for another, the least expected in return is gratitude. A fair exchange would be the same generosity, or something close to it. If that is not given, gratitude remains the minimum due. What, then, of Allah?</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا نَسْأَلُكَ رِزْقًاۖ نَّحْنُ نَرْزُقُكَۗوَ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>We do not ask you for provision; indeed, it is We who provide for you. What, then, is the right of gratitude owed to the One who takes nothing and gives everything?</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">An-Nahl: 53</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَا بِكُم مِّن نِّعْمَةٍ فَمِنَ اللَّـهِۖثُمَّ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Every good thing in a person''s possession has been given by Allah.</p>
<p>When human beings make something for a purpose, or invent something for a purpose, they claim the right to require that it be used properly. They expect it to serve the purpose for which it was made. If it is misused, they feel entitled to be upset. They feel entitled to demand justice, and even to bring the offender to court.</p>
<p>What, then, of the purpose for which Allah created?</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Adh-Dhariyat: 56</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَا خَلَقْتُ الْجِنَّ وَالْإِنسَ إِلَّا لِيَعْبُدُونِ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>I have not created jinn and mankind except to worship Me. How, then, am I fulfilling Allah''s right? Does He not have the right to be displeased and angry when I fail to fulfill those rights?</p>
<p>I must think deeply about the divine formula that Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> has given through this verse:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Fussilat: 53</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَنُرِيهِمْ آيَتِنَا فِى ٱلأٓفَاقِ وَفِىٓ أَنفُسِهِمْ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>He will show His signs in the horizons and within the self, so that the human being may come to recognize who Allah is and what He wants from him. May Allah bless us all with the wisdom to fulfill His rights and obligations, and enable us to treat Him with the honor and dignity He deserves.</p>
<p>If anyone in this world treated me the way I treat Allah, I would be deeply offended. I have been created in the image of the One who possesses the ultimate rights. That is all I truly am: an image, a reflection of a distorted version of the divine attributes. I am not real in myself. I am an illusion, like a reflection in a mirror, present for a moment and gone the next. But Allah is real. His rights are real. His obligations are real.</p>
<p>I will end with a beautiful story I heard recently.</p>
<p>A man once heard about a saint known for deep learning. He decided to travel to him and ask for advice. After a long journey, he finally reached the saint, greeted him, and said: I have heard that you are a great scholar and a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ولی</span>, a friend of Allah. Please give me some advice!</p>
<p>The saint said to him: My advice is this: “If you do not consider Allah your superior, then at least consider Him your equal.”</p>
<p>When the man heard this advice, he said to himself, “What kind of foolish advice is this? Who would be so foolish as not to consider Allah his equal? Is this what I traveled all this way for? I have wasted my whole journey visiting this so-called saint.”</p>
<p>He was thinking all this, and the saint was reading his thoughts.</p>
<p>The saint said to him: You must be tired after your long journey. Spend the night in this inn, and leave in the morning.</p>
<p>The man agreed and spent the night there.</p>
<p>The next morning, after he woke and offered his morning prayer, a servant came to his door carrying a large tray. On one side of the tray was a fresh breakfast for the man:</p>
<ul>
<li>4 eggs</li>
<li>4 pieces of buttered toast</li>
<li>2 pieces of chicken</li>
<li>2 glasses of orange juice</li>
</ul>
<p>On the other side was yesterday''s food: some stale bread and lentils.</p>
<p>Just as the man was about to eat, a beggar came to the door and called out to him. He said, “In the name of your creator I ask you for help.</p>
<p>“I have not eaten for two days! For the love of your master, please give me something to eat.” So the man gave the stale bread and lentils to the beggar, and he ate all the fresh food himself.</p>
<p>After breakfast, he prepared to leave and decided to visit the saint so he could bid him farewell. He found the saint and said his goodbyes. Just as he was about to go, the saint says to him: “My son! Remember my advice! If you do not consider Allah your superior, then at least consider Him your equal.”</p>
<p>The man lost his temper and yelled at the saint: “What kind of foolish advice is this? Who in this world would be so senseless as not to consider Allah his superior? Even a child knows something so simple, and you are giving me such absurd advice?”</p>
<p>The saint became furious at the man. He says: “Listen! This morning, when you sat down for breakfast, there was fresh food and stale food on your plate. A beggar came to you and asked you for food in the name of your Creator. He asked you for food for the love of your Master.</p>
<p>“Had you considered Allah superior to yourself, you would have eaten the stale bread and lentils and given the fresh food to the beggar. Had you considered Allah your equal, you would have shared your fresh food with him. You would have eaten two eggs and given two eggs to him. You would have eaten two pieces of toast and given two pieces to him. You would have enjoyed one glass of orange juice and let the beggar enjoy the other. O wretched creation! Forget superior; you did not even consider Allah your equal.”</p>
<p>I end with the same advice to myself: “If we do not consider Allah our superior, then, in view of His rights over us and all His favors upon us, let us at least consider Him our equal.”</p>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'the divine formula for recognizing allah';
INSERT INTO chapter (slug, anchor_key, idx, title, html, word_count) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'the horizons and the soul', 13, 'The Horizons And The Soul', '', 4902);
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>I have considered the Quranic formula that gives me an understanding of who our Creator is and what his intentions are. That formula is declared in the verse:</p>
<p>Formula</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/86/718f2946-a675-46a9-ae87-0aaea1e7e6f8.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Fussilat: 53</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَنُرِيهِمْ آيَتِنَا فِى ٱلأٓفَاقِ وَفِىٓ أَنفُسِهِمْ حَتَّىٰ يَتَبَيَّنَ لَهُمْ أَنَّهُ ٱلْحَقُّ ۗ أَوَلَمْ يَكْفِ بِرَبِّكَ أَنَّهُۥ عَلَىٰ كُلِّ شَىْءٍۢ شَهِيدٌ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>We will show them Our Signs in the universe, and in their own selves, until it becomes absolutely clear to them that this is the truth.</p>
<p>The signs in the horizons disclose the nature and power of Allah. The signs within our souls disclose the intentions of Allah, because Allah created us all in his own image, and he demands for himself only what we demand from others as rights.</p>
<p>In these analogies between the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظاہر</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">باطن</span> — the horizons and the soul — I find insight into the inner workings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دین</span>. Rasul Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said in a HADEES:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِنَّا اللہ اَسَّسَ دِینَہُ عَلیَ مِثاَلِ خَلقِہِ لِیَستَدِلَّ بِالدِّینِ عَلَی الدُّنیَا ؛ وَ الدُّنیَا عَلَی الدِّین</span></p></blockquote>
<p>I understand from this that Allah founded his religion on the pattern of creation. He made the world a proof for his religion, and he made his religion a proof for the world. The physical world and the spiritual world therefore rest on the same principles and patterns. Through the physical world, the human intellect can grasp signs that lead it toward the spiritual world. The horizons and the soul, or the self, are therefore keys by which I learn about Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>.</p>
<h3 id="horizon---refraction-of-attributes">Horizon - Refraction Of Attributes</h3>
<p>He scattered the knowledge of who he is across the horizons. Light gives a simple image of this meaning. Light is called white light, and in itself it appears colorless.</p>
<p>White Light</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/86/a4e369cc-73b8-4b20-ad70-e25c3f6e278e.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>When that light passes through a prism, something hidden becomes visible. A prism is a medium of glass. When white light strikes it, the light refracts; it bends. Because of that bending, a whole spectrum of colors appears, from violet to red and everything in between.</p>
<p>In the same way, the light of Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> strikes creation. The Quran tells us that Allah is the light of the heavens and the earth:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللہ نور السموت والارض</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Divine Light</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/86/c6127d05-d962-40d3-b020-fef39599aca6.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>I understand that when the pure light of Allah''s essence touches creation, and when it enters the physical realm, it refracts into the full spectrum of the divine attributes. This is why the universe contains so many different manifestations of Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>''s essence.</p>
<p>This <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالم</span> is the prism through which Allah''s various attributes become visible in creation. Through it, I come to know Him in many ways:</p>
<ul>
<li>In one instance, creation shows that Allah is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">کریم</span>, the generous.</li>
<li>In another, the universe shows that He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جبار</span> (the compeller).</li>
<li>In another, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالم</span> reveals that He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قھار</span> (the subduer).</li>
<li>In another, He appears as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عزیز</span> (the powerful).</li>
<li>In another, He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُزِل</span> (the humiliator).</li>
<li>In another, He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">معز</span> (the bestower of honor).</li>
</ul>
<p>So the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالم</span> serves as a colossal theater for the divine manifestations of Allah''s attributes, and through those manifestations I come to know Him.</p>
<p>I understand the arrangement in this way: the human intellect learns by defining and limiting things, just as the body works by breaking down food. For the intellect to digest knowledge, it must take in the whole, divide it into parts, and understand each part on its own. Only then can it gather those parts again and understand the whole.</p>
<p>The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالم</span> presents these parts through the divine attributes. I reflect on each attribute individually, then bring them together to form a fuller understanding of my creator. This is an essential theme of Quran. It calls me to reflect on the heavens and the earth, the horizons and the souls, because through that reflection I recognize my creator and His abilities. Then it turns my attention inward, toward the soul, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نفس</span>, so that I may understand the purpose of my own creation.</p>
<p>What, then, is the purpose of human creation?</p>
<h3 id="soul-purpose-of-creation">Soul: Purpose Of Creation</h3>
<p>Scholars state that human beings were created for three reasons. This distinction is beautiful because it allows for purposeful existence not only for Muslims, but also for people of other beliefs, and even for people without belief.</p>
<p>The purposes of creation are these:</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/86/b55d6057-fdd9-410d-84b2-9b42960d764a.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>The first, he says, is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عمارۃ</span>. Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> said:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Hud: 61</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُوَ أَنشَأَكُم مِّنَ ٱلْأَرْضِ وَٱسْتَعْمَرَكُمْ فِيهَا</span></p></blockquote>
<p>We brought you forth from the earth to cultivate it.</p>
<p>The second reason, he says, is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبادۃ</span>:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Adh-Dhariyat: 56</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَا خَلَقْتُ ٱلْجِنَّ وَٱلْإِنسَ إِلَّا لِيَعْبُدُونِ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>We only created the jinn, beings of the unseen realm, and human beings, beings of the visible realm, for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبادۃ</span>, for willing worship. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ابن عباس</span> said in a narration that the purpose behind the creation of human beings was also <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لیعرفون</span>: to know God. To know God is to love God. In the stages of love, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبودیۃ</span> is one of the levels of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">محبۃ</span>, of love. It is one of the higher levels of love. Therefore, He created the jinn and human beings to love Him.</p>
<p>The third and final reason for the creation of human beings is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلافۃ</span>:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-A''raf: 129</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَيَسْتَخْلِفَكُمْ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ فَيَنظُرَ كَيْفَ تَعْمَلُونَ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>We made you <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلفاء</span> because the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلیفۃ</span>, the caliph, is the one who stands in someone else''s place. He is the one who is left behind: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ُخَلَفَہ</span>. He remains in order to stand in the place of another. In other words, a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلیفۃ</span> is a steward. Allah created us as His stewards on this earth.</p>
<p>Why? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَيَنظُرَ كَيْفَ تَعْمَلُونَ</span>, so that He can witness what we do with all the outward and inward gifts He has blessed us with.</p>
<p>There are two types of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلافۃ</span>. We have been made a caliph over ourselves, and then over others. For the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلافۃ</span> of the self, Allah said:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Baqarah: 30</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَإِذْ قَالَ رَبُّكَ لِلْمَلَٓئِكَةِ إِنِّى جَاعِلٌۭ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ خَلِيفَةًۭ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Allah said to the angels, I have decided to create a caliph on the earth. In <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَۃُ الصَّوَادِ</span> He said to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">داود علیہ السلام</span> . He said:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Sad: 26</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَدَاوُۥدُ إِنَّا جَعَلْنَكَ خَلِيفَةًۭ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ فَٱحْكُم بَيْنَ ٱلنَّاسِ بِٱلْحَقِّ وَلَا تَتَّبِعِ ٱلْهَوَىٰ فَيُضِلَّكَ عَن سَبِيلِ ٱللَّهِ ۚ إِنَّ ٱلَّذِينَ يَضِلُّونَ عَن سَبِيلِ ٱللَّهِ لَهُمْ عَذَابٌۭ شَدِيدٌۢ بِمَا نَسُوا۟ يَوْمَ ٱلْحِسَابِ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Oh <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">داؤد</span>! We have made you a caliph in the earth. This is the second kind of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلافۃ</span> mentioned in the Quran.</p>
<p>There are two kinds of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلافۃ</span>:</p>
<ul>
<li>ruling oneself;</li>
<li>ruling others.</li>
</ul>
<p>But the condition for ruling others is that one must first rule oneself. A person must first take control and mastery of his own <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نفس</span>.</p>
<p>Here, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">داؤد</span> is told that he has been placed in the earth to rule over others. He was made ruler over <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَنِی اِسْرَائِیلَ</span>, so that:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَٱحْكُم بَيْنَ ٱلنَّاسِ بِٱلْحَقِّ</span>;</p></blockquote>
<p>he may judge among them with the truth. Then He says:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَا تَتَّبِعِ ٱلْهَوَىٰ</span>;</p></blockquote>
<p>do not follow your caprice. In other words, one must rule oneself before ruling others. If that does not happen,</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَيُضِلَّكَ عَن سَبِيلِ ٱللَّهِ</span>,</p></blockquote>
<p>the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ف</span> here is for causation. If a person follows caprice, whims, and appetites, they lead him away from the path of Allah.</p>
<p>So what are we as caliphs of Allah? How are we doing as Allah''s representatives on earth? Allah says:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Fussilat: 53</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَنُرِيهِمْ آيَتِنَا فِى ٱلأٓفَاقِ وَفِىٓ أَنفُسِهِمْ حَتَّىٰ يَتَبَيَّنَ لَهُمْ أَنَّهُ ٱلْحَقُّ ۗ أَوَلَمْ يَكْفِ بِرَبِّكَ أَنَّهُۥ عَلَىٰ كُلِّ شَىْءٍۢ شَهِيدٌ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>We will show them Our Signs in the horizons and within our own souls. Allah makes the truth of who we are known through the signs around us and within us. Among those signs are the conditions visible in the horizons today, which reveal how we are doing as caliphs of Allah, just as a physician reads the symptoms of an ill patient. Allah says in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَۃُ الرُّومِ</span></p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Ar-Rum: 41</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظَهَرَ ٱلْفَسَادُ فِى ٱلْبَرِّ وَٱلْبَحْرِ بِمَا كَسَبَتْ أَيْدِى ٱلنَّاسِ لِيُذِيقَهُم بَعْضَ ٱلَّذِى عَمِلُوا۟ لَعَلَّهُمْ يَرْجِعُونَ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Mischief, corruption, and pollution have appeared on land and sea because of what human hands have earned. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لِيُذِيقَهُم</span>, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لام</span> again gives the reason.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لِيُذِيقَهُم بَعْضَ ٱلَّذِى عَمِلُوا۟ لَعَلَّهُمْ يَرْجِعُونَ</span>: so that Allah may give them a taste of some of what they have done, so that they may turn back. Allah allows our harmful actions to bring harmful consequences so that we may return to Him.</p>
<p>I follow the scholars who say that ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظَهَرَ ٱلْفَسَادُ</span> is actually <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">النقص فِى ٱلْبَرِّ وَٱلْبَحْرِ</span>. It means that the blessings of the earth become fewer. The earth loses what once made it abundant.</p>
<p>It also means <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">القحط</span>: droughts, famines, and the harms that strike agriculture. The fruits of the land diminish. Lands that once grew crops stop producing. The benefits of the oceans also decrease: fish, whales, and the other creatures that live in the sea.</p>
<p>All of this happens because of what we earn through our sinfulness. The human being was created in the best of forms, in the very image of his creator, and with all his divine attributes. Yet when he turns away from his divinity and from his original state, he becomes capable of extreme corruption.</p>
<p>This was the very concern the angels expressed when Allah mentioned the creation of Adam to them. They said</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Baqarah: 30</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَالُوٓا۟ أَتَجْعَلُ فِيهَا مَن يُفْسِدُ فِيهَا وَيَسْفِكُ ٱلدِّمَآءَ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Are you seriously considering creating a species therein who will create <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فساد</span> and spill blood?</p>
<p>The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فساد</span> carries many meanings in English: rottenness, corruption, decay, decomposition, putridity, depravity, wickedness, viciousness, iniquity, immorality, weakness, perversion, wrongness, and incorrectness. All these meanings are gathered inside <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فساد</span>.</p>
<p>The English word pollution is defined as an undesirable condition of the natural environment, contaminated by harmful substances as a result of human activity.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِمَا كَسَبَتْ أَيْدِى ٱلنَّاسِ</span>: this is the result. Even in our own language, pollution names one of the dominant problems of the planet.</p>
<p>I am speaking of human responsibility for the present condition of the earth. The hardships that now surround us, including famine, extreme weather, earthquakes, and floods, are nothing other than <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِمَا كَسَبَتْ أَيْدِى ٱلنَّاسِ</span>: what our own hands have earned. Many countries have fallen into severe crisis because forests have been set on fire. Climate change raises temperatures, and higher temperatures bring more fires.</p>
<p>Another form of this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فساد</span> is in the air itself. In India, it has been estimated that half a million people die from air pollution. Fifteen thousand people died from terrorism, and the United States government moved heaven and earth to wage a war on terrorism. Yet half a million people suffer and die from air pollution. Where is the war against that?</p>
<p>Noise pollution is another part of the same corruption. In Pakistan and India, the levels are plain in the constant honking, yelling, and screaming, and in the noise of rickshaws and taxis with broken silencers. All of that is human-made <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فساد</span>.</p>
<p>I know today that the world is moving quickly toward desertification. The reason is the loss of what is called topsoil. Desertification means that land once covered with growth becomes desert. This has already happened across large stretches of fertile land, and it continues to spread.</p>
<p>I was reading a book that said there was a time when all of North Africa was so fertile that, before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بنو حِلاؤ</span> entered Africa, a squirrel could run from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سنجاب</span>, from Egypt all the way to Morocco, without its feet ever touching the ground. The land was that fertile. But when the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بنو حلاؤ</span> came, they were like locusts. They devoured the resources completely. Desertification is therefore another example of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِمَا كَسَبَتْ أَيْدِى ٱلنَّاسِ</span>: what our own hands have earned.</p>
<p>Another aspect of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فساد</span> appears in the words of Allah:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Ar-Rahman: 10</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَٱلْأَرْضَ وَضَعَهَا لِلْأَنَامِ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>The earth has been laid out for the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">انام</span>. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">انام</span> comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نوم</span>, which means sleep. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أنام</span> are creatures that sleep. This verse, then, concerns all sentient beings. It is not only about human beings; it includes the animal kingdom. Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span></p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-An''am: 38</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَا مِن دَآبَّةٍۢ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ وَلَا طَٓئِرٍۢ يَطِيرُ بِجَنَاحَيْهِ إِلَّآ أُمَمٌ أَمْثَالُكُم ۚ مَّا فَرَّطْنَا فِى ٱلْكِتَبِ مِن شَىْءٍۢ ۚ ثُمَّ إِلَىٰ رَبِّهِمْ يُحْشَرُونَ</span></p><p class="tr">There is not a moving creature on earth, nor a bird that flies with its two wings, but are communities like you. We have neglected nothing in the Book, then unto their Lord they shall be gathered.</p></blockquote>
<p>I understand the verse to mean that no creature walks upon the earth, and no bird flies with its two wings, except that they belong to communities like human communities. Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> tells me that the kingdoms of animals are communities like ours. Then He says that He has left nothing out of this Book:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-An''am: 38</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَّا فَرَّطْنَا فِى ٱلْكِتَبِ مِن شَىْءٍۢ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Even animals are acknowledged in the Quran. I know that some animals mate for life. I know that animals make immense journeys, pilgrimages, and migrations to distant places, much as human beings do.</p>
<p>Today, because of the sins of mankind, dogs are put on Prozac. In the United States, dogs are given Prozac, an antidepressant, because they are depressed in the homes of depressed and miserable people. Dogs are not naturally depressed animals. But so many houses in the United States are sick that the dogs and cats themselves become sick, because they are sentient beings. They are emotional beings. They feel and experience emotions as human beings do.</p>
<p>I see the problem in the endangered species brought about by human hands. Among the world''s critically endangered species are some of the most beautiful and majestic animals. These are the very animals after which the ancient Arabs named their children: lions and tigers, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أسد</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فھد</span> . They named their men after <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُفْتَرِسَات</span>, after animals that attack their prey. They named their women after the prey itself, such as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ماھا</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غزالۃ</span>. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فھد</span> goes after <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ماھا</span>, and she does not stand a chance.</p>
<p>What strikes me is that the species endangered today are not the cockroaches. They are not the rats. They are not the snakes. These creatures are flourishing. The animals and species now endangered are the ones that represent divine qualities within the human being, qualities that are disappearing. The eagle, the peregrine, the lion, the tiger, and the elephant all stand for great human qualities that are vanishing.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Adh-Dhariyat: 21</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَفِىٓ أَنفُسِكُمْ ۚ أَفَلَا تُبْصِرُونَ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Within the souls themselves, the call is to reflect. I see plenty of cockroaches in human disguise. I see plenty of rats and snakes in human disguise. But I no longer find the courage of lions or the majesty of eagles in human form. These qualities have become extinct among human beings.</p>
<p>I can go on and on about this, and I am sure much more could be added. But this is what we are doing as the caliphs and stewards of Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. We are destroying the very earth that sustains us and gives us our livelihood. Then we complain that we have problems and ask why Allah does not solve them all.</p>
<p>Why should he? He is not our janitor. He is not our servant. He is not our errand boy. He created us in his image so that we could create <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جنۃ</span> on earth, just as he created <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جنۃ</span> in heaven. Adam was not created in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جنۃ</span> for amusement. He was created in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جنۃ</span> so that he could see and experience the beauty of that place. Then, when he came to the earth, he could cultivate the land and create that same <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جنۃ</span> on earth.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Hud: 61</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَ أَنشَأَكُم مِّنَ ٱلْأَرْضِ وَٱسْتَعْمَرَكُمْ فِيهَا</span></p></blockquote>
<p>He brought you forth from the earth to cultivate it.</p>
<p>That cultivation meant creating the same <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جنۃ</span> on earth. What did we do instead? Why did this happen? How did things become so bad and slip so far out of hand? It happened because of our corruption: because of our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فساد</span>, our departure from the divine attributes and majestic qualities with which Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> created us. This is why he created the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالمین</span> for us: so that we could study the signs on the horizons, the heavens and the earth, and learn from his divine attributes the ideal condition of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نفس</span>, the ideal condition of the soul.</p>
<p>But we, as human beings, as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِیفَۃُ اللَّہِ</span>, have failed terribly. We have shown that the angels had real grounds for concern. They knew clearly what they were saying when they said:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Baqarah: 30</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَتَجْعَلُ فِيهَا مَن يُفْسِدُ فِيهَا وَيَسْفِكُ ٱلدِّمَآءَ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Do you wish to create someone who will sow corruption on earth and spill blood? They knew clearly what they were saying.</p>
<p>So what have we turned away from? We have turned away from the divine attributes, from the character of Allah.</p>
<h3 id="akhlaq">AKHLAQ</h3>
<p>Character</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/86/ce70c60c-96bc-46d3-9609-28618152e57f.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>I begin with the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أخلاق</span>, which comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُلق</span>. This matters because we are called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلق</span>.</p>
<p>The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلق</span> means creation. It is tied to the body, and the body is tied to the horizons. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُلق</span> means character, and character belongs to the soul. Both words are made from the same letters, in the same order. In the science of Arabic, that shared form shows a close relationship in meaning.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلق</span> belongs to the body, which is nourished by the physical world. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُلق</span> is character, which takes its nourishment from the spiritual world. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلق</span> represents the body''s existence; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُلق</span> represents the soul''s existence.</p>
<p>Both words begin with the letter <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خ</span> (kha), a sound of friction in Arabic. It is a sound of effort. The body requires effort to develop, and character requires effort as well. Character demands great effort.</p>
<p>So if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلق</span> is the essence of the body, then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُلق</span>, character, is the essence of the soul. When we die, the body returns to the earth with whatever it gained from the physical world. The soul returns to the spiritual world with the character it developed in this world. That is our true earning. That is our real acquisition: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُلق</span>, character.</p>
<p>A soul that returns to its Master without developed <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُلق</span> is an impoverished soul. It has failed the purpose of its <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلقۃ</span>; it has failed the purpose of its creation. When the body is weak, it cannot benefit from what this physical world offers.</p>
<p>I know that this world offers many temporary pleasures. Yet if the body is extremely weak, and a person cannot even rise from bed, those pleasures cannot be enjoyed. In the same way, a soul that is empty and bankrupt of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أخلاق</span> cannot benefit from the pleasures of the afterlife.</p>
<p>How is character developed? Through revelation. The Quran, stated simply, explains the divine attributes of Allah. It shows what His attributes are and why they belong to Him. He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عظیم</span> because he created this vast universe. He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قوی</span>, all-powerful, because he did not merely create the universe; he created it with one single command: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">کُن</span>, “Be,” and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فیکون</span>, it was.</p>
<p>In essence, the Quran describes Allah''s attributes and explains why he is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ال الہ</span>. Our hearts witness these divine attributes in the light of revelation, and that witnessing creates <strong>Belief</strong>. The more the heart understands revelation, the more belief grows. That belief then nourishes and develops character.</p>
<p>Character, then, is nothing other than absorbing these divine attributes into the heart through revelation, and then reflecting those same divine attributes toward the rest of Allah''s creation. That is character: the heart internalized, developed, and trained in the light of these divine attributes, then made visible in our dealings with Allah''s creation.</p>
<h3 id="interactions-in-islam">Interactions In Islam</h3>
<p>That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أخلاق</span>, and that is Islam. Islam is about interactions. It is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">معاملات</span>. There are three types of interactions in Islam:</p>
<ol>
<li value="1">Interaction with Allah: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُقُوقُ اللَّہِ</span></li>
<li value="1">Interaction with the self: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُقُوقُ النَّفْسِ</span></li>
<li value="1">Our interaction with the creation of Allah: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُقُوقُ الْعِبَادِ</span></li>
</ol>
<p>Huqooq Allah</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/86/d8bc7f29-420b-4720-94a6-45362c80bbb2.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>Allah''s right over us is that we recognize his attributes in the heavens and the earth, and that, through recognizing them, we come to love him for everything he has done for us. From that love, we willingly obey him and serve him as faithful slaves, as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبد</span>. We worship him while knowing that only he deserves worship. That is the right of the Creator over his creation.</p>
<p>What, then, is the right of the self?</p>
<p>Huqooq Nafs</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/86/097a919e-1633-48a4-9509-d2ca569658fb.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>The right of the self is that we reflect on the attributes of Allah in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عالم</span> through the teachings of the Quran. We learn the attributes, qualities, and character of our master, then internalize and cultivate those qualities in our own hearts so that our character, our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أخلاق</span>, is formed.</p>
<p>We were created in the image of our master. We were created to represent our master on this earth. That means we must adopt the qualities and characteristics of our master. That is what a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلیفۃ</span> is: a representative. Representation is not possible unless we become like the one we represent.</p>
<p>For instance, Allah is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">کریم</span>. He is generous. Therefore, my soul has a right over me: I must train it in generosity.</p>
<p>The greatest generosity I can show my own soul is to protect it from sin and set it to work in good deeds. Allah is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الحق</span>. He is truthful. Therefore, my soul has a right over me: I must train it in truthfulness.</p>
<p>With whom must I be truthful? With myself.</p>
<p>The greatest falsehood in which the self is trapped is the delusion of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دنیا</span>. I must therefore train myself to wake from my delusions about this world. I must wake to the reality that I am here only for a short time. Everything I labor for in a worldly sense will be left behind. When I die, I will be left with nothing in this world except the title of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">میت</span>. My loved ones, the people for whom I labor day and night, will be the first to place me in the grave and throw dirt over my face. I must not sacrifice my afterlife for this temporary life. That is truthfulness to the self.</p>
<p>The right of the self, then, is that I internalize the attributes of Allah and train myself in them, so that my character develops and I realize the potential with which I was created. I was created in the image of God, to act like God. And God is nothing but just.</p>
<p>This is why one of the divine names of Allah is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المؤمن</span>, which means, in essence, the giver of tranquility, the One who brings peace. It comes from the root <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">امن</span>, which means peace.</p>
<p>Huqooq Ibaad</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/86/951440aa-8e7c-46e7-a2f8-04c4aed6c38b.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>Finally, I come to the right of others, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُقُوقُ الْعِبَادِ</span>.</p>
<p>The right of Allah is to recognize his divine attributes in creation, and to submit to him willingly and lovingly in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبادۃ</span>. The right of the self is to take those divine attributes into myself, so they shape my character and bring it into harmony with the character of Allah. I am to become like the Creator.</p>
<p>Only after that character has been formed does the final right, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُقُوقُ الْعِبَادِ</span>, come into force. It means reflecting the divine attributes of Allah in my dealings with his creation.</p>
<p>I trained myself to be generous with my own self. Now that generosity must appear in my dealings with others. I trained myself to be truthful and sincere with my own self. Now that truthfulness, that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صدق</span>, that sincerity, must appear in my dealings with others. I recognized how Allah forgives me and how patient he is with me. Now I must reflect those attributes by being patient and forgiving toward others.</p>
<p>This is not an easy task. That is why the first two rights must be fulfilled before the third right can truly be fulfilled. A man once came to the Messenger of Allah and said:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">یَا رَسُولَ اللہِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَصِلُ اَخِیہِ وَ یَقطَعُنِی  وَ اُعطِیہِ وَ یَمنَعُنِی</span></p><p class="tr">“Oh messenger of Allah, I give to him, but he withholds from me. I come to him, but he cuts me off. What should I do?” the Prophet replied, “That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِلَۃُ الرَّحِمِ</span>. What you are doing is right. Continue meeting him, even though he cuts you off.”</p></blockquote>
<p>That is Islam. And Islam is hard work. I must not think of this religion as easy. I must become one who strives. I must honor myself, because Allah honored me when he said:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Isra: 70</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَقَدْ كَرَّمْنَا بَنِىٓ آدَمَ وَحَمَلْنَهُمْ فِى ٱلْبَرِّ وَٱلْبَحْرِ</span></p><p class="tr">“We have ennobled the children of Adam. We have made you leaders over the land and the oceans. We gave you a heart. We gave you an intellect. We nourish both with our revelation. We give you the ability to overcome the worst part of yourself. And even when you fall from time to time, We have given you the means to renew and restore your soul through <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">توبۃ</span>, through repentance.”</p></blockquote>
<p>What a gift from Allah. I must do this with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أخلاص</span>, with sincerity, only for the sake of Allah. But it is not easy.</p>
<p>Three Dimensions of Islam</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/86/0f665992-7d40-40ca-833b-ab1b223125cd.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>I understand these as the three dimensions of Islam: the rights of Allah, the rights of the self, and the rights of others. Recognizing the attributes of Allah in the heavens and the earth teaches the rights of Allah (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُقُوقُ اللَّہِ</span>). Taking those divine attributes into myself, so that they shape my character, is the right of the soul (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُقُوقُ النَّفْسِ</span>). Reflecting those divine attributes toward the creation of Allah is the right of others (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُقُوقُ الْعِبَاد</span>).</p>
<p>The last of these is the hardest. It cannot be fulfilled unless the soul has first been lit by the character of Allah. This is why Rasul Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said in a hadees:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أتخذو من أخلاق اللہ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Adopt the character and the qualities of Allah!</p>
<p>I find a narration in which Rasul Allah once saw a man speaking to Maulana Ali with great disrespect. Maulana Ali answered him with kindness. The man still continued in his harsh and disrespectful manner, yet Maulana Ali kept answering him with kindness again and again.</p>
<p>When the man left, the prophet asked him, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">یا علی</span>! I saw this man speak to you with such disrespect and treat you so badly again and again. Yet each time, you showed him kindness and respect in return. Why was that?</p>
<p>Maulana Ali replied: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">یَا رَسُولَ اللہِ</span> If this man is not willing to give up his wretched and evil nature in order to deal with me, then why should I give up my good character in order to deal with him?</p>
<p>SubhanAllah. What a beautiful statement. If this man is not willing to give up his wretched and evil nature in order to deal with me, then why should I give up my good character in order to deal with him? In other words, if this man insists on holding to something ugly, why should I let go of something beautiful that I possess? That is the beauty of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أخلاق</span>.</p>
<p>I therefore follow the example of Rasul Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>, whom the Quran describes as reaching the pinnacle of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اخلاق</span>. Through his character, and through nothing but his character, he purified the most barbaric and vile people that had ever existed.</p>
<p>The history of Islam shows what kind of people he was sent to reform, and what mentality ruled them. They were worse than animals. Not a single virtue lived among them. They ate the flesh of elephants and crocodiles. When they ate a goat, they even boiled its hide and ate that too. They burned their enemies alive. They tore off their skin and wore it like a scarf. They cracked open a man''s skull and used it as a cup for wine. Their women dyed their clothes in the blood of their enemies.</p>
<p>They did not merely kill the enemy. They tied his camel to his grave and left it there to starve to death. Sons married their mothers. Daughters were buried alive. They used the children of their enemies for target practice. Even their prayers were barbaric.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">یا ربِّ فَلا تَجعَل حَیَاتی مَذِلَّۃُ ولا مَوتَتِی بَینَ النِّسآءِ نوائِحِ</span></p></blockquote>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ولاکن قَتیلاً تَمزَقُ الطَّیرِ حَولَہُ</span> . <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">و تَشرُبُ غِربانُ الدَّم من جوارح</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Oh Lord, do not disgrace me in my life by letting me die on a bed between grieving women. Give me a death where birds tear out my flesh and crows drink the fresh blood gushing from my limbs.</p>
<p>I see the state of the Arabs when Rasul Allah came among them. This was the people he was sent to reform. He changed them into <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَۃُ اللّٰہِ عَلَیْہِ</span>. Their names are not mentioned without saying <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَۃُ اللّٰہِ عَلَیْہِ</span>- May the mercy of Allah be with them.</p>
<p>That is why, on the day of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غدیر</span>, Rasul Allah asked:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلَسْتُ اَوْلَاکُمْ مِنْ اَنْفُسِکُمْ؟</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Am I not more beloved to you than your own souls?</p>
<p>They replied:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بلیٰ یَا رَسُولَ اللہِ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Indeed you are, O prophet of Allah.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كُنَّا الْحَيَوَانَاتِ</span>! <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كُنتُ جعلنا البشر</span></p></blockquote>
<p>We were animals! You made us human beings.</p>
<p>How did he do this? Through his flawless <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اخلاق</span>. He took the divine attributes of his creator into himself, formed through them a stainless character, and then reflected those same attributes back into creation with such force that the creator himself acknowledged:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Qalam: 4</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَإِنَّكَ لَعَلَىٰ خُلُقٍ عَظِيمٍۢ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Indeed, beyond all doubt, You have reached the highest and most exalted level of character.</p>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'the horizons and the soul';
INSERT INTO chapter (slug, anchor_key, idx, title, html, word_count) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'the womb of afterlife', 14, 'The Womb Of Afterlife', '', 3738);
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ</span>. ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ صَلِّ عَلَى سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ وَعَلَى آلِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ كَمَا صَلَّيْتَ وَسَلَّمْتَ وَبَارَكْتَ وَرَحِمْتَ وَتَرَحَّمْتَ عَلَى إِبْرَاهِيمَ وَعَلَى آلِ إِبْرَاهِيمَ فِي الْعَالَمِينَ إِنَّكَ حَمِيدٌ مَجِيدٌ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ اشْرَحْ لِي صَدْرِي وَيَسِّرْ لِي أَمْرِي وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِنْ لِسَانِي يَفْقَهُوا قَوْلِي</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللَّهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span>.</p>
<p>Now that we understand these meanings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span>, now that we understand these terms, I want to share with you a beautiful spiritual representation that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> has created for us through the example of the womb, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span>. To quickly reiterate these terms, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> is a combination of six things in classical Arabic. The definition is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّهُ الْمَلِكُ وَالسَّيِّدُ الْمُرَبِّي الْمُرْشِدُ وَالْمُنْعِمُ الْقَيِّمُ</span>. So a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> is an owner and he is the one with complete authority over its property. He is the one who nurtures his creation, guides it, benefits it by bestowing generous and undeserving gifts upon it, and he is the one who maintains it. So this is who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is, right? That''s His primary role with His creation, of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span>. And all His other attributes, they basically center around and they stem from His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّ الْبَرِيَّةِ</span>. So if someone asks, who is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>? The answer to that question is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span>. Now, as for His nature, His nature is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيم</span>. So the manner in which He executes, or the way He carries out the functions of His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّ الْبَيَّة</span>, it is through intense and consistent love, compassion and mercy.</p>
<p>So now that we understand these concepts, I want to share this example that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> has created for us through the womb. You know, by studying the womb, we can actually understand a lot about our master''s plan for us. Just by studying the relationship of the womb with its fetus. Because this womb, it is the first <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبَّ</span> of the fetus. And it demonstrates the exact same principles of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَانِيَّة</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيمِيَّة</span> in its interaction with it. This is why there is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيثٌ</span> in which <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span>, he said, this world is a womb for the afterlife. In other words, the story of our lives in this world, it tells a tale about the kind of life that we''re going to have in the afterlife, because this world, this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> that we''re living in, is the womb, or acts as the womb, and we are the fetus in that womb. And just as the brief and temporary residence in a womb is actually preparation for this worldly life, our stay in this temporal world is nothing more than a short duration of preparation for the afterlife. Now, if you look at the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span>, the womb, you''ll see all six principles of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبِّيَ</span> at work. It has ownership and it has authority over the embryo, right?</p>
<p>It nurtures the embryo, it helps the embryo grow and develop into a fetus. So the womb is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَٰلِكِ</span>, it is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّد</span> and it is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرَبِّي</span>. Then we see that the womb, it bestows these priceless gifts upon the fetus, to the development of its eyes, its nose, its ears, hands, feet, the heart, the intellect and all of its internal and external organs. So we can see that the womb is also the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُنْعِم</span>, the bestower of gifts. We also know that the womb, it manages the entire development process of the embryo from its conception all the way until it is fully developed. So the womb is also <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَيِّم</span>. So we can see that the womb, it actually acts as the very first physical <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبَّ</span> that we come in contact with. And this very first act, it actually demonstrates the design of how <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> created this mechanism. It demonstrates the act of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span> by carrying out all six functions of the definition of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكٌ، سَيِّدٌ، مُرَبٍّ، مُرْشِدٌ، مُهَيْمِنٌ، قَيِّمٌ</span>. Now, what happens once the baby is delivered into this world?</p>
<p>Who is the baby handed over to? It is handed over to its loving mother, who becomes the next immediate <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span> of that baby. Not the father, the mother. It''s the mother that becomes the next <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span>. Why? Because she feeds it. She takes care of it. There is this transference of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبِّيَ</span> that takes place. The mother now takes ownership of the baby. The mother now takes authority over it. The mother assumes the responsibility of nurturing it, helping it grow and develop, teaching it, guiding it, setting it to purpose, showering all these gifts and blessings upon it. She becomes the one who maintains that baby''s sustenance in all its various forms, physical, emotional, psychological.</p>
<p>So we can see that our mothers become the next immediate <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>. Right after the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span> of the womb, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَٰن</span>, it''s completed. Now father is also a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>, but his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span> gets executed through the mother. You see, the father always uses the mother as a means for her. He facilitates her functions of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span>. That''s the role of the father, but the primary caretaker of the child is always the mother.</p>
<p>So now let''s study this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيثٌ</span> to better understand the underlying principles that are at work. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span> , he said, . This temporal world is the womb of the afterlife. So let''s examine the fetus and the womb of its mother. Is it the womb actually doing all the work? Or is it the mother doing all the work behind the scenes and letting this womb act as an intermediary between herself and her child?</p>
<p>Is it the womb or the mother? We know it''s always the mother, right? The mother is the one taking care of the baby''s needs. She is the one eating all the right things. She is making sure she doesn''t bump into things so that her baby doesn''t get hurt. She''s the one getting all the ultrasounds done, taking all those painful but necessary blood tests, all those injections. So she is the one actually suffering for the baby. Baby is living comfortably in the womb, but it''s the mother doing all the work, right? The mother is the one doing all the hard work all along. But from the perspective of the fetus, who is doing all the work? The womb, not the mother. You see, the fetus has absolutely no knowledge of its mother.</p>
<p>All it knows, all it recognizes, all it understands is the womb. If I ask the fetus, who do you think is feeding you? Who do you think is nurturing you, taking care of you, sustaining you? Who is doing all the work that is necessary for your health and development? What will the fetus reply? The womb. The fetus has absolutely no knowledge of the existence of its mother. You see? What mother? Where''s the mother? I don''t see a mother. All I see is the womb.</p>
<p>There is no mother. It''s just cause and effect. When I tell this fetus, no, no, it''s not the womb, it''s the mother. She is the one feeding you. She''s the one taking care of you. This womb is only an intermediary. It''s a means. And the fetus says, you know, what are you talking about? It''s the umbilical cord that''s feeding me. That''s what gets me this blood that is carrying all the nutrients. I get fed by that, the umbilical cord. The umbilical cord is responsible for my development.</p>
<p>There is no mother. And so I explain, no, no, there is a mother. She is all around you. You are within the love, the care, the concern, and the mercy of your mother. This womb wouldn''t exist if she didn''t exist. And this womb that you''re in, it''s actually temporary. This is not the world you were created for. Believe me, this world is filthy, it is dark, it is immensely constricted. There is an amazing and a beautiful world that exists outside this womb that has been created just for you. And that world is so magnificent that you cannot even imagine its glory right now within this womb. Trust me, this womb is filth compared to that world. So if you think this womb is kind and merciful to you, let me tell you, it doesn''t even compare to the love and the mercy of your mother.</p>
<p>Because your mother is going to take care of you in the next world. She will shower you with so much love that you have absolutely no idea. You cannot even imagine that while you''re in this womb. But the fetus says, what are you talking about? I am really, really happy here. Everything I need is being taken care of for me right here. Why do I need to have another world? I don''t know why you keep talking to me about this mother and another world and an afterlife. There is no mother. There is no world outside this womb. You''re crazy. Okay, show me.</p>
<p>Show me this world that you keep talking about. Show it to me right now. I want to see it. Prove to me that there is another world. Prove to me that there is actually a mother. I''m not gonna believe you unless and until I see that world and until I see that mother. And I say, I can''t show it to you right now, but I promise when you transition out of this world, this womb, you''ll see both of them. Oh, so now I''m supposed to trust you. I''m supposed to trust your belief system, your intellect? What if you''re wrong? Now this is us with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> in this world. There are so many of us denying the existence of the mother, living in the comforts of this womb, of this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>.</p>
<p>So now, I begin this logical path of explanation and I say, look, you have been given these eyes, but there is so little to see here. You''ve been given these ears, but there''s so little to hear. You''ve been given taste buds, but there is so little to taste here. You have a nose with this magnificent ability to smell, but there''s not a single fragrance here. You have arms, you have feet, you have all these amazing gifts, but you cannot benefit from them within this womb. So think about it. These are of no use to you inside this womb, which means there must be another world that is out there, a world where you will be able to use and benefit from these gifts. Think about it. Your only purpose in this womb, in this world, is to draw your sustenance from your mother and develop these organs. In other words, you''re here so that you can prepare them for the next world where you will actually be using them, where you will actually benefit from them. But does the fetus understand that?</p>
<p>No. It doesn''t. To the fetus, the concept of another world and the existence of a mother is just ridiculous. Just as the concept of a God and an afterlife is absurd to so many of us. So now what happens? This fetus continues to live in its ignorance and its arrogance until the nine months complete. Now what happens? Contractions begin. The fetus starts panicking. What''s going on here? What''s happening? This is what was promised to you.</p>
<p>I told you this day would come. I told you this world is temporary. So now with those contractions, this beautiful world of the fetus, this magnificent world it was living happily and comfortably in, it starts crumbling around it. It starts falling apart all around it. Now, isn''t that exactly the description of the Day of Judgment? When the earth is shaken up with its earthquakes. And the earth discharges its burden. And man says, what is wrong with this world? What is wrong with this earth? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْمَئِذٍ تُحَدِّثُ أَخْبَارَهَا بِأَنَّ رَبَّكَ أَوْحَىٰ لَهَا</span>. On this day, it will report the news of which your master has commanded it. ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْمَئِذٍۢ يَصْدُرُ ٱلنَّاسُ أَشْتَاتًۭا لِّيُرَوْا۟ أَعْمَلَهُمْ</span> ‎.</p>
<p>On this day, people will separate out, and they will be shown the results of their deeds. ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَمَن يَعْمَلْ مِثْقَالَ ذَرَّةٍ خَيْرًۭا يَرَهُۥ</span> ‎. So, whoever did an atom''s weight of good will see it. ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَن يَعْمَلْ مِثْقَالَ ذَرَّةٍۢ شَرًّۭا يَرَهُۥ</span> ‎. And whoever did an atom''s weight of evil will see it. So the world of the fetus now comes to an end, and it is forced out of it, it is pushed out of it against its will. You see, does the fetus have a choice in whether it wants to stay in the womb or not? No. Whether it wants to or not, whether it''s ready or not, once its time is up, that''s it. It is pushed out into the next world. It dies in the womb in order to be born again in the next world, you see. Its death in the womb results in its creation in this world. That''s what the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">انْتَقَلَ</span> means. It comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَقَلَ</span>, which means a transference. It also means a copy or a replica. That''s what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَقَلَ</span> means, a copy or a replica.</p>
<p>Why? Because the next world is going to be a copy, it is going to be a replica of the first. If you did good in the world, in this world, you will find good in the next world. If you did evil in this world, you will find evil in the next world. It''s going to be a replica, it''s going to be a copy. If the fetus has not properly developed its eyes, its ears, its brain, its limbs in the womb of its mother, it is going to suffer the consequences of that in the afterlife of this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. So now this temporal world of the fetus is destroyed and it comes into this huge world that he was informed about, this world that was promised to him in the womb. He is now forced into this world, this afterlife that he chose not to believe in. And then, as soon as it''s out of the womb, who is the baby handed to? The mother.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. The mother that he said did not exist, the mother he denied his entire life in the womb. So you can see that the fetus goes through the exact same confusion of the seen and the unseen while it''s in the womb of its mother. That''s so many of us go through in this life. The fetus that accepts the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span> of its mother through the womb comes out fully developed and is now capable of enjoying all the benefits that the afterlife has to offer, which is this physical world for the fetus. But the fetus that rejected the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span> of the womb, it comes out underdeveloped, blind, deaf, mute, physically challenged, mentally challenged, incapable of benefiting from the joys and pleasures that this world has to offer. We are all going through this exact same process in life. Why? Because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الدُّنْيَا رَحِمُ الآخِرَةِ</span>, this world is the womb for the afterlife. And here we have our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> telling us, look, follow the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span>, keep yourself clean, keep yourself pure, do your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span>, complete your fast, pay your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زَكَاة</span>, fulfill your obligations of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَجّ</span>. And what do we say?</p>
<p>Why? How is this going to help me? It''s not going to help you here. That''s not the purpose. The obligations of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span> is not meant for this world. It''s the sustenance for the development of our spiritual limbs. That''s what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span> is. It''s a means like the womb. It''s a tool. It''s a mechanism, a means, an intermediary for the development of our spiritual limbs, for the development of our spiritual organs that are going to help us live a good life. After this world is over, that is where we will benefit from these fully developed spiritual organs. In the afterlife, all we''re doing here in this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> is developing them in order to use them in the next world, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَنَّة</span>.</p>
<p>But how do you develop them? Through the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span>. Because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَنَّة</span> is described as a world that is incredibly beautiful, a world that is governed by a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> who is intensely merciful, who is constantly merciful. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span> is the umbilical cord providing the sustenance for the development of our spiritual existence, of our spiritual being. Now whether we believe in it or not, whether we want to or not, whether we execute it or not, that next world is inevitable. It''s coming. Right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْجَنَّةُ حَقٌّ، وَالنَّارُ حَقٌّ، وَالْمَوْتُ حَقٌّ، وَالْبَرْزَخُ حَقٌّ، وَالْمِيزَانُ حَقٌّ، وَالصِّرَاطُ حَقٌّ</span>. Paradise is a reality. Hellfire is a reality. Death is a reality.</p>
<p>Rising from graves is a reality. The scales in which our actions will be weighed, that is a reality. The straight path is a reality. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَأَنَّ ٱلسَّاعَةَ آتِيَةٌۭ لَّا رَيْبَ فِيهَا</span>. And that appointed moment, it is coming. There is absolutely no doubt about it. That is the world in which justice will be exacted. Every incident for which you said, where is justice? On that day we will see justice. The innocent people that suffered momentarily in this short span of life, this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>, this world, their momentary suffering will be balanced out with never-ending gifts and pleasures in the afterlife. And the one who caused those sufferings for fleeting gains and pleasures, their pleasures will be counterbalanced with grievous pain and everlasting torment. But that day of judgment is coming. These are all descriptions of the day of judgment, when this universe will be destroyed, this world that we are so comfortable in, it''s going to end. That day is coming when souls will be paired together, when you will finally meet those loved ones</p>
<p>Who left you behind in this world? And when that little girl who was buried alive will be asked, for what sin were you killed? This is the day where <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> will exact justice. This is the day of justice, not this world, not this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> that we''re living in. That day all the deeds will open up. And when the book of deeds will be made public, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَإِذَا ٱلسَّمَآءُ كُشِطَتْ</span>. And when the sky will be stripped away, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَإِذَا ٱلْجَحِيمُ سُعِّرَتْ</span>. And when that hellfire is set ablaze, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَإِذَا ٱلْجَنَّةُ أُزْلِفَتْ</span>. And when the paradise is brought near, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلِمَتْ نَفْسٌۭ مَّآ أَحْضَرَتْ</span> ‎. Every soul will know what it has brought with it. All good will be rewarded on that day and all bad will be punished on that day. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ أَكْبَرُ</span>.</p>
<p>And the fact of the matter is that we have such a benevolent, such a merciful, such a loving master who is just dying to reward us for the smallest good deed that we do in this world. You know, it has been narrated by the Prophet that there will be a man on the Day of Judgment and all this man ever did was lend money to others. He was a money lender. And usually such people are black hearted people because of interest, and they use that as a mechanism to gain worldly profit. But when this man sent out his servants to collect the money that people owed him, if the person was having a hard time returning back that money, if that''s what the servant would return and tell him, he would say, forget about it. So on the Day of Judgment, this man comes before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and he does not have any good deeds to his name, except that he used to do that.</p>
<p>And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, He displays this man to everyone and He says, will I leave him to be more generous than me? Will I leave this man to be more generous than me? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. He used to forgive people. Do you think I am not going to forgive him today? This is the master, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span> that we fail in our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَبُو ضِيَاء</span> towards. On this day, every single good deed will be balanced with a reward from an immensely beneficent, from an incredibly loving and compassionate master. All our delusions of grandeur in this world, they will be gone. On that day, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُفْلِسٌ</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَنِيٌّ</span>, the destitute and the affluent, they will be separated.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span> once asked his companions. He asked, you know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنِ الْمُفْلِسُ</span>? Who is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُفْلِسٌ</span>? Who is the destitute? They said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المُفْلِسُ مَنْ لَا دِينَارَ وَلَا دِرْهَمَ عِنْدَنَا يَا رَسُولَ اللهِ</span>. The destitute is the one who does not have any <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">درهم</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينَارٌ</span> to his name. And the Prophet said, no. He said, the greatest destitute of my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمَّة</span> on the Day of Judgment, the greatest destitute of my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمَّة</span> on the Day of Judgment, will be the one who comes with all of his prayers and all of his fasting and all of his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زَكَاة</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَيَأْتِي قَطْ شَتَمَ هَذَا وَقَذَفَ هَذَا وَأَكَلَ مَالَ هَذَا وَسَفَكَ دَمَ هَذَا وَضَرَبَ هَذَا</span>, but in addition to all of these good deeds, he cheated this one, he lied to this one, he usurped the wealth of this one, he spilled the blood of this one, he struck this one. And so his good deeds will be taken from his account and put into the account of this one and this one and this one. And when his good deeds run out, before justice is fully served, the sins and faults of the remaining victims, they will be taken from their accounts and added to his accounts. And so despite living an entire life full of worship, full of prayers, full of fasting, paying <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زَكَاة</span>, because of his injustices that he committed with other people, because of the hurt that he caused to other people in this world, this man will have to give up all his good deeds and he will have to take on all the horrible sins of his victims and he will finally be thrown into Hellfire.</p>
<p>This person, he said, will be the greatest destitute of my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمَّة</span>. May <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> protect us all from being among these destitutes on the Day of Judgment.</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آخِرُ دَعْوَاهُمْ أَنِ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى رَسُولِهِ</span> he <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ خَاتِمِ النَّبِيِّينَ وَسَيِّدِ الْمُرْسَلِينَ وَعَلَى أَخِيهِ وَوَصِيِّهِ عَلِيِّ بْنِ أَبِي طَالِبٍ أَشْرَفِ الْوَصِيِّينَ وَعَلَى الْأَئِمَّةِ مِنْ ذُرِّيَّتِهِمَا الطَّيِّبِينَ الطَّاهِرِينَ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَيْهِمْ أَجْمَعِينَ الْفَاتِحَة</span>.</p>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'the womb of afterlife';
INSERT INTO chapter (slug, anchor_key, idx, title, html, word_count) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'allah - being vs attributes', 15, 'Allah - Being vs Attributes', '', 4609);
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ</span> ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ صَلِّ عَلَى سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ وَعَلَى آلِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ كَمَا صَلَّيْتَ وَسَلَّمْتَ وَبَارَكْتَ وَرَحِمْتَ وَتَرَحَّمْتَ عَلَى إِبْرَاهِيمَ وَعَلَى آلِ إِبْرَاهِيمَ فِي الْعَالَمِينَ إِنَّكَ حَمِيدٌ مَجِيدٌ رَبِّ اشْرَحْ لِي صَدْرِي وَيَسِّرْ لِي أَمْرِي وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِنْ لِسَانِي يَفْقَهُوا قَوْلِي السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span></p>
<p>Now that we understand the linguistic meanings of the basic terms that I''ve explained so far in these sessions, now it''s time to start with the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَسْمَلَة</span>, which is actually the first verse of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎. So Arabs had this way of shortening these commonly repeated statements. So if they wanted to refer to ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, they would, instead of saying the statement completely, they would just shorten it down to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَسْمَلَة</span>, right? And so, for example, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا حَوْلَ وَلَا قُوَّةَ إِلَّا بِاللَّهِ</span>, they just simply would call it <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَوْقَلَة</span>. Similarly, they would use <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span> for ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> ‎, and so on. So the term ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, it actually refers to the first verse of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>, which is ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎.</p>
<p>Now, today what I want to focus on is this concept of being and attributes, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is a being, He''s an entity, and then He has all these various attributes. And this is quite a complicated topic, so, you know, I tried to make it as simple as possible, but there are literally books written on this, so it''s very difficult to condense this huge topic. But if you look at the statement of ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ from a grammatical point of view, you''ll see that in the context of mentioning <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, it uses a noun and it uses two adjectives, right? The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللَّهِ</span>, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is a noun representing an entity, a being, and then you have two attributes of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيمِ</span>, right? These are the adjectives or the attributes describing the nature of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, that He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and His nature is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span>.</p>
<p>So let''s talk about this distinction between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s name and His attributes, which is essentially the distinction between the concepts of a noun and adjectives if you look at it from a grammatical point of view. Attributes or adjectives are tools that have been provided to us by language in order for us to understand something. In other words, it is through the combination of these two things, noun and adjectives, or name and attributes, that we actually understand an entity, right? So if you want to gain a complete understanding of an entity, you need to understand its attributes. In the Arabic language, this is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَاتٌ وَصِفَاتٌ</span>, being and attributes.</p>
<p>So for instance, we have a soul and we have a body, which together constitute our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَات</span>, our being. But then soul has certain spiritual attributes, right? It can be kind, it can be brave, intelligent, generous, or it can be weak, it can be coward, it can be dumb, right? In the same way, the body has its own set of physical attributes: fat, thin, black, brown, white, tall, short. And once we understand these spiritual and physical attributes that are describing the entity, which is the soul or the body, we essentially come to recognize the nature of that being.</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/152/92005ff6-ab51-4acb-a733-01fbbe1e5f0e.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>So the union of the two, they provide the identity and a complete description of that identity, which then enables our intellects to understand the nature of that being. But the thing is, attributes cannot exist without the being, right? It is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَات</span> that gives existence to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِفَاتْ</span>. Attributes can only exist after the existence of the being itself. So if there is no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, there cannot be a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span>. So that is why the name <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is greater in its rank than a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span> on the basis of its precedence.</p>
<p>So now if we start breaking down the statement of the ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, the term <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> points to His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَات</span>, to His being. You know, He is a deity named <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is the title of His essence. As He says in the Quran, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّنِىٓ أَنَا ٱللَّهُ لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّآ أَنَا۠ فَٱعْبُدْنِى وَأَقِمِ ٱلصَّلَوٰةَ لِذِكْرِىٓ</span> ‎, no doubt I am <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, and there is no one worthy of worship and obedience except Me. Now this noun <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> has adjectives, which are His 99 <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِفَةُ سُورَةِ الفَاتِحَةِ</span>, His attributes describing the nature of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, right? These are His various manifestations in His interactions with creation.</p>
<p class="ar-block">So now you have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيم</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المَلِك</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْقُدُّوسُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّلَام</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُؤْمِن</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُؤْمِن</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَزِيزُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْجَبَّار</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُتَكَبِّرُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْخَالِقُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْبَارِئ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُصَوِّر</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْغَفَّار</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْقَهَّار</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْوَهَّابُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَلِيمُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْكَابِدُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْبَاسِطُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْخَافِضُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْفَاتِحَة</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُعِزُّ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُذِلُّ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّامِر</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْبَصِيرُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَكَمُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَكِيم</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَدْل</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَللَّطِيفُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَلِيم</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْكَبِيرُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَظِيم</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْغَفُورُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْعَلِىُّ ٱلْكَبِيرُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَافِظ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَسِيبُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْكَرِيم</span>,</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المُقِيم</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّقِيبُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْجَلِيلُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُجِيبُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْوَسِيرْ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْوَدُود</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمَجِيد</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْبَاعِثُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الشَّهِيدُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْوَكِيل</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْكَاوِي</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المَتِين</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْوَلِيُّ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمِيدُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُحْصِي</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُبْدِي</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المُعِيدُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُحْيِي</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُمِيءُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَيُّ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْقَيُّوم</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْوَاجِدُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمَجِيد</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْوَاحِدُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الصَّمَدُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْقَاضِيل</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُقْتَدِرُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُقَدِّم</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُؤَخِّر</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْأَوَّلُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الآخِرُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الظَّاهِرُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْبَاطِنُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْوَلِيُّ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُتَعَالِي</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْبِرُّ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">التَّوَّاب</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُنْتَقِمُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَفُوّ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّؤُوفُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المَلِك</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُلْك</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذُو الْجَلَالِ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيُّ كَرَمٍ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُقْسِطُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْجَامِعَة</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْغَنِيُّ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُكْنِي</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المَنِيَّة</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَذْدَر</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">النَّفِير</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">النُّور</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْهَادِي</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">البَادِر</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">البَاقِي</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الوَارِف</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّشِيدُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الصَّبُورُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَنَّانُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمَنَّانُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الدَّيَّانُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">البُرْهَان</span>.</p>
<p>So now, these are all these attributes that describe the nature of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. And to use the example of the prism and the light that I stated in one of the previous sessions, now you can think of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, as the term <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, as one pure light, right? He is the light of the heavens and the earth. Now, when this pure light of the name <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> comes into the physical realm, His divine attributes refract within creation. The light of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> splits into these 99 attributes within His creation.</p>
<p>So now we get all these different manifestations of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s essence based on His interaction with His creation. So in one instance we see that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَرِيم</span>, He is generous, but then in another instance we see He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَبَّار</span>, the compeller. In another instance we see He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَحَرْ</span>, the subduer. In another we see that He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَزِيزُ</span>, the powerful. In another we see He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسِيل</span>, the one who humbles, but then He''s also <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْمُعَوِّ</span>...</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>, is the one who bestows honor. So His essence manifests through all these different attributes within creation.</p>
<p>This world then becomes a theater for the divine manifestation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s attributes, enabling us to know who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is, not directly through His essence, but through the refraction of His attributes and His creations. This is why it is said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا تَفَكُّرَ فِي اللهِ، وَلَكِنْ تَفَكَّرْ فِي خَلْقِ اللهِ</span>, do not reflect upon <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, but rather reflect upon the creations of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. Why?</p>
<p>Because it is creation, it is nature that manifests all of His attributes for us. So if you want to understand <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, look outside yourself and look within yourself. We will keep showing them our signs within their own, within the horizons and within their own souls, until it becomes perfectly clear to them that all of this is from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَقّ</span>.</p>
<p>But then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is infinite, and so are His attributes. This is why, when we attempt to understand <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, we get bewildered by His attributes, because in a sense we cannot know what God is any more than we can know what God isn''t. You see, because when we speak about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, we are speaking about this infinite creator of the heavens and the earth. And the word infinite means without a limit. Now, the word in Arabic for definition is the same word that we use for a limitation or a boundary. A definition is what defines or gives finiteness to something, a concept. It limits it, it demarcates it, it puts it into perspective. So when we talk about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, we cannot define God. Because to define Him would mean to give Him a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُطّ</span>, a boundary, right?</p>
<p>We would have to place Him in a limit, which is impossible, because, you know, He is without any limit. The way the intellect works, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَقَالَ</span> means to create a boundary. Right? That''s the only way human intellect works. That''s the only way we know how to grasp or understand something, by creating a boundary, by creating a limit. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> cannot be limited. And this is why scholars say that all of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَقِيدَة</span>, this concept of creed, it can be reduced into two statements. He is unlike anything. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا يُشْبِهُ ذَاتَهُ الذَّوَاتُ وَلَا مُعَطَّلَةٌ تُوصَفُ الصِّفَاتُ</span>. That His essence is not like any other essence, and His attributes are not in any way metaphorical. He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْغَنِيُّ عَنِ الْجَمِيعِ وَالْجَمِيعُ مُفْتَقِرٌ إِلَيْهِ</span>. He has no need of anyone, and everyone is in need of Him. So this statement actually negates need for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and it enforces need for the rest of creation. It negates independence for us.</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/152/12b65610-07b1-45a5-be3a-0ebb449a5e2f.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>Now, here is where we get into another central problem with religion, and that is the problem of transcendence and immanence. Transcendence is when we speak about God without anything, you know, any reference in this world that can guide us to Him. There is nothing in the world for which we can say, you know, that is God or this is like God, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is above and beyond anything they ascribe to. So when we talk about transcendence, we''re talking about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> being above and beyond everything that we know of, right? That''s why we say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَكَ</span>, which is in Arabic the word, it means transcendence, and it comes from which means to purify, to get rid of filth, you know, to transcend something, to be above and beyond something. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is transcendent.</p>
<p>But then pure transcendence has a lot of problems by itself. Because then now <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> becomes the God of the scientist. Like Einstein believed in a God, but he did not believe in a personal God, right? He believed in a transcendent God. The God he believed in was the God of the deist, who has nothing to do with his creation. And then that again creates a major problem. But then there is also this concept of immanence, the idea of God being close to us in the world. So for example, like in Hinduism, right, the Brahman class, they don''t really believe in idols. You know, we generally, when we think of a Hindu, we think they are idol worshipers, but this class of Brahmans, right, they don''t really believe in idols.</p>
<p>It''s really a kind of trick to help all these stupid people of the lower class that they believe, who they feel that still need to evolve. So there''s this understanding in their tradition that the lower classes, or the caste system that they have, which they call the varna. So if you''re in a lesser varna, you cannot understand the Brahma. That''s what they believe, because he has gone through several lifetimes to get this ability to perceive. And it is something that only he can perceive, and only he can understand the Godhead, as they call it. Right, so Brahma understands the God that has no attribute, which they call the nirguna. But they have this idea that the lower class people actually need to see something in order to understand God, and so they gave them all these idols. It''s the Brahman class that gave idols, this concept of idols, to the lower classes, to the lower varna, who they actually knew were not gods. But they thought that it helps the common people understand God. And this is the problem of immanence in religion, which is creating likeness for God. You understand?</p>
<p>The way Christianity deals with this problem is obviously through Jesus, because Jesus becomes this incarnation of God, right? God enters into flesh. And in our tradition this is what is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُلُول</span>, where God enters the flesh, and some of the deviant Sufis, they actually claim the state. So this is not something specific to Christianity. You know, we know that there was a group called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نُصِرَ</span> that claimed divinity for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْلَانَا عَلِيّ</span>.</p>
<p>And it actually also happened for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِمَامٌ جَعْفَرٌ صَادِقٌ</span> and other Imams as well. So now, how do we understand this concept of immanence in our religion? What is the relationship between transcendence and immanence? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَنْزِيهٌ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَجْرِيد</span>? Because you do need an access to your master, right? If you have a completely transcendent God, he becomes this cold, distant, uncaring, unloving God. On the other hand, if you give God the attributes of creation, you now have a whole new problem, and that''s what gives birth to these concepts of anthropomorphism, falling into this idea that God is petulant, that God has emotions, that God has anger, that he has pleasure in an anthropomorphic way. And this is something that, you know, penetrates the Muslim understanding as well. There are Muslims who think that when these words are used in the Hadith or the Quran, so for example when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> says the one who faced the anger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, they think that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s anger is like our anger, that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> becomes angry in an emotional sense. And that''s not true, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is above and beyond anything that is ascribed to him. But we do believe that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَضَب</span> has a meaning, and we believe that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِضًا</span> has a meaning. You know, we believe that these words that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> used in the Quran, like <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَّضِىَ ٱللَّهُ عَنْهُمْ وَرَضُوا۟ عَنْهُ</span>, they were pleased with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> was pleased with them. But don''t think your pleasure with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is the same as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s pleasure with you. Don''t think those two words mean the same thing. In the context they are being used, each means a specific thing in relation to that thing that it is predicating. So when we talk about the attributes of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, we have to be extremely careful. When we try to create an understanding of these attributes, we have to be extremely careful, because we in a sense can only say about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> has said about Himself. We cannot, you know, any description that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> needed for our understanding, He described it Himself in the Quran. But then now here we enter into another problem, which is the problem of interpretation. Because even though <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is telling us things about Himself in the Quran, even though He is self-disclosing to us, He is self-disclosing through language.</p>
<p>And language is filtered through the human mind. And the human mind has the capacity to interpret what is being said in so many different ways. So now we have a whole new set of problems. And this is why we stick to the Prophet and his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">واسع</span>, right? Because they are the purest filter for us. And they''re still a filter, right? The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span> is also a filter, but he is the purest filter. And this is why the prophets have a capacity that other human beings do not have, and that is that, you know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> has chosen them to be these filters for revelation. And that is why the best understanding of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> is the understanding that our Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span> has articulated to us. And this is what he said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ لَا نُحْصِي ثَنَاءً عَلَيْكَ أَنْتَ كَمَا أَثْنَيْتَ عَلَى نَفْسِكَ</span>. O <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, we are unable to praise you. You are as you praised yourself. In other words, I cannot truly praise you. Because one of the reasons that we praise is based on our knowledge of the praiseworthy, right? If you don''t know the qualities of someone or something, you cannot really praise it. You cannot really praise them.</p>
<p>And this is why in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s self-disclosure to us, He is describing for us the reasons why He is praiseworthy, the reasons why He is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمِيدٌ</span>. He has described to us, explained to us in the Quran the praiseworthy attributes that He has and why. This is how understanding the attributes of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> enables us to come to know <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. But these attributes are known to us through our human experience. And that''s where you get into the problem of immanence. You know, there has to be some correlation between what we understand about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and what we understand about ourselves, or what we see in the world, because otherwise we just simply cannot know <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. It just wouldn''t be possible. And so in dealing with the attributes of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, you are still working with things in this world, you see.</p>
<p>So when we say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> is, for instance, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَدِيم</span>, right? And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَدِيم</span> becomes a problematic word because it''s a technical word. It is not found in the Quran. It is not actually an attribute of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. It''s more of a technical word. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قِدَم</span> is what they call a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> attribute, because it negates <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span>.</p>
<p>I know this is getting complicated, but basically it negates occurrence. That''s what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَدِيم</span> means. It negates occurrence, it negates the fact that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدَثَ</span>, that He came into existence. No, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> did not come into existence. The idea of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قِدَمٌ</span> is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> is as He always was and He will always continue to be the same way, without any change or any time factor. And this is where pre-eternity becomes the best word in English that we can use. When we say pre-eternity, I don''t even know what that means. I really don''t. But it''s a word that, I mean, how can you describe pre-eternity? You cannot even describe eternity, because eternity means forever. And so how do you use pre with eternity? How can there be something before that is eternal, right?</p>
<p>So I really don''t know what that word means. I cannot. I have a sense of what that word means, but I cannot really describe it. But that''s the word that, that at best what we have come up with as a way of describing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s existence. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. So you can see that we run into all these problems when we try to understand <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. And so <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْلَانَا عَلِيٌّ عَلَيْهِ السَّلَامُ</span>, he gave us this simple, simple formula for recognition of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَجْزُ عَنْ دَرَكِ الْإِدْرَاكِ إِدْرَاكٌ</span>. Well, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَحْثُ أَنْزَتِهِ كُفْرٌ وَإِشْرَاقٌ</span>. My inability to recognize <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is recognizing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. My inability to recognize <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, my inability of my intellectual powers to create a boundary, to create a definition around <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, the meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, the understanding of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, is recognizing Him. And any attempt to discuss His essence, it will inevitably result in assigning and attributing partners with Him, creating likeness for Him. So accepting our inability to know <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is our true recognition of Him.</p>
<p>And this is why the most essential act in worship in Islam is the act of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سِجْدَة</span>, putting our intellects on the ground and surrendering its capacity to recognize <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. Now, Quran gives us an incredibly concise statement to help us understand <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. Because the way in which we understand this world around us is through things, right? The Arabic word for a thing is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَة</span>, right? The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَة</span>, it means thing, and this word, it comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَاءَ</span>, which means will, the will of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. Everything, every being, every <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَات</span> that exists in this world has its own individual set of attributes, right? Every noun has its adjectives, all of which points to what they point to, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْ شَاءَ</span>, to the will and the purpose of their creator. So every <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَة</span> is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَات</span>, it''s a being, it''s an entity which has its own set of attributes, or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِفَاتْ</span>. And they are a sign, they are a means, they are a tool for us to understand the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْشَأ</span> of the Creator, the will and the purpose of the Creator.</p>
<p>But when it comes to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> Himself, when it comes to the Creator Himself, there is no thing, there is no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْء</span> that can be compared to Him in any way, shape or form. And so the Quran beautifully explains this concept to us through one single word in an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَةٌ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَمِثْلِهِ</span>. No matter what you do, no matter how high your imaginations fly, no matter how deep your thoughts dive, there is absolutely no way for you to understand <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, because there is no thing, no being, no attribute in existence, whether it''s physical or spiritual or metaphysical or conceptual or theoretical, there is nothing that is like <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. There is absolutely nothing that you can use to compare it to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> or to create a likeness for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>.</p>
<p>Now I want you to take a moment to really let this paradox sink in. He created this magnificent universe all around us. Every single entity with its attributes is reflecting His divine existence. It''s reflecting His own attributes for us, so that we can gain a semblance of understanding of His powers, His glory, His majesty, His wrath, His love, His everything. And then once we do manage to get some feeble understanding of His attributes in creation, He says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَيْسَ كَمِثْلِهِۦ شَىْءٌۭ</span>. Look, whatever little now that you think you know about Me, understand that I''m not like that. I''m way beyond it. And what''s so incredible is that He does this linguistically through the use of one single word. This is not a word that is commonly used, and it was not even used in classical Arabic. The ancient Arabs actually never used this word. This is a word that is used in only in eloquence of the Quran.</p>
<p>So from a grammatical standpoint, the word is actually two words. There is the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَ</span>, and then there is the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِثْلِهِ</span>. These are both words that are used for comparison, but they are never fused together in this manner. They are always used individually. So let''s try to understand the subtlety in the usage of this word ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎. It''s really beautiful.</p>
<p>So in Arabic, when you want to compare something in attributes — now you understand the difference between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَات</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِفَاتْ</span>, right? The noun and adjectives, being an attribute. So anytime you want to create a comparison in attributes, you add the letter <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَ</span> before it, o<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَ</span>y? So for example, if I wanna compare the bravery of Zed to a lion, right?</p>
<p>So if I wanna say that Zed is brave like a lion, I will have to use the letter <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَ</span> to do that. So I''ll say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زَيْدٌ كَالْأَسَدِ</span>. I''m saying Zaid is like a lion. Now what am I comparing here? I''m comparing the attribute of bravery of a lion with Zaid. So I''m using the letter <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زَيْدٌ كَالْأَسَدِ</span>. But when I need to establish a comparison in essence, I cannot use the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَ</span>. You understand? Grammatically that would be wrong. I will now have to use the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِثَالٌ</span>.</p>
<p>So for example, I will say . A cub is similar to a lion. The son of a lion is like the lion. Here I''m using the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِثَالٌ</span>, because I''m not comparing in attributes. I''m actually comparing the essence of these two entities, the lion and the cub. So this is a rule of the Arabic language, that when you know when you''re establishing comparisons, both words <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِثَال</span>, they are used in very precise ways. And we find this distinction all over Arabic poetry. So for example, there is this poet who is praising the generosity of a king and comparing it to an ocean, right? Now what is generosity? Is it being, or is it an attribute? It''s an attribute. So he''s comparing the generosity of this king to the ocean. So he has to use the letter <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَ</span>. So he says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَالْبَحْرِ يَقْضِي فُؤُولَ الْكَرِيِّ بِلَا عَلِيَّةٍ جُودًا وَيَبَّأُ صُولَ الْبَعِيدِ سَحَائِبَا</span>. He says, the king is generous like the ocean. When I move closer to him, he gives me pearls, and when I move away from him, he sends clouds filled with rain.</p>
<p>So here he uses the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَ</span>, because he is comparing the attributes of generosity. But when the being or the entity needs to be compared, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِثَالٌ</span> has to be used. So the poet says, I am a lion and this is my son, indeed the son of a lion is like the lion. So now, because the comparison is being done in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَات</span>, in the entity itself, the poet is using the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِثْل</span>. So I hope this gives you a much better understanding of this subtlety, because you know, that is what makes this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَةٌ</span> so beautiful.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> says. To compare Himself, what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> does in this verse is that He fuses both the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِثْل</span> together to create this one word, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَامِفْلِحْ</span>. Why is that? Because we just learned that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَ</span> is used for attributes and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِسِّيلْ</span> is used for the entity itself. So what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is saying is that whether you try to compare me to attributes of creation, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَ</span>, or if you try to compare me to the essence of creation, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِسِّيلْ</span>, in both cases, you will fail, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَيْسَ كَمِثْلِهِۦ شَىْءٌۭ</span>. There is nothing that exists in attributes, and there is nothing that exists in essence that you can use to compare to me. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. Can you appreciate the beauty of this word?</p>
<p>It''s incredible. This is eloquence at its best. I mean... There is nothing comparable to Me in attributes. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِثْل</span>. There is nothing comparable to Me in beingness, in existence. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَمِثْلِهِ</span>. There is nothing comparable to Me in attributes or in being. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَيْسَ كَمِثْلِهِۦ شَىْءٌۭ</span>. There is no thing, whether physical, metaphysical, spiritual, conceptual, theoretical, practical, there is simply no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْء</span> that you can use to create a comparison or a likeness for Me, not in attributes, not in essence. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>.</p>
<p>So now that we understand this concept, what the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللَّهِ</span> does is that it condenses all the attributes of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> into two primary attributes: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيمِ</span>. In other words, the two attributes that will always be present in each and every single interaction of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> with His creation are the attributes of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيم</span>. Intense mercy, consistent mercy. So when we say ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, in essence we are remembering the essence of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. Right? Because it is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> who is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span>. So we''re remembering, reminding ourselves of the essence of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> which is manifesting for us through these two primary attributes of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>.</p>
<p>So what this does is that in a sense it implodes all of His remaining attributes. It sucks in all of His remaining attributes into this concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>. You see, in other words, regardless of the way in which <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is interacting with us right now, His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> is present in that manifestation. Even if He is manifesting in my life right this moment as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْجَبَّار</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْقَهَّار</span>, His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَانِيَّة</span>, His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيمِيَّة</span>, His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, His love, His care, His compassion, His concern, His mercy, it''s always present in His interaction with me. There is a lot more to be, you know, explained about this. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span> we''ll move this conversation forward in the next session.</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَآخِرُ دَعْوَانَا أَنِ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى رَسُولِهِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ خَاتَمِ النَّبِيِّينَ وَسَيِّدِ الْمُرْسَلِينَ وَعَلَى أَخِيهِ وَوَصِيِّهِ عَلِيِّ بْنِ أَبِي طَالِبٍ أَشْرَفِ الْوَصِيِّينَ وَعَلَى الْأَئِمَّةِ مِنْ ذُرِّيَّتِهِمَا الطَّيِّبِينَ الطَّاهِرِينَ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَيْهِمْ أَجْمَعِينَ، الْفَاتِحَة</span></p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/152/5b739499-b576-4bf4-b91f-f4f906ded16a.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'allah - being vs attributes';
INSERT INTO chapter (slug, anchor_key, idx, title, html, word_count) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'the ism of allah', 16, 'The ISM Of Allah', '', 4087);
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ</span>. ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ صَلِّ عَلَى سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ وَعَلَى آلِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ وَبَارِكْ وَسَلِّمْ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ اشْرَحْ لِي صَدْرِي وَيَسِّرْ لِي أَمْرِي وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِنْ لِسَانِي يَفْقَهُوا قَوْلِي</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span>.</p>
<p>In the previous session, we talked about the concepts of t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>nscendence and immanence of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. So we learnt that in one way, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is immensely distant out of His loftiness and because of His magnificence. But then at the same time, He is extremely close to our hearts and our consciousness, as the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> says that He''s closer to us than our jugular vein. And what that ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sically means is our consciousness, because when you cut the jugular vein, the first thing that you lose is your consciousness.</p>
<p>So in one instance He is extremely distant from us and in another He is extremely close. And there''s this beautiful verse in Urdu, which I really like, that I think that describes His immanence and His t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>nscendence in a really beautiful way, in which the poet says, Ke duri ki yahad hai, ki tasavur bi khata hai. And what the poet is saying there is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is so t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>nscendent, He is so distant from us that even imagining anything about Him is a sin. But on the other hand, He is so close to us that He is closer to me than my jugular vein. And I think that is a really beautiful way in which the poet expresses this concept of t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>nscendence and immanence.</p>
<p>We also looked at the distinction between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and His attributes, and we saw that how He is incompa<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>ble by both of these. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَيْسَ كَمِثْلِهِۦ شَىْءٌۭ</span>, and the word ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, it''s actually a combination of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَـ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِثْلِهِ</span>, one used for attributes, the other used for essence. And the verse reveals to us that He is incompa<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>ble through attributes as well as through essence.</p>
<p>So now that we''ve covered this noun <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and His adjectives <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span>, today I want to talk about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِصَام</span>, right? Because it starts with ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎. The Alif is dropped because of a g<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>mmatical rule which I''ll talk about later, but that the ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ is actually joined with the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِصَام</span>. And the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِصَام</span> in A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bic, it means two things. It means a noun, you know, as in the g<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>mmatical term noun, but and it also means name, because every noun has a name. So the classical A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bs, they just used it interchangeably.</p>
<p>Now from a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>ic perspective, this concept of name is extremely important, because it is the names, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَسْمَاء</span>, that elevated Adam over the angels and made him worthy of their prost<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>tion. Also because these names were something that were directly taught to Adam by none other than <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> Himself. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> taught Adam all the names. So the concept of name is actually extremely important in the human psyche, because we are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَنِي آدَمَ</span>, we are the children of Adam.</p>
<p>Now, what is the name? A name is ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sically the ability of symbolic thought. That is the essence of a name. So naming something is taking a symbol, which is linguistic in nature, and then applying that symbol to something which is either material or empirical, conceptual or even theoretical, right? Those are all entities that are actually named. We give a name to them. Or a name can be something that can be used as a means of abst<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>ction in order to create sepa<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>tion in things.</p>
<p>So you can have a broad category called cars, and then you can further break them down, further abst<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>ct each one of them by giving them additional names, right? Mercedes, Toyota, BMW, Audi, all of these. So now this is something that only we can do. Animals cannot do this. Animals cannot use symbolic language. This is something that only human beings are capable of.</p>
<p>It has been agreed by the A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>b scholars that human beings have ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sically three dominant modes of symbolic language. We have linguistics, which is the highest form, and then we have mathematics, which, believe it or not, actually comes under linguistics. This has always been the mindset of the ancient people. It''s only the 20th century and the Western civilization that elevated mathematics over language. Today, language has taken a secondary place, and that is because people don''t understand the power of language, especially the A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bic language.</p>
<p>And this is why even Muslims today, they try to place mathematics over the linguistics of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> when they want to try to prove it as a mi<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>cle. And, you know, no doubt <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> is mathematically perfect. I''m not, you know, discrediting that, and actually I''ve shown you a lot of examples of that in the sessions on is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> a mi<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>cle. But the fact of the matter is that what makes <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> a mi<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>cle is not its mathematical perfection, but <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>ther its linguistic perfection.</p>
<p>And this is something that as Muslims we really need to understand and appreciate. And, you know, this need to prove <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> mathematically, it actually comes from this feeling of inferiority before the god of modern science, which of course there is mathematics. You know, this comes from Newton and Einstein and all these other people who elevated mathematics and said that it is the only real way of understanding reality, right? And the reason they say that is because mathematics is precise and it is exact. But then these people did not study classical A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bic, because A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bic is precise and A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bic is exact. In fact, it is precise and exact to a degree that mathematics cannot even come close to. And this is why the mi<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>cle of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِعْجَاز</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, is not in its mathematics, it is in its linguistic perfection and eloquence.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> is a linguistic mi<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>cle. But the recognition of this, this concept of recognizing the fact that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> is a linguistic mi<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>cle, it actually comes from our ability to recognize things through names. Because names have meanings, right? They help us identify, they help us recognize things. So the actual power of recognizing the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> as a linguistic mi<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>cle, it comes through our ability of understanding names. And this is why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اسْمٌ</span> is so important, to understand this concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اسْمٌ</span>.</p>
<p>And when it comes to classical A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bic, even the letters become prominent, even the sounds become prominent. In fact, you know, if you take the A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bic language, so when I say a tree, for example, in English, right, you see something that has a trunk, that has b<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>nches and leaves. You know, that''s what comes to your mind when I say the word tree, right? If it''s a palm tree, it has a trunk and then it has these palm fibers. Or, you know, if it''s a fir tree, then it''s got all these spikes instead of leaves. But ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sically there are three types of trees, right? You have the fir trees, you have the palm trees, and then you have these with leaves. So when we say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَجَرٌ</span> in A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bic, what is the difference between a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَجَرٌ</span> and a tree? I mean, we could argue that they''re completely arbit<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>ry, right? And this is a dominant opinion among most linguists in most languages, that names really have no connection to the thing that is named, other than the fact that they are ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sically signs that human beings have collectively agreed upon.</p>
<p>So their opinion is that the A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bs saw the structure of a trunk with the b<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>nches and leaves and they decided to call it <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَجَرٌ</span>, whereas in English they decided to call it a tree. But the ancient A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>b would actually completely disagree with that. They would argue that there is a deep relationship between the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَجَرٌ</span> and this thing out there that we see. Because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَجَرٌ</span> is made up of three letters. This word, this name, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَجَرٌ</span>, is made up of three letters: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِيمْ</span>, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>. Now, whenever you have the letter <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>, this is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرْفُ التَّفَشِّي</span> in A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bic. The letter <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> is always used for something that spreads out, that has <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>diation, like <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَمْسٌ</span>, the sun. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَعْر</span>, hair — hair has <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>diation because it spreads out, it''s all spread all over the body. Shajar has <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>diation because a tree has these b<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>nches that spread out. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> is a very specific word and it has a very specific meaning. Then there is the letter <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِيمْ</span>, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرْفُ الشِّدَّةِ</span>, right? It''s a strong letter.</p>
<p>It is used to represent things that are strong in their nature, because the ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sis of a tree is strength. It is strong. It''s the trunk, right? That''s why it has the letter <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِيمْ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِسْم</span>, the body, the main structure of the human being, strength. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَبَل</span>, a mountain, starts with the letter <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِيمْ</span> because it''s powerful by its very nature, right? So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِيمْ</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرْفُ الشِّدَّةِ</span>. Then there is the letter <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>, which is the letter of repetition. Trees are constantly regene<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>ting themselves, they''re repeating themselves. So the ancient A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>b would argue that there''s a deep relationship between the letters and the thing that is named itself. And this is something — I mean, these are philosophical de‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎tes about the nature of language — but the A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bs would definitely say that there is a secret in names. You know, in classical A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bic names are not arbit<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>ry. There is actually a very good reason why a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَجَرٌ</span> is called a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَجَرٌ</span> and a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَمْسٌ</span> is called a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَمْسٌ</span>, with those particular sounds in those particular order.</p>
<p>And this is why we know that these sounds, they have an effect. The sounds of the A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bic letters have an effect on the human consciousness. There is no denying that. The recitation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> has a powerful effect on human consciousness, just by the very nature of its sounds. So even if you don''t understand a single word, just listening to a good recitation can deeply move your heart. And if you look up on the internet, you''ll find so many examples of people converting to Islam simply after hearing a recitation or hearing the adhan or something like that, because these sounds, they, you know, they''re not arbit<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>ry. They have a very deep effect on the human consciousness. So A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bic is an incredible, mi<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>culous language in every way, shape, or form, and it''s very different from other languages of the world.</p>
<p>But what I''m trying, the reason I''m explaining all this to you is because I want you to understand that all of its power, the power of this A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bic language, it actually boils down to the human ability to understand names. You know, these <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَسْمَاء</span> that are provided for us within the f<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>mework of language. So it''s no coincidence that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> started the statement with ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, and actually you''ll see, what I''ll explain as we go along, you''ll see the power of what that statement means. Now we understand the meaning of names, right? So let''s talk about the purpose of language. What is the purpose of language? The purpose of language gene<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>lly is communication, right? We want to be able to deliver communication, express ourselves. But if you actually analyze the purpose of language itself, it actually is unification. Language unifies.</p>
<p>This is the secret of the human being, that we have this ability to abst<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>ct, differentiate, and identify things uniquely through names. We as human beings, we have this ability to see the one in the many. That''s the only way we can communicate. I mean, this is really profound. Think about it. The only way we can communicate is by seeing the one in the many. What am I talking about? So for example, if we talk about a lecture, let''s say, right? So for example, I tell you that there''s going to be a session next Sunday at 9:30 a.m. How do you know what the word session means in that context? How do you know what that statement means? The word session in that context, because the word session has many different meanings in different contexts.</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/153/0afc12f6-52de-4003-bce7-6cefb9664849.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>So, I could say, you know, I''m recording a religious session, or the school is in session, right? The courtroom is in session. Yasmin had a great tennis coaching session. The Fede<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>l Act was passed in the session of 1908. So the word session can have so many different meanings in different contexts. So but when I say there''s going to be a session tomorrow, how do you know which meaning of the word session I''m talking about? The only way you know that is because you are ext<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>cting that one particular meaning of that word within that context out of so many other possibilities. And this is something we all do instinctively all the time. This is just an ability that we human beings have, because we have this inherent capability to ext<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>ct the one out of the many. And when we''re not able to do that, when we''re not able to zero in on one particular meaning, we are then forced to ask, wait a minute, I just want to make sure, did you mean this or did you mean that? Why do we do that? Because we''re trying to find that one particular meaning. You see that? So every sentence, every ph<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>se, every word, it needs to boil down to one particular thought from a sea of possibilities.</p>
<p>And so what language, and in particular names or these nouns, what they enable us to do is they enable us to speak intelligently about the oneness and the unified nature of words. Because that is who we are. We are all unifiers by our very inherent nature. This is what we as human beings were created to do. We are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوَحِّدٌ</span> by our natures. We were designed to make one out of many through language. This is why Adam was given the gift of names, this gift of language, because using names we can unify. Language is a sign of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> within our existence, within the human existence, that that is what you have been created to do: to perform the ultimate unification, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span>, because that''s what the word unification means in A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bic, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span>. We were created for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span>, to single out the one <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهْ</span>, the one true <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, from all these possibilities and all these deities in our life posing as false gods. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. So you can see how profound this concept of name or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْم</span> becomes.</p>
<p>So now that you understand that, the next question is where does the process of this recognition of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> begin? It begins with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْمُ اللهِ</span>. It begins with the name of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>.</p>
<p>Now, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْم</span>, linguistically, it comes from two root words. It comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> and it comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَسَمَّى</span>. O<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَـ</span>y, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْم</span>, it actually comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَسَمَّى</span>. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>, it means lofty, something very high, like the sky in A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bic is called a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَمَا</span>, right? And the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَسَمَّى</span>, it actually means a sign or a symbol. Alright, so <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْم</span> is ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sically a combination of two things. It is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَسَمَّى</span>. So it is ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sically a sign for those, it''s a lofty sign for those who can see. So if you break this word down, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْم</span> linguistically, it actually means a lofty sign, coming from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَسَمَّى</span>, a lofty sign. That''s what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْم</span> is. It''s an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَمَ</span>. It''s something you put onto something in order to signify it. So it''s essentially a signifier that indicates the signified, right? So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْم</span> is a lofty sign.</p>
<p>In fact, in Su<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>h <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الرُّومِ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> says, from His signs, the creations of the heavens and the earth.</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/153/72fefb64-1e7f-4756-b3e4-8aa9ceacb74a.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>And in the variance, your tongues and your colors. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> relates the tongues to the heavens, right? Because you have the heavens first and then the earth, and He says, and then He says, you know, in the variance of your tongues and your skin color. So He relates tongues to the heavens and He relates the skin color to the earth. And this is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَبَق</span> in A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bic, which is this concept, it''s a type of antithesis where you use opposites to explain something. So He talks about heaven and earth and then He relates them to tongues and colors, like, you know, our skin color and our speech.</p>
<p>So there is a relationship between tongues and heaven, and there is a relationship between skin color and the earth. Because speech comes from heaven. You see, speech, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> taught human beings, or He taught Adam, or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>ther He imprinted within Adam language. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَّمَ</span> means to imprint, right? The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> imprinted within the human being the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. That''s lite<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>lly what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَّمَ</span> means, to imprint something. Language was imprinted within the human being. And this is why all human beings have this common ability to learn languages.</p>
<p>Now, complexions is something that comes from the earth, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> said that Adam was created from all the topsoil of the earth, right? From the white, the black, the brown, the yellow, the red. So you get all these variances in human complexions because Adam was created from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اُدْمَ</span>.</p>
<p>You see, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اُدْمَ</span> in A<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>bic, it means topsoil. And Adam means the one who was created from topsoil. So all human beings have these different complexions, just as there are all these colors of the soil. You have white soil and then you have pure white, right? You have red soil, yellow soil, black and then ebony black. So complexions are related to the earth, and language or speech is related to the heaven. Language is meaning, complexion is sensory. You see? Language is meaning, complexion is sensory. There''s a relationship between meaning and sensory. They''re both joined in the human being.</p>
<p>So what is significant and what is palpable, what we understand and what we can see, touch and feel. So the sensory and the meaning are gathered in these two phenomena, the phenomena of language and the body. Because the essence of human being is language. We are defined as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَيَوَانُ النَّاطِقُ</span>, the speaking or the articulating animal. Because what is the essence of language? It is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>tionality, right? Language is meaningful. So the essence of the human being is language. And that''s why, you know, when we judge a person by their skin color, it is just so wrong, because you''re judging them by something that is just incidental to their nature, right? But when you judge somebody by what they believe in, by how they speak, by what they say, by whether they''re truthful or not, whether they''re virtuous or not, whether they stand for the truth or not.</p>
<p>I mean, that''s how human beings should be judged. That''s why Dr. King, he talked about being judged by the content of cha<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>cter. In one of his speeches, he said that we should judge people by the content of cha<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>cter. And how do you know the content of cha<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>cter? By the essence of that person. And what is the essence? Their belief, because that''s what''s going to manifest from them. So judging somebody by the skin color is just wrong at so many levels.</p>
<p>Anyway, so <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْم</span> means a lofty or a heavenly sign. Now, the statement of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span> begins with the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْم</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, the lofty signs pointing to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, because that is the only way we can know <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, through the power of symbolic understanding, right? We cannot know <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> as we know things. We cannot know <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> the same way we know an MP3 player or a laptop or a computer because, you know, essences are hard to understand even with things, but when it comes to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, it''s absolutely impossible. So the only way we can know <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is through symbolic language, which are these symbols within language and these symbols within existence, which he calls his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَاتٌ</span>, right?</p>
<p>So he has his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَاتٌ</span> within language, which is in the form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, and then he has his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَاتٌ</span>, his lofty symbols within existence in the form of the heavens and the earth, and everything in between. So now I''m bringing this entire concept all together, right? So you understand this idea of unification accomplished through language, and these signs, because if you look at the signs, there are numerous signs. There is a whole ocean, this vast ocean of signs around us, but they all condense down to one particular, they''re all pointing to one particular entity, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. Right? So that''s what we find in the horizon. We will keep showing them our signs, our numerous signs within the horizons and within their soul, until it becomes absolutely clear to them that this is all coming from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَقُّ</span>, from the one truth, one reality, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>.</p>
<p>But and if you look at the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, it directs us to his 99 divine names, right, as familiar linguistic symbols so that we can gain some concept of who he is. That''s why he uses these familiar names. So for instance, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> says he is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span>, right? He is the owner or the king of the day of judgment. How are we able to understand that? How do we understand what that means? We only understand that because we understand the concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span>, right? We understand the concept of a king. And this enables us to relate to and appreciate the position that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> will take on the day of judgment. He will assume the position of a king or the ultimate owner. But if you really think about it, we are only able to understand that because of the symbolic reference. But then we also learn that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is not limited to our concept of what a king means. He''s way more exalted than that. Right, so he''s — we get an idea that he''s a king, but then we also understand that it''s not like the kingship that we know of, but at least that concept of king, it gives us some understanding of his position and his relationship with us, right? It just, at the very least, it gets the point across.</p>
<p>So there is a distinction between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and the name of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. There''s a distinction between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِصَام</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِصَام</span> are all these lofty signs and symbols that He created for us in the horizons and within our souls in various numerous dimensions. They enable us to know Him. That''s an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اسْم</span>. That is what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْمُ اللهِ</span> means. A lofty heavenly sign or a symbol that helps us recognize <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. You see? Because I recognize you by your name, you recognize me by my name. So name is what helps us recognize.</p>
<p>So now that we understand the linguistic meaning of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْم</span> as a lofty heavenly sign, what we can now appreciate about ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ is, you know, now I''m talking in the linguistic sense, that what we understand from it now is the lofty signs in the heavens and the earth and everywhere around us pointing to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and helping us recognize Him as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيمٌ</span>. ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, with the lofty heavenly signs that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> has created for me in order for them to help me recognize that He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span>, the intensely merciful and the consistently merciful.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>, in the next session we will look at this preposition ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ that is joined with the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ</span>, and we will explore the depth of this entire ph<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَا</span>se, and we will see the power behind reciting ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, and you will see how perfectly this statement serves as the crown of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>. Right, it''s sitting on top of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> like a crown, because it really, it really deserves that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقَامٌ</span>.</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى رَسُولِهِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ خَاتَمِ النَّبِيِّينَ وَسَيِّدِ الْمُرْسَلِينَ وَآلِهِ وَوَصِيِّهِ عَلِيِّ بْنِ أَبِي طَالِبٍ أَشْرَفِ الْوَصِيِّينَ وَعَلَى الْأَئِمَّةِ مِنْ ذُرِّيَّتِهِمَا الطَّيِّبِينَ الطَّاهِرِينَ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَيْهِمْ أَجْمَعِينَ، الْفَاتِحَة</span></p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/153/a12c19db-131b-4892-9838-c09bc76e3b15.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'the ism of allah';
INSERT INTO chapter (slug, anchor_key, idx, title, html, word_count) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'in the name of allah the merciful', 17, 'In the Name Of Allah The Merciful', '', 4137);
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ الرَّحْمٰنِ الرَّحِيمِ، اللَّهُمَّ صَلِّ عَلَى سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ، وَعَلَى آلِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ، كَمَا صَلَّيْتَ وَسَلَّمْتَ وَبَارَكْتَ وَرَحِمْتَ عَلَى إِبْرَاهِيمَ، وَعَلَى آلِ إِبْرَاهِيمَ، فِي الْعَالَمِينَ إِنَّكَ حَمِيدٌ مَجِيدٌ، أَشْهَدُ أَنْ لَا إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا اللهُ، وَحْدَهُ لَا شَرِيكَ لَهُ، وَنَشْهَدُ أَنَّ مُحَمَّدًا عَبْدُهُ وَرَسُولُهُ، وَنَشْهَدُ أَنَّ مَوْلَانَا عَلِيًّا وَصِيُّهُ، وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span>. Now that we''ve completed most of the terminology from a linguistic perspective in ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, today I want to talk about the letter ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ itself, which is a preposition, and what it means is &quot;with&quot; or &quot;in.&quot; So when you say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span>, what you''re actually saying is, I am beginning in the name of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. But you''re omitting the &quot;I am beginning.&quot; That''s just understood. It''s called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">محذوف مُقَدَّر</span>, right, in grammar. But the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَا</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span> is actually connected to an omitted verb. And this is something very important to understand, believe it or not. Because verb or an action is essentially the ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sis of all existence. You know, there are two schools of thought.</p>
<p>One believes that, you know, that the ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sis of existence is a noun. And there''s another school of thought, which is the more dominant school of thought, which is also something that we subscribe to ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sed on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَمْ</span>, is that the ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sis of existence is not a noun, but rather a verb. Because if the ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sis of existence is just a noun, then you''re simply talking about a state, right? Like, for example, if we look at our state of being, right, as a human being, we''re just simply here, like I''m being here, I''m existing here, and there''s no action involved in my being. But when you bring a verb into it, what you''re doing is you''re bringing the element of time into it, right? It''s either past, present, or future, because nouns do not have a past, present, future. A tree is a tree. But once a tree begins to blow in the wind, now there is a time associated with it, right? Did it blow in the past? Is it blowing in the present, or will it blow in the future when the wind comes? So the tree swaying is an action of the essence of the tree. Without that action, the tree is just a tree, right?</p>
<p>It would just be a being. So, and this is a very interesting de‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎te about existence itself. Is the root of existence movement and action, or is it being? Because if you think about it, this is also true about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> relationship with this universe, with us, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> has action, right? But His action is created. His actual essence, His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَاتْ</span>, is passive. There''s no action in His essence when we talk about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is essence and attributes, but both essence and attributes are nothing more than states of being. But action is a substantive that moves into time. And that''s why the understanding in theology is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is beyond time and space. So His action is something that manifested within creation or bringing about creation, right? He brings things into existence through His action, through His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَاتِحَة</span>.</p>
<p>But then that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَاتِحَة</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَا دَسَّ</span>. So, like as the Quran says, you know, when He decides an affair, what does He do? He says to it &quot;be&quot; and it becomes, right? So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> brings creation into existence through a command, which is actually a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَاتِحَة</span>, it is an action. And so the preposition is actually connected to the omitted verb of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> action of creation. So when we recite the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span>, what we''re saying, you know, when we say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span>, what we''re saying is that I am beginning with the recognition of the lofty signs within <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> creation. Because those signs are a witness and a proof of His intense and consistent love, compassion, care, and mercy, His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>. This is the context referring to the primordial act of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, the original creation. So this is the interpretation of that statement in the transcendent realm: that it is an acknowledgement of the fact that I''m beginning with the recognition of these lofty signs that exist for me within creation that are pointing to and that are bearing witness and acting as proof and evidence of His intense and consistent love and mercy. His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَانِيَّة</span> and His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيمِيَّة</span>. So that is the interpretation of this verse in the transcendent realm. But what about its meaning in the imminent or the personal realm? Because as we talked about these two concepts earlier, there is the transcendent God and then there is the imminent God. So in the transcendent realm, we understand it because there are all these signs around us and they are depicting and illustrating <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> love and mercy for us. But what about in my personal life?</p>
<p>How does <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ</span> apply to me personally? So in order to understand that, let''s break down the statement into its grammatical components. And this may seem unnecessary or too technical, but actually there''s a very deep connection here. So first of all, when do we recite <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span>? Let''s start there. When do we recite the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span>? We recite it at the beginning of every action. So now let''s look at this statement from a grammatical point of view to understand this better. What are the various grammatical components of this statement? So it obviously begins with the letter ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, which I just explained as a preposition. Then we have the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْم</span>, which is a genitive masculine noun.</p>
<p>This noun on its own, this noun would be written as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْم</span> with the alif, and meaning, you know, name. But because it''s joined with that preposition, the alif is dropped and the ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ fuses with the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْم</span> and it becomes <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ</span>, making it an indefinite noun. Then we have the proper noun <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> itself, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span>. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span>. So then we have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, which is the proper noun. And finally, the statement ends with two adjectives of mercy, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span>. So we have a preposition, we have an indefinite noun, we have a proper noun, and we have two adjectives in the statement. What''s missing? From a grammatical point of view, what''s missing here to complete this sentence? A verb, you see? Because we know that grammatically a statement is incomplete without a verb. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> left the phrase ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ as an incomplete grammatical sentence. He omitted the verb. You see that? Why did he do that?</p>
<p>Because we conceptually complete the sentence by providing the verb through our action. You see, we complete the grammatical structure of the sentence by providing the verb through our action. This is why we recite the ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ at the beginning of every action, every task, every <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَاتِحَة</span>, thus providing the verb to complete the structure of the statement. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. I mean, this is just incredible, to design a statement like this, right? So now if I''m about to read the Quran and I say ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ as I''m about to begin, my action of reading the Quran, of reciting the Quran, is what is now completing the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span>. So now what I''m saying essentially is, with the name of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, in the name of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> who is intensely and consistently loving and merciful, I begin reading this book. And so now my action of reading the Quran, or regardless of what that action is, no matter what it is, right, it could be anything, but that action itself now gets empowered by the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span>. But what''s even more amazing is that if we look at this linguistically, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sed on everything that we''ve learned, what I''m really saying, let''s say, let''s take the example of reciting the Quran, when I say ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ before reciting the Quran, what I''m really saying is that my ability to read and this Quran that I intend to read is a lofty sign pointing to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> incredible love and mercy, His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَانِيَّة</span> and His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيمِيَّة</span>. So what this does is that it puts <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> love and His mercy right at the center of my focus as soon as I recite this phrase. That''s what triggers in my mind <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> love and mercy, because whatever I''m doing is now becoming a sign for me, a lofty heavenly sign pointing to the love of my Creator. You see that?</p>
<p>My very act of reading, regardless, or not reading, whatever it is, it could be anything, any action, it becomes a lofty sign and a symbol. It becomes a witness and it bears testimony to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَةُ اللَّهِ</span>. And it completely transforms my perspective on that action that I''m about to do. And this transformation is now happening even before I''ve actually started doing it. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. So if I say ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ before I begin driving, that entire experience of driving, it becomes a reminder of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> love and mercy for me: my ability to drive, the resources enabling me to own a car, my mental capacity to follow the signs, my brain power to remember what all these signs means, when to start, when to stop, my ability to assess traffic, my intuition, my instincts of when to stop, when to go fast, when to slow down, all the facilities that my car provides to me, the scenery outside the car. The wind coming in, the refreshing wind coming in through the windows of the car, every single thing that I can think of becomes a lofty reminder that points me directly to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> intense and consistent love, care, concern, and mercy for me. It is because of His mercy this action is happening. It''s all reminding me of the one who gave me this ability, provided me with all these resources in order to experience and enjoy His mercy. This is the power of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللَّهِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَا شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p>It is incredible. I mean, it always blows my mind every time I think about it. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> first act was ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sed on His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, right? He gave us this ability to act. And then every action that we do now becomes a witness to His love and His mercy. All of that so eloquently, so precisely, so concisely captured within the phrase ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎. I begin this task, this action, which is a lofty heavenly sign reminding me of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> intense and consistent love, care, concern and mercy. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ أَكْبَرُ</span>. Now, what is the purpose of reciting the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span> before we begin any action? Why do we do it? From our perspective, like I said, it reminds us of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> love and mercy and naturally makes us love Him ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ck. It''s our love manifesting in the form of worship and obedience.</p>
<p>That''s what the ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, if you think about it in this way, the way I''ve explained to you, and if you actually become conscious of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> love and mercy in our life, because this is what the statement does, it just puts that love right in front of us, and if we actually are able to experience it, it increases us in our love for Him. But what does it do from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> perspective? It fills our action with what is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>. That''s what the ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ does. It fills our action with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>, as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِذَا أَتَيْتُ رَضِيتُ وَإِذَا رَضِيتُ بَارَكْتُ وَلَيْسَ لِبَرَكَتِي نِهَايَةٌ</span>. When you obey me, it pleases me. When I''m pleased, I bless you with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>, and there is absolutely no limit to my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>.</p>
<p>So now, what is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>? It is generally translated as abundance. Right now, what has abundance got to do with the ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎? What has it got to do with the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْم</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>? So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الرَّحْمَٰنِ</span>, it is the last verse of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الرَّحْمَٰنِ</span> in which He says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَبَرَكَ ٱسْمُ رَبِّكَ ذِى ٱلْجَلَلِ وَٱلْإِكْرَامِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَبَارَكَ اسْمُ</span>, so it''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَبَرَكَ ٱسْمُ رَبِّكَ</span>, again due to the grammatical rules, they can convert, they converge, they fuse together, so <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَبَرَكَ ٱسْمُ رَبِّكَ ذِى ٱلْجَلَلِ وَٱلْإِكْرَامِ</span>. The name of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, the name of your master, is glorious and benevolent. His name is full of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>. But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> does not mention in this verse which name of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is full of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>, right? He just says the name of your master is blessed. But which name? So what did the Surah begin with? It began with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span>. And what is the first attribute of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> that is mentioned in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span>? So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is telling us something about this attribute <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span>, that it is full of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>.</p>
<p>But then we also know that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> Himself is full of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>. Right now we just talked about the name of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> being full of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>, but then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> Himself is full of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>, because He says in the Quran, your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> who is the best of creators is full of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>. So I want you to keep these two things in mind: the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْم</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is full of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>, and the name <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> itself is full of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>. So the first two words in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span>, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span>, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْم</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, they''re both loaded with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>. Right?</p>
<p>Now what do you think will naturally happen to our action when we recite these two powerful words before beginning whatever it is that we''re doing? It will naturally, obviously fill it with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>. In other words, by reciting ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ before performing an action, not only are we reminding ourselves of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> intense and consistent love and mercy through the act itself, but we''re also asking <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> to fill that action with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>.</p>
<p>Now let''s look at this word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span> and understand what it means linguistically. So there is no word in the English language that can fully capture the meaning of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>. So we need to look at this like a composite word, like <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span> and all these other things that we''ve understood. So we''ll have to look at it through a combination of certain concepts. In classical Arabic, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span> is defined as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">البَرَكَةُ النَّمَاءُ وَزِيَادَةٌ</span>. It is growth and abundance. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span> is defined as growth and abundance. Now the first word is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَنْعَمْتَ</span>, which means growth.</p>
<p>Something that rises, something that increases in size, makes something that progresses consistently, increasing in the results all the time, or something that goes further and further and increases. It increases beyond expectations. So if you plant a seed of one single fruit in the ground, how many fruits should you get in return? Just one. That would be fair because you planted the seeds of one fruit. But you take the seeds from that one fruit and after you plant it in the ground, you get this enormous number of fruits in return, each producing hundreds of thousands of seeds. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>. That is the concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>, an unprecedented growth. You planted one seed and eventually got thousands of seeds as a result. It''s a growth beyond expectations. That is what is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>. Because if you think about it, what is the comparison of a tree with a seed?</p>
<p>The tree came from the seed, but those two things are completely beyond comparison. A seed is tiny, a tree is huge. A seed is delicate, a tree is firm. A seed has no value, but the tree bears hundreds upon hundreds of fruits, season after season, year after year. So if you really think about it, what we get from a seed, this tree that we get from a seed, when you compare it to what we actually invested there, there''s really no comparison. And that is the concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>, a ridiculous growth, an unlimited increase.</p>
<p>Then the Arabs say that referring to the rain that keeps on coming and coming and coming. So continuity is another characteristic of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>. So it is growth, it is increase, but then there is a continuity associated with it. It doesn''t stop. It is extensively abundant. That is included in the meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>.</p>
<p>Then the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span> also includes the meaning of stability in the Arabic language. So for example, the Arab would say, you know, when it''s like when a camel just digs itself into the ground and it doesn''t move and it just doesn''t budge, that is also a quality of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>. And this is actually a really beautiful concept, because if you think about it, when you grow something, right, and you grow it too much, so for example,</p>
<p>you know, my kids, they play with Legos, right? My son, he builds this tower, just block upon block upon block. He just puts this one huge tower. Now, as the tower is getting tall, does it become more stable or does it become less stable? It becomes less stable, right? The taller the tower is, the less stable it becomes. It will easily fall and break, right? And there''s not going to be any tower end left anymore. That''s how it works. So when something increases rapidly and it increases at a very fast pace, right, it becomes unstable. But when something has <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span> in it, that means it will increase in quantity, it will keep increasing in quantity beyond expectations, yet remain as stable as the camel sunk into the sand. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>.</p>
<p>In other words, that increase of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span> is not going away. It''s not going to be unstable. It''s not an unstable increase. It''s a very consistent and stable increase. The more it grows, the stronger it will get. Because we find unstable increase all around us in this world, right? Our economy, I mean, goes up, crashes. Governments, they take over really fast, they disappear really fast. Fashion industry, right, there are all these trends that pick up really fast, disappear just as fast, so, you know, something else takes its place. But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span> is something that increases and increases and increases, but then it remains stable, it doesn''t go away. So that''s the linguistic understanding, the concept of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>.</p>
<p>So the name of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْمُ اللهِ</span>, is filled with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>. The name <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> itself is filled with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>. His blessed name is being mentioned since the day creation came into existence.</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/159/300d20b6-e90b-4023-9d00-29a23096e187.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>And it only keeps increasing and growing with every single passing moment. That is an example of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> name. It keeps increasing and growing with every single second, and it will continue to grow till the very end of time, until there is no time left, right? I mean, worshipers come and go, but the one who is worshiped, he remains forever. The concept of worship has <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span> in it, because it remains forever. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has been worshiped from the beginning of time. People who glorify the names of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, they come and go. Many came before us, many will come after us, we will all be gone one day, but the glory of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> will always remain forever, eternal. This is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> name.</p>
<p>So now, to summarize, when I recite ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ before each and every action that I do, my action itself turns into a reminder, a heavenly lofty sign pointing to the loving care and mercy of a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span> in my life. And what that does is it creates <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span> in the results of my action, you see. It creates an abundance in my action. There''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيثُ سُورَةِ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span> in which he says, every single act is sterile or infertile without . Every act that is done without the remembrance of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> will have no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span> and no abundance in it. Now what does that mean?</p>
<p>In practical terms, what does that mean? What does abundance have to do with this? So think about it. If you have an abundance of anything, let''s say money, easiest example, right? Let''s say you have an abundance of money. What does it free you of? If you have a never-ending supply of money, what does it liberate you from? It frees you from ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> ‎. It liberates you from the shackles of anxiety and depression, the two biggest killers of our time. It frees you from ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> ‎.</p>
<p>Because you''re no longer concerned about that aspect of your life, of where''s the money gonna come from. So you''ll never feel anxious about it and you''ll never feel depressed. You get anxious about something ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sed on fear. Whether it''s going to happen or not. That makes you anxious. Loss creates depression. And if you have that abundant supply of money, at least as far as money is concerned, you will never be anxious and you will never be depressed. Right?</p>
<p>Now imagine if you have the abundance, a never-ending supply of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَةُ اللَّهِ</span>niya and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيمِيَّة</span> in your life. If you have a never-ending supply of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَةُ اللَّهِ</span> in your life, if you have this abundance of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْرُ اللَّهِ</span>, this close connection with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, will anything or any situation in life, no matter how ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎d it is, will it be able to affect you? Will it make you feel anxious or depressed? Impossible. Completely impossible. Because no matter the outcome, you''re experiencing the joy of closeness, and you''re experiencing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهِ</span> love simply by the act itself, being reminded by <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span>. It immediately puts that into focus for you, because this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span>, all of these things that are working against us, make us forget <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. But this is why we say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span>, because if you think about it, if this is executed properly, we would be saying <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span> like a thousand times a day.</p>
<p>And every time we would say it, we would be reminded of this. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. And so what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span>, what he meant by that statement, that every state, every act is sterile and infertile without <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span>. What he meant by that is, without the love of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, without a focus on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, without a proximity to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, without the remembrance of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, no amount of physical abundance will satisfy you in this world.</p>
<p>And we find the greatest proof of this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> when we look at this world around us. Millionaires, billionaires who are extremely anxious and constantly in a state of depression. Lottery winners committing suicides, actors, stars, rocks, these singers, all the money in the world, all the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> in the world, the best clothing, the most fancy cars, houses where they get lost, they can''t find an exit. Right? So much abundance of physical material, constantly anxious, constantly depressed, and they end up committing suicide. So physical abundance means nothing if there is no connection with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p>On the other hand, on the flip side, we find these Muslims, these true Muslims and believers, these <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُؤْمِنِينَ</span>, who despite the worst of situations, they seem the most content and happy. I mean, I''ve personally met people like that. Just blows my mind, right? They''ve just, you know, two sets of clothes in their closet, drinking brown water, they cannot, they cannot even get clean water, but they''re content and happy like you won''t believe. Why? Because they''re connected to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p>And that connection, it creates <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span> in whatever little they have, to such a degree, to such an extent, that they are actually sincerely happy. They are content and so satisfied with what they have. So much so that they don''t even desire more. Imagine that. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>. How do you take that away from someone? But that is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللهِ</span>.</p>
<p>May <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> fill all our hearts with His love and His mercy, and enable us that every time we perform an action we remember Him from this day forth, and every time may He make that experience of that action, whatever it is that we''re doing, turn it into an experience of joy and a remembrance of His love and mercy in our lives. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَغَرَضُ دَوَامٍ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ عَبْدِ الْعَالَمِينَ وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى رَسُولِهِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ خَاتِمِ النَّبِيِّينَ وَسَيِّدِ الْمُوسَى وَالْأَقِي وَوَصِيِّ آلِ النَّبِيِّ تَعَالَى</span>...</p>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'in the name of allah the merciful';
INSERT INTO chapter (slug, anchor_key, idx, title, html, word_count) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'linguistic perfection of hamd', 18, 'Linguistic Perfection Of HAMD', '', 4557);
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ</span> ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ صَلِّ يَا اللَّهُ عَلَى سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ وَعَلَى آلِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ كَمَا صَلَّيْتَ وَسَلَّمْتَ وَبَارَكْتَ وَرَحِمْتَ وَتَرَحَّمْتَ عَلَى إِبْرَاهِيمَ وَعَلَى آلِ إِبْرَاهِيمَ فِي الْعَالَمِينَ إِنَّكَ حَمِيدٌ مَجِيدٌ رَبِّ اشْرَحْ لِي صَدْرِي وَيَسِّرْ لِي أَمْرِي وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِنْ لِسَانِي يَفْقَهُوا قَوْلِي</span></p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span> So in the previous session, we learned that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> created us as nouns, right? And just speaking in grammatical terms, we are all nouns in our essence, but then from the moment we wake up until the moment we go back to sleep, in fact, even while we''re asleep, what we''re constantly doing through our actions is creating verbs. So the power of ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ is that it provides a context for our verbs. It provides a context for our actions by turning them into these reminders, right? By turning our actions into these heavenly, lofty signs, these <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْم</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, reminding us of His intense and consistent love, care, kindness, concern and mercy, which is His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَانِيَّة</span> and His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيمِيَّة</span>. And once we experience His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span>, right, once we experience His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span> through our interactions with this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الم</span> that He created for us, because all of our actions are only happening because His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span> enables and empowers us to do that. Right? So I''m able to drink water because He gave me this physical capacity to pour water into a glass. And then He made water subservient through His laws of nature so that it obediently falls into the glass when I pour it. It doesn''t refuse. It doesn''t change its direction, right?</p>
<p>It doesn''t just start rising upwards in mid-air. It follows and obeys these laws of gravity that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> created, allowing me, enabling me to drink it. Now what is this if not a sign of the mercy and love of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, where He enabled me and water to interact in this manner? This is His action, His proof of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span> executed with intense and consistent love and mercy. So now once I recognize and, more importantly, once I experience this love, what should be my natural response to that love? It has to be ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> ‎. All praise and all gratitude belongs to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>, the master of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَالَمِين</span>. This is why the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَسْمَلَة</span> directly connects with the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>.</p>
<p>Now in today''s session, I want to do a linguistic study of this word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span>, to demonstrate the perfection of the statement so that we can appreciate why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> used this particular word and why He used it in this particular fashion in this verse. You know, why didn''t He choose a different style for saying the same thing? So let''s begin with the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span> itself, because this form of His diction, it veils many, many valuable lessons for us to learn from. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span> is actually a composite word that means two things.</p>
<p>It means praise and it means gratitude. Right? It''s praise plus gratitude. Now there are two other distinct words in the Arabic language for praise and gratitude. The commonly used word for praise is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَدَحَ</span> and the word used for gratitude is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span>. Now what''s the difference? Praise, as we all know, is a gesture of appreciation. When you are moved by something that is beautiful or colorful or scenic, you know, when something intrigues you in some way, when something impresses you, what do you do? You praise it. You verbalize your appreciation by praising it. That''s the very natural human response to something that our soul finds beautiful or impressive.</p>
<p>Now gratitude is something entirely different. Gratitude is done in response to a beneficial act that was done for you. Right? If somebody lends you money or does something nice for you, you say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْرًا</span>. So gratitude is always done as a response to a beneficial act. So you see the difference? Praise is done when something impresses you, or when you appreciate something about someone. Whereas gratitude is done in response to a favor or a kindness that was done for you. Now even though these two terms are used interchangeably in our lives, they''re actually both mutually exclusive. Someone you thank is not necessarily someone you praise. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> clearly distinguishes between these two concepts.</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/160/9f1d99bf-6c97-4f5b-baaa-b62c9928ac0a.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>So for instance, in the story of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوسَى</span>, we know that he was adopted and raised in the house of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِرْعَوْن</span>, right? And then years later when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوسَى</span> returns to deliver <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s message to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِرْعَوْن</span>, he says to him, &quot;How dare you? How dare you talk to me this way? Did we not raise you as a newborn in this house? Did you not spend many years of your life growing up here? How dare you speak to me like that?&quot; In other words, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِرْعَوْن</span> is saying, aren''t you grateful? I raised you for so many years and now you''re going against everything that I taught you? To that, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوسَى</span> responds, yes, you did in fact show me that favor, and for that I am grateful. So even though <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِرْعَوْن</span> is someone who will never be praised, especially not by a Prophet, but even when he does a favor, he will be thanked. So gratitude can exist without praise. You see?</p>
<p>In the case of parents, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ لُقْمَان</span>, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَوَصَّيْنَا ٱلْإِنسَنَ بِوَلِدَيْهِ حَمَلَتْهُ أُمُّهُۥ وَهْنًا عَلَىٰ وَهْنٍۢ وَفِصَلُهُۥ فِى عَامَيْنِ أَنِ ٱشْكُرْ لِى وَلِوَلِدَيْكَ إِلَىَّ ٱلْمَصِيرُ</span> ‎. And we have made it compulsory, we have enjoined upon human beings the care for their parents. His mother carried him increasing in weakness upon weakness, and then in his weaning, which is in two years, so be grateful to me and to your parents, because to me is the final destination. So be grateful to me and both of your parents. But then He says, but if your parents are working against you, in forcing you to disbelieve and commit <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِرْك</span> against me, then do not obey them. But you still have to be what? You still have to be grateful to them. In other words, if your parents are forcing you to disbelieve in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, you don''t have to praise them, but you still have to be grateful to them. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِبْرَاهِيم</span> is a good example of this, right? He does not praise his father, who according to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> was an idol worshipper, but he is still grateful to him. So, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمَدَّاح</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الشُّكْرُ</span>, praise and gratitude, these are two completely separate things. They are mutually exclusive. You can praise without being grateful, and then you can be grateful without praising someone or something. But what''s interesting about the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span> is that it necessarily means both things together. It''s mutually inclusive.</p>
<p>When <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>, He is saying praise is for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> and gratitude is also for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, both at the same time. You understand the difference? What the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span> does is that it makes both these concepts of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَدَحَ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span> mutually inclusive. One cannot exist without the other within <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span>. It means praise and it means gratitude. So now let''s look at why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> did not use the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَدَحَ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span> and instead used the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span>. So let''s start with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَدَحَ</span>. Why didn''t <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> say all praise is for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>? The problem with the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَدَحَ</span> is that it is a praise that does not necessarily have to be genuine. You see? Because we all know that praise can be insincere. Right? A kid walks in the house with a really bad report card, and as soon as he enters the house, he goes, &quot;Mom, oh my God, your food, it smells so delicious.&quot; And she hasn''t even started cooking yet. Right?</p>
<p>So he''s praising her, but it''s not a genuine praise. You get it? Because praise can be fake. There is fake praise done for kings, for bosses, for people in all these high positions all the time. Isn''t it? People falsely praise appearances all the time, right? Wife asks the husband, you know, &quot;Does this dress make me look fat?&quot; &quot;Oh no, you look beautiful.&quot; That''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَدَحَ</span>. This is why in classical Arabic, when you praise someone''s looks, you never say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمِدَ</span>. Because everybody knows that the person that is being praised did not make their own face. If they''re pretty, if they''re beautiful, if they''re handsome, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> made them that way.</p>
<p>So saying you''re so beautiful would just naturally be considered <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَدَحَ</span>. Because it is understood in language itself that praising looks and all these external features, it is inherently a false praise. Now <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span>, on the other hand, cannot be a false praise. It cannot be insincere praise. Why? Because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span> is generated in the heart by the awareness of the magnificence and the glory of the one that is being praised. I''ll say that again. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span>, the praise element of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span>, that occurs when the heart becomes aware of the magnificence and the glory of the one being praised. The heart and the intellect together recognize that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> truly deserves that praise. So therefore <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span> can never be a false praise. Because the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is not dependent on a personal need.</p>
<p>It is never motivated by a personal agenda. You see, it stems simply from being impressed by who He is, not based on what He does for us. So I hope that makes it clear. Now let''s think about the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span>. What if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> had said, &quot;All gratitude is for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>&quot;? What would be the problem with that? Gratitude, or thanks, it originates from the acknowledgement of a favor. Someone does something for you that you appreciate, you thank that person for that favor. You have a flat tire, someone comes along and helps you, you feel grateful. And you express that gratitude by saying thank you. So thanks can only happen when you have a reason to thank someone, right? It is a reaction to a favor.</p>
<p>It''s never something you start with, right? You don''t just walk onto a street, see a stranger passing by and go, &quot;Hey, you, thank you,&quot; right? You never do that. When <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> uses the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span>, which is a combination of both praise and gratitude, there are certain rules that apply linguistically. First rule is that the praise aspect of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span> has to be genuine. It can never be fake. Second, the gratitude aspect of it is not reactionary. You see? So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span> is sincere praise and it is not a gratitude that is in response to what He does for us. Therefore, linguistically, saying <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span> is far more powerful than saying <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْحَمْدُ لِلَّٰهِ</span>, and it is far more powerful than saying <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الشُّكْرُ لِلَّهِ</span>, because it is a lot more comprehensive than both of them.</p>
<p>Okay, so now what if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> had said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمَدْحُ وَالشُّكْرُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span>, &quot;Praise and gratitude belongs to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>&quot;? What would be the problem with that? Even if we remove the shortcomings of both these words, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَدَحَ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span>, the problem now is that when you put the word &quot;and&quot; in between the two things, which is the letter <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَوْ</span>, right? When you add that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَوْ</span>, that &quot;and&quot; in the middle, the problem is that it separates both words even in meaning linguistically. So when I say linguistically, what I''m saying is that praise belongs to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> for certain things and gratitude belongs to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> for other things. They''re not always combined because the words aren''t combined. You see, this is a very cool feature of Arabic language: you never say what you don''t mean. So if we use the two words gratitude and praise and join them with an &quot;and&quot;, in classical Arabic that means sometimes I''m in the mood to praise Him but not necessarily thank Him. And then there are other times when I''m in the mood to thank Him but not necessarily praise Him. But when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>, that means for every act that He does or doesn''t do, for every blessing He gives or holds back, genuine praise and unconditional gratitude is due to Him, both at the same time. We do not get to pick and choose which one.</p>
<p>So now if you own a nice car and you say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>, what have you just done? You''ve genuinely praised <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> for giving human beings this knowledge and this ability to create cars, to design and manufacture them, and you thank <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> for letting you own one. So now you''re praising and thanking <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> both at the same time, and all of that is eloquently captured in the beautiful word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>. This is why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span> is the attitude of a true believer, because it is optimistic by its nature. You know, sometimes you''re going through a difficult time and somebody says, &quot;How are you doing?&quot; and you say, &quot;Oh, you know, life sucks, but <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>.&quot; So you''re not really praising <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> or thanking Him when you say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span> that way, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span> is not a phrase that believers just throw around, because it is the reminder of an attitude.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is never interested in what comes out of our tongues. He''s more interested in what''s in our hearts that is connected with what comes out of our tongues. So when a believer says <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>, regardless of the situation or circumstances, the sentiment that resides in his heart is that indeed, without a doubt, all praise and all gratitude belongs to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> regardless of my situation. If I''m going through a difficult time and I say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>, what I mean is, indeed my praise and my gratitude is for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, because no matter what I''m going through, it could always be worse. Someone right now in this world is going through a much harsher version of what I''m going through right now, and he can then say the same for somebody else, and that person can say the same for somebody else, because no matter what problem we go through, it could always be worse. So I''m grateful to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> that He is lenient with me. I''m grateful to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> that He has provided me with the resources to fix my situation, because there might be someone who just doesn''t have the same resources that I do. And I praise Him for His majesty. And this acknowledgement of how the one who gives help can take it away and then restore it whenever He desires. Because my pain is supposed to remind me of all the times <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> had me living in comfort in which I forgot about Him. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>, sincere praises and unconditional gratitude belongs to the one who reminded me of His grace and His blessings by temporarily or even permanently taking it away. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>, sincere praises and unconditional gratitude belongs to the one who filled my life with undeserved blessings, even in my state of pain, even in my state of difficulty. So an understanding of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span> can really empower us, and it can completely transform our attitudes towards life itself as believers.</p>
<p>Now let''s look at this term from a grammatical perspective. In language, we have nouns and we have verbs, right? We all know the difference. Verbs, they have tenses, whereas nouns, they don''t. There''s no past tense nouns or present tense nouns or, you know, future tense noun. So now when I say I praise <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, I praise <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, am I using a noun or a verb? I''m using a verb. It''s a present tense verb. But when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says praise belongs to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, is the word praise a noun or a verb? Here it''s a noun. You see, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> could have used a verb.</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/160/c5cc90e2-d33d-4464-a9a9-070907dbb34e.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>He could have said <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>, I praise <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. He could have said <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَحْمَدُ اللهَ</span>, we praise <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> did not use a verb. He used a noun. Why? Because when I use a verb, there''s obviously going to be a tense associated with it. So when I use a present tense to say I praise <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, then I''m only talking about the present. I''m not saying anything about the past or the future. You see? If I use the past tense, then I''m not guaranteeing anything about the future or the present. Because the verb limits and confines the action within time. It makes the action temporary.</p>
<p>But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> used a noun, which by its nature is permanent. Therefore, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s genuine praise and unconditional gratitude, it has been described with permanence. And what that means is that I may be praising <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> right now, but the praise of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has always been there and will always be there. I''m not going to be here in this world forever, but the praise and the gratitude of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is always going to be here forever. His praise and His gratitude is not dependent on me. It''s not dependent on you. It''s not dependent on this world. This is a huge reality encapsulated within <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>, this concept of permanence in the praise and gratitude of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. It is a recognition of the fact that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span> is not dependent on my temporary existence. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span> is not because of me. See, when you use a verb, somebody always needs to do the verb, right?</p>
<p>It''s called the subject, or the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَاتِحَة</span> in Arabic. There has to be a doer for an action. You can''t just go into a conversation and say, failed the test. Who failed the test? Right? You can''t just say disappeared. What disappeared? Oh, your book disappeared. You understand? When you use a verb, you always need someone to do that work. But a noun doesn''t need a doer. A noun doesn''t need a subject. It is independent all by itself, right? An apple is an apple. It can exist all by itself, independent of any doer. So when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> used the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ</span>, He made it a noun. He made it independent, because He doesn''t need anyone to actually do the praising, and He doesn''t need anyone to be grateful. Had He used a verb instead, that would have meant that He needed someone to praise Him and He needed someone to be grateful to Him.</p>
<p>So He removed the construction of time and space by using a noun. Now what this means is that even if there was not a single creation in the world, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> would still be there. It would still exist. Even after this world, this universe fades into non-existence, His praise and His gratitude will remain. Because it does not require a doer or a subject. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. All of this is captured by one single word, used grammatically as a noun.</p>
<p>You know, sometimes we have these questions. Kids sometimes ask this question, like, why does He need to be praised? Why does He need our gratitude? So just the usage of this word, the grammatical way in which the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ</span> is used, clearly answers that question, that He does not need our praise and He does not need our gratitude.</p>
<p>Okay, now let''s look at some other possible variations, because I wanna—I''m just going over this just to show you the perfection that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has used in just one single verse. Imagine the entire <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. Every single verse of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> is perfect in this manner. And so just by appreciating one single verse, we can really appreciate the entire <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَضْرَة</span> Ali Sahib used to give this beautiful example, that if you want to find out that, you know, if you''re cooking rice and you want to know that the rice has been properly cooked, you just check a couple of grains. You don''t check every single grain, right? So I love that analogy, because, you know, that''s exactly what we''re doing. We''re just looking at a couple of verses and studying its perfection, and that pretty much gives us an idea of the entire perfection of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>.</p>
<p>So let''s look at some other variations here. What if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> had said, right, what if He used a command: praise <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, the master of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَالَمِين</span>. Now wouldn''t that have been better? Because after all, He is a master.</p>
<p>So a command would just naturally make sense. So why didn''t He make it a command? I''ll give you an example to help you understand. I tell my daughter to bring me a glass of water. Right? I say, &quot;Hey Adam, bring me a glass of water.&quot; I give it a command. Now once she hears me, she has only two possible choices. She can either bring me the glass of water or she can bring me the glass of water, right? Those are the only two clear choices as far as I''m concerned. So now, if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> commanded us to praise Him, you know, as a command, what are the two things that could have happened?</p>
<p>Some would praise Him, some would obey His command, and then there would be others who would disobey Him. Because when you tell someone to do something, the ball is now in their court. Right? It depends on them to comply with your wishes, with your command. Maybe they''ll choose to do it, maybe they won''t. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> did not talk about His praise and His gratitude in a way that is dependent on us. You see, He did not put the ball in our court. He said, whether you do it or not, whether you are grateful to me or not, whether you choose to praise me or not, my praise and my gratitude, my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span>, is always there. It has always been there, it will be there forever. Human beings will come and go, nations will come and go, generations after generations will come and go, this universe itself will come and go, but the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> will always be there. It is simply a matter of fact.</p>
<p>So now, what if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> had said, certainly, for sure, absolutely no doubt about it, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span> is for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>? Wouldn''t that sound a lot more powerful than just saying all praise and all gratitude belongs to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>? Right? It does sound more powerful, doesn''t it? So how come <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> didn''t say so? If you remember from our sessions on the miracles of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, you know, when we had that topic of conversation, when we talked about conversation and communication itself, I told you there are two kinds of communications, right?</p>
<p>A communication can either be informative or it can be emotional, right? It can be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جُمْلَةٌ إِنْشَائِيَّةٌ</span> or it can be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جُمْلَةٌ خَبَرِيَّةٌ</span>. Either you have speech that is expressing your feelings and emotions, or you have speech that is communicating information. Those are the two kinds of speech that human beings are capable of. That''s it. Right? So for example, right now, as I''m teaching you about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ</span>, am I delivering information or am I expressing my emotions? I am delivering information. This is informative speech. But when I''m talking to my friend and I''m telling my friend how I feel about someone or something, what kind of communication is that? That is an emotional conversation.</p>
<p>So those are the only two types of speech that we as human beings are capable of. The thing is that in Arabic linguistics, when you use <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّا</span>, the statement can only be informative. You see, it can only be factual. It can never be emotional. That''s just a linguistic rule, that if you use <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّا</span> before a statement, it just becomes informative. Indeed, we sent this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> in the night of power. It''s information. It''s factual. It''s not an emotional conversation. Because the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ</span> removes the emotion out of the statement. But if you don''t use the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ</span>, then that communication can be informative, but it can also be emotional. You understand?</p>
<p>So by not using <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> made His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span> an open statement. We can now talk about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s praise and gratitude with other people in order to teach them about it, to give them information about it, but we can also make it a statement to express our own emotions. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. Can you appreciate the beauty of this? So using this style right now, by saying ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> ‎, I am sharing with you, I am teaching you about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span>. A few hours later, when I''m standing in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span> before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> and I use the exact same phrase, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> ‎, I will be expressing my praise and my gratitude to my master. The same statement will now become an emotional conversation for me. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. So you can see how perfect the usage of this word is in the context of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>.</p>
<p>So I hope this really gives you a deeper appreciation of the perfection of this language and how one thing flows into another, how ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> ‎ connects with ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, and how perfect the usage of this word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span> is. So, to summarize this entire concept of praise and gratitude in grammatical terms, we are nouns and we create verbs through our actions.</p>
<p>Right? We are nouns, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> created us as nouns, as beings, as essence and we create verbs through our actions. Now <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is also a noun and He created verbs through His action. This is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> creating us in His own image. Right? Now on a daily basis we are interacting with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> and He is interacting with us, right, through His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبِّيَ</span>, over His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الم</span>, He is interacting with us and we through our worship and obedience and praise, we are interacting with Him. Now, in this interaction, listen to this carefully, in this interaction between us and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, for the recognition of the noun that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is, He is worthy of our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَدَاح</span>. He is worthy of our sincere and independent praise. And for all His actions influencing our existence, He is worthy of our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span>, our ultimate and unconditional gratitude. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span> covers both aspects of our existence and His existence. You see?</p>
<p>For the recognition of the noun that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> is, for the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَعْرِفَةٌ</span> of His essence, which is impossible, but as much as we can learn about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, our natural response to learning about understanding who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> is, He becomes worthy of our sincere praise. And then for what He does for us, how He influences our existence, He becomes worthy of our unconditional gratitude. Both these concepts are beautifully, perfectly, eloquently encapsulated in this one single phrase: all forms of sincere praise belongs to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> for who He is. And all forms of unconditional gratitude, whether it''s on a personal level or a universal level, it belongs to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> for what He did, for what He does and for what He will do. Because His actions are constantly manifesting on the stage that we call the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الم</span>. Therefore, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> ‎.</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَآخِرُ دَعْوَانَا أَنِ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ، وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى رَسُولِهِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ خَاتَمِ الَّذِيِّينَ وَسَيِّدِ الْمُرْسَلِينَ وَعَلَى أَخِيهِ وَوَصِيِّهِ عَلِيِّ بْنِ أَبِي طَالِبٍ أَشْرَفِ الْوَصِيِّينَ وَعَلَى الْأَئِمَّةِ مِنْ ذُرِّيَّتِهِمُ الطَّيِّبِينَ الطَّاهِرِينَ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَيْهِمْ أَجْمَعِينَ، الْفَاتِحَة</span></p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/160/80c720a9-7ae8-4b6b-b674-701c4dfd0b3e.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/160/00660fed-358b-498c-bb18-8cc7ff5c2952.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'linguistic perfection of hamd';
INSERT INTO chapter (slug, anchor_key, idx, title, html, word_count) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'abundant and consistent mercy', 19, 'Abundant and Consistent Mercy', '', 6450);
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ</span> ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ صَلِّ عَلَى سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ وَعَلَى آلِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ كَمَا صَلَّيْتَ وَسَلَّمْتَ وَبَارَكْتَ وَرَحِمْتَ وَتَرَحَّمْتَ عَلَى إِبْرَاهِيمَ وَعَلَى آلِ إِبْرَاهِيمَ فِي الْعَالَمِينَ إِنَّكَ حَمِيدٌ مَجِيدٌ رَبِّ اشْرَحْ لِي صَدْرِي وَيَسِّرْ لِي أَمْرِي وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِنْ لِسَانِي يَفْقَهُوا قَوْلِي السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span></p>
<p>In the previous session, we did a linguistic study of this word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ</span>. We learned that it is a combination of sincere praise and unconditional gratitude that is owed to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> for His constant, relentless, never-ending <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوِيَّة</span> over His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَالَمِينَ</span> that He created for us. And then it''s not just <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْدٌ</span>, but rather it is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ</span>, which means the ultimate praise, and it means the absolute gratitude belongs to Him by virtue of Him being our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبٌّ</span>.</p>
<p>So now the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَة</span> continues and He says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيم</span>, the Abundantly Merciful and the Consistently Merciful. We''ve looked at this word in great detail in one of the previous sessions, so I''m not going to go into the meanings of it linguistically or otherwise so much, but rather today I want to explore some of the examples of His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>. But just a quick word on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> itself is, you know, generally we know that it means mercy. But it is actually a type of mercy that brings two things together. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> is the type of love, the type of mercy that actually brings two things together in a way such that the principles of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span> can be applied to that thing. Right? And this is why the womb of a mother is called a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْم</span>, because it brings the baby, the fetus, and the mother together, so the mother can nurture it, so that she can shower her love over this fetus, so that she can help sustain the fetus and then make it grow. So the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> is so powerful that, you know, our general understanding of these words like mercy, you know, they fall short in really capturing its essence.</p>
<p>So now in this next verse, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, He highlights His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> by dedicating an entire verse just to mention the quality of His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span>. So what according to Him is the essence or the defining attribute of His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span>? It is His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>. It is His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمٰن</span>, His mercy, His love, His care, His concern that manifests through His abundance in His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَانِيَّة</span> and His consistency through His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيمِيَّة</span>. And this is why when we look around us, when we study this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَمْ</span>, we find this one thread of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> flowing through the entire fabric of His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آلم</span>. It is this glue that lovingly holds this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آلم</span> together. It''s that magical magnetic force that just naturally pulls us towards <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> and it keeps us connected with our Master. And so just to put this concept in visual terms, you can imagine <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> as this gravitational field, this force of love that pulls us towards Him. And what we experience from that pull is our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْدٌ</span>, our ultimate praise and our absolute gratitude that is owed to this magnificent, this abundantly and consistently merciful <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبٌّ</span> of this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَالَمِينَ</span>, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيمِ</span>.</p>
<p>Now, if you look at the gratitude aspect or the gratitude component of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ</span>, if we just focus on this one aspect of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span>, what is the reality of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span>? What is the manifestation of sincere gratitude? It''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>, right? It is this constant awareness of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s presence. It is the constant recognition and awareness of His blessings. And this is a very high station. This type of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span>, it holds a very lofty <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقَام</span>, and not everyone is truly capable of achieving these high ranks of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span>. And this is why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says in the Quran, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَقَلِيلٌۭ مِّنْ عِبَادِىَ ٱلشَّكُورُ</span>. Very, very few of My servants are truly <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَكُورٌ</span>. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَكُور</span> is very different from the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَاكِرٌ</span>. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَاكِرٌ</span>, the grateful, is the one who is grateful for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s blessings. But the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَكُور</span>, he''s the one who''s grateful for calamities and tribulations also, not just blessings. He actually feels gratitude when horrible things happen to him.</p>
<p>Because most people at best, they''ll become patient or resigned, but the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَكُور</span>, they actually say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span> as they are experiencing these calamities, because the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَكُور</span>, they know that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> only does good for the believers, right? There''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيثٌ</span> in which <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span> said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَجَبًا لِأَمْرِ الْمُؤْمِنِ فَإِنَّهُ أَمْرُهُ لَهُ كُلُّهُ خَيْرٌ</span>. How wondrous is the affair of the believer! His affair, all of it is good. So if you''re a true believer, then it''s all good. And that''s why if you really do believe, you enter into the state of constant gratitude, even for the tribulations. It''s always <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>. And always a good thing to remember is what Ibn Abbas <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَضِيَ اللهُ</span> said, that no matter how bad it is, it could always be worse. And there are people like that. There are even in the non-Muslim community, you''ll find people like that, you know, they always look at the good side. Well, it could be worse. Think about this, you know, count your blessings. So there are people that just naturally, instinctively have this understanding. Now, in this verse, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says He is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبٌّ</span> that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيمِ</span>. How does His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> manifest in our lives? Today I just want to focus on how His love presents itself in our lives. How does <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> demonstrate His love for us? Through His blessings, right?</p>
<p>Through His gifts, His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِرْمَا</span>. When I as a parent, when I want to demonstrate my love for my children, how do I do that? By bestowing gifts upon them, right? Valuable, desirable, beneficial gifts. Gifts that the child understands that he or she could not attain by their own efforts. Now think about the blessings, the gifts that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has bestowed upon all of us. Incredible gifts, infinite gifts. If you attempt to enumerate the blessings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, you will never be able to enumerate them. Gifts that are not only impossible to count, their value is just impossible to measure. Just look at one single <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعَمَ</span>. Look at the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعَمَ</span>, for instance, of the eye. That is just one <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعَمَ</span>, sight.</p>
<p>First of all, think about this, blinking. Just think about blinking. You don''t even notice that you blink. Now, if you didn''t blink, have you ever seen pictures of Aristotle Onassis? You know, when he got really old, the richest man, one of the richest men in the world, he owned his own private fleet of ships, he had to use band-aids to hold up his eyelids because he lost the function of the muscle that returns the eyelid and his eyelids just dropped all the time. So he used band-aids to hold them up, but then he had to put drops in his eyes all the time. Because if your eyelids don''t close, they get dry and you start getting dust in them. Because one of the things that blinking does is it brings the dust back down. You see, they''re like windshield wipers, right? They bring the dust down and then what happens is that the dust, it just moves to the side and accumulates in the corners of the eye. So when you wipe your eyes, you''ll get some of that dust out of the eye. And you don''t even think about that. That''s just one part of the eye.</p>
<p>But then if you think about the intricate network of these nerves behind the eye and the myelination of that sheath, the fact that there''s this constant synaptic responses, the fact that there''s neural transmission, because that breaks down for some people, right? They go blind, they lose the function of the sight. I mean, we don''t think about all of this. Think about the electric work that goes in the function of an eye. Colors, just think about colors. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> could have made everything black and white, right? Look at all the subtle colors that He put in this world that we can experience. Our eyes can experience an incredible variety of colors. I mean, that''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِرْمَا</span>. Then look how, you know, how some of these colors, they just give you pleasure in just seeing them. You don''t even think about it.</p>
<p>Today we know that there''s vibration. If you look at color, there are seven colors. All colors go down to seven primary colors. I think in color theory they go down to three, but basically everybody agrees that there are seven colors in a spectrum. If you look at visible light, each one of these colors has an effect upon the soul, right? Red has a certain vibrational resonance. It''s a lower resonance than violet. Violet is at the high spectrum, red is at the low spectrum. Red, when you see the color red, it stimulates you. You don''t even know that. They know that now in color theory because, you know, all the study that''s been done on people, that if you put them in red rooms, it has a different effect on them than if you put them in blue rooms. It''ll actually change their moods because these colors have a resonance.</p>
<p>there''s an electromagnetic radiation and it''s all having a psychological effect on us and we don''t even know it. Now, the thing about the world: if you go out and look at green, green will make you feel good, right? That''s why people love to look at grass, they love to visit these gardens, they love to look at trees. You know, it has a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يُنَعِّش</span>, as the Arabs would say. Yeah, it''s called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَابِعَةٌ</span>, spring. This is why we say in our prayers that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> make the recital of Quran the spring of my heart: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ اجْعَلِ الْقُرْآنَ رَبِيعَ قَلْبِي</span>. What does that mean? Because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَابِعَةٌ</span> is that thing which brings your heart back to life, because spring brings your whole being back to life. People start feeling good. People get this thing, they call it SAD, Seasonal Affective Disorder, SAD. There are people who get depressed in the wintertime. It''s an actual psychological state. But then spring comes around and they forget how depressed they were in the winter. They start feeling wonderful. Why are they feeling wonderful now? Green. You see, the color starts changing.</p>
<p>There''s photosynthesis that''s happening. All this chlorophyll is starting to come back to life. Flowers start blooming again. Flowers. Look at the flowers. I mean, what is that? A flower? Why do people like smelling a flower? That''s another name of scent. Just the sight of a beautiful flower. Just the sight, right? Beauty.</p>
<p>When the eye looks at something beautiful, why do we all respond the same way? When you see something beautiful, and most people, we all share a common taste in beauty, when we see something beautiful, we''re attracted to it, right? There''s an attraction. Why? It''s because beauty is an attribute of God, and what you''re seeing in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الٓمٓ</span> is a reflection of that divine attribute of your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبٌّ</span>. So you''re essentially attracted to the divine, but you think you''re being attracted to the flower. No, it''s the beauty of the divine manifesting through the flower. That is real attraction. Real attraction is not in this ephemeral beauty that fades away. It''s the manifestation of the divine in creation that''s the real attraction. But we don''t realize that. So there are all these things about the eye that we just don''t think about. I mean, look at the eyelashes, the eyebrows. They''re serving a purpose, right? Eyelashes serve a purpose.</p>
<p>I mean, when anything comes near our eye, we instinctively close our eyelids to protect the eye. I mean, that''s just amazing. So there are all these things about just the eye that if we really start thinking about it, I mean, look at the abundance in just the eye, the abundance in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span> of the eye. Just look at the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَانِيَّة</span> of this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>, with one single gift. And that''s based on what we just know so far. There must be so many blessings that we don''t even know about yet. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. What about light? Right? The external factor that makes sight possible, that makes the eye useful, that makes the eye functional, which in turn makes people happy or depressed.</p>
<p>Why do people get depressed? You know, one of the reasons they think is because the serotonin level, as they drop, which is the key hormone in making us feel happy. That''s what they call serotonin. They call it the happy hormone, right? And one of its factors of release is based on light. So you''re taking in light during the day and you''re feeling good and happy and cheerful and you don''t even think about it. There are all these hormones that are related to melatonin. That''s based on light, right? Melatonin is what makes us sleepy. That''s why people that are constantly in artificial light, they have difficulty sleeping. But the eye is what''s doing all of that, right? The eye is dictating.</p>
<p>That''s why we get tired. It''s all based on the eye. So even our sleep patterns are being affected by the human eye. And we don''t even realize that. When we feel awake, when we feel drowsy, it''s all the result of the eye. And this is just the eye alone. We cannot even enumerate all the blessings of the eye, which is one <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span>. This is just <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةُ الْعَيْنِ</span> that we''re talking about here. We cannot even enumerate the blessings of this one organ. There are untold, infinite blessings in it. And it''s just one <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِرْمَا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ أَكْبَرُ</span>.</p>
<p>So the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَكُور</span> is the one who is constantly experiencing the state of gratitude, its presence and the lack of it. But this comes from this absolute certainty and this is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقَام</span>, this is a station that is related to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِضًا</span>, which is complete contentment with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. It''s a state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَحَبَّة</span>. It''s a state of this intense love because once you fall in love, you just cannot see anything else. I mean, there''s this incredible beauty of love. You know, when you''re in love, you can''t see faults, right? Even when they do exist, you just can''t see them. If you love Laila and anybody tells you, you know, Laila did this or she did that, you get angry. Why? What? Laila would never do that. No, no, no.</p>
<p>I saw Laila did this. You''re lying. You''re a liar. Laila would never do that. Why? Because he''s in love with Laila. No matter what you say about Laila, he''s not going to believe you. You know, that''s real love. People don''t even know that type of love anymore. So if you''re in love with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, He just cannot do anything that would harm you or hurt you. Impossible, nothing, because you''ll say it''s me. You see, I did it to myself. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> didn''t do that. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> would never do that. I did that to myself, or that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is doing it because, you know, like the father that punishes the child. You see, I mean, you see a father punishing the child. The father loves the child. Why is he punishing it? Because he knows that in that punishment is benefit for the child. He''s disciplining the child, right?</p>
<p>He''s bringing the child back on the right track. Through that punishment, he''s reminding the child of something worse. You know, if I see my son putting his hand in the fire, I''ll slap his hand. Why am I doing that? Because I want him to taste a light pain in order to avoid a much greater pain, you see? Because I know that the pain of this slap is nothing compared to the pain of the flame. And when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> gives us a tribulation, those are nothing compared to Hellfire. Nothing. So if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is purifying us to prevent us from doing something that He doesn''t want us to do, then the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَكُور</span> realizes that, that this tribulation is the love of His Creator for him.</p>
<p>You know, the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>, he once saw a mother nursing her child. You know, and this is a man, as they say, you know, the man of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> takes discernment from everything, right? He takes <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبْرَة</span>, a discernment, a lesson from everything. His glance is discernment and his speech is wisdom. So, there''s always a reason why he speaks. So, the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>, he once saw a woman nursing her child, and he said to his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحَابَة</span>, Do you think that this woman would throw her child into the Hellfire? And they said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا وَاللَّهِ يَا رَسُولَ اللَّهِ</span>. She would never do that. He said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has more mercy for His servants than this woman has for her child. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ أَرْحَمُ بِعِبَادِهِ مِنْ هَذِهِ الْأُمِّ لِوَلَدِهَا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has more mercy for His servants than this woman has for her child.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, you know, in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْإِسْرَاءِ</span>, when He talked about His Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نُوحٌ عَلَيْهِ السَّلَامُ</span>, He said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّهُۥ كَانَ عَبْدًۭا شَكُورًۭا</span>. Indeed, he was a grateful slave, because of the amount of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَعْوَة</span> that he did. You know, people today they get fed up after a week of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَعْوَة</span>, they say, you know, they give advice two times, three times and that''s it. Oh, you know, I give up, these people never listen. I mean, this is 950 years of doing what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> asked him to do. That''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَكُور</span>. And one of the things mentioned in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السِّيرَةُ</span> is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نُوحٌ عَلَيْهِ السَّلَامُ</span>, he never once went to relieve himself, except that he came back saying, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي أَذْهَبَ عَنِّي الْأَذَى</span>. Praise and gratitude be to the one who removed this harm from me. Even just having <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span> for something like relieving himself.</p>
<p>Because what a blessing that is, right? I mean, think about — you know, I don''t know if you''ve ever read these stories of Bahlol. As kids, we used to read them. And there''s this one story of Bahlol in which he went to visit Harun Rashid, the palace of Harun Rashid, who was a king at that time. And he asked Harun Rashid, you know, would you like anything? And Harun said, bring me back a glass of water. So they brought him water. As he was about to drink it, Bahlol asked, if you will refuse this water, right, you were dying of thirst, but you were refused this water, what would you give for this water? And he said, I would give half of my kingdom for it if I couldn''t have it. And then Bahlol said, if you drank this water and then you couldn''t release it, what would you give to release it? He said, I''d give the rest of my kingdom. So Bahlol asked, then what is the worth of your kingdom that is not worth a cup of water or the release of it as urine? In other words, what do you really possess? You know, if it, all this kingdom that you possess, it doesn''t even equal a cup of water or the release of it.</p>
<p>So think of that, the blessing of just eating, drinking, and digesting it. I mean, you don''t have to tell your hydrochloric acid to be released. You don''t have to push any buttons here, right? You eat something and if it''s protein, then it just breaks down into amino acids and your body then reassembles them as muscle tissue. If it''s fat, it breaks down into fatty acids, then your body reassembles them. If it''s carbs, it breaks them down, takes them to the brain, produces energy, right? If you have extra, then insulin is released, takes it to the liver, stores it as glycogen, so that when later on you''re out of energy, the liver can release that glycogen, break it down for you, and produce energy. You don''t have to do any of this. You don''t have to tell the body to do any of this, and yet it''s all doing that for us. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ أَكْبَرُ</span>.</p>
<p>I mean, that''s what — how to be grateful for the blessings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. Islam is all about being grateful to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. That''s what Islam is. We just, just the simplest of things. Right now, we''re all at 98.6 degrees.</p>
<p>Who do you think is maintaining our bodies at 98.6 right now? Seriously, who''s maintaining that? You know, right now, I could have a fever and I could be shivering. If I was sick right now, right? But if I was sick right now, even though it''s a moderate temperature in the room, I could be shivering, I could be feeling cold, and it wouldn''t even be cold. If I had a fever, I would be boiling up right now in a moderate climate. You''d all be fine, but if I had a fever, I would, you know, want to take off all my clothes and I''d be all hot and sweaty. Think about that. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is keeping us all at 98.6. Even if it''s 100 degrees outside, our bodies are releasing fluid as sweat in order to maintain this 98.6 on average. Even if it''s 100 degrees outside, why don''t we boil up to 100 degrees? What created that inner thermostat that keeps us all at this balance? If it''s cold out, why don''t we all drop down and freeze? Because we would if, you know, if we go out in ice cold temperatures long enough, we''ll freeze. But for a period of time, we won''t.</p>
<p>We''ll all stay at 98.6. Why? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ أَكْبَرُ</span>. We go swimming in cold water and our temperature maintains itself. This is all from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. All manifestation of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَانِيَّة</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيمِيَة</span> of our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ</span>.</p>
<p>And the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَكُور</span> is constantly aware of the abundance of these blessings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span>, but then they also look at those that are less fortunate than them in their worldly affairs. They use that as a resource to draw even more gratitude, right? There''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيثُ سُورَةِ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span> in which he said about this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> that you should always look at people who are less fortunate than you are in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>, because you''ll always feel grateful. There''s always people who are less fortunate than you are today. I guarantee you, no matter how miserable you think you are, you''ll always find people in much more misery than you are right now. So you say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>.</p>
<p>I mean, if you don''t have a lot of money, you know, in this country, the United States, or even most of us living in Pakistan and India, we''re considered well off, we''re considered, you know, decently rich. In biblical times, to put a jar of honey on your table was considered a sign of immense wealth. Think about that. A jar of honey on your table was a sign of great wealth. Really. The majority of people living in America today would be considered royalty in previous times. The houses that we live in, they would be considered palaces.</p>
<p>The facilities that we have, this idea of having running water, just running water. The fact that you can take a hot shower just by turning a knob, people don''t even think about this. There are places today in this world, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَاللهِ</span>, there are women who have the sole job of bringing water every day. It takes them about two hours to go get the water. It takes about an hour to fill up, you know, these <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قِرْبَة</span>, what they call. Loaded on the back of donkeys in these villages and then two hours to come back home. Five hours, five hours every day just to bring drinking water to their village, and that''s their entire life. Can you imagine what they must feel when they drink water? What do you think they would say after all that, five hours of hard work, when they take that first sip? What do you think they say? ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> ‎.</p>
<p>That water, it''s brown. Most of them have kidney problems. Most of them have urinary problems because of the sediment in that water. People have forgotten this. We could have been born in that village. You and I could have been born in that village, drinking that brown water, having those kidney problems. I mean, we never think about these things, do we? Just think about the blessings, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعَمَ</span> of this house that we live in. Think about the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعَمَ</span> of water, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعَمَ</span> of food on our table, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعَمَ</span> of clothes to cover up our nakedness, to keep us warm. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span> of a bed. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span> of just being healthy. I mean <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةُ الْعَافِيَةِ</span>, that you got up in the morning and you weren''t sick.</p>
<p>Think of how many people got up this morning and they had to empty out a bag that had their urine in it. A bag that they have to change every hour. Think of how many people that got up this morning that couldn''t go to the toilet or they bled blood. Think how many people there are with asthma. They couldn''t breathe this morning. They woke up gasping for air. Really? Think about how many people spend their last night coughing. They couldn''t sleep because of this cough. Really? That''s why, I mean <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span>, showing gratitude to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, you really have to wonder about our lives, right? Just the blessings that the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> has bestowed upon us and our complaints, my God! Our constant never-ending complaints against God.</p>
<p>What did I do to deserve this? What have I done to this? You exist. That''s enough as a crime, really. If you really think about this, that''s enough as a crime, to think that you exist beside <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. To think that we share existence with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. We are criminals. Just for having that thought, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ</span>. There is nothing worthy of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَهِيَّة</span> except <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. And yet here we are, right, walking around, just thinking, you know, these little contingent creatures living on this insignificant planet, in this insignificant galaxy, that are totally dependent for every breath, at every instant, on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, and we think we are independent. We think we are self-sufficient.</p>
<p>We think we are in control. If I''m in such a state of heedlessness with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, I''m a criminal. If I forget <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> for one instant, I am a criminal and I stand condemned. And then anything that happens to me, I deserve it. Because I''ve forgotten who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is and who I am in relation to that. Oh man! What has deluded you about your master? What has deluded you about your Lord? Why are you in the state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غُرُور</span>? Total delusion. Thinking that you don''t need me at every instant of your life. Just to draw your next breath. Just to have your next heartbeat.</p>
<p>I mean heartbeat. What is a heartbeat? An electric impulse. Who''s activating it? It''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. You think it''s your brain. It''s not your brain. Your brain is not doing that. They don''t even know what''s doing it. I mean, you can cut all your nerves to the brain and the heart will still beat, right? What makes the heart beat? And yet, here we are, these insignificant creatures complaining about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, judging Him for His faults.</p>
<p>That''s a crime. Or we sit on our high horses, you know, right next to Him judging others. Our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُلَمَاء</span>, I mean just looking down upon others based on their knowledge that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has bestowed them with. That''s not why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> gave you that knowledge, to look down on others. He created us all as brothers to embrace each other. See, there''s two ways to embracing, right? There''s the embrace of an equal. And then there is the embrace of the superior. Did you ever think about that? What''s the embrace of an equal? You embrace somebody who''s the same as you. If I embrace you, you''re my brother.</p>
<p>We have an equal embrace. We''re the same height. I put my arms around you. You put your arms around me and we have an embrace. That''s the embrace of an equal. What''s the embrace of a superior to an inferior? How do you embrace a child? The child can put his arms around you. You pick them up, right? You lift them and you embrace them and you hold them. See, that''s Islam. That massage, that temperament, the temperament of Islam is to take all these other sects and religions and those who are inferior in knowledge and then just embrace them.</p>
<p>That''s the embrace of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. He is so great that He will even allow somebody that denies Him to exist. I mean, if you want to think about greatness, that is greatness. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>. He''ll feed them, He''ll nurture them, give them rain, keep their bodies healthy, give them a wife, children that love Him. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> does all of that, even though that person denies His very existence. But all that does is show the greatness of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>.</p>
<p>You know, there''s a story of Ibrahim <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَيْهِ سَلَامٌ</span> that one night, it was famous about him that he never ate alone, right, Ibrahim <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَيْهِ سَلَامٌ</span>? He always found somebody to eat with. But one night he couldn''t find anybody, so he went out looking for someone and then he finally found this man and he brought him home. And this man was a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُشْرِك</span>. So before this man ate his food he blessed it in the name of his idols, and when Ibrahim heard that he said, you know, you can''t eat any of my food, you''re an idol worshiper, get out of here. So the man left. But then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> revealed to Ibrahim, He said, Ibrahim, I have been feeding him for 40 years. And I''ve been patient with his idolatry for 40 years. You couldn''t be patient with him for just one meal. And so, Ibrahim, he went and he found that man, he apologized to him, invited him back to his table. And the man asked why? What suddenly changed? And he told him. And this man became a Muslim. That is Islam. You know. This ability to embrace everyone. I mean that''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَعْوَة</span>.</p>
<p>What is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَعْوَة</span>? In Arabic, even in Urdu, you say, you know, what is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَعْوَة</span>? What does that word mean? It''s an invitation, isn''t it? Now if I put a gun to your head and I say you''re coming to dinner tonight at my house, is that an invitation? Seriously, is that an invitation? It''s not. You''re not inviting someone, you''re forcing them to come. And you know, there''s no compulsion in religion. An invitation is only valid if you allow the person the possibility of refusing that invitation. Now, who would allow the person that possibility? A generous host, right?</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has made a house, He called it <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَارُ السَّلَامِ</span>, He invited all of His creation to it and He gave them the option to refuse to come to that house. So if people refuse it, why are we so fearful of them? Why are we so afraid of them? Right? See, the thing about people, you know, that when they are ignorant, they fear. That''s what a fundamentalist is, that''s what an extremist is. It is somebody who''s afraid of the other. He''s afraid of having his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span> challenged. He''s so afraid that he won''t even allow for the other to exist because he can''t. He''s not going to allow that possibility. He wants them eliminated, my way or the highway. And we have this in our own community at our own levels, right? If you''re not going to follow what I''m saying, that''s it. And that''s really a disease. That''s not Islam. Islam is the only religion that''s ever allowed other religions, other ideologies to exist side by side with it, because it creates this environment of invitation. It allows the other people to experience something different and then be attracted to it.</p>
<p>But because we present this harshness in our behaviors and then call it Islam, no wonder people are getting turned off from Islam in multitudes. Because we have no care about a fellow human being. We don''t look at the other person as a human being. We look at them as either Muslim, non-Muslim, Christian, Jew, believer, non-believer, in agreement with me, in disagreement with me, my enemy, right? There''s no... One of the great scholars of Islam, he said that the door that he came to his Lord through was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَعْظِيمُ الْمَخْلُوقَاتِ</span>, exalting the creation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. He said, ... I never saw a scholar except I exalted him because of the knowledge that he was carrying. But then it got to a point where <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَا رَأَيْتُمْ مُؤْمِنًا أَوْ مُسْلِمًا مَهْمَا كَانَ حَالُهُ إِلَّا عَظَّمْتَهُ لِانْتِسَابِهِ الْعُبُودِيَّةَ لِلَّهِ</span>. I never saw a believer or a Muslim man, no matter what his state was, but I exalted him just for being a servant of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. Just for attempting to submit to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p>But then he said that I didn''t see anyone, even the non-Muslims, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَّا أَعْظَمَ طَاعَتِهِ لِأَنَّهُ مَحَلُّ الْإِرَادَةِ اللَّهِ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى حَيْثُ أَوْجَدَهُ بِقَوْلِهِ كُنْ فَيَكُونُ</span>. Except that I never saw anyone, anything in this universe except that I exalted it because through his existence, I saw the will of the divine manifesting before my eyes. So he said I never saw a rock or a tree or anything or anyone except that I saw it as a manifestation of the divine will. It was God that put that thing there. That''s what he was acknowledging. And then he said the one who exalts the sanctified things of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, he is the one who is most loved by <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. And what is more sanctified in this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الٓمٓ</span> than the creation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>? These things that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has created as His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَجَلِّي</span>, right? His divine manifestations that invite us all to marvel over it, praise it. They draw out from us the praise component of this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْدٌ</span>. I mean, if you look at people, right? When you see people who can do something slightly amazing, you see this beautiful singer singing a song in his or her beautiful voice or playing an instrument in a concert. What do people do? They just clap, right?</p>
<p>They just stand up, whistle and clap. They do all these things. They give this artist a standing ovation, to praise him or her because of what''s happening in their hearts. They just see something extraordinary and they want to praise it. They cannot help themselves because they just saw something that affected their soul. So when they see somebody who mastered the piano, for instance, you know, who got up and played some extraordinary piece of Beethoven, they just stand up and they clap and they clap and they clap. And the point is to say what an extraordinary thing you''ve just done before my eyes, right? It was truly incredible. That''s what they feel like doing because they find it so amazing. And yet we''re here in this incredible existence, this theater of amazing creations, stars in the heavens, galaxies of them. You know, we''ve got Hubble telescopes that look into the furthest corners of the universe, into this massive expansiveness. We have flowers that bloom before our eyes that demonstrate these beautiful colors, this incredible scent. We watch flowers that move across as the sun moves in the sky, you know, flowers that follow the sun.</p>
<p>There are, the Arabs call them <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدُ شَمْسٍ</span>, you know, slaves of the sun. We have animals that do the most extraordinary things. Elephants that can carry these extraordinary weight on their backs. But then ants, little tiny ants, the most extraordinary in their creation, that they can carry many times their own weight. All of these things are happening right before our eyes. We have women with the fetus developing in their wombs. You know, mitotic cell division happening at these rapid rates. All of these things happening everywhere, constantly within us, outside of us. And we don''t feel compelled to give <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> a standing ovation. To stand before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> at least five times a day and say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ أَكْبَرُ، اللهُ أَكْبَرُ، اللهُ أَكْبَرُ</span>. Really to stand in ovation before our master, like people stand before some poor soul who has perfected some little trick on a musical instrument, can just pipe out a tune, and we''re so compelled to jump up out of our seats and clap and, you know, Bravo, Bravo. And we can just get up to praise the master of this world, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيم</span>, and just say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ أَكْبَرُ</span>. I mean, really, what is wrong with us?</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>.</p>
<p>What has deluded us? What has taken us away from the praise and the gratitude of our master? I just am so guilty of all of this. It''s so hard to even admit and acknowledge this, but we''re all in the same boat of ingratitude. And our Prophet constantly went into <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَجْدَة</span> out of gratitude and praise for every little thing. He saw an eclipse. He went and gave God an ovation. He prayed. He stood up and, you know, he prayed, went into <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَجْدَة</span> as a testimony that he was witnessing something incredible. He was witnessing a reminder of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَانِيَّة</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيمِيَّة</span> of his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبٌّ</span>. Everything, every instant was a reminder for him. And yet here we are, blessed beyond measures.</p>
<p>May <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> strengthen our hearts to witness and to experience, and give us this ability to just marvel over the blessings of His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَانِيَّة</span> and His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِيمِيَّة</span>. Because may He help us experience the joy of true and sincere praise and unconditional gratitude for not only everything that He has done for us, but also for what He has withheld from us. For not only the joy in our lives, but also for the grief and the sorrows that humble us, that remind us, that break our intoxication of this world, that sober us, that pull us off our high horses and just throw us down on the floor. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى رَسُولِهِ سَيِّدِ الْمُحَمَّدِينَ خَاتِمِ النَّبِيِّينَ وَسَيِّدِ الْمُرْسَلِينَ وَعَلَى آلِهِ وَصَحْبِهِ عَلِيٍّ النَّبِيِّ طَالِبٍ أَشْرَفِ الْوَصِيِّينَ</span>.</p>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'abundant and consistent mercy';
INSERT INTO chapter (slug, anchor_key, idx, title, html, word_count) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'day of judgment - linguistics', 20, 'Day Of Judgment - Linguistics', '', 2951);
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ</span> ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ صَلِّ عَلَى سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ وَعَلَى آلِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ وَبَارِكْ وَسَلِّمْ كَمَا صَلَّيْتَ وَسَلَّمْتَ وَبَارَكْتَ وَرَحِمْتَ وَتَرَحَّمْتَ عَلَى إِبْرَاهِيمَ وَعَلَى آلِ إِبْرَاهِيمَ فِي الْعَالَمِينَ إِنَّكَ حَمِيدٌ مَجِيدٌ رَبِّ اشْرَحْ لِي صَدْرِي وَيَسِّرْ لِي أَمْرِي وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِنْ لِسَانِي يَفْقَهُوا قَوْلِي</span></p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span> In the last few sessions, we completed our discussion on these first three verses of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>: ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> ‎, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span>, all of which are a testimony to Allah''s abundant and consistent love and grace. He is the master of everything that exists in creation, and for that He is owed <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْحَمْدُ</span>, which is sincere praise and unconditional gratitude.</p>
<p>Today we''re going to look at the next verse, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span>. We''ll begin with a linguistic analysis of these three words to get a better understanding of exactly what this means. So this verse, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span>, there are two forms of recitation for this verse. There is the Kufi style of recitation, which is what we recite generally, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span>, but there''s another school of thought which recites this as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span>, and actually both forms are valid. So we''ll look at both variations.</p>
<p>The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span>, as we know by now, it means an owner, right? Even in Urdu the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span> means owner. And it comes from these three root letters, mem, lam and kaf, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ</span>, and the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ</span> actually means property. So the one who owns property is called the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span> of that property, right? Now there''s another word in Arabic that shares the same three root letters, and that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُلْك</span>, which means kingdom, and the one who rules over a kingdom is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span>, king.</p>
<p>So Allah talks about both in the Quran. There is this concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِلْقِيَةٌ</span> and there is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُلْقِيَة</span>. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِلْقِيَةٌ</span> means ownership and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُلْقِيَة</span> means kingship. And Allah ascribes both these attributes to Himself. So He is not only the owner, He is also the king. Because He is the one who judges, the one who decrees, who legislates, who has the ultimate authority over everything in His kingdom.</p>
<p>There is no one above the king that a plaintiff can plead his case with. So, Allah says in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الزُّمَرِ</span>, He says, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَا قَدَرُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ حَقَّ قَدْرِهِۦ وَٱلْأَرْضُ جَمِيعًۭا قَبْضَتُهُۥ يَوْمَ ٱلْقِيَمَةِ وَٱلسَّمَوَتُ مَطْوِيَّتٌۢ بِيَمِينِهِۦ ۚ سُبْحَنَهُۥ وَتَعَلَىٰ عَمَّا يُشْرِكُونَ</span> ‎ They did not give Allah a just estimation that was due to Him. And on the day of the resurrection, the entire earth will be grasped by His hand and heavens will be rolled up in His right hand. Now, what does He mean by having it all in His right hand? It basically means, it''s an ancient Arabic expression that actually means under His complete control, right? This is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِلْقِيَةٌ</span>, complete ownership.</p>
<p>In another verse, He says, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُلِ ٱللَّهُمَّ مَلِكَ ٱلْمُلْكِ تُؤْتِى ٱلْمُلْكَ مَن تَشَآءُ وَتَنزِعُ ٱلْمُلْكَ مِمَّن تَشَآءُ وَتُعِزُّ مَن تَشَآءُ وَتُذِلُّ مَن تَشَآءُ ۖ بِيَدِكَ ٱلْخَيْرُ ۖ إِنَّكَ عَلَىٰ كُلِّ شَىْءٍۢ قَدِيرٌۭ</span> ‎ So, this verse begins by saying that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُلِ ٱللَّهُمَّ مَلِكَ ٱلْمُلْكِ</span>, declare that Allah is the king of all kingdoms. Right? Here He talks about Himself as being the king, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span>. So you can see that both forms are used in the Quran. He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span> and He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span>.</p>
<p>He is the owner of the day of judgment and He is also the king of the day of judgment. And both these terms have this implication that nobody will be able to question Him about any of His decisions, and He alone will pass all judgment and make all the final decisions. So that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span>.</p>
<p>The next word is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْم</span>, which is actually quite an interesting word in Arabic, like <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span>. Now generally this statement is translated as a day, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْم</span> is translated as day, owner of the day of judgment. But linguistically, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْم</span> does not actually mean a 24-hour day. What it means is a time interval. It can mean any period of time. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْم</span> could be used for a moment. It could be used for an hour. It could mean a day, it could mean a month, a year. It could also mean several thousand years.</p>
<p>So, it is simply a duration of time allocated for, you know, a specific purpose. That''s what a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْمِ</span> is. So, for instance, in the Quran, Allah says, ... The angels and the spirit ascend to Him during a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْمِ</span>, the extent of which is 50,000 years. So, here Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> specifies <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْمِ</span> as being equal to 50,000 years. In <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْمُزَّمِّلِ</span>, He says, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْمَ تَرْجُفُ ٱلْأَرْضُ وَٱلْجِبَالُ وَكَانَتِ ٱلْجِبَالُ كَثِيبًۭا مَّهِيلًا</span> ‎. That moment when the earth and the mountains will convulse and you will see that mountains will turn into a heap of sand that is being poured down. Here, He uses the same word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْم</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span>, for a moment. So the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْم</span> can be used for various periods of time, including time itself. So, in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>, He says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span>. Owner of the time period of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span>. Right?</p>
<p>So, now let''s look at <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span>. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span> in the Arabic language comes from the root letters <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَيْنٍ</span>, which has several meanings. One of the meanings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَيْنٍ</span> is, you know, it is a type of rain that falls over and over again until it brings the dead earth back to life. Another meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَيْنٍ</span> is to discipline something. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْ دَنَا نَفْسُهُ</span>, whoever disciplines his soul. It also means a way of life. ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَكُمْ دِينُكُمْ وَلِىَ دِينِ</span> ‎, you have your way of life and we have ours. Right? But the root meaning of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَيْنٍ</span> actually means debt. Our religion is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينُ الإِسْلَامِ</span>, the Deen of Islam, and literally this can be translated as the debt of submission. In other words, the idea is that we have been endowed with innumerable and invaluable blessings by our Master.</p>
<p>And so for all those blessings, we owe our Creator a debt of submission. Allah says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَفَغَيْرَ دِينِ ٱللَّهِ يَبْغُونَ</span> (Do they seek other than the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span> of Allah?) <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَهُۥٓ أَسْلَمَ مَن فِى ٱلسَّمَوَتِ وَٱلْأَرْضِ طَوْعًۭا وَكَرْهًۭا</span>, while all creatures in the heaven and the earth have bowed down to Him in submission, willingly or unwillingly. So what Allah is asking here is that do they seek other than repaying this debt when in reality everyone and everything in existence is paying its debt to its Creator? They''re all in a state of submission, willingly or unwillingly.</p>
<p>Now, there are areas of submission that the human being recognizes. One of them is our call to nature. We have to do certain things that we just cannot avoid. And one of them is, for instance, to eat. That is part of our nature. When we get hungry we must eat or we die. We need to drink water when we are thirsty. If we don''t drink, we die. We have to relieve ourselves once we have eaten or taken drink. We don''t really have a choice in these matters, do we? We have to do them whether we want to or not. We have to sleep. When sleep comes upon us, we can fight it for a while, but eventually we have to submit or we go into acute psychosis.</p>
<p>So there is this idea of willing or unwilling submission to nature itself. But then there is also submission to our own beings, to our own physical bodies. We have limitations. We can eat, but there is only so much that we can eat. We can only drink so much. We can only do so much physical work before we get exhausted. Right? We have limitations in our movements because of the structure of our bodies. So there is a type of submission of the self, given that we are temporal creatures that are limited by our physical aspects.</p>
<p>Now the Arabic word for a boundary or a limit is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>. So all of creation have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُدُودٌ</span>, right? We are all confined within our limits, within our boundaries. We cannot go outside those boundaries. Water cannot flow upwards. A rock cannot fly. A bird cannot bark. A dog cannot sing. Honey will always stay sweet. Citrus will always stay sour.</p>
<p>So in the Quran, there is this idea of limitations, that everything is in a state of submission to its limitations. Every creature is confined within its boundaries, its <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُدُود</span>, and they are confined within it willingly or unwillingly. So in other words, they are all in a state of submission. And then Allah follows the statement with a reminder that they will all be returned back to their owner. And to Him is their return. Now if you deeply reflect upon creation itself, you''ll see that there are things that are emerging and then they are returning back into non-existence. Everything that has life comes into existence and then returns back into non-existence. Plants, vegetables, the animal kingdom, and ultimately even the mineral kingdom, they''re all returning to non-existence after they came into existence.</p>
<p>So in other words, everything that is in existence is paying back its debt to its creator, except the human beings, you see. It is only human beings who are not actively paying back their debt of submission, their <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينُ الإِسْلَامِ</span>. But the difference here is that we are also not being asked about our debts right now. So when will we be asked? And who will ask us about these debts? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span>, the owner and the king of that time period when the debt of submission falls due. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْمِ الدِّينِ</span> is that time period when the debt of submission falls due. It''s payback time. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَيْفَمَا تُدِينُ تُدَانُ</span>. How you transact with others is the way you will be transacted with. This is why this time period is also called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْمُ الْحِسَابِ</span>, the time of accountability. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span> said in one of his Hadiths, he said, take yourself to account before you are taken to account.</p>
<p>So there is a debt of submission that we owe Allah. But then there is also a debt that Allah will owe us on that day. You see? When a master issues a command to his slave, the fulfillment of that command is a debt that the slave owes to his master, because there are only two possibilities: he either obeys the command or he disobeys it. In both cases, a return or a recompense falls due upon the master. You see, if the slave obeyed his master and executed his command properly, then his master owes him a reward. But if he disobeyed his master''s command, the master now owes him punishment. That''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْمِ الدِّينِ</span>, that''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْمُ الْحِسَابِ</span>, the day when that debt falls due. This is why Allah calls upon people to actually owe him a debt. He says in the Quran, who will indebt Allah with a beautiful debt? So Allah is asking us for a loan. Who will indebt Allah with a beautiful debt?</p>
<p>Why is Allah asking us for a loan? So that Allah multiplies its rewards many, many times over and returns it back to his life. So in this verse, Allah is actually asking His slaves, who among you will give me a loan? Right? Who among you will indebt me with His willing submission of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُبُودِيَّة</span>, so that I can multiply its rewards many, many times over and pay it back to you. For He has the most noble rewards. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. Even on that day of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِسَاب</span>, this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span> is full of love and mercy. So that is what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span> linguistically means. The king or the owner of the time period when the debt of submission falls due. Now let''s look at some of the implications of this verse. The most important aspect of this verse is that it addresses this notion of an unjust world.</p>
<p>We know so many people today have given up faith and this belief in the existence of a creator based on the injustices that they see in this world, right? They look at all of this injustice that goes on around in this world and they use that as proof against God. Now this can be based on an injustice that happened to them personally or injustices that happens to another. And their argument is that if there is a God that is so loving and caring, then how come He let all of this happen? Now, what''s interesting about that, especially to us as a Muslim, is that nowhere in the Quran, Allah claims or promises to give us a good life in this world. Nowhere. In fact, what He promises every human being is quite the opposite. He says, in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْبَقَرَةِ</span>, He says, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَنَبْلُوَنَّكُم بِشَىْءٍۢ مِّنَ ٱلْخَوْفِ وَٱلْجُوعِ وَنَقْصٍۢ مِّنَ ٱلْأَمْوَلِ وَٱلْأَنفُسِ وَٱلثَّمَرَتِ ۗ وَبَشِّرِ ٱلصَّبِرِينَ</span> ‎. And no doubt about it, indeed, surely we shall test you with fear, with hunger, with loss of wealth, with loss of life, children, with loss of crops. But then he says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَبَشِّرِ الصَّابِرِينَ</span>, and for the patient ones there are glad tidings. In <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْمُلْكِ</span>, he says, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَبَرَكَ ٱلَّذِى بِيَدِهِ ٱلْمُلْكُ وَهُوَ عَلَىٰ كُلِّ شَىْءٍۢ قَدِيرٌ</span> ‎. Blessed is the one whose hand has the ultimate dominion and is able to do everything.</p>
<p>‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلَّذِى خَلَقَ ٱلْمَوْتَ وَٱلْحَيَوٰةَ لِيَبْلُوَكُمْ أَيُّكُمْ أَحْسَنُ عَمَلًۭا ۚ وَهُوَ ٱلْعَزِيزُ ٱلْغَفُورُ</span> ‎. Who created death and life to test you and see which of you is the best in action. According to the Quran, this world was never meant for justice. According to the Quran, this life is a test, and this world that we live in is an examination hall, and everyone is being tested. Some with poverty, some with wealth, some with sickness, some with health. In fact, the healthy and the rich are going through a much more difficult test than the sick and the poor. Because they will be held accountable for these gifts. You know, I gave you all this, show me what you did with it. And this is why it''s no surprise that the greatest man to walk the face of this earth, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ ﷺ</span>, he chose poverty over riches. But the point is that this world is imperfect and it is unjust by its very design. So now let''s take a moment to think about this perfect world. If I asked you to describe your perfect world to me, if I asked you to design a perfect world, what kind of world would you design? Think about it. What components would your perfect world comprise of?</p>
<p>We will all agree unanimously that in our perfect world there would be complete justice, there would be perfect harmony. No one in the world would sleep hungry. There would be no disease, there would be no pain, there would be no suffering. It would be a world where everybody is treated equally, justly. Love and peace will prevail. There will be absolutely no ugliness in that world. There will only be beauty. People would love and help each other. The proverbial lion and the deer would drink peacefully from the same river. Isn''t that the perfect world that we all dream of? Now if you think about it, what did I just describe to you? I just described <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَنَّة</span>.</p>
<p>Isn''t it? Isn''t all of this exactly the description of paradise? So you can see that this world is simply one side of the equation. The problem with someone who is only seeing this world is that he is looking at just one side of the equation, and there are a bunch of imbalances on this side. If you do not believe in a Day of Judgment, in a day of accountability, then you are simply left with a world that is completely unfair. But if you believe in a Day of Judgment, all the injustices on this side of the equation will get balanced with perfect justice on the other side of the equation. So in fact, the injustices of this world is proof and testimony to the existence of a perfect world. Logically, just speaking logically, rationally as human beings, what we see in this world, we have to believe because it is our innate desire to have this perfect world. Where do we get this concept of a perfect world? How come even the atheist, the one who does not believe in God, does believe in the dream of a perfect world? Because that is the other side of the equation. So just this, the inferior version of this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>, is proof of the existence of a perfect world. So in this verse Allah talks about this transition that will take place from this world into the next.</p>
<p>There is a transition from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبُوبِيَّة</span> to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِيَّة</span>. From lordship to ownership or kingship. From the love, care, concern and mercy of Allah in this world to the one in which He takes on a different role. The role of the owner and the king of the day when the debt of submission falls due. So this verse actually makes the transition from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُخْرَى</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>, in the next session we''ll look at some more implications of this verse and how it affects our lives. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَآخِرُ دَعْوَانَا أَنِ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ، وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى رَسُولِهِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ الْخَاتِمِ النَّبِيِّينَ وَسَيِّدِ الْمُرْسَلِينَ، وَعَلَى أَخِيهِ وَوَصِيِّهِ عَلِيِّ بْنِ أَبِي طَالِبٍ أَشْرَفِ الْوَصِيِّينَ، وَعَلَى الْأَئِمَّةِ مِنْ ذُرِّيَّتِهِ الطَّيِّبِينَ الطَّاهِرِينَ، وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَيْهِمْ أَجْمَعِينَ، الْفَاتِحَة</span></p>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'day of judgment - linguistics';
INSERT INTO chapter (slug, anchor_key, idx, title, html, word_count) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'motivating factors for piety', 21, 'Motivating Factors For Piety', '', 5963);
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ</span>, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ صَلِّ يَا اللَّهُ عَلَى سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ، وَعَلَى آلِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ، كَمَا صَلَّيْتَ وَسَلَّمْتَ وَبَارَكْتَ وَرَحِمْتَ وَتَرَحَّمْتَ عَلَى إِبْرَاهِيمَ، وَعَلَى آلِ إِبْرَاهِيمَ، فِي الْعَالَمِينَ إِنَّكَ حَمِيدٌ مَجِيدٌ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ اشْرَحْ لِي صَدْرِي، وَيَسِّرْ لِي أَمْرِي، وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِنْ لِسَانِي، يَفْقَهُوا قَوْلِي</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَلَامٌ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span>.</p>
<p>So in the previous session, we learned that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span>, linguistically what it means is the owner, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِ</span>, which is king. So the owner or the king of the time period when the debt of submission falls due. And we talked about the imperfections of this world which necessitate this idea of a perfect world which is the hereafter.</p>
<p>Today we look at this verse in some more detail and I want to help us understand its purpose and its relevance in the surah, especially from the point of view of this transition that we see taking place here. So if you look at the theme of the surah so far, we saw that from the beginning, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s love and His mercy was being highlighted, right? I begin with the lofty symbols of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> that are pointing to His immense love and mercy. All sincere praise and all unconditional gratitude is due to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> for His mastery, His lordship over this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَمْ</span>, which is everything that can be known in existence. So, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> ‎, the abundantly merciful, the consistently merciful.</p>
<p>So, if you look at this, right, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s love is being highlighted in the surah, and now suddenly there is this switch that we see in tone, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> changes His tone to say, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span>, owner of the time when the debt of submission falls due. Right? Why this sudden change in tone? So scholars explain that this transition is extremely profound, and its purpose is to engender this desire for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> in our hearts. So it is what the Arabs called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْبَاعِث</span>, which is what basically stimulates you to do something, something that motivates you. It''s the motivating factor about something.</p>
<p>Because if you think about it, if love is not balanced with some system of accountability, it is human tendency to start taking advantage of that kindness. We see this all the time in our life. If you know anyone in your life who is just love, love, love all the time, someone who will let people get away with murder, what you''ll see is that as much as this person might be loved, they will never be taken seriously. In fact, they will be taken advantage of. Isn''t that the case? So understanding this weakness in human nature, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> changes His tone to remind us that look, even though I am a very merciful and a very loving God, but you have to take Me seriously.</p>
<p>Don''t think you can take undue advantage of My love, because I am also your creator, I am also your owner, I am also your king, and I have set a time period where I will take you to account for your debt of submission. Because nothing is happening right now to you in this world. You are free to do whatever you want, right? If you commit a sin right now in this world, lightning is not going to strike you down. So what that freedom allows human nature to do is that it allows us to become heedless, and this verse is basically reminding us not to be heedless. It creates a sense of accountability and it also creates a sense of urgency to make the most of whatever time we have remaining.</p>
<p>Because no one knows when and where he''s going to die, right? Except that we all know that we are going to inevitably die someday. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَا تَدْرِى نَفْسٌۭ مَّاذَا تَكْسِبُ غَدًۭا وَمَا تَدْرِى نَفْسٌۢ بِأَىِّ أَرْضٍۢ تَمُوتُ</span>. No soul knows what he will earn tomorrow, and no soul knows in which land it will die. So this concept of an inevitable death and this idea of returning back to the creator for accountability, what it does is that it creates this awareness of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> in our hearts, which is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>. This word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>, it comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وِقَايَة</span>, which means a strong sense of fearful awareness.</p>
<p>So, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> is the awareness of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s constant presence in our lives. So, scholars have stated that what this verse <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span> does is that it motivates us towards <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> in a number of ways. So, today I want to go over some of those motivating factors that have been outlined by scholars.</p>
<p>The first, they say, is the fear of punishment in this world. This is a worldly type of retribution which is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِقَاب</span>. So human nature is such that we just naturally tend to fear pain. In fact, fear of pain is probably a greater immediate concern than the desire for pleasure. There were these studies done that when given two choices and the possibility of one over the other were equal, people chose to avoid pain rather than acquiring pleasure. So the avoidance of pain, the fear of pain, actually supersedes the desire for pleasure. And so what this fear allows us to do is that it enables us to delay our gratification.</p>
<p>They did a study in the US which was, I think, a 30-year study. It was very interesting. What they did is that they took these five-year-old kids and they offered them a cookie. And they said that you can have this cookie right now, or if you wait 10 minutes you can have two cookies. And so the children were very different in their choices. And what these people did is that they monitored these kids over a 30-year period. And what they found almost consistently was that the children that delayed their gratification for a greater reward, the children that took one cookie and waited, that left that one cookie and waited 10 minutes to get the two cookies, basically delaying their gratification, they found that these kids were far more successful in their lives than the ones who wanted it and took it immediately. It was a very interesting study.</p>
<p>So the delay of gratification, right, this temporary deprivation for the hope of something greater, is very important. And this delay is the basis of a lot of what is going on in the Quran. This idea of delaying gratification, because the people of this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>, they desire instant gratification. Right? No, you love this, the instant gratification, the present life of this world, and you keep delaying, you keep leaving the Hereafter. So basically you put off what''s coming and you just want this immediate pleasure.</p>
<p>And this is something we all are guilty of. We all fall into lust. It''s an immediate pleasure. It''s instant gratification. It''s an immediate satisfaction. And we often end up sacrificing doing the right thing to gain this immediate pleasure. And we don''t think of repercussions. But that''s the way we are, that''s just human nature.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span> is essentially what the scholars say, you know, it is the fear of punishment in the next world. One of the things about the Islamic and essentially all Abrahamic faiths is that there''s an idea that just as you have physics, you also have metaphysics. So physics are laws that we can discern from this palpable world, right? This world, this sensory world that we can touch and feel, it has certain laws.</p>
<p>And these are the laws of cause and effect. If you do something in this world, so every action has to have a reaction. So for instance, if I hit this table, there''s going to be the sound, right? If there was no sound, then there''s a suspension of a physical law and we''d have to look into it. Like, why did that happen? Why was there no sound? Because according to the laws of physics, my striking this table should produce a sound. So there are these laws of, if you jump off a building, there''s going to be an effect from that cause. Right? So just as there are physical laws, there are also metaphysical laws. There are laws of cause and effect that transcend the physical world. And they move into the spiritual world or the psychic world.</p>
<p>So for instance, if I shout at you, if I scream at you, there is going to be a psychic effect. It''s going to have an emotional effect. Where are emotions? We don''t truly understand where emotions lie, except that we just know that they''re part of our consciousness. But if I shout at you, you will feel something inside. You will either feel depressed or you will become angry. And that''s coming from another realm, right? It''s not from the physical realm. That''s from the realm of emotions. So just as there are physical effects, there are also spiritual effects, there are also psychic effects. So for instance, the Arabs, they have this term, what they call which literally means water of the face. But the idea is that it''s a kind of purity or a kind of impurity that people have on their face.</p>
<p>You''ll see this, people who have sinned a lot in their lives, their evil begins to show up on their faces and you get a type of metaphysical darkness in their face. In the same way, people that do a lot of good deeds, you''ll see that their faces are affected by that. They have a kind face. You see this luminance or this brightness radiating from their face. And this is why the Arabs used to call a face, they would call it <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الصَّحِيفَة</span>, which means the register, because it registers everything. Somebody once said that by 50, everybody has the face that they deserve. So if you''re 50 years old, you have the face that you deserve. If you''ve had a good life, it''s going to show up on your face. So that fear of another worldly punishment is one of the things that will compel a person to do good and stay within boundaries within this world by delaying gratifications. The next motivating factor for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> is desiring a benefit. That''s the next one.</p>
<p>So just as people fear punishment, they also desire benefit. This is at the most basic human foundation, right? Stick and carrot. This is how children are disciplined, stick and carrot. It''s how societies work, stick and carrot. So really much of society is based on these principles. And that''s why the highest people, the elite, the most intelligent people are the ones that actually transcend these stick and carrot factors and they move into another realm. People like Maulana Ali, who often said in his prayers, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا إِلَهِي، مَا عَبَدْتُكَ بِشَوْقِ جَنَّتِكَ، وَلَا مِنْ خَوْفِ نَارِكَ، بَلْ وَجَدْتُكَ مُسْتَحِقًّا لِلْعِبَادَةِ فَعَبَدْتُكَ</span>. My master, I do not worship you out of a desire for your paradise, nor do I worship you out of fear of your hellfire. Instead, I truly found you worthy of my worship, and for that reason alone I worship you. And this is a very high station of worship that completely transcends this carrot and stick philosophy. It''s the ideal form of worship. But the majority of people are at this base level, and because the Quran speaks to all of humanity, it talks in this general manner. There''s a Hadith in which the Prophet said, move according to the level of the least amongst you. Move according to the level of the least amongst you.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. So you have to move with the least amongst you. You don''t speed up and leave people behind. Right? No, that''s not the way of Islam. And this is why if you carefully study this religion, you''ll see that most of it is designed to protect the weakest people. Right? Because there are people who can stand on their own and they don''t have a problem with that, but then there are also people that cannot do that and they have their human weaknesses. And so the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span>, it protects those people by preventing them from certain things. And this is why the Quran speaks against things like gambling, for instance, right? The Quran says that there is good in gambling, actually, it does say that.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَسْـَٔلُونَكَ عَنِ ٱلْخَمْرِ وَٱلْمَيْسِرِ قُلْ فِيهِمَآ إِثْمٌۭ كَبِيرٌۭ وَمَنَفِعُ لِلنَّاسِ وَإِثْمُهُمَآ أَكْبَرُ مِن نَّفْعِهِمَا</span>. They ask you concerning alcohol and gambling. Tell them in them is a great sin and also a benefit for mankind, but the sin is far greater than their benefit. So Quran actually says that there is some good in gambling. I mean, there are lessons to be learned in gambling, right? Risk is an interesting phenomenon in commerce. There''s a strong relationship between gambling and commerce. But the harms of gambling are far greater than its benefits. And it is a known social science, this fact that around 3% of people that go into gambling casinos, they become addicted and it destroys their lives. And this is why if you''ve ever been to a gambling casino, I mean, I''ve been inside this casino at Atlantic City many years ago, and it was one of the most frightening things I ever saw. It was like a circle out of Dante''s Inferno. And you just see all of these people in this zombie-like state, just sitting on their seats, putting in quarters into the machine one after the other and pulling this lever. I mean, it''s a scary sight. And then there was this pawn shop in the middle of that casino with this big sign outside that said, We take wedding rings. That is just so sinister.</p>
<p>What kind of people are these? How do you justify a job like this, just depriving people in this manner, just taking advantage of human weakness? But that''s the reality of our world, right? And gambling is based on the carrot phenomenon. It''s a carrot phenomenon. You know, gamblers think that if I put in money and I pull the lever, and it''s all designed so that winning will happen enough times to keep you there, right? That''s part of the design. So when people leave, they say, oh, you know, I made such and such. They never actually think about how much they lost. They''ll always tell you how much they won. So that''s the phenomenon of the carrot, you know, and it''s used a lot, and that''s one of the worst things about it.</p>
<p>But then obviously from the worldly things, you know, these things that are pleasurable that we find in the slide, that give you instant pleasure, they''re always followed by a far greater pain, because that''s just the design of it, isn''t it? I mean, in our case, we all can give examples of that. And this is why the Prophet, he said, Paradise was surrounded by distasteful things and fire was surrounded by delightful things, pleasurable things. So if you''re walking towards paradise, you will inevitably encounter distasteful things. It''s just part of the design. And so now it''s up to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَقِيل</span>, this person of intellect, to recognize this, and that''s why the Quran frequently talks about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَدَبُّر</span>, which means to reflect deeply upon something. You know, Quran frequently asks us ... Why don''t you reflect deeply? It actually uses the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَدَبُّر</span> a lot, which is deep reflection. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَدَبُّر</span> comes from a root which means to take something to its end. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُبُر</span> is the behind of something.</p>
<p>It''s the end of something. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَدْبَار</span> is what''s behind you. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَدَبُّر</span> is going through something until you get to its very end. So that''s why when you have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَدَبُّر</span> as a quality, you just look down the road, right? You think about the consequences. You think about where this action will end up. Whereas this person who''s just stuck in this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَجَلَ</span>, this haste of instant gratification, doesn''t really just go beyond the present, and they''re just stuck right there and they don''t think about the future. And there''s this interesting theory in theology about this divine experience versus a demonic experience. And the way you differentiate between a true divine experience and a true demonic experience is that a demonic experience is always accompanied with an expansion of the self. In other words, it is very exciting and it''s a very joyful experience, the demonic experience. But then it is always followed with despair.</p>
<p>On the contrary, a true spiritual experience, a true divine experience is exactly the opposite. The initial experience is incredible constriction and it''s incredible fear and dread, but then it is followed by a sense of expansion and joy. So, you know, anyway, so the benefit, acquiring a benefit is the second motivating factor for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>. The next motivating factor, scholars say, and benefits of this verse, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span>, the other benefit that it provides is that it gives us the grounds for the fear of a reckoning. That you''re going to be reckoned on the Day of Judgment. And so this fear of reckoning, it should instill in our hearts this desire to do good. You see, anyone who has a business, they have what they call internal audits. If you have a business and you live in a country like the US or England, for instance, where they have these revenue services, these are external agencies that will come in and they will examine your books, they will examine your business. And if you''ve been fudging the books, then you''ll get penalties. It''s not just that you will pay what you owe in back taxes and things like that. They will actually add penalties and they will add interest and they will do all these kinds of things.</p>
<p>So companies that are worried about that external reckoning or that external auditing by the revenue services, what they do is that they conduct an internal audit, and so if there''s anything that needs correcting, they will correct it internally. And that''s what''s called in our terms, it''s called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُحَاسَبَة</span>, and it''s part of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>, where you actually take your own self to account. The hadith says: Take yourself to account before you are taken to account. And this is why it has been said that when a true believer, when he lies down to sleep at night, he reflects on his actions for the day. And the things that he found that he did wrong, he asks <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> for forgiveness and he makes a determination to correct those things in the next day. So this is why this concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُحَاسَبَة</span> is an extremely important concept within Islam.</p>
<p>The next benefit of this verse is that it creates this concept of shame or modesty from the awareness of God. That <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is aware of our actions, that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is watching us. So we should have this shame or this modesty before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَيَّا</span>. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَيَا</span> in Arabic is probably better translated as modesty, which is related to shame. There''s a sense that you don''t want to do something in front of a person or in front of your Lord out of love and respect. There are certain things that children won''t do in front of their parents out of respect. The Prophet, he said: Every religion has a peculiar characteristic, and the characteristic of my religion is modesty. Anthropologists, they divide cultures into two types of cultures.</p>
<p>They divide it as shame based cultures and then guilt based cultures. So Western cultures generally tend to fall into the guilt culture. And a guilt culture, it cultivates within people moral components inside of themselves where If they do something wrong, they feel bad about it on the inside. And so one of the things a lot of people know about people in the West is that they will often find it very difficult to do wrong things without actually feeling bad. In fact, there''s a whole industry in America to help people overcome this feeling of guilt, self-help books, self-improvement courses. There''s a whole industry that capitalizes on this idea of helping people overcome guilt. So in guilt cultures, there''s an internal mechanism. In shame-based cultures, the mechanisms are external. And so people, they don''t do wrong things out of fear of others. They don''t do wrong things out of fear of others.</p>
<p>which generally creates hypocrisy in cultures. And India and Pakistan and many Asian countries are very much rooted in the shame based culture where people don''t have an internal component and they''re only controlled from external factors. So when such a person is in his own country, in his own community, he finds it shameful to do certain things, but when he goes to another country, where nobody knows him it''s no longer shameful to do those same things right they start doing them and and they don''t really feel any guilt inside or any shame inside and this is why you know a lot of kids from India and Pakistan when they come to the US for studies or things like that or you know any other Western country they go completely crazy they start drinking they start partying they do drugs and even though they never did those things when they were back home It just becomes completely acceptable overnight because they''re no longer in the culture where to do those things was shameful. And also, a different country or a different city, it provides anonymity, right? When people move into different countries, into different environments, because back home, everybody knows you, right? They know your parents. So if you do anything, the entire community is going to hear about it. The entire <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَمَاعَة</span> is going to hear about it. In a different city or a different country, there is this anonymity. So it''s difficult to sense that shame, which is why guilt cultures are very important in advanced societies and shame, it works very well at the tribal level, right?</p>
<p>It works quite well in small cultures, but when you move to advanced cultures like the West, it breaks down. now Islam is rooted in a shame based culture but what it does is that it transcends the shame that is rooted in people and it grounds it in shame before God so it''s not so much of an internal factor it is still an external factor right meaning it''s more shame based but it has to do with this modesty, this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَيَّا</span> before God, before the prophet, before the angels, right? Because you have these two angels on each side recording everything that we do. So the idea is that you become aware of the spiritual presence that then prevents you from doing wrong things and this is called which is a very high station it is a station of vigilance or this constant awareness of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s presence based on his word in the Quran indeed <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> sees everything and is aware of everything The use of this term is so beautiful. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَقِيب</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَقِيب</span> is the jealous lover. You know, like if you''re in love with someone and that person is in love with another person, so that other person becomes your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَقِيب</span>, right? And it is the nature of a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَقِيب</span> that you''re constantly gonna spy on them, constantly be aware of every move they make, right? That''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَقِيب</span>.</p>
<p>the jealous lovers. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is watching you like a jealous lover. He''s watching every single move of it. The metaphors, the analogies are so beautiful in the Quran. So there is this idea of shame. There''s this very interesting story over a hundred years ago when British ruled India, they say they lived a pickpocket, right? He was a Muslim, but his job was just picking pockets of people. He was a thief. In fact, he was a member of a gang who did this. There was an entire gang dedicated to just, they would go out and then they would pick pockets all day and then bring all the earnings in the night. So one day, this man, he picked the pocket of an Englishman, of a Britisher, and that wallet had a lot of money in it. So he was quite happy initially.</p>
<p>But then he thought of something, and then he went and returned that wallet back to that Englishman. And that night when he returned back to his gang, the leader would ask everyone how much did they earn that day. And so when his turn came, he said, &quot;You know, I did pick the pocket of this rich Englishman, this Christian, but then I decided to return it back.&quot; And when asked why, he said, &quot;You know, I thought that on the Day of Judgment, if Christ came to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span> and said, A man of your nation picked the pocket of a man from my nation, my Prophet would have to feel shame before him on my account. And I just cannot be the reason to put my Prophet to shame before Christ. And so I returned his wallet to him.&quot;</p>
<p>Right? And I mean, this was the level of awareness of just people a hundred years ago, because they were concerned about putting our Prophet to shame. And in his last sermon to his nation, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ صَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>, in his last <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُطْبَة</span>, one of the things he said was ............................................. put me to shame on the Day of Judgment. You know, when a classroom produces a bad result, the teacher feels shame, right? Isn''t he asked, &quot;What''s the matter? Why did so many children fail in your class today?&quot;</p>
<p>And our Prophet, his last request to us was, &quot;My people, do not shame me before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> on the Day of Judgment.&quot; There is this verse in Urdu poetry, which I love so much. It''s wojo geet tum neso na nahi, mere umre bhar kari astha. Mere dard ki tivo das tanjis at tum haseema ragai.</p>
<p>So there''s the story about — I don''t know if this story is true or not, but nevertheless it demonstrates this beautiful love that this man had for his Prophet, and it was grounded in shame, because he felt ashamed at the idea of being the reason for the Prophet''s shame. So it was still an external factor, but it transcended the realm of people and it was rooted in a higher dimension. And may <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> give us all this awareness of understanding that, just having this awareness of not just being ashamed before the Prophet, but also putting the Prophet to shame before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, based on our — you know, this man who strived twenty-three years of his life for us, and we cannot even produce the most basic result for him.</p>
<p>The next motivating factor for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> is gratitude over the blessing of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, for all the blessings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, by being obedient. So the next thing that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span>, this verse, helps us with is obtaining <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> through the recognition of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s blessings and demonstrating the gratitude for those blessings through obedience. So it''s this awareness that every blessing that you have, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> gave it to you. If God gave you sight, the gratitude for that sight is to use it for the sake of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. If He gave you hearing, the gratitude for hearing is to use it for the sake of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. If He gave you physical strength, everything should be used for what it was made for.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, He did not create His creation and desire disobedience from them. He enables disobedience, which is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خِذْلَان</span>. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is the enabler. He allows disobedience to happen, but He''s not content with it. He does not want His servants to be in a state of ingratitude, even though He allows it. So it''s very important to have gratitude for the blessings by using those blessings in obedience. That is the definition of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span>: to use the gifts that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has given you to perform things that are pleasing to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p>The next motivating factor is knowledge. So knowledge actually provides a reason to have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْلَا</span>. Now, if you notice, there is this ascension that is taking place in the motivations here.</p>
<p>We started off with the base levels, which is the fear of punishment and the desire for a reward. Then it transcended, it went higher into this idea of a reckoning, and then just gratitude through obedience, and now a higher realm of knowledge, which is the next motivating factor for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْلَا</span>, which is based on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>, right? Because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says, &quot;Indeed the ones who are truly fearful of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, who have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُشُوعٌ</span>, they are the scholars.&quot; The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُشُوعٌ</span> is a type of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> that comes from a fearful awareness of God through knowledge. It comes from awe before God that is based on knowledge of who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is. And it is amongst His creation, amongst His servants, it is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُلَمَاء</span> that have this knowledge.</p>
<p>And then the magnification of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s majesty, right? And this is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقَامُ الْهَيْبَةِ</span>, which is the station of awe, and which is a higher station than, you know, just even just basic gratitude. Because it comes from this awe that is produced by the knowledge of who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is, right? It''s called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقَامُ الْهَيْبَةِ</span>. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُحِبٌّ</span> is someone who has this awe for a greater, like for the Sultan, right? There''s an awe that goes with it.</p>
<p>so you know when you go visit a Sultan, there''s a whole way that they have made things in order to instill this awe in your hearts for him. So for instance, if you go to the White House to meet the President, you''ll see these Marine guards with guns standing outside that have these big bayonets. I mean, there''s a reason why the bayonets are there. I mean, they could have just had the guns, right? Why do you need those bayonets? But the bayonet is there to instill this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>, right? Bayonet is this little sword-like thing, this knife-like thing that is attached to the gun, to the barrel of the gun. Why do they need that? In case of an event, they''ll probably shoot rather than stab, but why is there that knife? Because it''s meant to instill this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> in our hearts. It''s the same with just swords outside the palace of a king in Arabia. It creates <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>. And then you go in and then there are all these long corridors made of marble and this expensive stuff, and they make you walk through all of these magnificent corridors with all of these elegant decorations. Why? The purpose is to humble you, because you''re about to meet the Sultan, you''re about to meet the President, the Prime Minister, the King. And so this whole experience that they make you go through is to produce this kind of humility within your heart, to put you in your place, you see?</p>
<p>And no matter where you go, they all have the same type of rituals. Whether you go to the Queen''s palace or you go to the White House or to go to the Sultan''s palace, they''re all designed to create this awe in your heart for the Sultan, which is important. Because if the Sultan does not generate this awe, then why should people obey him in the first place? And this is also why traditionally <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُلَمَاء</span> dressed a certain way. This is why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span> encouraged <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُلَمَاء</span> to dress in this certain particular way so that it instills this awe, this respect in the hearts of the audience. Otherwise, why is anybody going to listen to them? This is why we find the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِسْوَة</span> on the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْكَعْبَة</span>, that black cloth, because it''s a sanctified place, right? That <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِسْوَة</span>, it illustrates the magnificence of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْكَعْبَة</span>. It creates this awe within our hearts. So it''s a very important aspect of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَعْظِيم</span>. And then the final stage, you know, is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِدْقُ الْمَحَبَّةِ</span>, the sincerity in one''s love for God, right?</p>
<p>It gives this ultimate form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>, which is the highest motivating factor for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>. And this is, you know, one of the poets, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">محْمُودٌ الوَرَّاقُ</span>, he said, &quot;You disobey <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> but then you claim that you love Him? This is impossible and a very strange type of logic that I don''t understand. Had your love been sincere, you would have been obedient.&quot; Because a lover always obeys his or her beloved. So the nature of love is that you want to obey the one that you love. Then he says, a woman asked about the state of the one who was in this burning passion for her, right? The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَاشِقٌ</span> is the one who is in this burning passion for the beloved. So she''s asking this man about her lover, and she says, &quot;So for the sake of God, tell me about his state. And don''t stint in your description and don''t exaggerate.&quot; In other words, I want to hear the truth. So she asks this man, tell me about his love. Tell me what kind of love does he have for me. And so this man says, if this man knew that he would die from thirst, and if you said don''t touch that water, he wouldn''t touch it.</p>
<p>So if your lover is gonna die of thirst and you said don''t touch that drink of water, he won''t touch it. He''d rather die than disobey you. So the point of that, what the poet is emphasizing, is that it is the nature, obedience is the nature of love. The very nature of love is to be obedient. So the more you love God, the more you will obey God. And that''s the idea. And so the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُلَمَاء</span> have stated these, think of it like a staircase, right? And think of all of these motivating factors as rungs. So it begins with the fear of punishment, then the next step is desiring the rewards, then it is having this concept of reckoning, this accountability before God, right? And then shame, the next level is having shame before God, His prophet and His angels. Then the next level is gratitude, then the next is knowledge, and finally you reach the final stage of love, which is sincere love manifested through obedience.</p>
<p>So it''s a very, you know, it''s a very beautiful and a very powerful verse, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span>, and its purpose is to motivate us towards <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>. And so you have, you know, with ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> ‎, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span>, from these three verses, we get a complete picture of who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is. Right? So it provides a complete description, a very concise description of who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is.</p>
<p>The next, the following verses that we will study in future sessions, so these next verses, they give us an understanding of what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> wants from us and what we want from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. And so this is how the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> is split into these two parts. You have the first, these beginning three verses, which tells us in third person who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is. And then, from there, it begins this transition of what we are asking <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> for. So, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>, we''ll explore those verses in the following sessions, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَآخِرُ دَعْوَانَا أَنِ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ، وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى رَسُولِهِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ خَاتِمِ النَّبِيِّينَ وَسَيِّدِ الْمُرْسَلِينَ، وَعَلَى أَخِيهِ وَوَصِيِّهِ عَلِيٍّ النَّبِيِّ طَالِبٍ شَرَفِ الْوَصِيِّينَ، وَعَلَى الْأَئِمَّةِ مِنْ ذُرِّيَّتِهِمَا الطَّيِّبِينَ الطَّاهِرِينَ، وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَيْهِمْ أَجْمَعِينَ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span></p>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'motivating factors for piety';
INSERT INTO chapter (slug, anchor_key, idx, title, html, word_count) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'worship and assistance', 22, 'Worship and Assistance', '', 4490);
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ</span> ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ صَلِّ عَلَى سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ وَعَلَى آلِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ كَمَا صَلَّيْتَ وَسَلَّمْتَ وَبَارَكْتَ وَرَحِمْتَ وَتَرَحَّمْتَ عَلَى إِبْرَاهِيمَ وَعَلَى آلِ إِبْرَاهِيمَ فِي الْعَالَمِينَ إِنَّكَ حَمِيدٌ مَجِيدٌ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ اشْرَحْ لِي صَدْرِي وَيَسِّرْ لِي أَمْرِي وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِنْ لِسَانِي يَفْقَهُوا قَوْلِي</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللَّهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span>.</p>
<p>We completed the linguistic study of the first four verses of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>. So now we have, after giving us an introduction of who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is through the first, or rather the beginning verses, then he says, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ</span> ‎. It is only you that we worship or willingly enslave ourselves to. And it is only you that we seek help from. Now here, there''s something really interesting in this verse. If you notice, there is a complete change of tone over here. So the previous verses, they were all in the third person, right? They were informational. So ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, begin with the name of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, extremely merciful, intensely merciful. All praise and glory belongs to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, who is the master of all universes or everything that is known, the abundant, the abundantly merciful, the consistently merciful, owner of a time when the debt of submission falls due. So these are all statements. If you notice, they''re all in the third person, in which <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is just basically informing us about who He is.</p>
<p>But then, He suddenly changes the tone and He turns it into a second person conversation. He turns it into a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span>, as if we are now speaking directly with Him. It is only you we worship and it is only you we turn to for help. The tone of this verse is not that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is the one that we worship and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is the one that we turn to for help. It''s not in the third person. It''s as if we are in direct conversation with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> at this point. And this is a beautiful linguistic tool in the Arabic language that is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الِٱلْتِفَاتُ</span>, which is a sudden change in the pronoun of the speaker, or the one that is being spoken to. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> constantly changes the conversation in the Quran from third person to first person, then to second person, and then back to third person, right? And you can see this in almost every single <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>. He sometimes refers to Himself using I, sometimes He uses we, right? Sometimes he uses him. These are all linguistic tools to not only keep the reader alert and awake, but also a mechanism to highlight what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> wants from us. So in the case of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>, you can see that the tone now suddenly changes to a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span>, it changes to a supplication. It is only you we worship and it is only you we turn to for help.</p>
<p>And who is the one saying that? It is you and I that are saying that. So we are now in direct conversation with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. And this style of switching suddenly from third person to second person is a means of letting us know that this is in fact what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> wants from us. You understand? When we say ... because this is all revelation coming from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, right? We are not the authors of Quran. So this is all revelation coming from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. So now imagine a book like Quran, something so meaningful. How is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> to have this conversation with us where suddenly now He wants us, while He''s describing Himself, He wants us to know what He wants from us. So by changing his tone to ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ</span> ‎, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is expressing His intention of wanting us to say that we willingly worship you and we freely give up our freedom and desires to become your slaves.</p>
<p>In other words, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is not commanding us into slavery by saying <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتَعْبَدُوا أَنْفُسَكُمْ لِ</span>, enslave yourselves to Me. Instead, He wants us to say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ</span>, it is only you that we want to worship. In other words, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is asking us to reach this conclusion of our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وُضِيءَ</span> on our own, through the study of the previous three verses. The idea is that when we truly understand the meaning of ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> ‎, when we truly understand the meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span>, and when we truly understand the meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span>, and when we recognize what an incredibly loving, merciful, and powerful <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ اللَّهُ</span> is, our natural and logical conclusion to that understanding has to be . You see? Our natural response has to be a willing desire of submission. So the idea here is that if you haven''t reached that conclusion all by yourself, then don''t say it. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is not forcing us to say it. This is us saying it to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> out of our own free will. So I wanted to start the explanation of this verse by making that distinction.</p>
<p>Now let''s look at this verse linguistically. The first word is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ</span>, which means only you. So, linguistically, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ</span> excludes everything and everyone except the object of the conversation. So, think of it like a clause of exclusivity. So, what we''re saying is you, only you, and no one else but you, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ</span>.</p>
<p>The next word is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَعْبُدُ</span>, which means we worship. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَعْبُدُ</span> comes from the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>, which we all know means worship, which in turn comes from the root word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>, which means slave. Now, what is the connection between worship and slavery? What''s the relationship there? The relationship is that worship is a demonstration of one''s slavery. Worship or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> is a demonstration of one''s acceptance of slavery. It is a proof of one''s slavery. It is an act of devotion and dedication that establishes one''s slavery.</p>
<p>So I''ll give you a very basic example. If I stood on the top of a building and I screamed at the top of my lungs for two hours that, you know, I''m a slave of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, I''m a slave of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, I''m a slave of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, but then when it''s time for prayer, if I do not pray, I can say that I''m a slave of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> all day long, but did I back it up? Did I prove that statement? No.</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/166/23fd8cf4-d085-43b5-a0e9-eb75f7b8751d.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>So our worship, our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>, is actually proof and evidence of the fact that we are in fact the slaves of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. Otherwise it''s just an empty claim. That''s how these two words are connected together. So it means it is only you and no one else but you that we worship or willingly enslave ourselves to.</p>
<p>Now, what''s really powerful about this concept is that by desiring this enslavement of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, we are essentially desiring freedom from everything else. Becoming the slaves of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> provides freedom from every other slavery of this world. So essentially what we''re recognizing through <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ</span> is that we do not want to become the slave of this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>. We want to become the slave of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> and in the process of that slavery, through the process of that slavery, we want to free ourselves from everything else. So by recognizing our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمُّ الْقُرْآنِ</span> to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, what we get is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُرِّيَة</span>, which is true freedom. In completing the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمُّ الْقُرْآنِ</span> to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, we are no longer slaves to ourselves and that''s a truly free person. If I can control myself, then I''m a slave to myself. Somebody can say, I''m free, I''m free.</p>
<p>But then when the food shows up, they just cannot stop themselves. And that''s not freedom as far as the Muslim is concerned, right? He''s not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدُ اللهِ</span> at that point. He''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدُ الْهَوَى</span>. He''s a slave of his own passions. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدُ اللهِ</span>, the slave of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, is that when the situation arises, he has <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. He has that mindfulness of the presence of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. It''s not that the temptation goes away, right? The desire is still there because we''re all humans, so the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَهْوَة</span> might still be there, but the slave of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدُ اللهِ</span>, can control it because he''s not the slave of his passion, he is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّد</span> of it, right? He''s not the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدُ</span> of his passion, he is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّد</span> of his passion, he is the master of it. That is someone who is really free, you know, that''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الدَّهْرُ</span>. But this level of mastery can only be attained through the slavery of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p>So, you know, there are many kinds of slaveries in that respect, right? The tongue is the same, right? There are so many people who just can''t stop backbiting. They just can''t stop gossiping. I mean, you can tell them, you can point it out to them a million times, but they just can''t stop. I mean, it''s just amazing. And in most cases, they''re not even aware of it. I mean, I''m sure I do things like that all the time, but because this just exists within us, this inability to control our tongues, to speak badly about others, to say things that we really shouldn''t be saying. And I''m just guilty as the next person. But this is the human weakness that people find it hard to control. We find it hard to control these things. So learning to control the tongue is also a major thing in Islam.</p>
<p>So when we say &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ</span>,&quot; it is only you we desire to enslave ourselves to, what''s implicit in that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span> is that we are essentially desiring freedom from the enslavement of everything else. Everything that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>: money, houses, cars, children, food, these branded clothes, friends, relatives. I mean, there are millions and millions of shackles that we wear around ourselves, and you know, and we don''t even realize it. And what the slavery of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> does is that it frees us from all those shackles.</p>
<p>The next thing that I want to point out about this verse is the fact that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, he uses the plural form here. He says, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ</span>,&quot; it is only you we worship. Now, let me ask you a question. The majority of the time when we pray, are we praying by ourselves or in a group? Generally, we''re praying by ourselves, right? So then, can I say when I''m praying by myself, can I just say, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ أَعْبُدُ</span>,&quot; it is only you that I worship? I mean, wouldn''t that be more logical?</p>
<p>So then can I change it to the singular form when I''m just praying by myself? No, I cannot. It''s not permissible. It''s not allowed. I cannot change it. I have to use the plural form. So that''s very interesting, right? Why did <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> fix the statement in the plural form? Scholars explain that by fixing the statement in the plural form, what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is emphasizing is this importance of unity. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is emphasizing this concept of unity, that even when I pray by myself, I do not speak just for myself. I speak on behalf of the whole <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمَّة</span>, on behalf of my entire community.</p>
<p>I say, ... because the ultimate way to establish unity is through the worship of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. That''s why they say that a family that prays together stays together, a community that prays together sticks together. So the message in this verse and the following verses is to be united, to stay united as one unit, as one family, one community, one nation. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has forced this idea of this togetherness, you know. So when I''m doing my prayers, I am just naturally praying for you. When you''re doing your prayer, you''re praying for me, even when you''re praying alone. So this plural form, this unity, this, the sense of brotherhood, the sense of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِلَّة</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمَّة</span>, right, all of this is just present in this plural form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَعْبُدُ</span>.</p>
<p>So we say &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ</span>,&quot; it is you that we worship. And the next thing we say is &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ</span>,&quot; it is only you that we seek assistance from. Now the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَسْتَعِينُ</span>, it comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِعَانَة</span>, which in turn then comes from the root word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَوْن</span>, and it means assistance. Now, there are many other words in Arabic for help, right? There is one common word is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَصْر</span>. There is also <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسَاعَدَة</span>, there is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَجْدَة</span>. There are all these different kinds of help. But these are all different kinds of help.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَصْر</span> is a type of help that has a greater sense of urgency to it. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَصْر</span> is the kind of help that we ask for. It''s the kind of help that we seek in emergency situations. Major, major things, right? When the help of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> arrives at the most difficult of times, understand that victory will be near. When the help of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> arrives and you are victorious in battles, right? Again, major help. Keep us steadfast and help us over the nation of disbelievers. Major emergency situation, major challenge. So when I''m in a situation where I cannot do anything to get myself out of that, then I need somebody else to do it for me. And in those kind of emergency situations, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَصْر</span> would be used. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَوْن</span> is used for small matters. It''s the help that I need in little, little things, right?</p>
<p>So, I need a glass of water. I can get up and get it myself, but if you''re just right next to the cooler, I can say, &quot;Hey, can you get me a glass of water?&quot; And if you bring me that glass of water, what you did is you assisted me, right? That''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَوْن</span>. It''s seeking assistance in daily tasks, little, little things. So why is this distinction made in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> where the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَوْن</span> is used instead of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَصْر</span>? It''s because a true believer is someone who turns to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> all the time. That''s the quality of a true believer. He turns to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> for everything, for all the little, little things in his life, you understand. He doesn''t just think of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> in major situations. He remembers <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> in the little, little things all throughout his day, you know, like a lover that remembers the beloved all the time.</p>
<p>And this is exactly what we find in the daily routine of our Prophet. He woke up in the morning. What was the first thing he did? The second he woke up, he praised <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي أَحْيَانَا بَعْدَ مَا أَمَاتَنَا وَإِلَيْهِ النُّشُورُ</span>&quot; He went to the restroom to relieve himself. &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الْخُبْثِ وَالْخَبَائِثِ</span>&quot; he sought the protection of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span>. Right? He got on his horse.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ الَّذِي سَخَّرَ لَنَا هَذَا وَمَا كُنَّا لَهُ مُقْرِنِينَ وَإِنَّا إِلَى رَبِّنَا لَمُنْقَلِبُونَ</span> Before he would start eating his food, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ As soon as he finished eating his food, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> ‎ He stepped out of his house to leave somewhere. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللَّهِ تَوَكَّلْتُ عَلَى اللَّهِ وَلَا حَوْلَ وَلَا قُوَّةَ إِلَّا بِاللَّهِ الْعَلِيِّ الْعَظِيمِ</span> It is used for seeking assistance of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> constantly, in even in the most tiny matters. So the first reason for using our own is this idea of constant remembrance.</p>
<p>The second reason we constantly seek His assistance is because we recognize that we are not the ideal slaves, right? We understand that we are weak human beings who desperately require assistance every step of the way, in every little thing that we do. Because we are dominated by our desires of this world. We are challenged by innumerable distractions every single waking moment of our lives. So we are constantly walking this slippery slope. So we know that it''s not going to be easy to maintain our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدِيَّة</span>, because we are surrounded by the worst four enemies that are constantly working against us.</p>
<p>First is our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span>, our soul that is weak to begin with. Then we have this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>, the temptations of this world. Then we have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَوَىٰ</span>, which is our overwhelming desires and passions. And then there is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> himself. So we are surrounded on all sides by these powerful enemies. And we recognize that, and so we admit that our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَبُو ضِيَاء</span> and our servitude is not going to be worthy of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span>. But we''re willing to try, right? We''re still willing to try. And in that trying, we''re going to need our master''s assistance in every little thing that we do. So when we fail over and over again, we will still constantly need <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> to pick us up, because only He can protect and assist us. So, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ</span> ‎. It is only You that we worship, but then it is only You that we seek assistance from in every little thing that we do. So the statement is made up of these two parts.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ</span> is making that willing commitment to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. That yes, I do want to become your slave so that I can be free from everything else. I desire freedom. The reason I am making this commitment to you is because I desire freedom. That''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ</span>. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ</span> is asking Him for assistance in getting that job done. So that''s the second reason. And the third reason is that we seek the closeness of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. We desire the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْب</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. This is also the reason why this conversation switched from third person to second person, right? Because when we say we are now directly speaking with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. So it''s a more intimate conversation.</p>
<p>And we''re using that intimate conversation for two things, for worship and for assistance. So, what scholars tell us is that what this verse teaches us is that we all want to be near and we all want to be dear to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. We all want to be loved by <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. We want to be His beloved. Right? But the ultimate way to establish that nearness, that love of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, is by worshiping Him and by calling out to Him for assistance. That''s why in a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيثٌ قُدْسِيٌّ اللَّهُ</span> very explicitly says: my slave does not get closer to me more so than by doing what I have told him to do, and that is the five times daily prayer. So if you want to be close to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, pray five times a day and keep calling Him for assistance, because there is no better time to call Him for assistance than with prayer.</p>
<p>The next topic that I want to talk about is the sequence, the sequence in which these two things are used, these two words are used, right? So if you look at it, the first thing that is mentioned is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>. Worship is mentioned first, and that is followed by <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ</span>, which is the assistance of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. Now why is that important? Because the sequence that you find in the Quran between things, that is never coincidental, right? It always has a very specific purpose. It''s always very precise, because this is divine, this is divine revelation. So like nothing is just arbitrary in the Quran. So why is worship mentioned before seeking assistance? Because the best way to seek the help and the assistance from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, the best way to ask <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> for anything and everything that we may need or want, is to ask after worshipping Him.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدُ اللهِ</span> ibn Masood, he would tell his students, &quot;Save your needs and your necessities for the obligatory prayers.&quot; So whenever you need something or whenever you''re dealing with something difficult, a difficult situation, a challenge in life, don''t worry. There is going to be an obligatory prayer right around the corner. Use that as an opportunity to ask <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> to help you solve your problems. Not to say that you cannot remember <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> during the entire day, which obviously we should, that''s the idea of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ</span>, right? But the proper sequence, the proper protocol would be to worship <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> and then seek His assistance.</p>
<p>The last thing I want to point out is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِكْمَةٌ</span>, the wisdom in the repetition of this word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ</span>. So you can see that the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ</span> is used twice here, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ</span> and then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ</span>. So the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ</span> is repeated twice. Now grammatically speaking, if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> omitted the second <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ</span>, and if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> had just said ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ</span> ‎, it is only you we worship and seek assistance from.</p>
<p>Right? Just eliminate the second <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ</span>. That would have been grammatically correct. So then why did <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> repeat Himself when it''s not grammatically necessary? Does this mean that the second <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ</span> is not absolutely necessary to the meaning? No. And let''s look at that. So there''s a wisdom behind that. So when we say ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ</span> ‎, when we remove the second <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ</span>, both words <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَعْبُدُ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَسْتَعِينُ</span>, they connect back with the same <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ</span>. Which means they become associated with that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ</span>. But when I say ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ</span> ‎, they both now have their own <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ</span>.</p>
<p>So what that does grammatically is that it makes both independent of one another. It makes worship and assistance independent of one another. Why is that important? Why did <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> do it this way? Because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> said worship and then seek assistance, right? That''s the optimal method. That''s the right protocol. That would be ideal. That''s how we''re supposed to do it. But then there might be some of us who might have lost our way. There might be some people who are not praying to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. They''re not worshipping <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p>And yet, because of life, they just run into some difficulties or some tragedies. And in that difficult moment, they need <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s assistance. They need <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s help. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> very subtly, He separates the two, just a little bit. He makes them independent for the sake of the people to let them know that even if you aren''t worshipping Me, even if you aren''t praying to Me, don''t worry, you can still seek My help and My assistance. My door is still open to you. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللهِ</span>. So worshipping Me and then seeking assistance would be the ideal way, in submitting yourselves to Me, willingly, freely enslaving yourselves to Me and then seeking My assistance, that would be the more logical way of doing things. But even if you''re not doing that, even if you''re not worshipping Me or enslaving yourselves to Me or submitting to Me, I will not deny My assistance to you. You can still always turn to Me. You can still always come to Me, talk to Me.</p>
<p>I will still be there for you. That''s the beauty of ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ</span> ‎. Now, considering the relationship between a master and a slave, what is the slave constantly in need of from his master? Commands, instructions, right? If there are no instructions from a master, if there are no commands coming from a master, then technically that means you''re free. So as the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدُ</span>, we need some instructions from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, right? Now the Arabic word for instructions is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هِدَايَة</span>, which comes from the root word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُدًى</span>. So the next logical progression in this line of thought is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا اللَّهُ</span>, now that I have enslaved myself to You, now that I''m committed to You, now that I''m depending on Your assistance to fulfill my commitment, what is it that You want me to do? How can I pay off this debt of submission that I owe You? How can I move towards You? How can I get closer to You? Give me some instructions, give me some <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُدًى</span>. And so, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> progresses into . Guide us, instruct us towards the straight path.</p>
<p>Scholars point out something very important here in this sequence of declarations. So we just learned that is a commitment, right? It''s a declaration, it''s a promise, it''s our sincere as we call it. Right? That is followed by a call for assistance, which is then followed by asking for instructions. So if you think about the sequence here, we''re not saying give me the instructions first, if I think I''ll be able to do them, if they make sense to me, if I feel like, you know, I''m able to find the time to do them, then I will do them. No. All of that has already been covered in the earlier verses. Right? Starting from we are just making the commitment.</p>
<p>We''re making that promise of that we will do whatever you tell us to do, because that''s all part of being a slave. Right? You don''t become a slave and then say, you know, I''ll do it when it makes sense to me. The making sense of it was all done in the previous three verses. Those were the verses that make us realize that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is worthy of our . Those are the verses where we get convinced of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s mastery over us. Right? Those were the verses where we completed our homework, where we did all our research, they complete the theory portion of this whole message. Starting from now, it''s practical time, it''s all action.</p>
<p>And the reason this is important is because people ask this question, you know, why do we have to take ahead when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السُّلَيْمَان</span> is, when we take, when we make this commitment, people say that, we know, why do I have to make this promise, why is this so important? Because that is the action of commitment. You can think and ponder and plan all you want, but until you actually commit to something, you cannot produce a change. I mean, isn''t that true in every aspect of our lives? Weight loss, the biggest problem of our times.</p>
<p>We can read all about weight loss. In fact, according to a study, obese people are usually the ones who know the most about losing weight, how they need to lose the weight, because they''ve read up so much about it, they''ve watched so many videos on this. But until they commit to a lifestyle of good diet and a lifestyle of exercise, they cannot actually lose the weight, right? So you have to make a commitment to produce change.</p>
<p>And this is why we have I commit only to you and I seek only your assistance, and then we have guide us, give us instructions. So the more sincere we are in our commitment, the more we can expect <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> to help us on our journey towards him. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>, in the next session we will look at the remaining verses. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَآخِرُ دَعْوَانَا أَنِ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى رَسُولِهِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ خَاتِمِ النَّبِيِّينَ وَسَيِّدِ الْمُرْسَلِينَ وَعَلَى أَخِيهِ وَوَصِيِّهِ عَلِيِّ بْنِ أَبِي طَالِبٍ أَشْرَفِ الْوَصِيِّينَ وَعَلَى الْأَئِمَّةِ مِنْ ذُرِّيَّتِهِ الطَّاهِرِينَ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَيْهِمْ أَجْمَعِينَ، الْفَاتِحَة</span>.</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/166/2f3e642f-eccf-48a3-8184-e170c2c0a613.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/166/1bd391c8-bf59-4171-9cfd-5c663dd0f904.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'worship and assistance';
INSERT INTO chapter (slug, anchor_key, idx, title, html, word_count) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'guidance to the straight path', 23, 'Guidance To The Straight Path', '', 5890);
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ</span> ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ صَلِّ يَا اللَّهُ عَلَى سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ وَعَلَى آلِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ وَبَارِكْ وَسَلِّمْ رَبِّ اشْرَحْ لِي صَدْرِي وَيَسِّرْ لِي أَمْرِي وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِنْ لِسَانِي يَفْقَهُوا قَوْلِي السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللَّهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span></p>
<p>We''re studying <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>, and we''ve completed the first four verses: ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> ‎, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ</span>. So now in this session we''re going to look at <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ</span>. This verse is commonly translated as &quot;guide us to the straight path,&quot; right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ</span>, guide us to the straight path.</p>
<p>Now let''s look at what does that mean. Normally we think about it like, you know, so when I say to you, &quot;guide me to your house,&quot; what am I asking for? I''m asking for instructions, right? Directions. That''s what guidance essentially means. It means instructions or directions. Now, how are you going to give me these instructions? What are the different ways in which you can give me instructions or directions? So for instance, you can say, take this particular road and go three miles on it and then turn left, right? That is how typically you will give instructions for a particular destination. You''ll just list it out for the person. Another way you can do that, you can tell me, you can guide me by saying, you know what, just stand at this bus stop, and take this particular bus and then get off at this particular stop. So that''s another way you can guide me. This is guidance through the bus, right? It is guidance through a medium or through an intermediary.</p>
<p>So what you''re saying in the second mode of instructions is that you don''t have to do anything. The bus will do all the work for you. You''re telling me that all you have to do is make sure that you''re at the right bus stop at the right time and you catch the right bus, and then just let the bus get you to your destination. That''s the second way of giving instructions, through an intermediary or through a medium. The third way is that you can say, look, I''m headed home right now, why don''t you just jump in the car with me and I''ll take you all the way, right? Those are the three ways by which you can guide me or direct me to your house. First is through a set of instructions. Second is through a medium or an intermediary. And the third is by literally taking me to that destination yourself. It''s very important that you understand these three, the differences between these three modes of instructions.</p>
<p>Now, what is so cool about this verse, and listen to this carefully, is that asking for guidance through a set of instructions or through a set of directions in Arabic, it is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِهْدِنَا إِلَى صِرَاطِ الْمُسْتَقِيمِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِهْدِنَا إِلَى صِرَاطِ الْمُسْتَقِيمِ</span>. Guide me through a set of instructions. Asking for guidance using a medium would be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِهْدِنَا بِالصِّرَاطِ الْمُسْتَقِيمِ</span>. The letter ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ would be included with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاط</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِهْدِنَا بِالصِّرَاطِ الْمُسْتَقِيمِ</span>. Asking for completely assisted guidance, which is the third form, in which you are literally holding my hand and taking me to the destination, that would be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِهْدِنَا عَلَى صِرَاطِ الْمُسْتَقِيمِ</span>. You see?</p>
<p>Now, what''s interesting about the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَاتِحَة</span> is that we are not asking <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> for instructions by saying, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِهْدِنَا إِلَى صِرَاطِ الْمُسْتَقِيمِ</span>. We''re not asking Him for instructions by saying, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ</span>. And we''re not saying, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِهْدِنَا عَلَى صِرَاطِ الْمُسْتَقِيمِ</span>. We are not using any of those words. We are just simply saying .</p>
<p>You see that? We''re omitting the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>, we''re omitting the ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, and we''re omitting the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَا</span> in our request for our instructions. Now why is that relevant? Because according to the rules of Arabic linguistics, when you don''t use any of these words, what you actually mean is all of them at the same time. You see, the principle of Arabic is that if you are using none of these words, you''re linguistically using all of them, otherwise you would have to use these separate sentences.</p>
<p>So now when we say , what we''re really saying is, direct me to the straight path through your instructions, which is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. We''re saying, guide me to the straight path through your intermediaries, through the mediums, through the example of people like <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span> , <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِنَا عَلِي</span> , the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَإِيمَا</span>, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span>, and then finally, hold my hand and walk me along this path until I reach my destination. Grant me your constant companionship as I walk along this path. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. It''s amazing.</p>
<p>See, a school counselor gives us guidance, guides the students, right? That''s the job of a school counselor. His guidance is ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sically information, right?</p>
<p>A traffic officer''s guidance is information. The GPS guiding you in your car is information, but <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s guidance is more than just information. Because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s guidance has to do with the personal choices that we have to make every single moment. So we''re not just asking for directions, we''re also asking Him for the strength and His assistance in helping us make those right decisions. These are two very different things. Because sometimes we can have all the right information and we can still make the wrong decisions.</p>
<p>Also, if you think about it, you know, guidance, what did we say guidance is? It''s just a set of instructions, right? How many times do we need information? One time. You know, if I''ve been given the right information, I don''t have to ask for it again. Once I know the direction to your house, that''s it. I''m not going to keep asking you again and again. But how many times do we ask <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> for guidance? Over and over again, as a standard, right? It''s like drinking water. You cannot say I drank water yesterday, so I''m never going to need it again. It doesn''t work that way. If you want to survive, you have to drink again and again, every single day.</p>
<p>![](images/167/7500cce9-7ea7-43ad-a8ea-d087e3‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎3830.jpg)</p>
<p>So just as our bodies become thirsty for water and food, our heart keeps starving for guidance. This is why we keep coming ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ck to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> in every single prayer with this request of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ</span>.</p>
<p>Now let''s look at this word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِهْدِنَا</span>. So guidance we already talked about. But what''s really interesting is that if you look at the root word for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِهْدِنَا</span>, it comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُدًى</span>. So in this context, the derivative is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُدًى</span>, which means guidance, which I just explained to you. But what''s interesting is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُدًى</span> is also the root word for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَدِيَّة</span>. Now what does <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَدِيَّة</span> mean? It''s also used in Urdu. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَدِيَّة</span>, it means a gift. Now for an Arab living in the desert when he''s lost, right? Think about an Arab who lives in the desert and he got lost in the desert.</p>
<p>What is the greatest gift that you can give him? Even more than water. Because water is not going to be good enough. It will last him maybe one more day. Food is maybe going to last him a couple more days. So from an Arab''s perspective, especially an Arab who is lost in the desert, what is the greatest of all gift for him? Guidance, directions ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ck to his home, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ck to his family. That''s the ultimate gift. So we''re asking <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> not just for any gift, we''re asking <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> for the ultimate gift. Because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s guidance is the ultimate gift for a believer. So it becomes so much more than just instructions or informations, you see? It completely changes the perspective of a believer from just simply a set of rules.</p>
<p>It creates the sense of gratitude because instructions and directions are now perceived as a gift, a gesture of love, a gesture of care. That''s when you give a gift to somebody, right? You don''t give a gift to somebody you hate. You always give a gift to somebody you love. So now these instructions and this set of information of how to live our life, it no longer feels like this burden that we have to bear, but rather it feels like a gift. That is the perspective that changes when you understand the linguistics of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُدًى</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُدًى</span>.</p>
<p>Now let''s look at <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ</span>. What is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ</span>? So it is generally translated as a path, right? But in the Arabic language, there are certain rules that go with the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ</span>. The first rule is that it can only be used for a straight path. Okay? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ</span>, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الفَاتِحَةِ</span>, it can only be used for a straight path. If a road is bumpy, or if it curves, or if the road is wavy, it cannot be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الفَاتِحَةِ</span>.</p>
<p>Right? So by definition, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الفَاتِحَةِ</span>, it means that the path has to be straight. So the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الفَاتِحَةِ</span>, right, just with the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ضُمَّ</span> at the top, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَة</span>, it is actually used for a perfectly straight sword. That''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَة</span>. If the sword is curved, it is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيْف</span>. You know some of these swords that we see in old movies? So, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِنَا عَلِي</span>''s sword <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذُو الْفِقَار</span> is a good example of that, right? It''s, we know that it''s curved, right? The shape of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذُو الْفِقَار</span> was curved. So what, what is that, what did <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span> say? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا فَتَى إِلَّا عَلِيٌّ، لَا سَيْفَ إِلَّا ذُو الْفَقَارِ</span>. Not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا سُورَةَ إِلَّا ذُو الْفَقَار</span>, right? He said <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا سَيْفَ إِلَّا ذُو الْفَقَارِ</span>, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَة</span> by definition is a curved sword, right? So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاط</span> means a perfectly straight path. That''s the first thing. The second rule is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ</span> can only be used for a wide path. By definition, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ</span> cannot be used for a narrow path.</p>
<p>It has to be a wide path. A path that allows multiple people to travel on it simultaneously. So in our world, just think of it like a major highway with multiple lanes, except that it''s just a perfectly straight highway. You understand? Now, this is something very cool. There are other words in Arabic that can be used for a path. There are various different ways. So, for example, there is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَبِيل</span>, which means a path. There is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَاتِحَة</span>, which means a path. There is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَارِق</span>, which means a path. Now, what''s really interesting about these words is that there are plurals ava<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>ble for all these words.</p>
<p>So, for instance, for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَبِيل</span>, there is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبُل</span>. Right? The plural of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَبِيل</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبُل</span>. For <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَارِق</span>, the plural is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَوَارِق</span>. For <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَاتِحَة</span>, the plural is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِجَاج</span>. What was really interesting is that there is no plural for the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ</span>. It is a singular word. And this is why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاط</span> also means the only ava<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>ble path. So imagine, just imagine these two islands that are connected by a single bridge, right? There is water under the bridge and the water between these two islands, it''s very turbulent. Think about it that way, right?</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/167/8d9fab28-3712-415b-982e-31adf5393271.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>You cannot take a boat to go from one side to the other or one island to the other. The only way for you to get from point A to point B in this case is this one single wide bridge. There is absolutely no other path for you to take except this bridge. You understand? So in this case where there is no alternate route ava<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>ble, then that bridge, that one singular wide path would be called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ</span>. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ</span> linguistically has these peculiar characteristics. It has to have, it has to be a wide path, it has to be a straight path, and it has to be the only path to that destination.</p>
<p>So now what does all of this mean for us? It means that there can be many, many paths ava<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَى</span>ble to us in terms of how we choose to live our lives. There can be many religions, there can be many belief systems, all of which can be correct. But the path that directly leads to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, it can only be one. It means that even though there are many different religions, many different philosophies, all explaining how to reach God, all explaining how to live your life, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الفَاتِحَةِ</span> indicates that there can only be one true religion, the perfect religion.</p>
<p>Think about it like that. The perfect religion. So there is good, there is better, that is best, that is perfect. If you start breaking it down in that way, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ</span> indicates the perfect religion. Right? It''s a religion, a path that will be the shortest distance between point A and point B, connecting the slave to his master.</p>
<p>Now, if this was not interesting enough, which obviously it is, what really blows my mind is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> did not just say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ</span> in this verse, he said <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الصِّرَاطَ</span>, right? He used the definite article, the. This, the path, the particular path. Now <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ</span> already meant one single path by its definition, then why say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الصِّرَاطَ</span>? Because even though <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> is the one true religion, there are many, many sects within <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span>, and by making this word a definite, by adding the definite article to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الفَاتِحَةِ</span>, making it <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الصِّرَاط</span>, what is being taught here, that there can only be one perfectly true sect within <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> itself. Isn''t that incredible? So just by understanding the linguistics of the word, it can answer all of our questions, you know, because people ask this all the time. I used to ask this question all the time, you know, why does there only have to be one true religion?</p>
<p>Why can''t every religion be a true religion? It can''t because the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> does not have a plural. So it can only be singular. It must be straight, which means it must take the shortest route to connect us with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. Right, because the shortest distance between two points is a straight line. Also, it must be a wide path, which means there has to be acceptance for others along the way. That''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ</span>, but then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الصِّرَاطَ</span> means that there is only one sect, one lane that will, the fastest lane, right? Think about it on a highway, you have the slow lane, you have the fast lane, so this is the fast lane that will directly lead to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> using the shortest distance. It''s the most direct path to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللهِ</span>. All of these meanings are encapsulated in the single word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّبْعُ</span>...</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الصِّرَاطَ</span>.</p>
<p>And this is not to say that the rest of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> and all the other sects are all gar‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ge. No, of course not. All it means is that it''s going to take them longer to get there, but they will eventually get there. Everybody is going to eventually get there. And this is why all Muslims of all sects, regardless, right, they pray for this knowledge of this direct path to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, because all Muslims are praying and saying, including us, I mean, we just, you know, knowing the straight, we, so even if I say that is the, is the, is the fastest path, is knowing that path and actually walking the path are two different things. So that is why we''re also saying, we''re also constantly praying for, for, because if you think about it, if we, if we strongly believe, we truly believe in our hearts that we are on the right path, on the right, we are the right sect within the world of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span>, then why do we have to constantly keep asking for this guidance? Why do we have to keep every prayer, why do we have to keep saying, we already are on the right path? But being on the right path and walking the path are two completely different things.</p>
<p>Just by standing on the path, I do not get to my destination. I have to walk the path, right? So there''s so many other nuances within this which I''m skipping over, but I''ve given you the ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sics, and if you really think about it and if you really reflect on it, it''ll open up so many more, it''ll open up a world of understanding for you. Anyway, so that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّرَاةُ</span>.</p>
<p>Now the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْتَقِيمَ</span>, it is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ</span>, right? How is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْتَقِيمَ</span> translated generally? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ</span>, guide us to the straight path. But we already established that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ</span>, it already implies straight, right? Because if it wasn''t straight, it wouldn''t be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ</span>. So now why is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> repeating Himself? He''s not. Because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْتَقِيمَ</span> does not just mean straight, it actually means upright.</p>
<p>The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْتَقِيمَ</span>, it comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَامَ</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتَقَامَ</span>, which means to stand up. Not so much to go forward, but to go upward, right? It''s from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قِيَامٌ</span>, which means to stand upright. So in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span>, in our prayers, you know, we have ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎, we have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُكُوْر</span>, we have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُجُود</span>, right? So what does ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ mean? Means to stand up straight. That''s what ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ is. So when we say we are asking <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> for guidance on a path, which heads which way? Straight up it is. We''re asking guidance for a path of spiritual elevation.</p>
<p>Now think about this. What happens when I travel on a path that puts me on an elevation? The more I travel on it, the higher I will rise. Isn''t that true? Imagine this: a path that is just a physical path. Think of it as a ladder, like this ladder that is going from the ground all the way up to the sky.</p>
<p>As I''m climbing this ladder, what am I rising above? I''m rising above this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>, isn''t it? And as I rise higher, who am I getting close to? I''m getting closer to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. Now, what happens to the view as you rise higher? It becomes better, it becomes broader, right? You start getting a better perspective on things. We already established that walking this path is all about gaining knowledge. So the more we learn, the more knowledge we acquire, the more aware we become of the realities of this world and of the afterlife. This path of spiritual elevation helps mold and form our perspective.</p>
<p>But then there''s a negative side to this as well, because the higher you climb, the more danger there is of falling. So if you are, you know, one foot high and you fall off from a stool, you''ll be okay, right? If you''re 20 feet high and you fall, you''re not going to be so okay. If you''re 100 feet high and you fall, right? So in other words, the person who is traveling on this path for a long time, is he in less danger or is he in more danger?</p>
<p>He''s in more danger. And this is why you''ll see that the higher the rank of the person, the more <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُشُوعٌ</span> this person shows in his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span>, it just manifests, the more, because he has so much more fear in his heart, the, you know, the more <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُشُوع</span> he has, the more he cries on his prayer mat during <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span>. And this is why we find the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحَابَة</span> exercising extreme caution at every single step of their way. Not because they love to be miserable, which is the impression we get sometimes reading these books of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَة الْفَاتِحَة</span>. I mean, at least I used to think that way. I''m like, what is wrong with these people? Like, why? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> has not asked us to be miserable in life. He has asked us to enjoy life and have, lead a good life and all of that. Then why are these people suffering or putting themselves through these hardships of constant prayer, constant fasting? Just, right? Because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> never demanded that. Yet we find, if we read the books of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>, we find these people just striving to the nth degree, and it never made sense to me until I understood the linguistics of this. Why did they do that?</p>
<p>They did that because of this caution and this superior perspective. You know, we look at them and we think that they are suffering. But if we had a chance to ask them, they would say that they were enjoying it, they were loving it, because they were excited about their destination. And so the work to get there no longer felt like work, you understand? So if you have a goal, if there is something that you want to achieve and you''re really crazy about achieving it, you''re really motivated about getting there, the effort to get there is actually enjoyable. You don''t really care about it. Think about a rocket. A rocket that is headed towards space.</p>
<p>That''s literally... it is on a path that is directed upward. Now, we know that this rocket, it needs a lot of fuel, it needs a lot of force, it needs a lot of energy to overcome the gravity of this Earth. But once it breaks through the gravitational field and it enters space, there is no effort required for any kind of movement. All it needs is a bit of thrust here and there, and it''ll just glide to wherever it wants to go. So that''s what the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحَابَة</span> were aiming for. That''s why they put so much work, because then once they perfected it, once they rose above the perspective of this inferior <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>, once they became aware of the unlimited and infinite space, the infinite possibilities of the solar system, g<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَا</span>xies, this universe, then this insignificant <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> became worthless to them.</p>
<p>So if you really grasp this concept, and if you start looking at it from this perspective, you know, you can really reconcile a lot of their behavior in this world. So we say... when we say it in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>, when we pray, what we''re actually saying is: grant us the gift, the gift of guidance. Grant us the gift of true knowledge through your book, through the practical examples of your beloved and chosen people. And then, as we begin our spiritual journey towards you, following the footsteps of your chosen ones, accompany us every single step of the way.</p>
<p>![](images/167/e1618e1c-a163-4415-9af3-b9d008a48‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎0.jpg)</p>
<p>Support us, assist us as we walk through this path that is straight, that is wide, and that is the only path that elevates us towards You. Bring us into this fast lane. Help us move along this fast lane. Help us see things clearly as we rise above this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> and move towards You, and then protect us from falling ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ck into this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>. This is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُنْ</span>, the assistance that we beg for daily in our five daily prayers.</p>
<p>Then we say, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَطَ ٱلَّذِينَ أَنْعَمْتَ عَلَيْهِمْ</span>, the path of those whom You bestowed Your favors upon. Now, what''s interesting is that this verse, it actually uses the past tense. Right? On the path of those on whom you bestowed your favours. So why the past tense? The reason is because all these role models, they exist in the past.</p>
<p>So you know, if you''re a student and you want to get some good career advice, who do you seek that advice from? Do you seek it from your fellow students or do you seek that advice from graduates? Obviously the graduates, right? You''re not going to ask your friend who is studying with you in the same school, in the same class, about career advice. So if you want to become a doctor, you''ll take advice from graduate doctors, you know. What course did you take? How did you get into this program? What are the things that I need to focus on?</p>
<p>So the verse says, the path of those on whom you showered your blessings, the path of the prophets, the path of the messengers, the path of the martyrs, all these righteous people, right? The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَنْعَمْتَ</span> actually comes from the root word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعَمْ</span>, which means something that is smooth and soft, right? There''s this word in Arabic for cattle, you know, cows and sheep. In Arabic they''re called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَنْعَمْتَ</span> because they walk very slowly, they walk in a gentle manner. So this verse is identifying and specifying the path that was requested in the previous verse. You know, when we said, oh <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, guide us on this path, towards this path of spiritual elevation, what is that path? It is the path of those who were successful in reaching you, those who got through the trials of this world in a soft and smooth manner and achieved spiritual elevation.</p>
<p>That is... then we say... not on the path of those who received your wrath, your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَضَبٌ</span>, who became the recipients of your anger, and not on the path of those who are lost. Now this is really interesting, because when the verse talked about blessings, right, when it talked about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَنْعَمْتَ عَلَيْهِمْ</span>, the verse reads, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَطَ ٱلَّذِينَ أَنْعَمْتَ عَلَيْهِمْ</span>, the path on whom you showered your blessings, right? But when it came to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَضَبٌ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْمَغْضُوبِ عَلَيْهِمْ</span>, the ones who receive the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَضَبٌ</span>. You see that? It leaves out the subject. It just says those who received your anger. Not those that you were angry upon, but those who received your anger. See, when I say he got beaten, the question that comes up is who did the beating? It could be one, it could be many, right?</p>
<p>So what this is telling us is that those who receive <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s anger, they''re not just receiving <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s anger, they''re receiving the anger of messengers, the anger of angels, the anger of other believers, the anger of the heavens, the earth, right? They''re receiving the wrath and the anger from all directions. And so this verse, it does not mention <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> when it talks about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَضَب</span>, but it specifically mentions <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> when it talks about blessings, the ones you showered blessings upon. But it is not those who you became angry with, because anger and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَضَبٌ</span> was never the intention of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> to begin with, because He is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰن</span>. So whatever they did, they did it to themselves. You see, they earned it for themselves. But the ones who received blessings, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> helped them along the way towards the right path, you know, with the help of instructions, with the help of guidance, through messengers, through all of these intermediaries, and then also by helping them along the way with His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُدًى</span>, with His knowledge.</p>
<p>So in other words, it means on the path of those who you granted blessings. And what is that blessing? We talked about this earlier, that''s knowledge. So essentially this is saying on the path of those that whom you granted knowledge, the knowledge of realities, and then the ones whom you granted the examples of how to put that knowledge into action through role models like <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span>, through <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْلَانَا عَلِي</span>, through the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِمَامُ</span>, through the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الدُّعَاءُ</span>, and then that personal divine companionship along the way. All of that is encapsulated in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَطَ ٱلَّذِينَ أَنْعَمْتَ عَلَيْهِمْ</span>. Not on the path of those whom you gave knowledge but they chose not to act upon it, and not on the path of those who act but they have no knowledge of what they''re doing. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ضَالِّينَ</span> means the one who is lost, who does not know any better. And notice that the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سِرَاطَ</span> is not used with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْمَغْضُوبِ عَلَيْهِمْ</span> and it is not used with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ضَالِّينَ</span>. You see, it''s only used with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَنْعَمْتَ عَلَيْهِمْ</span>, because that is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ الْمُسْتَقِيمَ</span>, the one that has the blessings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>.</p>
<p>Now, let me try to explain this difference between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْمَغْضُوبِ عَلَيْهِمْ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ضَالِّينَ</span> with a simple example. So I have two children, right? I have Eram and Amir. Eram is 13, Amir is 7. So, you know, they both love chocolate chip cookies, right? So I buy a fresh box from the store and, you know, I give that box to Eram and I say, both of them, I put that box in front of both of them and I say, look guys, I know this is your favorite cookie and, you know, can you see how nice this is and how fresh it looks? Right here, smell it, smell it, right? So I put that in front of them and then I tell them, look, all of this stuff is so cool, but you cannot have them. I''m going to place this right here on the table right in front of you. And then I''m going to go downstairs in my ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sement to record this session on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَاتِحَة</span>. And when I come ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ck, we will eat these together because I brought it for all of us to share together. But until I return, don''t touch these cookies. I''m telling Eram this, right, because she''s the older one.</p>
<p>And I''m also telling her, make sure Amir does not eat them as well, because he''s still a kid. Right? So I explain all of this to Eram and then I go in the ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎sement, I do my session, right? And then I come ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎ck upstairs and what do I find? I find these two cookie monsters sitting on the couch, right? They''re both just wolfing all of these cookies down. Now, who am I going to get mad at? Eram or Amir? Obviously Eram. Why? Because I explicitly, very clearly told her not to eat those cookies. I did not tell Amir.</p>
<p>He doesn''t know what''s going on, right? But I specifically told Eram not to eat the cookies. So obviously, I''m going to be a lot more angry with her because, you know, she knew better. I''ll tell her that, I''ll say, I clearly told you not to eat these cookies and you still disobeyed me. Right? And while I''m shouting at her and scolding her, Amir is looking at me innocently and he''s still like eating these cookies. But you understand the difference, right? The difference between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْمَغْضُوبِ عَلَيْهِمْ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ضَالِّينَ</span> is that the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَغْضُوب</span> are the ones who were given the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَة</span> of knowledge. They were shown the right path and they still chose to disobey <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. They still chose to do the wrong thing. But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ضَالِّينَ</span> are the ones who had no clue that they were doing the wrong thing. It doesn''t mean that they''re innocent.</p>
<p>It doesn''t mean that they will not be taken to account, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> gave all of us intellects, right? Which is a proof and a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُجَّة</span> for and against each and every one of us. Right? It is a proof that we need to seek knowledge. So, &quot;Oh, I just didn''t know&quot; is not a good enough excuse, because that''s what the intellect was given to you for, so that you would gain knowledge. So the are the people who should have strived to gain the necessary knowledge of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and then do the right thing. They should have made that effort to know better. So they''re not off the hook by any sense, but they''re still better than . which are the people who absolutely knew what the right thing to do was and they still chose not to do it. So that''s the difference between these two terms. So this is the real message of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَاتِحَة</span>, this b<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَا</span>nce between knowledge and action.</p>
<p>Right? So, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللَّهِ الرَّحْمَٰنِ الرَّحِيمِ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ الرَّحْمَٰنِ الرَّحِيمِ مَالِكِ يَوْمِ الدِّينِ</span>. These verses are all about knowledge. These are all about seeking knowledge of our Master, getting to know Him, getting to understand Him, understand what He wants, why He created us, what does He want from us? What''s this concept of mastery? What''s this concept of slavery? And what are the advantage of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُبُودِيَّة</span>? And how can we achieve freedom through this? And this idea that we are the ones in need of Him, because He has something that He can give to us that we want, rather than Him wanting us to do something. So that is all encapsulated in these verses. And then we have . This is our recognition and our acceptance of this knowledge being the one true knowledge.</p>
<p>You know, and us making a commitment to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, and after that we have: guide us to the only straight and wide path of knowledge that elevates us and brings us closer towards You. Right? Guide us to the path of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span>, give us the ultimate gift of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span> through the path of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span>, which is Your ultimate blessing. This path that is this religion that is the perfect b<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَا</span>nce between knowledge and action. So guide us along this path through the examples of role models of the people of the past that You have favored, the ones who were wise, who had wisdom. You remember the definition of wisdom: to have correct knowledge and then to act upon it. That''s a wise person, who has the knowledge and then acts upon it. So make us among those people, of these role models of the past, that when they were provided knowledge they acted upon it. Do not make us among those who gain knowledge but then do not act upon that knowledge, because those are the people who will receive Your wrath from all directions on the Day of Judgment, because of the ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> ‎d decisions that they made. And do not make us from among those who act without knowledge, because they are the ones who are lost. So the essence of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> is this b<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَا</span>nce between these two things. It is b<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَا</span>ncing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْد</span> with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَمَلٌ</span>, knowledge with action. It is, you know, b<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَا</span>ncing, it is the b<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَا</span>nce between gaining the knowledge of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, right, striving to acquire the knowledge of His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span>, making a commitment, this promise, this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَهْدِ</span>, which has to come before the action, and only after that comes the instructions, right, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هِدَايَةٌ</span>.</p>
<p>And then we ask <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> to make us among those who created this b<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَا</span>nce between knowledge and action. And this is why Maulana Ali says knowledge without action is a c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَا</span>mity on the soul, and action without knowledge is pure ignorance, it''s perversion. So success, it lies in the middle. It lies at the center of this b<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَا</span>nce between knowledge and action. That is the true manifestation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَبْهُدِيَّة</span>. A slave who obeys through knowledge, who strives to understand the wisdom of his master''s commands and then practically acts upon it, realizing that this is for his own benefit. And that is the entire essence of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَآخِرُ دَعْوَانَا أَنِ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ، وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى رَسُولِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ خَاتِمِ النَّبِيِّينَ وَسَيِّدِ الْمُرْسَلِينَ، وَالْآلِ وَالْوَصِيِّ عَلِيِّ بْنِ أَبِي طَالِبٍ أَشْرَفِ الْوَصِيِّينَ، وَإِلَى الْأَئِمَّةِ مِنْ ذُرِّيَّتِهِ الطَّيِّبِينَ الطَّاهِرِينَ، وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَيْهِمْ أَجْمَعِينَ، الْفَاتِحَة</span></p>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'guidance to the straight path';
INSERT INTO chapter (slug, anchor_key, idx, title, html, word_count) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'staying steadfast on the path', 24, 'Staying Steadfast On The Path', '', 8471);
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ بِسْمِ اللَّهِ الرَّحْمَٰنِ الرَّحِيمِ اللَّهُمَّ صَلِّ عَلَى سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ وَعَلَى آلِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ كَمَا صَلَّيْتَ وَسَلَّمْتَ وَبَارَكْتَ وَرَحِمْتَ وَتَرَحَّمْتَ عَلَى إِبْرَاهِيمَ وَعَلَى آلِ إِبْرَاهِيمَ فِي الْعَالَمِينَ إِنَّكَ حَمِيدٌ مَجِيدٌ السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span> So now that we''ve completed <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> I wanted to do a session on this idea of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِقَامَة</span> which is remaining steadfast on the straight path and this is one of the major challenges of walking the path and it is something that we all probably can relate to we all as Muslims we want to do the right thing we want the straight path but we''re not always able to for various different reasons and then we end up getting frustrated with ourselves and sometimes with religion itself. Now this is something that I have personally struggled with extensively in my life. But then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span> I came across some material and some perspectives that has helped me a lot. And so I thought I would like to share that with you hoping and praying that it will do the same for you. In <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> we request <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> for guidance we say guide us towards the straight path now this verse is the essence of the Surah the initial verses they are basically building up to this one right giving us an understanding of who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ الرَّحْمَٰنِ الرَّحِيمِ مَالِكِ يَوْمِ الدِّينِ</span> but this is it is called the prayer of all prayers. Guide us, guidance. Guide us through instructions, role models and then through your divine companionship. Now the question is what exactly is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ الْمُسْتَقِيمَ</span>? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> actually defines it for us in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ يَاسِين</span>.</p>
<p>where he says and no doubt about it you should worship me for that is the straight path so <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُبُودِيَّة</span> the willing submission is how <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> defines the straight path The person who willingly submits himself or herself to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is the one who has found the straight path, which <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span> we all have. But now the big question is how do we walk the path? Because we never get to our destination by just finding the correct path. We get to it by actually walking it, right? So how do we walk this path? And what is the term? Is there a term for walking this path? There is. And that term is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>, which is a strong mindfulness for the presence of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> in our lives. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>, commonly translated as piety, is essential for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَبُو دِيَّة</span>, for attaining the ranks of servitude. And that is exactly what we asked for when we said, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ</span> ‎ It is only you we worship Again, worship being the straight path.</p>
<p>And it is only you we seek help from. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اسْتَعَانَ</span>, right? What is this method of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اسْتَعَانَ</span>? Seeking <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s help? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> explains that in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْبَقَرَةِ</span>. He says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَأَسْتَعِينُ بِالصَّبْرِ وَالصَّلَاةِ</span> and seek my help through patience and prayer and the same word is used <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَسْتَعِينُ</span> so we have now just solved this equation right we all desire <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ الْمُسْتَقِيمَ</span> the straight path <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْتَقِيم</span> as I explained earlier a path that has <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتَقَامَ</span> built into it right a path of spiritual elevation How is that achieved? Through <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُبُودِيَّة</span>, willing servitude to the will of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. How do we walk this path? By developing piety, by developing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>, an acute awareness of who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is, by establishing a connection with Him. And how do we connect with Him?</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/168/afef5a3f-8851-4eea-9ec8-f3a9bdc1d0ee.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِالصَّبْرِ وَالصَّلَاةِ</span>, through patience and through prayer. Now we all know about the daily prayers. But what does <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَبْر</span> mean here in this context? It means to be steadfast and consistent with our prayers. And then the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَاتٌ</span> continues to say, And no doubt about it, for sure, this is truly an extremely difficult task for most people. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَّا</span>, except, those who possess the knowledge and who possess the knowledge and the awareness of the majesty of their master. People who have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>. So it all ultimately boils down to consistency and being steadfast, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتَقَامَة</span>. Now what is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتَقَامَة</span>? It comes from the same root word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَمَا</span> That was used for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْتَقِيم</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطُ الْمُسْتَقِيمِ</span>, the same root word, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَامَ</span>.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتَقِمْ</span> means to stay firm on something. And how do we stay firm? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> answers that in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> when He says, . He says, establish the prayer, be firm, stay firm on your prayer, stay firm on your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span>. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِقَامَة</span> is to establish something firmly or to be solid in something, to be firm in it. Right? So resilience is one of the words that is used. Grit, some people use the word grit. But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِقَامَة</span> is essentially the ability to stand firm and be consistent in our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span>, in our worship and our belief. To give you sort of a visual representation from the physical world of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِقَامَة</span>, think about a solid tree. A solid tree, it stands firm even when the wind blows, right? Even if a storm hits it, it stays its ground.</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/168/59d175a9-8e91-4862-8d53-491dab98e134.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>Now, the question we''re trying to address is, how can we be like that? How do we develop the ability to stay strong on this path despite the storms of our situations, of our challenges, of our environments? So you must have noticed if you read the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> that in many verses <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, He always mentions two things when He talks about faith. He always couples <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span> with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَمَلْ</span>. He couples faith with action. So we understand that there are two parts to what we are supposed to do. There is the inner part and then there''s the outer part. What''s the inner part? It''s the belief, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span>, the faith. Another way we can think about this in terms of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> is that there is a worship for the heart and then there is a worship for the limbs. The body, the tongue, the ears, the eyes. You understand?</p>
<p>There are the internal <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَات</span> and then there are the external <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَات</span>. The internal has to do with our belief system, with our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span>. How do we think of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>? How do we view Him? How do we respond internally with the things that He has asked us to do? And why He has asked us to do those. That''s all internal. It has to do with our certainty or with our doubt, right? So certainty is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَقِين</span>, doubt is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَقَّ</span>. So we are either certain about it or we have doubts about it. But that all has to do with our internal mechanism, our internal worship system. What are the external <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَات</span>?</p>
<p>Those are the obvious ones, the five time prayers, reading <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, you know, guarding our ears, eyes, all of that, you know, these are all external type of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَات</span>. Now, if I asked you, what do you think is essential for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِقَامَة</span>? Is it the internal or the external? What would you say is essential for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِقَامَة</span>? Our Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span> in a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيثٌ</span>, he stated, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ فِي الْجَسَدِ مُضْغَةً، إِذَا صَلُحَتْ صَلُحَ الْجَسَدُ كُلُّهُ، وَإِذَا فَسَدَتْ فَسَدَ الْجَسَدُ كُلُّهُ، أَلَا وَهِيَ الْقَلْبُ</span> Indeed, within the body, there is a lump of flesh. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِذَا صَلُحَتْ صَلَحَ الْجَسَدُ كُلُّهُ</span> If it is set right, if it is set straight, then the entire body will be set right.</p>
<p>But if it is corrupted, then the entire body will be corrupted. Indeed, beware it is the heart. So what we''re learning from this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيثٌ</span> is that the master of the body isn''t actually the mind. Which sounds a bit strange because many people believe that the mind is what is in control. Well, news flash. It''s actually your heart that is in control. It''s your heart that controls your mind. Now what do I mean by that? The type of heart that you have will control your thoughts. It''ll control your mind and eventually it''ll control your actions. So whatever your heart truly desires, your mind will think of ways to fulfill those desires. And then the limbs will actually make it happen.</p>
<p>They will execute it. You see? So the heart is the king. What the king wants needs to happen. Who helps the king figure out how to make it happen? That''s the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَزِير</span>, that''s the mind, right? That''s the brain. And then there is the army, which is the limbs that actually carry out and execute what the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَزِير</span> comes up with for the king. So the mind actually becomes a tool, an instrument to execute whatever is in the heart. And this is why our Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>, he said if you take care of this lump of flesh, which is the heart. Now everyone be clear that when I say the heart, I''m not talking about the physical heart, right? I''m talking about the spiritual heart, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَلْب</span>. So if this heart is purified, when you take care of that heart, when you feed the heart, when you rectify the heart, then all your actions will automatically become pure. But when the heart is corrupted, when it is starved, when it''s not taken care of, when it is deprived of its spiritual sustenance, then the rest of the actions will just naturally get corrupted. And what''s really interesting, that if you look at, like, if you want to see this theory in its practical form, let''s look at the chronological theme of revelation. Just think about how the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> was revealed.</p>
<p>What were the first verses to be revealed? They were not about drinking alcohol and they were not about preventing people from committing fornication. They were not about the rules of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَلَالٌ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span>. The first verses to be revealed were all about building <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. They were all about building an attachment with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, about understanding the Day of Judgment, realizing and learning that we will be taken to account for our actions, that we''re not just living in this life without a purpose like animals, that there is a purpose to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيدٌ</span>, there is a purpose to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s oneness. So the initial verses of revelation were about, you know, the Day of Judgment, they were about retribution, they were about loving <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, they were about understanding what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has done for us, it was about understanding what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> wants from us, about life and death, about, you know, basically who He is and what He wants. Why? Because all those initial verses of revelation, they were building a foundation for us, they were setting the foundation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِقَامَة</span>.</p>
<p>So now if we go back to the example of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاة</span> tree, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> says in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ إِبْرَاهِيمَ</span>, when He gives the example of a good word, He says that a good word is like a good tree. ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَمْ تَرَ كَيْفَ ضَرَبَ ٱللَّهُ مَثَلًۭا كَلِمَةًۭ طَيِّبَةًۭ كَشَجَرَةٍۢ طَيِّبَةٍ أَصْلُهَا ثَابِتٌۭ وَفَرْعُهَا فِى ٱلسَّمَآءِ</span> ‎. Do you not see how <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> sets forth the example of a good word, which is like a fruitful tree? Its roots are firmly fixed into the earth and its branches rise up to the sky. So in other words, its foundation is solid. That''s what the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> uses. And then its branches rise up towards the sky. See, you can become elevated. Right, you can grow, you can get higher, you can force yourself into a path of spiritual elevation, but if you don''t have solid roots, it''s not gonna last long, and this is what happens with so many people.</p>
<p>And this is something that I have struggled with myself, so I can actually speak from experience, that I can, I have forced myself into the prayers, fasting and this and that, and I do it for a while and I push myself and I push myself and I just grit my teeth and I do it, but doesn''t last. Something happens and suddenly everything goes out the window. And this was happening for a while until I finally understood these things, because what I realized is that the taller a tree gets, the deeper its roots have to go. So if you study the way <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has designed a tree, you''ll see that the taller it is, the more deep and wide its roots are spread within the earth. In fact, even mountains we know have foundations, and the taller it is, the more it is grounded within the earth. Everything, anything in existence, in order for it to be solid and firm and to have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِقَامَة</span>, it must have a solid foundation.</p>
<p>And so <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is telling us, and even in the training that He gives us, He did not start out with the rules, He started out with building our foundation. And this is why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>, which is essentially the summary of the entire <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, right? It''s a description of what you are about to find in the remaining 113 <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>. It begins, look how <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span> begins, by creating an understanding of who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ، الرَّحْمَٰنِ الرَّحِيمِ، مَالِكِ يَوْمِ الدِّينِ</span>. These are all verses about knowing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, about setting this foundation. And once this foundation is built, which will naturally engender <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوَكُّل</span>, which is, you know, our reliance and trust in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, only then we are taught to ask for the rules and guidance. But look at the order of these verses, right? The beginning verses are designed to build a solid foundation.</p>
<p>So that when the rules are revealed, we can just have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِقَامَة</span>. You understand? If we look at the example of the tree again, when a wind blows, if the roots of the tree are deep and they are firm within the ground, what happens to the tree? Well, its leaves might move, the branches might shake and break, but the tree stays standing. Right? It''s solid. But what happens to a tree that does not have deep roots? It easily gets knocked over. And I believe that the problem with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الإِسْلَام</span>ic training these days is that we are raised in such a way that we just don''t have solid roots. Our training method is the rule shaming method. It''s this rule oriented or rule shaming method. This is the method where you grow up as a kid and the only thing you are really taught about is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span> and hellfire.</p>
<p>And the emphasis is on if you do <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span>, you will go to hellfire. And that''s how many of us were raised and that''s how many of us are raising our children. Well, it is true that there are things that are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span>. It is true that there is hellfire. It is true that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has anger and wrath. This is all true. But why are children being taught that above His mercy? Why are we not teaching our children that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is the most merciful? And that even if you make a mistake, if you make a mistake and you repent, you can always try again. Why is this not the message that is being taught? And the answer, unfortunately, is because this is to control the behavior of the child. So many of us use fear as this control mechanism.</p>
<p>The only problem is that this might have worked maybe somewhat in my generation, more so in the previous generation, but in the new generation, it just backfires. When we''re using false fear or false shame, unhealthy shame to control someone''s behavior, we''re not building solid roots. What we''re building is avoidance. Imagine that you grew up in a household where you''re always being told, let''s take police as an example, right? You''re always being told that you better watch out for the police. They''re going to throw you in jail. Don''t make a mistake. You better not do this. You better not do that because, you know, the police is going to catch you and they''re going to throw you in jail. They''re going to punish you. So you are basically taught to be afraid of the police.</p>
<p>Why? Because police is made out to be this very wrathful and scary entity. Now, if you grow up with this kind of training, years and years of this kind of material being fed to you, best case scenario, you will learn to avoid the police. That''s the best case scenario. But will you want to be close to the police? Will you want to be best friends with the police? Will you confide your problems to the police? Will you turn to the police for help when you''re in trouble? No, because you don''t trust the police and you''ve been told that they''re just there to punish you. You see? And so what''s happening is that this type of training is not building <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتَقَامَ</span>. It''s not building any form of stability in our youth and in our own selves.</p>
<p>It''s building avoidance. So many people, when they are raised this way, when they grow up, they don''t want to have anything to do with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. They actually want to avoid <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> and religion completely. Because they say, you know what, everything is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span>, no matter what I do, I''m going to end up going to hell anyway. Everything I do is wrong, so what''s the point in trying? You know, and to make it worse in our teachings and these, you know, there are some, I''ve heard someone say that, you know, if you don''t do <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَمَاز</span> this way and if you don''t do <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُكوع</span> this way and such that your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَمَاز</span> will be thrown in your face. I mean, this is, you know, a child making an effort to actually stand up for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَمَاز</span>. And then you tell him that, okay, so everything I do is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span> and I''m going to get punished for it, but the good things that I do are not good enough. You tell me, why should anyone come to a religion like that?</p>
<p>So what happens is that everybody starts avoiding religion and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> together, all together, because it''s backfiring. And the reason it''s backfiring is because we are trying to grow this tree of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الإِسْلَام</span> within ourselves and our children without creating any solid foundation. So even miraculously if this tree sustains long enough to grow, it then is not able to survive the smallest earthquake, you see? It cannot stand the smallest storm. So we need to focus on building a solid foundation so that when life hits us, because we are going to be shaken in life, there''s no doubt about that, I mean, look at the times we live in with all these temptations around us, these desires going crazy, right, the tree, this tree of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الإِسْلَام</span> within our hearts, it will be shaken from time to time. We will be tested through trials.</p>
<p>That''s not something that should come as a surprise to anyone, right? That is something that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> clearly says in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. Do you really think that you will enter paradise without going through that which those who came before you went through? They were struck with calamity, they were struck with adversity, they struggled, and then they were shaken. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زُلْزِلُوا</span> in Arabic, it actually comes from the same root as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زِلْزَال</span>, which means an earthquake. So they were shaken like an earthquake, and they were tried so much and they were shaken so much that even the messengers and those with the messengers, they started asking, when will the help of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> arrive? Indeed, the help of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is near. The help of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> isn''t just there. It doesn''t just exist as a concept. It''s there right now with you as you''re going through the test. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says that we are closer to you than your jugular vein. And what''s interesting about that, you know, a lot of people don''t understand what that means. That''s the verse.</p>
<p>What does that mean? It means that we are closer to you than your own consciousness. See, jugular vein, you cut the jugular vein, what''s the first thing that the person loses? Consciousness, right? So what that means is that we know what you are about to think before you think it. It''s that kind of proximity. So we are going to be shaken from time to time. Now, how does a building withstand an earthquake? Again, the answer all goes back to the type of foundation it has. That is what will determine its <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِقَامَة</span>. So the question we have to ask ourselves is what type of foundation do we have with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>?</p>
<p>When I am tested through pain or through pleasure, will I be able to withstand it? Will I be able to be firm on the straight path? And if the honest answer to that is no, that means I need to work on my relationship with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, not work on the rules. Not try to increase my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span> or my fasting, and I need to first work on my relationship. The rules, the actions will just follow. And this is why scholars have said that the first verses that were revealed, they did not say give up alcohol and give up fornication, because people would have said, you know, we''ll never give up those things, and they would never have if it started with that, because they had not yet built a foundation with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. They had not yet built the roots. So if you come to a person from the very start and you give them rules, they''re just not going to follow it, because it goes against what they want. And that is the very mistake that many of us make with ourselves and with our children. I made that with myself. So we''re not building a foundation, we''re constantly throwing rules at them. We''re not talking about building an attachment with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, we''re not teaching them to love <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, we''re not teaching them what it really means to put our reliance on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. Where is that being taught? The only thing being taught to our children is that, you know, this is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span>, that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span>, it''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span> to eat this, it''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span> to drink that, it''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span> to dress like that, it''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span> to say this. I mean, that''s it, right?</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/surah-al-fateha/image/168/dbd071d9-0c31-4c3e-9250-c83d94f7a905.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>And the problem with that method is that everything about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الإِسْلَام</span> is being rejected. And that is exactly what the scholars said, right? That if you start off with the rules, if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> had started off with the rules, even with the companions, these were the best people that existed, but even they would have rejected the rules. And this is why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> did not start with the rules. I mean, look at <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جُزْءُ عَمَّ</span>, right, the 30th chapter of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, it''s basically Meccan, majority of the verses in that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جُزْء</span> were revealed in the initial stages of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الإِسْلَام</span>, in the first 10 years of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الإِسْلَام</span>. Where do you find rules in that? Do you find any rules? Not really. It talks about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, it talks about His love, His mercy, it talks about the Day of Judgment, it talks about human beings, it talks about their relationships with themselves, among themselves, with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, it talks about, you know, human psychology, it talks about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>, it talks about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوَكُّل</span>, it talks about retribution, it teaches about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَنَّة</span>, it teaches about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَهَنَّم</span>, right, it focuses, you know, on all these things that are relationship building, it''s all foundation building stuff in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جُزْءُ عَمَّ</span>. Because if you want <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِقَامَة</span>, you have to build a foundation in the love of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p>See, this brings me to an interesting point about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>, that when we generally talk about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>, we think in terms of shame and fear. Right? And you might say, well, that is a good thing, isn''t it? I mean, what''s wrong with fear? Well, interestingly, today, psychologists, they are telling us that there is healthy fear and then there is unhealthy fear.</p>
<p>Okay, there is healthy fear and then there is unhealthy fear. Let me give you an example. You know when you do something really bad and you''re really scared of your parents or your loved ones finding out? Sometimes what do we do? Do you run to the parent or your loved one or do you run away from them? You run away from them and you hide, right? So there''s basically two ways to respond when you hurt someone or when you make a mistake. So imagine that you just hurt your closest friend or you hurt your spouse. There are some people who feel so bad about it, so ashamed about it, what they do is that they hide. They try to avoid the person. They try to hide from the issue. They try to avoid the conversation.</p>
<p>They distance themselves from the person, right? Now is that a healthy way to deal with it? Does that actually solve the problem or does it make it worse? It makes it worse. And this is exactly what we do with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. When many of us feel like we''ve made a mistake and we''re so ashamed, instead of going towards <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, we run away from Him. We try to hide. So we don''t want to pray anymore because, you know, we feel so ashamed. And we don''t want to talk about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, we don''t want to talk about religion, because it makes us feel ashamed. But then what''s a healthier way to deal with this? When you''ve hurt someone, what''s the healthiest way to fix it? To go to the person and what? Apologize. To go to the person and say, I''m sorry, I regret it. I admit it. I made a mistake. I''m going to try to do better.</p>
<p>Give me another chance. I will try to do better. And if I fail, I will try again. And I will try again. And I will try again. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَسْتَغْفِرْ</span>. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span>. That is what we''re supposed to do. Because that is healthy fear. That is the healthy response when you make a mistake. That instead of trying to hide from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, instead of saying, you know, I just don''t want to do anything with religion, I want nothing to do with God, I''m not even gonna try, I''m not gonna pray because, you know, I''ve committed so many sins, you know, I just feel so ashamed to face <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. That''s an unhealthy type of shame. But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> is not unhealthy fear. It is not unhealthy shame.</p>
<p>It is healthy fear. It is the kind of healthy shame that will help you try to stay away from what displeases or what angers <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. And when you do what angers <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, you will rush to apologize and you will rush to repent. You will try to make things better.</p>
<p>And the reason I''m mentioning all of this is because I feel what''s happening is that we are just feeding the wrong kind of fear and shame. Because, you know, when you talk about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> or you think about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> like a scary policeman, I mean, you''re encouraging yourself, you''re encouraging your children and the people around you to actually avoid <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. You''re subconsciously teaching them to run away from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, not to run towards Him, not to apologize, to fix it. So healthy fear will motivate us to get closer to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> and repent. Unhealthy shame will make us run away from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> and it''ll make us avoid Him out of shame. So in order for us to have this ability to stay firm on our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span>, to have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتَقَامَ</span> on the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطَ الْمُسْتَقِيمَ</span>, we all need to learn to develop this healthy fear of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, which is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>. Now how do we do that? How do we build healthy fear?</p>
<p>You know what the seed of healthy fear is? What the seed of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> is? This is interesting. Actually, let me put it this way. Let''s back up. What''s the seed to unhealthy shame? We know that a seed is the essence, right? It''s the seed that grows into a tree. But it all starts at the seed level. So if you want to address something, we should always look at the root cause, right? The seed. Now, if you look at the seed of unhealthy shame, you know what it is?</p>
<p>It''s thinking that you are supposed to be perfect. It''s this myth of perfection that lies at the root of our problems. It is when you''re taught and you start believing that you''re actually supposed to be perfect. And if you believe that, then you will react with unhealthy shame when you make a mistake. Why? Because you feel like I was supposed to be perfect. And I''m not perfect and I''m so ashamed of myself, I''m not even going to try anymore because this is too hard. So what that does is it leads us to despair. But what was the root cause? What was the seed? That you thought that you were supposed to be perfect. Because that''s what you were taught by your family, your teachers, your culture, whatever.</p>
<p>Now let''s go to the root or the seed of healthy <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> and healthy <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَسْتَغْفِرُ</span> and healthy <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span>, you know, going back to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. Do you know what the root is? Knowing, understanding that all human beings will make mistakes. It''s actually the opposite. It is realizing that the human being is imperfect. Understanding that we have been created as imperfect human beings. When you know that, when you understand that, then you can easily respond with repentance, because you know that you''re human and so you messed up. Okay, I messed up. I screwed up. I screwed up big time. But I''m gonna fix it. I''m gonna try and repent. I''ll try again. I''ll try harder. Even if I make the same mistake over and over and over again and I keep repenting over and over and over again, I will not give up trying to do better. That is the essence of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span>. Imperfect human beings trying to take one tiny step towards perfection. That''s what we are trying. Trying to make tomorrow better than yesterday.</p>
<p>That''s what we are supposed to do. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>, he said, all children of Adam will make mistakes, but the best of them are the ones who repent. So we have to let go of this crazy myth of perfection, because it''s the root cause of so many of our problems. It is the reason why we keep, you know, falling into despair. Because if you''re told you''re supposed to be perfect and you end up realizing that you''re not perfect, or you cannot be perfect, or it''s just too difficult to be perfect, that is frustrating, that is irritating. But when you understand that we are created with this, you know, this design of being imperfect. How can you go against the divine design? Tell me that. Just tell me that. How can you go against the divine design? How can anyone do that? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says that He created us imperfect. Look at the descriptions of human beings in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>.</p>
<p>I mean, seriously. This, like, you know, clay, pottery clay. We created man weak. We made him impatient. Indeed, man has been created anxious. Look at, I mean, these are some descriptions of human being in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. Weak, built from filthy liquid, hasty, impatient, anxious. So how can we go against that? It''s impossible. And when you try to go against that, you will end up hitting with despair. But then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, he balances out our weakness by telling us, oh my servants, my servants, my beloved servants. What type of servants? Who have transgressed completely against their soul by their constant sins?</p>
<p>Do not despair of the mercy of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. You know, when we commit sins, okay, everybody get this straight, when we commit sins, when we do something that has been forbidden, we are not hurting <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. Just be very clear about that. When we do something good, we''re not doing any favors to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. When we do something bad, we''re not hurting <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. We''re only hurting ourselves, because we cannot wrong <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, right? We cannot hurt <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. We cannot take anything away from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. His kingdom is infinite. Everybody be very clear about that. And by doing anything good, we''re not doing him any kind of favors. So, you know, by doing, when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> forbids us from something, it''s for our own benefit. It''s for the benefit of our spirituality. It''s for us to move along the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صُرَاطُ الْمُسْتَقِيمِ</span>. So by doing wrong, by committing what is forbidden, we are only hurting our own souls. So be very clear about that. You know, it''s like if you drink poison, are you hurting yourself or the doctor? The doctor is telling you don''t drink poison because the doctor understands that what you''re drinking is poison. You''re drinking it because it tastes good. It feels good. It makes you high. But if you drink that poison, are you essentially hurting yourself or the doctor? If you refuse then to take the medicine, the antidote for that poison, are you hurting yourself or are you hurting the doctor? You''re hurting yourself.</p>
<p>So just realize that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is not the one that we are wronging by committing a sin. We are hurting none other than our own souls. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says, tell my slaves, my beloved slaves, the ones that I have created with love and compassion, and these people who are hurting themselves by doing wrong, who are transgressing against their own selves, who are transgressing against their own souls, do not despair in the mercy of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is telling us not to despair, but many of us, we despair, because we have this wrong concept about ourselves and this wrong concept about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. Our egos just cannot bear the fact that we are not perfect. So we think of ourselves as, you know, that I''m supposed to be perfect, I better be perfect, and if I''m not perfect, then I''m worthless. So we all have this, you know, this all or none mindset. I mean, I either have to be an angel, otherwise I am going to be the devil.</p>
<p>This is something I thought only I used to struggle with, but I found out that this is very common, because I used to always think like that. There''s no way I can be good, there''s no way I can be an angel, but yeah, I can be a devil, that comes easier to me, so I''m just going to go that way. In my head it was all black or white. But we have to stop that. It changed my life when I stopped thinking like that. You know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَنِي آدَمَ</span> is not one or the other. The children of Adam, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَنِي آدَمَ</span>, were not angels and were not a devil.</p>
<p>A believer is actually someone who is flawed, contrary to everything that we''ve been taught so far. A believer is actually someone who is flawed but who continually strives to repent, and who tries really hard to get it right, who doesn''t give up, who constantly strives to get back up after he falls, who never gives up. That is a believer, that is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُؤْمِن</span>, not somebody who is flawless. This is why the Prophet said that all children of Adam will make mistakes, but the best are those who repent.</p>
<p>Letting go of this false sense of perfection will allow us this ability to get back up when we fall. And you want to hear something else that''s really counterintuitive? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> feeds off this false sense of perfection. I mean, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> really does a number with it. He uses it as a weapon against us. You know how? When you make a mistake or when I make a mistake, he''ll come to us and he''ll say, look at you. How shameful is you? You''re gonna pray now after you did this? What kind of a terrible person are you? Do you not have any shame? Look what a hypocrite you are.</p>
<p>Just look at you. Now you''re gonna stand before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> after doing this. He''s not even gonna look at you. Who would look at you? Would you look at somebody else who did that to you? Would you be merciful on somebody who did something like that to you? Of course not. So then if you wouldn''t be merciful towards somebody like that, then why do you expect <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> to be merciful to you? You don''t deserve His mercy. You don''t deserve His love. Does that sound familiar? These kind of thoughts?</p>
<p>This is how <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> uses this tool of false perfection against us. He''ll put us through this unhealthy shame. He''ll drag our face through this unhealthy fear and shame, and then when we''re done shaming ourselves and bashing ourselves up, then he''ll come to us from a different angle and he''ll say, do you really want to believe in a God who puts you through this mental torture? I mean, think about it. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> created you as imperfect human being and He''s now demanding perfection of you. What kind of a horrible master is this? I mean, what kind of ridiculous religion is this? Look at the contradiction in it. Just leave it, right? Look how <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> works, right? He''s the one who put us through this unhealthy shame, then he comes at us from a different angle. How could <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> do this to you?</p>
<p>How could He put that alcohol in your presence? Didn''t He know that you''re weak in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>? He says that He knows everything. He says He''s closer than your jugular vein. He knew that you would drink that alcohol. Why did He put it in your presence? Whose fault is it? Why would He do that to you? It''s not your fault you drank that alcohol. It''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s fault, clearly. Look at that. It''s just logic. I mean, look at this religion. How stupid is that, you see? And you know what we do? We say, you know what, that makes perfect logical sense. If really <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, you know, He should have known better, so you know what, screw religion. But if we realize that we are not perfect and that we were never created to be perfect, then when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> comes to us in these ways, and he gives us all these brilliant ideas, we say, you know what? Thank you for that. Yes, I know I made a mistake, but my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is Most Merciful.</p>
<p>My <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is not like me. My <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, I may not forgive another person if he does something wrong to me. I may not forgive this other person if he does it again. I will definitely not forgive him if he does it again and again and again, the same thing. But I am not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. But my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is not like me. My <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is way more merciful than I can ever be, than all of humanity combined can ever be. Because my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is, He is the Most Forgiving. He will forgive me if I try again, and even if I fail after that, and if I repent again, He will forgive me again. But I will keep on trying until I get it right. That''s what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> wants, us to have hope in His mercy.</p>
<p>There''s this beautiful <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيثٌ</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span> in which, it''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيثٌ</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُدْسِيّ</span>, in which <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is talking to us through the Prophet, and He said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا ابْنَ آدَمَ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says, O son of Adam, as long as you call upon Me and ask of Me, I shall forgive you for what you have done and I will not even care about it. I won''t even care what you did. That''s how easily I will forgive you. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا ابْنَ آدَمَ، لَوْ بَلَغَتْ ذُنُوبُكَ عَنَانَ السَّمَاءِ ثُمَّ اسْتَغْفَرْتَنِي غَفَرْتُ لَكَ</span>. O son of Adam, were your sins so big, so many, to reach the clouds of the sky, and were you to then ask forgiveness of Me, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفَرْتُ لَك</span>, I would instantly forgive you.</p>
<p>I don''t have time to get into the linguistics of this, but it''s so beautiful. Instantly forgive you. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا ابْنَ آدَمَ، إِنَّكَ لَوْ أَتَيْتَنِي بِقُرَابِ الْأَرْضِ خَطَايَا ثُمَّ لَقِيتَنِي لَا تُشْرِكُ بِي شَيْئًا لَأَتَيْتُكَ بِقُرَابِهَا مَغْفِرَةً</span>. O son of Adam, were you to come to Me with sins as great as this earth, and were you then to face Me, ascribing no partnership with Me, I would bring you forgiveness as great as your sin is. That''s who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is. I mean, according to this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيثٌ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says that if someone goes to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> with sins that fills the area between the heavens and the earth, I mean, do you know how much that area is? I mean, think about it, the area between the earth and the sky filled with sins. Who has that many sins?</p>
<p>That''s a' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'staying steadfast on the path';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || ' lot of sins, right? That''s a lot. And what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is saying, that if anyone comes to Him with sins filling the area between the heaven and the earth, and then they sincerely repent to Him, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> will give them forgiveness which is as big or more. So that is who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is. He is not a policeman who is waiting to throw us in jail. He is the Most Merciful. But the problem is that we are not teaching His mercy. We''re just teaching His wrath. We''re not teaching any attachment to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. We''re just teaching rules. We''re not talking about the roots, we''re talking about the leaves and the branches. You have to eat like this, you have to dress like this, you have to walk like this, you have to eat, sleep and spit like this.</p>
<p>I mean this is allowed, this is not, you know, these micronutrients, this micro-ingredient is not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَلَالٌ</span> within the pizza. I mean, it''s kind of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَلَالٌ</span>, but it''s not 100. I''ve actually seen a bottle of water that says <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَلَالٌ</span> certified on it. A bottle of water that says <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَلَالٌ</span> certified on it. It''s water. *<strong> </strong>** it. I mean, this is missing the fruit, what do we call it, the forest for the trees, right? This is it. This is missing the forest for a leaf on a tree. We''re not focusing on the roots. So what ends up happening is we end up creating either this aversion and this hatred for religion, you know, completely abandoning it, or then there is this external shell, this lifeless shell of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> that just crumbles to the ground with the first trial. I mean, don''t get me wrong, I do not belittle the importance of the outer worship.</p>
<p>Of course, it''s a combination of both things. That''s how I started by saying that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span> is the outer worship coupled with the inner worship. That''s why I began with that, right? It''s both <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آمَنُوا</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَمِلَ صَالِحَاتٍ</span>. But what I''m saying is that you cannot do one and not the other. The problem, what I''m saying is that many of us are focusing only on the outer aspects, and if you just focus on the outer, it''s like growing a tree without roots. So when the wind comes, it''ll just knock it down. We all need to have solid roots, which is the relationship with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p>So, how do we build these roots practically? First, I told you, we remove this myth of human perfection from our minds. We have to set a different understanding, that we as human beings, we are not perfect, we were not created to be perfect, and a true believer is somebody who acknowledges that imperfection and then takes the step to better himself. So we are created as human beings to keep repenting and keep trying again and take small tiny steps every day towards that perfection, which we may or we may never achieve, which is fine.</p>
<p>But at least we''re walking in the right direction. We''re not walking in the opposite direction. I mean, can you imagine a child that is learning to walk for the first time, this child, right? He''s learning to walk and he falls down and he says, you know what? I''m just going to stop trying. This is too hard. I''m just going to sit here and that''s it. I''m never going to walk. That child will never learn how to walk. Can you imagine a person driving and the moment he makes a wrong turn, they just switch off their car. You know, they put it in park and they just say, you know what, forget it. I''m not going to drive.</p>
<p>This whole car is stupid. This whole route is stupid. I''m done. That''s it. I made a wrong turn. Imagine, okay, imagine your GPS saying you made a wrong turn. Sorry, right? You turn left instead of right and your GPS goes, you know what? You made a wrong turn. Just stop trying. I''m not giving you any directions. Turn off the car.</p>
<p>Your trip is over. Does the GPS do that? No. What does it do? It does something completely different. What does it say? Recalculating, rerouting. What is that in Arabic? That''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span>. That''s actually literally <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span> because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span> means to return, return. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span> is our internal mechanism rerouting us back to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. That''s the process of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَسْتَغْفِرُ</span>.</p>
<p>The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span> in Arabic literally means to turn around and return. How many times does the GPS of your car reroute you? Just once? If you take several wrong turns, what does the GPS say? You know what? You''re an idiot. It''s pointless trying to give you directions. You just keep making the same wrong turns. I''m not going to guide you anymore. You know, does the GPS do that? No, it keeps rerouting you. How many times?</p>
<p>Millions, billions of times if it has to. Infinite number of times. Tell me I''m wrong. A GPS, a man-made machine, is capable of doing that. You think <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> cannot do that? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. So we have to change the way we think. We have to be able to get back up after we fall. We have to learn to repent and we have to learn to keep going and keep trying. That is the first thing for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتَقَامَ</span>. Now what we can do to develop this foundation</p>
<p>is actually very simple. It''s a simple three-ingredient recipe. The first is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span>. That''s like the oxygen of the heart. That is where we connect with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. That''s where we have that communication with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. That''s where we go to the person we''ve wronged and we say sorry. It''s what keeps us spiritually, psychologically and mentally connected with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. So no matter what you do, even if you''re doing the worst things possible, don''t ignore your prayers. Keep getting there, keep connecting. If you can do five, do three. If you can do three, do two, do one. At least try to pray once a day, and then rejoice in that prayer. Be really happy and proud of that prayer. I mean, no matter how distracted or imperfect you think it was, you know what, if you did a fast and furious kind of prayer.</p>
<p>This is another problem we face, that again stems from this myth of perfection. If I asked you to list out for me all your sins and all your mistakes that you committed today, you''ll probably be able to give me a long list. But if I asked you to list out for me all your good deeds of the day, I bet you''re going to struggle with that.</p>
<p>Can I write this down? Is it good enough? Does that count? First of all, you won''t even remember half the things you did. Because we don''t appreciate the good that we do as much as we beat ourselves over the wrong that we do. You see? So from now on, every good deed that you do, from tasting that salt before, if you remember to taste that salt before your food, to wearing your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَة</span>, to, I don''t know, to smiling at someone, every little thing. I want you to be grateful for that. I want you to acknowledge it. I want you to be happy about it. I want you to remember <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> when you do that. I want you to, you know, make the angels write that in your book by saying <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>. Keep reminding yourself of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> after every good deed, and then feel really happy about it and really proud about it. Just for remembering to say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللَّهِ</span> before something, you know, from saying <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ اللَّهِ</span> to saving somebody''s life and everything in between, I want you to be happy and rejoice in every good deed that you do. You did a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span>, you did one <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span>, just the first, and you were Amazon shopping while you were doing that. You know what, you did <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَمَاز</span>, you did <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span>, you stood up to remember <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, you dropped whatever you were doing and you connected with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. Be happy about that. Try to do better next time, but be happy. Don''t crap on what you did. Don''t discredit the effort that you made.</p>
<p>This is how you build relationship with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, slowly, small chunks, by thinking about Him, by remembering Him, consciously realizing that this is something you''re actually doing to please Him every time you do it. So be easy on yourselves. Enjoy what you''re doing. Enjoy the good that you are doing to make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> happy. That''s the first. Second is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span>, the daily, the remembrance of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> through supplications. That''s the GPS rerouting us. And the third is the recitation and understanding of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. I mean, you have to stay connected to it daily, even if one verse a day, I don''t care. If you do this daily and you understand it and you''re consistent with it, you will build <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِقَامَة</span>. You will build this resilience on the straight path, this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الصِّرَاطَ الْمُسْتَقِيمَ</span>. I mean, these are things that have really, really helped me in life, and I really hope it''ll do the same for you. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَآخِرُ دَعْوَانَا أَنِ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ وَبِالْعَالَمِينَ، وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ وَعَلَى رَسُولِهِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ خَاتِمِ النَّبِيِّينَ وَسَيِّدِ الْمُرْسَلِينَ، وَعَلَى أَخِيهِ وَوَصِيِّهِ عَلِيِّ بْنِ أَبِي طَالِبٍ أَشْرَفِ الْوَصِيِّينَ، وَعَلَى الْأَئِمَّةِ مِنْ ذُرِّيَّتِهِمَا الطَّيِّبِينَ الطَّاهِرِينَ، وَسَلِّمْ عَلَيْهِمْ أَجْمَعِينَ، الْفَاتِحَةُ</span></p>' WHERE slug = 'surah-al-fateha' AND anchor_key = 'staying steadfast on the path';
