INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'linguistic meaning of allah', 'student:418c605f521a73fc', 1, 'Another derivative of the word الہ comes from وَلِہَ which means to b…', 'Another derivative of the word الہ comes from وَلِہَ which means to become astounded', '<p>The honest answer is that <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَهَ</span> and <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِهَ</span> come from a <strong>different root</strong> than <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الهٔ</span>. The root of <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَهَ</span> is <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">و</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ل</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ه</span>, while the root of <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الهٔ</span> is <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أ</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ل</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ه</span> — two separate entries in the classical Arabic lexicon, not parent and child. A traditional dictionary lists them under different headings, and a morphologist would not derive one from the other.</p><p>What the chapter is actually doing is building a <strong>semantic portrait</strong> — gathering words from neighboring roots that circle the same human experience: the aching devotion, the desperate turning-toward, the loss of self in a beloved. A concept as vast as what <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الهٔ</span> names has echoes scattered across roots that touch the same emotional terrain, and the chapter arranges them like facets of a single gem.</p><p>In Arabic scholarship, what the chapter calls a composite word is closer to what linguists call a <strong>semantic field</strong> — a cluster of roots that illuminate one another because they orbit the same reality. None of these words is invented; each is attested in classical Arabic with the meaning the chapter assigns it. The insight is not in claiming they share a root; it is in placing them side by side so that together they map a territory no single root could cover.</p><p>One final nuance: in Semitic linguistics, the first radical of a root can sometimes alternate between a glottal (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أ</span>) and a semi-vowel (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">و</span>) across related ancient languages, hinting at a distant <strong>shared ancestry</strong>. But that is the archaeology of Proto-Semitic, not a claim the chapter makes — and it does not change the practical Arabic reality that <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أ</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ل</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ه</span> and <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">و</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ل</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ه</span> are two distinct roots.</p>', '["وَلَهَ (frantic love): from the root و-ل-ه, meaning to be distracted and distraught — as a mother undone by the loss of her child. The root captures the state where the mind loses its grip before overwhelming love or grief. It yields مُوَلَّه, the one driven to distraction by the absence of a beloved.", "أَلَهَ (to worship): from the root أ-ل-ه, the base of الهٔ itself, carrying the senses of seeking refuge, being bewildered, and surrendering completely. It is what a baby does turning to its mother for milk — and what a soul does when it finally meets the one who holds it entirely."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'linguistic meaning of allah', 'student:ec6068654e66a22d', 2, 'By the negation and the destruction of these self created idols, emer…', 'By the negation and the destruction of these self created idols, emerges the understanding of Allah', '<p>The heart is not a blank container — its shape is worn into it by longing itself. Every <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> we construct, every career we prostrate ourselves to and every beloved we lose our minds over, is the heart pressing something against its own contours and calling it a fit. When those things are torn away, the contour does not disappear. <strong>What remains is the shape of the longing itself.</strong></p><p>The meanings of the word <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> — the one you worship, lean on, turn to in desperation, obsess over, are astonished by — are not a random list. They are the architecture of the human heart. Self-created idols do not invent these capacities; <strong>they merely hijack them,</strong> pouring themselves into a mould they did not make.</p><p>This is what makes <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا اِلَہَ اِلَّا اللَّہُ</span> so precise. The <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا</span> is not just grammar. It is a deliberate act of clearing — removing the career, the beloved, the numbing habit, the mirror held endlessly up to our own self (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نفس</span>). Each removal does not deepen the emptiness; it sharpens the outline. And when every pretender is gone, the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِلَّا</span> points to the only being who satisfies every definition of <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> <strong>without failing even one</strong> — who can be loved with the full force of the heart''s capacity and will not diminish, who can be leaned on completely and will not buckle, who can be turned to in desperation and will always receive.</p><p>The story of Prophet <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أیوب</span> makes this luminous. At the height of his suffering, he found that Allah was speaking to him every single day. When his illness was cured and the conversation grew quieter, he grieved. He had discovered the one <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> whose presence is more sustaining than health itself. The idol of comfort had been replaced by <strong>the source of all comfort</strong>.</p><p>Ismaili teaching distinguishes between divinity (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الهية</span>) and creation as two fundamental modes of existence. Everything we ever idolised belongs to creation. Only Allah is <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الهية</span> itself — not a being who happens to deserve worship, but the very ground from which the concept of <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الہ</span> draws its meaning. Negating the created does not leave absence. It reveals <strong>what was always there before the idols were built.</strong></p>', '["الهية: from root أ-ل-ه. الهية is the abstract noun for divinity — the quality of being what the heart was always built to turn toward. The root already carries the meanings of worship, desperate longing, and passionate love; الهية concentrates all of them into a single ontological category. Only Allah possesses it in its absolute, uncreated form.", "هوى: from root ه-و-ي, meaning to fall or plunge downward. هوى is the gravitational pull of desire — the heart''s descent toward what it craves. The root tells the whole story: desire is a falling, and whatever the heart falls toward becomes, by definition, its الہ.", "نفس: from root ن-ف-س, meaning to breathe. نفس is both breath and self — the living, moving interior of a person. The connection to breath is exact: the نفس is never still, always either expanding toward Allah or turning inward to feed its own hungers.", "توحید: from root و-ح-د, meaning to be alone, singular, one. توحيد is not merely a belief in oneness but the act of making one — unifying the heart''s allegiances until only Allah remains at the centre. The root holds both the statement and the practice inside a single word."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'linguistic meaning of rehma', 'student:d5594a13764ad1d1', 1, 'All word that match this pattern will always possess these 3 characte…', 'All word that match this pattern will always possess these 3 characteristics. They will be extreme; they will be immediate; and they will be temporary.', '<p>The teacher''s examples — hunger, anger, thirst — use the body''s inability to sustain intensity as the reason for temporariness. The same holds for a divine attribute, but through a different mechanism. <strong>The limit is not in Allah; it is in creation.</strong> The <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فعلان</span> pattern describes the quality as it lands on, and is experienced by, whoever receives it — not what the source can sustain.</p><p>Think of the sun. It neither tires of shining nor is diminished by being felt. But you cannot stare into it without going blind. Allah''s <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمة</span>, in its most intense mode, is not constrained by any limit in Him — but creation can only absorb so much before it overwhelms the faculties that allow us to recognize and benefit from it.</p><p>The etymology of <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمن</span> reveals this most beautifully. The root <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ر</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ح</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">م</span> gives us <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحم</span> — the womb — the most total and unconditional care we know. <strong>It provides everything: warmth, nourishment, protection, without asking anything in return.</strong> And yet the child must leave — not because the womb''s love ended, but because continued dwelling would prevent the very growth the love intended. The intensity withdraws for the sake of the one being loved.</p><p>This is precisely why <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span> and <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحیم</span> always appear together:</p><ul><li><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span>: extreme, immediate, and temporary — like the womb''s total immersion</p></li><li><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحیم</span>: permanent and enduring — the mother''s love after the child is born</p></li></ul><p>Neither alone is sufficient. One gives the overwhelming present burst; the other gives the quiet guarantee that holds across time. Together, they are <strong>the only honest way to describe love that is both fully present and always there.</strong></p><p>Ibn Abbas said simply: Allah is <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span> in this life and <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحیم</span> in the next. The intense mercy pours on everyone now — believer and disbeliever alike, as rain falls on all. That distinction is not about Allah''s capacity. It is about the conditions under which creation receives.</p>', '["رحمن: from root ر-ح-م (رحم). رحم means the womb — the origin and enclosure of new life. Real Quranic derivatives: رَّحِيم, رَحْمَة, رَّحْمَٰن. The root carries a tenderness so total it holds the beloved entirely within itself. رحمن adds the فعلان pattern: extreme in force, immediate in action, and — like the womb''s own care — temporary in its most intense form.", "رحیم: same root ر-ح-م, shaped by the فعيل pattern (as in كريم, حكيم, جميل). This pattern marks a settled, permanent disposition — a quality that defines the one who carries it, not merely an action happening right now. رحيم is the love that outlasts every moment: abiding, steady, and structurally guaranteed."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'linguistic meaning of rehma', 'student:38970a9b0d62eabe', 2, 'the love and care of Allah will go away for disbelievers on the day o…', 'the love and care of Allah will go away for disbelievers on the day of judgment', '<p>The apparent contradiction dissolves once we understand what <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span> is saying at the grammatical level. Arabic has a phonetic pattern — the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آن</span> form — that always carries three qualities together: extreme in degree, immediate in time, and temporary by nature. <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غضبان</span> means furiously angry, right this moment, but no one sustains that fury forever. <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جوعان</span> means ravenously hungry, right now, but hunger ends. <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span> works the same way: overwhelming, immediate, and not designed to last indefinitely.</p><p><strong>The universality of <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span> is real</strong> — it falls on everyone without discrimination, the way rain falls from the sky. The believer drinks that rain and the denier drinks it equally. We are told:</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَتِی وَسِعَتْ عَلَیٰ کُلِّ شَیْءٍ</span> My mercy encompasses all things. Al-A''raf 7:156</p><p>None of that universality contradicts the temporary character baked into the word. The door is open to everyone during this life; the invitation never expires here. Temporary does not mean stingy — it means the intense, overwhelming surge of mercy belongs to this world specifically.</p><p>When someone asked Ibn Abbas about the difference between <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span> and <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحیم</span>, he answered with quiet precision: in this life, Allah is <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span> for everyone; in the next life, he is <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحیم</span> for the believers. <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحیم</span> follows the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">یم</span> pattern — the same pattern as <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كريم</span> and <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حكيم</span> — which marks something permanent and deeply characteristic, not immediate but lasting. When the universal surge of <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span> concludes at the end of this world, <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحیم</span> continues — but for those who chose to walk through the open door while the light was on.</p><p>A door is wide open to everyone during the day. The invitation falls equally on anyone standing outside. But it is still a daytime door — when day ends, it closes. Together they leave no gap: <strong>the pair covers both this life and the next</strong>, the overwhelming and the enduring, the universal and the particular.</p>', '["رحمن: from root ر-ح-م, whose core meaning is warmth and tenderness — directly connected to رَحِم (the womb), the most intimate shelter of care that exists. The آن suffix drives the meaning to its extreme: intense, immediate, and temporary, just as غضبان means fury at its peak right now, impossible to sustain forever. Derivatives: رَحْمَة, رَحِمَ.", "رحيم: same root ر-ح-م, but the يم suffix — shared with كَرِيم (generous) and حَكِيم (wise) — marks a quality that is settled and enduring rather than a surge. Where رحمن names an overwhelming act of mercy happening now, رحيم names a permanent identity: one for whom mercy is simply who they are, reliably and without condition."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'quranic friendship', 'student:f0b8cccd61f3f94f', 1, 'Who is that ultimate صَدِيقٍ حَمِيمٍۢ that believers will have access…', 'Who is that ultimate صَدِيقٍ حَمِيمٍۢ that believers will have access to? The شفاعۃ of Rasul Allah ﷺ InshaAllah.', '<p>The passage presents the people of hell lamenting the absence of a <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صديق حميم</span> on Judgment Day, then asks who fills that role for believers. The answer given is the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شفاعة</span> of the Prophet <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>. But these are not two separate things — the friendship and the intercession are the same love, and <strong>understanding that changes how we read the whole passage</strong>.</p><p>Think about what a <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صديق</span> actually does. He tells you the truth you would rather not hear. He has no hidden agenda and never abandons you, no matter the cost. The Prophet <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> spent an entire lifetime doing exactly this — warning, teaching, and correcting his community when it was painful and unpopular. Every teaching he left behind was the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صديق</span> function in action. <strong>The friendship was already built long before Judgment Day arrived.</strong></p><p>Now consider the word <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حميم</span>. It carries the image of intense, penetrating heat — that inner warmth that rises in you toward someone you love deeply. The Quran describes the Prophet''s feeling toward his community as <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَءُوفٌ رَّحِيمٌ</span>, intensely compassionate and profoundly merciful. That warmth did not die with him. <strong>Intercession is what that heat looks like when the moment finally demands everything.</strong></p><p>The Quran makes the connection explicit. The people of hell list their losses in one breath:</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَمَا لَنَا مِن شَفِعِينَ وَلَا صَدِيقٍ حَمِيمٍۢ</span> We have no one to intercede for us, and no close, sincere friend.</p><p>Ash-Shu''ara 26:100-101</p><p><strong>To have no <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شافع</span> is to have no <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صديق حميم</span>.</strong> The Quran treats them as the same reality. The <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صديق</span> who spoke truth in this life is the same one who stands beside you when the account is opened.</p>', '["صديق: from root ص-د-ق, meaning to be completely true and to fulfill a claim without reservation. صَادِق means one who is truthful; صِدْق is truthfulness as a noun. The form فَعِيل signals that the quality saturates the person entirely — not someone who tells the truth sometimes, but one whose whole orientation toward you is free of any hidden layer.", "حميم: from root ح-م-م, meaning penetrating heat. The Quran uses حَمِيم for scalding water and يَحْمُوم for a dense, suffocating smoke — both forms of heat that cannot be escaped or ignored. Applied to closeness, the root describes an intimacy so intense it warms you from the inside, a presence that almost burns with affection toward the one you love.", "شفاعة: from root ش-ف-ع, meaning to make even by pairing, to add one thing to another. شَفْع is an even number, the opposite of وِتْر. A شافع joins themselves to the one who stands alone, so they face the weight together rather than apart. Intercession is, at its root, the refusal to let someone stand unaccompanied."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'quranic friendship', 'student:5758dc1c620f4afa', 2, 'the hate fills your heart so much that you cannot even remember their…', 'the hate fills your heart so much that you cannot even remember their name. So the verse says فُلَانًا خَلِيلًۭا', '<h3><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فُلَان</span> and the Deliberate Blank</h3><p>The word <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فُلَان</span> is Arabic''s standard placeholder for an unnamed person — the equivalent of &quot;so-and-so.&quot; When the Quran writes <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوَيْلَتَىٰ لَيْتَنِى لَمْ أَتَّخِذْ فُلَانًا خَلِيلًۭا</span>, it reaches for this conventional pronoun deliberately: <strong>the party is unnamed so the warning belongs to every reader, in every era.</strong> The wrongdoer is not a fixed historical figure — he is anyone who chose the wrong <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلیل</span>.</p><p>The teacher''s reading — that hatred burned so hot it erased even the name — is not wrong in its emotional logic. <strong>The Quran''s deliberate namelessness performs the same emotional work.</strong> Whether the name is gone because regret consumed it, or because Allah chose to leave it blank so it could stand for every name, the effect is identical: the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلیل</span> who once lived inside you is now ash, and the warmth of that friendship has curdled into bile.</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فُلَان</span> actually sharpens the anguish rather than softening it. A named enemy gives the wrongdoer something to point at — a face, a story, a defence. But <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فُلَان</span> strips all of that away. <strong>The universality is the punishment.</strong></p><p>The honest answer holds both truths at once. Grammatically, <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فُلَان</span> is a conventional placeholder, older than this verse. Rhetorically, it performs the very forgetting the teacher describes. The Quran, in its <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بلاغة</span>, chose a word that is <strong>at once a grammatical fact and an emotional verdict</strong> — and that is why a verse about a nameless friend cuts deeper than a verse about a named one ever could.</p><p>---</p><h3>Word Roots</h3><ul><li><p><strong><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلیل</span>:</strong> from the root <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خ</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ل</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ل</span> (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلل</span>), whose core meaning is a gap or penetrating space between things. <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خِلَال</span> means the interstices — the small spaces between objects; <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُلَّة</span> is the deep bond that fills them. So <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلیل</span> is someone whose love has worked its way into every crack of your heart — not beside it, but woven through it.</p></li><li><p><strong><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فُلَان</span>:</strong> no single agreed trilateral root; classical grammarians treat it as a frozen placeholder pronoun. One proposed connection is <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَلَ</span>, suggesting severance from specific identity — something cut free from a name. The word''s power is its blankness: it names no one and therefore names everyone who has ever been betrayed by the wrong friend.</p></li></ul>', NULL);
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'quranic friendship', 'student:f966288df16a6f52', 3, 'we''re all tied together by the same rope of عہد شریف', 'we''re all tied together by the same rope of عہد شریف', '<p>The passage describes the whole Ismaili community as a single compatible companion (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قرین</span>) — tied together like two camels by one rope: the Noble Covenant (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عہد شریف</span>). It is <strong>the sacred pledge every Ismaili believer makes with the Imam of the Time</strong> — a living spiritual contract between a human soul and its appointed guide.</p><p>The covenant is formally renewed in the covenant ceremony (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">میثاق</span>), the moment of a believer''s initiation into the community. But its roots reach back before creation itself. Allah gathered all souls before they entered the world and asked:</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَإِذْ أَخَذَ رَبُّكَ مِن بَنِى ءَادَمَ مِن ظُهُورِهِمْ ذُرِّيَّتَهُمْ وَأَشْهَدَهُمْ عَلَىٰٓ أَنفُسِهِمْ أَلَسْتُ بِرَبِّكُمْ ۖ قَالُوا۟ بَلَىٰ</span> And when your Lord took from the children of Adam — from their loins — their descendants and made them testify of themselves: &quot;Am I not your Lord?&quot; They said: &quot;Yes indeed!&quot; Al-A''raf 7:172</p><p>Every soul said yes. The covenant ceremony (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">میثاق</span>) is the moment that ancient yes is <strong>renewed consciously</strong> — by a person who now understands what they are pledging.</p><p>The Noble Covenant (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عہد شریف</span>) commits those who take it to three things:</p><ul><li><p>Recognition of the Imam of the Time as the divinely appointed guide — protective guardianship (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ولایۃ</span>)</p></li><li><p>Obedience to his authority in both the outer and inner dimensions of the faith</p></li><li><p>A life lived in sincere service to the community and to Allah''s commands</p></li></ul><p>This is why belonging to the community is not mere membership — it is <strong>a spiritual yoke that cannot be removed at convenience</strong>. When the Imam moves, the believer must follow; when the community is in need, no one bound by this pledge can simply stand aside. The companionship (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قرین</span>) the Noble Covenant (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عہد شریف</span>) creates was not chosen for comfort. It was sealed before this world began.</p>', '["شریف (noble): from root ش-ر-ف, meaning to be high, to stand at an elevated point. The root gives شَرَفَة — a balcony or parapet that looks down over all below. When the covenant is called شریف, it occupies the highest spiritual station possible: a pledge directed not among equals, but upward toward divine authority.", "قرین (companion): from root ق-ر-ن. قَرْن named the rope binding two camels side by side, and then the people of one generation bound by shared time. Both meanings share one image: being yoked — to another creature, to an era, or here, to the living covenant that ties the entire community to its Imam."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'the stages of love', 'student:44dee1f5d17b976f', 1, 'In these previous states there was still some نفس involved. There was…', 'In these previous states there was still some نفس involved. There was still some selfish reasons for loving the beloved', '<p>In the first four stages of love, every movement of the heart is ultimately a movement toward the lover''s own experience. Even something as tender as <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَرام</span> — the clinging ache where a person stares at photographs and rereads old messages — is, at its core, a form of self-management. The beloved''s image becomes a medicine the lover administers to themselves, a way of dulling the pain of distance.</p><p>This is what it means when we say the self (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نفس</span>) is still present in these stages. Whenever the lover asks, even silently — <em>what do I feel near this person, what fills me when I think of them?</em> — that is <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نفس</span> at work. There is nothing cruel in this. It is simply not yet love directed outward toward another.</p><p>What shifts at pure love (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وُد</span>) is the direction of the gaze. The love has been refined — <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَفوَۃُ الْمَحَبَّۃ</span>, cleared of sediment — until the lover''s own hungers no longer cloud it. <strong>The question is no longer what the beloved gives, but what the lover can give.</strong> This is why pure love does not change when beauty fades, when the beloved grows ill, when usefulness disappears. The wife who stays when her husband''s face is scarred, the husband who remains though his wife can no longer serve him — these are people whose love was never built on what the beloved was producing for them.</p><p>The test is simple and pitiless: what happens to the love when the beloved can no longer give the lover anything? If it fades, it was not directed at the beloved at all — it was directed at the self, with the beloved as its instrument. That is the moment we know the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نفس</span> has finally stepped aside.</p>', '["نفس (self/soul): root ن-ف-س, connected to the act of breathing. تَنَفَّسَ in the Quran means the dawn exhaled — the morning releasing its first breath. From this physical intimacy of breath comes نَفْس: the animating interior of a person, the self that belongs irreducibly to the one living it. What breathes cannot be shared; neither can the نفس.", "وُد (pure love): root و-د-د. The doubling of the final letter carries persistence — something that keeps returning warmth, like an ember that cannot quite go out. وَدُود appears in the Quran as a name of Allah: the One whose love is constant and self-giving, never exhausted by the needs of those it reaches toward. مَوَدَّة is the love Allah placed between husband and wife.", "صَفوَۃُ (the purest part): root ص-ف-و, meaning for water or sky to become clear — sediment settling, leaving only what is clean. صَفَاةٌ is a smooth bare rock with nothing clinging to its surface. صَفوَۃُ الشَّيء is the clearest, truest portion of a thing after everything murky has been allowed to drop away."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'the stages of love', 'student:735220f20775ac59', 2, 'It kills itself so that its beloved can love', 'It kills itself so that its beloved can love', '<p><em>Researched outside this library — the knowledge base carries nothing on this passage. Sources are listed at the end.</em></p><p>In the powerful image of the vine and the tree used to explain the concept of ‘<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عشق</span>’ (passionate love), the roles can be understood through the effect one has on the other. The vine, which wraps itself around the tree, represents the lover (‘<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عاشق</span>’), and the tree, which is enveloped and ultimately consumed, represents the beloved (‘<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">معشوق</span>’). This analogy beautifully and intensely captures the all-consuming nature of this elevated form of love.</p><p>The phrase that the tree &quot;kills itself so that its beloved can love&quot; is a poetic way of describing the <strong>complete self-annihilation of the lover</strong>. In this stage of love, the lover''s own identity, desires, and very existence are willingly sacrificed for the sake of the beloved. It is not that the vine, as the beloved, is actively &quot;taking&quot; in a selfish way; rather, the lover, as the tree, gives everything, dissolving its own being into the identity of the one it loves. The tree''s life force is drawn out and transferred to the vine, symbolizing how the lover''s entire focus and essence are poured into the beloved until nothing of the self remains.</p><p>This is not a destructive act in a negative sense, but the ultimate expression of devotion. The lover finds their own fulfillment and purpose in this effacement, enjoying the &quot;suffering&quot; and constriction because it is for the beloved. The &quot;death&quot; of the tree, therefore, is the &quot;life&quot; of the love itself. It signifies that the lover''s ego has been completely overcome, and their existence has merged with that of the beloved, which is the ultimate goal of ‘<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عشق</span>’.</p><p>***</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عشق</span> (passionate love): from the root <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ع</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ش</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ق</span>, connected to the noun ‘<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عشقة</span>’, the name for a type of ivy. The imagery is of a vine that clings to, cleaves to, and envelops a tree so completely that it can cause the tree to wither. This illustrates how this intense form of love surrounds and pierces the heart of the lover (‘<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عاشق</span>’), leading to a state of self-effacement before the beloved (‘<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">معشوق</span>’).</p><p><strong>Sources consulted</strong></p><ul><li><p>goodreads.com</p></li><li><p>ishqybusiness.com</p></li><li><p>facebook.com</p></li><li><p>ranasafvi.com</p></li><li><p>quora.com</p></li><li><p>wikipedia.org</p></li></ul>', NULL);
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'the stages of love', 'student:8b55ff970111654a', 3, 'The تیم is a word used for a slave, but its not at the level of an عبد', 'The تیم is a word used for a slave, but its not at the level of an عبد', '<p>The two stages differ not in the fact of service but in the quality of the will behind it. At the stage of <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تیم</span>, the lover has been so overtaken by love that their whole orientation shifts toward the beloved, and something in the heart cries out with genuine eagerness: <strong>&quot;What can I do for you?&quot;</strong> The joy is in the offering itself — in finding ways to be of use. The lover is still initiating, still reaching toward the beloved from heartfelt desire.</p><p>What lifts <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبد</span> higher is not a greater willingness to serve but the <strong>complete surrender of one''s own will</strong>. The lover no longer decides how to serve, because the word of the beloved has become their only instruction. When the beloved says &quot;jump,&quot; the lover does not weigh the request — they ask only &quot;how high?&quot; That unconditional obedience is what the root of <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبد</span> carries in its very bones.</p><p>This is why <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبد</span> became the most exalted title of the prophet <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>. On the night of the ascension, Allah did not call him prophet or messenger — he called him <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبده</span>, <strong>His slave</strong>. The poet asks: &quot;You claim to love your God and yet you disobey him?&quot; Where <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبودیۃ</span> is real, disobedience becomes inconceivable.</p><p>So the distance between these two stations is the distance between a servant who rushes forward and a self that has <strong>quietly ceased to exist</strong>. <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تیم</span> is the lover asking how they can help; <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبد</span> is the lover who has stopped asking, because their own wanting no longer speaks.</p>', '["تیم (devoted slave): from the root ت-م-م (تمم), meaning to be complete, to bring something to fullness — as in تَمَام, wholeness itself. When this root flows into تیم, it points to a devotion that has reached totality: the heart is entirely given over to the beloved, holding nothing back, completed by the one it loves.", "عبد (slave, worshipper): from the root ع-ب-د (عبد), holding slavery and worship as a single meaning. In the Quran, عَبَدَ is to worship, عَابِد is the one who worships, عَبْد the slave. At the root''s core is a being who belongs entirely to another — will, movement, and existence all oriented toward the master."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'the stages of love', 'student:4a391c5d7063e279', 4, 'This is the title of the prophet that is greater than the prophet of…', 'This is the title of the prophet that is greater than the prophet of رسول', '<h3><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رسول</span> and <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبد</span> — Messenger and Slave</h3><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رسول</span> names a function — the one who carries a message. A messenger is defined by his task: deliver these words, complete this mission. The role is noble, but <strong>it is about what you do, not who you are</strong> to the one who sent you.</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبد</span> tells a different story. In the ten stages of love we have been walking through, <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تعبد</span> is the stage of complete, unconditional obedience — where the lover asks no questions and needs no reasons, because the pleasure of the beloved is reason enough. To be called <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبد</span> by Allah is not to be diminished; it is to be claimed. <strong>The language of belonging, not assignment.</strong></p><p>Consider what Allah chose when He wished to describe the Prophet at his most exalted moment — the night of the ascension, when no human had ever been taken so close:</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَنَ ٱلَّذِىٓ أَسْرَىٰ بِعَبْدِهِ لَيْلًۭا</span> Glorious is the one who took his slave on a journey in the night. Al-Isra 17:1</p><p>Not &quot;His messenger.&quot; Not &quot;His prophet.&quot; His slave — because at that moment, the relationship was not about a task to be performed. <strong>It was about an intimacy too sacred for any other word.</strong></p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رسول</span> implies distance: there is a sender, a message, a recipient somewhere else. <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبد</span> implies closeness — you belong entirely to the one who possesses you, and that possession, when it is Allah''s, is the highest station a soul can occupy. Love at its deepest moves from doing to being, from carrying out the beloved''s wishes to becoming so absorbed in the beloved that no self remains to negotiate with. The Quran''s choice was not accidental: <strong>the Prophet, in that supreme moment, was not merely Allah''s instrument — he was Allah''s own.</strong></p><p>---</p>', NULL);
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'what is worship?', 'student:21601e226f070fab', 1, 'His magnificence enables the capability of disobedience within his cr…', 'His magnificence enables the capability of disobedience within his creation', '<p>Free will (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إرادة</span>) is a structural feature built into the creature — <strong>it is the machinery by which a person can choose.</strong> Divine forsakenness (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خذلان</span>) is something else: the moment when Allah, in His infinite completeness, steps back and allows the creature to proceed unsupported by His divine aid.</p><p>True magnificence (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جلال</span>) is self-sufficient. A glory that could only sustain itself by compelling admiration would be fragile — not truly magnificent at all. The sun does not dim because someone closes their eyes to it. <strong>Allah''s magnificence remains entirely undiminished by those who turn from Him</strong> — which is precisely why His greatness is the ground on which disobedience becomes possible: an infinite grandeur has no need to compel.</p><p>Free will explains how a creature can choose; magnificence explains why the choice was left open. If worship were compelled, it would not be worship (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبادة</span>) — that love-laden, freely given devotion. A bow made under duress is performance, and <strong>performance was never what Allah asked of us.</strong></p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خذلان</span>, then, is not a failure of divine love — <strong>it is the shadow that real love casts.</strong> When a father steps back and lets his child walk without holding his hand, it is the fullness of his trust, not its absence, that makes the stepping-back possible. Allah''s magnificence is so encompassing that it can allow the creature to stumble. Free will is the door; <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خذلان</span> is what happens when the Creator, in the vastness of His greatness, chooses not to hold it shut.</p>', '["خذلان (divine forsakenness): from the root خ-ذ-ل, meaning to withdraw support and leave one stranded. خذل originally described a companion who abandons his fellow mid-battle. Its verbal noun, خذلان, names the state of being left without divine aid — the creature carried forward only by its own limited momentum.", "جلال (magnificence): from the root ج-ل-ل, meaning to be immeasurably great or majestic. The root captures something so vast it overwhelms the gaze. Paired with جمال (beauty), جلال names the awe-inducing face of the divine — a greatness before which even the faculty of understanding falls short.", "إرادة (will, intention): from the root ر-و-د, meaning to seek or move purposefully toward something. The root pictures a deliberate going-toward. إرادة is the faculty of directed intention — the inner reach toward a chosen end. In the creature it is derived and dependent; in Allah it is absolute."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'what is worship?', 'student:19fcc1a39de9ca57', 2, 'which is called خذلان but he is not content with it', 'which is called خذلان but he is not content with it', '<p>The passage uses <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خذلان</span> to name something very specific: <strong>Allah''s own act of withdrawing His divine support</strong> from a creature — not the creature''s disobedience itself, and not some neutral capacity sitting passively inside us waiting to tip either way.</p><p>Think of it this way. Allah''s support — His grace, His <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">توفيق</span> — is like a hand steadying a candle in the wind. When that hand is present, the flame holds. When Allah withdraws that steadying hand, the wind of temptation can extinguish it. <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خذلان</span> is the withdrawal of the hand, not the wind, and not the darkness that follows.</p><p>This is why the passage says Allah allows disobedience through <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خذلان</span> but is <strong>not content with it</strong>. He permits it — His magnificence is so vast that it contains even the possibility of turning away from Him — but He does not desire it. A king powerful enough to imprison every rebel may still choose not to, yet his heart is not at peace with the rebellion.</p><p>Disobedience is not described as a crime first — it is described as a <em>contradiction</em>. If <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبادة</span> is a lover''s obedience, then <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خذلان</span> is the moment the lover stops listening, not because the beloved became unworthy, but because the divine support that kept the heart attentive was graciously withdrawn. The creature was given room, and in that room, it failed.</p><p>Allah did not create us for that failure:</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَا خَلَقْتُ ٱلْجِنَّ وَٱلْإِنسَ إِلَّا لِيَعْبُدُونِ</span> And I created not the jinns and humans except that they should worship Me. Al-Dhariyat 51:56</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خذلان</span> is therefore the shadow side of our freedom — the proof that <strong>our obedience is chosen, not compelled</strong>. A love that cannot be refused is not love at all; it is mechanism.</p>', '["خذلان (divine abandonment): from the root خ-ذ-ل, to leave someone without help precisely when they need it. خاذل is the one who abandons; خذلان is the act of withdrawal itself. Its opposite is توفيق — divine assistance. The root carries the sting of a companion who turns away at the critical moment, making خذلان not indifference but a deliberate, purposeful withholding."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'womb - the source of mercy', 'student:44929a3a0e309976', 1, 'There is this famous HADEES in which Allah is actually speaking direc…', 'There is this famous HADEES in which Allah is actually speaking directly to the womb.', '<p>This narration belongs to a well-attested hadith found in Sahih al-Bukhari, narrated by Abu Hurayra, and it carries all the marks of a <strong>hadith qudsi</strong> — a sacred narration in which the Prophet <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> reports the direct words of Allah, rather than his own prophetic teaching. In Bukhari''s collection, the Prophet <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> recounts that the womb (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحم</span>) rose before Allah after creation and held fast to Him, and Allah replied to it, establishing its dignity and its tie to His own name. A second, complementary narration — found in Sunan Abu Dawud and corroborated in al-Hakim''s Mustadrak — carries the specific wording closest to what the passage quotes: that Allah said to the womb, <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شققت لك اسماً من اسمي</span>, meaning He derived a name for it from His own name, linking <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحم</span> to <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن</span>.</p><p>The narration is treated as hadith qudsi in character even where individual collections do not label it as such in their chapter headings. Both versions share the same essential revelation: Allah made the womb a living symbol of His own mercy, naming it from Himself so that every human being who passes through it carries, without knowing it, the first impression of divine love.</p><p>Slight differences in wording between collections are normal for hadith qudsi narrations — the meaning is preserved consistently across both.</p>', '["حديث قدسي (sacred hadith): حديث comes from the root ح-د-ث, meaning to occur or to speak — something that happened and was then told. قدسي comes from قُدْس, meaning holiness or divine sanctity. Together, hadith qudsi names a narration whose content originates directly with Allah, even though it travels to us through the Prophet ﷺ rather than through the Quran itself.", "شَقَّ (to derive, to split): from the root ش-ق-ق, meaning to cleave or to open something apart. When Allah says He شَقَّ a name from His own name, the image is of something being cut from a living whole — not a copy made at a distance, but a piece drawn directly from the source, so that the derived word carries the life of the original within it."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('surah-al-fateha', 'womb - the source of mercy', 'student:2c627696dfbf867c', 2, 'All of شریعۃ is just a reminder of that. Because as soon as any decen…', 'All of شریعۃ is just a reminder of that. Because as soon as any decent human being is presented and reminded of the love, care, concern and mercy of another person, he naturally responds with praise and gratitude.', '<p>The passage tells us we are <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">انسان</span> — and the Arabic word carries within it a classical trace: one root scholars point to is <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِسْيَان</span>, forgetting. We are not, by nature, creatures of malice; we are <strong>creatures of forgetfulness</strong>, and this single fact unlocks what <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شریعۃ</span> is for. He gave us a structure woven into every corner of life not from distrust, but because He knows that without it, we will forget.</p><p>Look at the rules of punishment in <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شریعۃ</span>. At first glance, they seem to speak of severity, not love. But consider what they are protecting: life, honour, the family — the very things Allah wrapped around us as expressions of His <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span>. <strong>A punishment in sharia is the womb wall</strong> — the boundary a mother''s body draws around the child so the outside world cannot reach it before its time.</p><p>The laws of contract carry the same mercy into the marketplace. When a deal is written down and witnessed, the person with less power cannot be crushed afterward. When debts are recorded, no one can deny what was agreed. This is Allah saying, through the structure of law: <strong>I have not forgotten the vulnerable</strong>. <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمۃ</span> does not only descend as a feeling from above; it is inscribed into the horizontal ties between people, so that mercy is present even when no one is looking up.</p><p>Inheritance law reaches that mercy forward through time. When a person dies, the rules are already in place to ensure that no widow is left without provision and no orphan without a share. Every domain of <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شریعۃ</span> — the courtroom, the contract table, the division of an estate — is another surface where <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحمن</span> keeps showing up. <strong>The whole of law is structured remembrance</strong>: not a reminder to fear Him, but a reminder that He never stopped caring.</p>', '["شریعۃ: from the root ش-ر-ع. Lane''s Lexicon gives شَرَعَتِ الدَّوَابُّ فِي الْمَاءِ — the animals entered the water and drank. The شَرِيعَة is the clear path that leads down to the watering place. Law named for a road to water: not a barrier keeping you out, but the way that brings the thirsty to what sustains them.", "رحمۃ: from the root ر-ح-م. The رَحِم is the womb — Lane describes it as the place of origin and the receptacle of the young. Every form — رَحْمَة, رَحِيم, رَّحْمَٰن — grows from this one root image: life held and sustained inside a space of total, unearned care."]');
